edia.org/wiki/Miyaura_borylation"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Miyaura borylation"},{"x":"Stille coupling"},{"x":"Tsuji–Trost reaction"},{"x":"Sonogashira coupling"}],"fig":{"r":[585],"cond":["B₂pin₂, Pd(dppf)Cl₂ (cat.), KOAc, dioxane, 80 °C"],"p":649},"ex":"Miyaura borylation: Pd-catalysed conversion of an aryl halide into an aryl boronic ester with B₂pin₂. The weak base KOAc stops the product from undergoing a Suzuki coupling.","mc":["Oxidative addition of the aryl bromide to Pd(0).","Acetate replaces bromide on palladium; the Pd–OAc bond activates the complex for transmetallation with B₂pin₂.","Reductive elimination gives the aryl Bpin and regenerates Pd(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Miyaura borylation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miyaura_borylation"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":650},{"m":651},{"m":652},{"m":653}],"t":"Fukuyama coupling","fig":{"r":[654],"cond":["IZn(CH₂)₃CO₂Et, PdCl₂(PPh₃)₂ (cat.), toluene, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"Palladium couples a thioester with an organozinc reagent, giving a ketone. Organozincs are mild, so esters, ketones and halides elsewhere survive, and the ketone is not attacked again. With Et₃SiH instead of the zinc reagent the thioester is reduced to the aldehyde (Fukuyama reduction).","mc":["Pd(0) inserts into the acyl C–S bond (oxidative addition), giving an acylpalladium thiolate.","The alkyl group moves from zinc to palladium (transmetallation).","Reductive elimination joins the acyl and alkyl groups, giving the ketone and Pd(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Fukuyama coupling: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fukuyama_coupling#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Fukuyama coupling"},{"x":"Hiyama–Denmark coupling"},{"x":"Kumada coupling"},{"x":"Buchwald–Hartwig amination"}],"fig":{"r":[654],"cond":["IZn(CH₂)₃CO₂Et, PdCl₂(PPh₃)₂ (cat.), toluene, rt"],"p":655},"ex":"Fukuyama coupling: Palladium couples a thioester with an organozinc reagent, giving a ketone. Organozincs are mild, so esters, ketones and halides elsewhere survive, and the ketone is not attacked again. With Et₃SiH instead of the zinc reagent the thioester is reduced to the aldehyde (Fukuyama reduction).","mc":["Pd(0) inserts into the acyl C–S bond (oxidative addition), giving an acylpalladium thiolate.","The alkyl group moves from zinc to palladium (transmetallation).","Reductive elimination joins the acyl and alkyl groups, giving the ketone and Pd(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Fukuyama coupling: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fukuyama_coupling#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":656},{"m":657},{"m":658},{"m":659}],"t":"Hiyama–Denmark coupling","fig":{"r":[660,661],"cond":["Pd(dba)₂ (cat.), KOSiMe₃, DME, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"Palladium couples an organosilanol (as its silanolate) with an aryl or vinyl halide, with no fluoride activator. Without fluoride, silyl protecting groups survive and the reaction scales well. The alkene geometry of the silanol is kept.","mc":["Pd(0) inserts into the aryl–I bond (oxidative addition).","The silanolate binds palladium through oxygen (Si–O–Pd), and the vinyl group moves from silicon to palladium.","Reductive elimination gives the aryl alkene and Pd(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Hiyama–Denmark coupling: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hiyama_coupling#Hiyama%E2%80%93Denmark_coupling_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Hiyama–Denmark coupling"},{"x":"Rosenmund–von Braun reaction"},{"x":"Heck reaction"},{"x":"Ullmann reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[660,661],"cond":["Pd(dba)₂ (cat.), KOSiMe₃, DME, rt"],"p":662},"ex":"Hiyama–Denmark coupling: Palladium couples an organosilanol (as its silanolate) with an aryl or vinyl halide, with no fluoride activator. Without fluoride, silyl protecting groups survive and the reaction scales well. The alkene geometry of the silanol is kept.","mc":["Pd(0) inserts into the aryl–I bond (oxidative addition).","The silanolate binds palladium through oxygen (Si–O–Pd), and the vinyl group moves from silicon to palladium.","Reductive elimination gives the aryl alkene and Pd(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Hiyama–Denmark coupling: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hiyama_coupling#Hiyama%E2%80%93Denmark_coupling_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":663},{"m":664},{"m":665},{"m":666}],"t":"Ullmann reaction","fig":{"r":[667,667],"cond":["Cu (bronze, excess), 200 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Copper couples two aryl halides into a symmetrical biaryl at high temperature. Electron-poor ortho-substituted aryl iodides work best. The copper-catalysed C–O and C–N couplings of aryl halides are the 'Ullmann-type' reactions (Ullmann condensation).","mc":["Copper inserts into the aryl–I bond, giving an arylcopper species.","A second aryl iodide adds oxidatively, giving a diaryl copper(III) species.","Reductive elimination forms the aryl–aryl bond."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ullmann reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ullmann_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Ullmann reaction"},{"x":"Fukuyama coupling"},{"x":"Buchwald–Hartwig amination"},{"x":"Hiyama–Denmark coupling"}],"fig":{"r":[667,667],"cond":["Cu (bronze, excess), 200 °C"],"p":668},"ex":"Ullmann reaction: Copper couples two aryl halides into a symmetrical biaryl at high temperature. Electron-poor ortho-substituted aryl iodides work best. The copper-catalysed C–O and C–N couplings of aryl halides are the 'Ullmann-type' reactions (Ullmann condensation).","mc":["Copper inserts into the aryl–I bond, giving an arylcopper species.","A second aryl iodide adds oxidatively, giving a diaryl copper(III) species.","Reductive elimination forms the aryl–aryl bond."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ullmann reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ullmann_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":669},{"m":670},{"m":671},{"m":672}],"t":"Rosenmund–von Braun reaction","fig":{"r":[673],"cond":["CuCN (excess), DMF, reflux"],"veil":true},"ex":"Copper(I) cyanide replaces the halogen of an aryl halide by CN, giving an aryl nitrile. High temperatures and excess CuCN make the work-up hard; catalytic CuI with NaCN is milder. The Sandmeyer reaction makes the same products from diazonium salts.","mc":["The aryl bromide adds oxidatively to copper(I) cyanide, probably giving an aryl copper(III) species.","Reductive elimination joins the aryl group and the cyanide.","This gives the aryl nitrile and CuBr."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Rosenmund–von Braun reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosenmund%E2%80%93von_Braun_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Rosenmund–von Braun reaction"},{"x":"Suzuki–Miyaura coupling"},{"x":"Hiyama–Denmark coupling"},{"x":"Ullmann reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[673],"cond":["CuCN (excess), DMF, reflux"],"p":674},"ex":"Rosenmund–von Braun reaction: Copper(I) cyanide replaces the halogen of an aryl halide by CN, giving an aryl nitrile. High temperatures and excess CuCN make the work-up hard; catalytic CuI with NaCN is milder. The Sandmeyer reaction makes the same products from diazonium salts.","mc":["The aryl bromide adds oxidatively to copper(I) cyanide, probably giving an aryl copper(III) species.","Reductive elimination joins the aryl group and the cyanide.","This gives the aryl nitrile and CuBr."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Rosenmund–von Braun reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosenmund%E2%80%93von_Braun_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":675},{"m":676},{"m":677},{"m":678}],"t":"Kulinkovich reaction","fig":{"r":[679],"cond":["EtMgBr (2 equiv), Ti(Oi-Pr)₄ (cat.), Et₂O, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"An ester, ethylmagnesium bromide and catalytic Ti(Oi-Pr)₄ give a 1-substituted cyclopropanol. A titanacyclopropane, formed from two ethyl groups on titanium with loss of ethane, acts as a 1,2-dicarbanion. Amides give cyclopropylamines (Kulinkovich–de Meijere) and nitriles primary cyclopropylamines (Kulinkovich–Szymoniak).","mc":["Two ethyl groups on titanium undergo β-hydride elimination with loss of ethane, giving a titanacyclopropane.","The titanacyclopropane adds to the ester carbonyl; loss of methoxide gives a ketone still bonded to titanium through its β-carbon.","That Ti–C bond adds to the ketone intramolecularly, closing the cyclopropane; work-up gives the cyclopropanol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Kulinkovich reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kulinkovich_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Kulinkovich reaction"},{"x":"Directed ortho metalation"},{"x":"Wurtz reaction"},{"x":"Nozaki–Hiyama–Kishi reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[679],"cond":["EtMgBr (2 equiv), Ti(Oi-Pr)₄ (cat.), Et₂O, rt"],"p":680},"ex":"Kulinkovich reaction: An ester, ethylmagnesium bromide and catalytic Ti(Oi-Pr)₄ give a 1-substituted cyclopropanol. A titanacyclopropane, formed from two ethyl groups on titanium with loss of ethane, acts as a 1,2-dicarbanion. Amides give cyclopropylamines (Kulinkovich–de Meijere) and nitriles primary cyclopropylamines (Kulinkovich–Szymoniak).","mc":["Two ethyl groups on titanium undergo β-hydride elimination with loss of ethane, giving a titanacyclopropane.","The titanacyclopropane adds to the ester carbonyl; loss of methoxide gives a ketone still bonded to titanium through its β-carbon.","That Ti–C bond adds to the ketone intramolecularly, closing the cyclopropane; work-up gives the cyclopropanol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Kulinkovich reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kulinkovich_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":681},{"m":682},{"m":683},{"m":684}],"t":"Kulinkovich–de Meijere reaction","fig":{"r":[685],"cond":["EtMgBr, Ti(Oi-Pr)₄ (1 equiv), THF, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"An N,N-dialkylamide (best a formamide), a Grignard reagent and a titanium alkoxide give a cyclopropylamine. The dialkylamino group is a poor leaving group, so the titanium intermediate opens to an iminium ion that closes the ring instead. Stoichiometric titanium (or MeTi(Oi-Pr)₃) gives much better yields than catalytic.","mc":["EtMgBr and the titanium alkoxide give a titanacyclopropane, with loss of ethane.","The amide carbonyl inserts into a Ti–C bond, giving an oxatitanacyclopentane.","It opens to an iminium ion and a titanium oxide; the remaining Ti–C bond attacks the iminium carbon, closing the cyclopropylamine."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Kulinkovich–de Meijere reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kulinkovich_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Kulinkovich–de Meijere reaction"},{"x":"Directed ortho metalation"},{"x":"Kulinkovich reaction"},{"x":"Kulinkovich–Szymoniak reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[685],"cond":["EtMgBr, Ti(Oi-Pr)₄ (1 equiv), THF, rt"],"p":686},"ex":"Kulinkovich–de Meijere reaction: An N,N-dialkylamide (best a formamide), a Grignard reagent and a titanium alkoxide give a cyclopropylamine. The dialkylamino group is a poor leaving group, so the titanium intermediate opens to an iminium ion that closes the ring instead. Stoichiometric titanium (or MeTi(Oi-Pr)₃) gives much better yields than catalytic.","mc":["EtMgBr and the titanium alkoxide give a titanacyclopropane, with loss of ethane.","The amide carbonyl inserts into a Ti–C bond, giving an oxatitanacyclopentane.","It opens to an iminium ion and a titanium oxide; the remaining Ti–C bond attacks the iminium carbon, closing the cyclopropylamine."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Kulinkovich–de Meijere reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kulinkovich_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product under these conditions?","o":[{"m":687},{"m":688},{"m":689},{"m":690}],"t":"Kulinkovich–Szymoniak reaction","fig":{"r":[691],"cond":["EtMgBr (2 equiv), Ti(Oi-Pr)₄ (1 equiv), Et₂O","BF₃·OEt₂"],"veil":true,"key":["BF₃·OEt₂"]},"ex":"A nitrile, EtMgBr and stoichiometric Ti(Oi-Pr)₄, then a Lewis acid, give a primary 1-substituted cyclopropylamine. Without the Lewis acid the azatitanacycle gives mainly the ketone, and more than two equivalents of EtMgBr give a tertiary carbinamine instead.","mc":["EtMgBr and Ti(Oi-Pr)₄ give a titanacyclopropane, with loss of ethane.","The nitrile inserts into a Ti–C bond, giving an azatitanacyclopentene.","BF₃ activates it so that the second Ti–C bond attacks the imine carbon, closing the cyclopropylamine."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Kulinkovich–Szymoniak reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kulinkovich_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Kulinkovich–Szymoniak reaction"},{"x":"Wurtz–Fittig reaction"},{"x":"Kulinkovich–de Meijere reaction"},{"x":"Nozaki–Hiyama–Kishi reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[691],"cond":["EtMgBr (2 equiv), Ti(Oi-Pr)₄ (1 equiv), Et₂O","BF₃·OEt₂"],"p":692,"key":["BF₃·OEt₂"]},"ex":"Kulinkovich–Szymoniak reaction: A nitrile, EtMgBr and stoichiometric Ti(Oi-Pr)₄, then a Lewis acid, give a primary 1-substituted cyclopropylamine. Without the Lewis acid the azatitanacycle gives mainly the ketone, and more than two equivalents of EtMgBr give a tertiary carbinamine instead.","mc":["EtMgBr and Ti(Oi-Pr)₄ give a titanacyclopropane, with loss of ethane.","The nitrile inserts into a Ti–C bond, giving an azatitanacyclopentene.","BF₃ activates it so that the second Ti–C bond attacks the imine carbon, closing the cyclopropylamine."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Kulinkovich–Szymoniak reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kulinkovich_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":693},{"m":694},{"m":695},{"m":282}],"t":"Nozaki–Hiyama–Kishi reaction","fig":{"r":[696,285],"cond":["CrCl₂, NiCl₂ (cat.), DMF, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"Chromium(II) with a nickel catalyst turns vinyl or aryl halides (and allyl halides) into organochromium reagents that add to aldehydes. Very selective for aldehydes, so ketones, esters and nitriles survive, and the alkene geometry is kept. Chromium salts are toxic; catalytic versions regenerate Cr(II) with manganese.","mc":["Cr(II) reduces Ni(II) to Ni(0), which inserts into the vinyl–iodine bond.","The vinyl group moves from nickel to chromium(III) (transmetallation), and Cr(II) reduces the nickel back.","The vinylchromium adds to the aldehyde; work-up gives the allylic alcohol with the E geometry kept."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Nozaki–Hiyama–Kishi reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nozaki%E2%80%93Hiyama%E2%80%93Kishi_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Nozaki–Hiyama–Kishi reaction"},{"x":"Directed ortho metalation"},{"x":"Wurtz–Fittig reaction"},{"x":"Wurtz reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[696,285],"cond":["CrCl₂, NiCl₂ (cat.), DMF, rt"],"p":697},"ex":"Nozaki–Hiyama–Kishi reaction: Chromium(II) with a nickel catalyst turns vinyl or aryl halides (and allyl halides) into organochromium reagents that add to aldehydes. Very selective for aldehydes, so ketones, esters and nitriles survive, and the alkene geometry is kept. Chromium salts are toxic; catalytic versions regenerate Cr(II) with manganese.","mc":["Cr(II) reduces Ni(II) to Ni(0), which inserts into the vinyl–iodine bond.","The vinyl group moves from nickel to chromium(III) (transmetallation), and Cr(II) reduces the nickel back.","The vinylchromium adds to the aldehyde; work-up gives the allylic alcohol with the E geometry kept."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Nozaki–Hiyama–Kishi reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nozaki%E2%80%93Hiyama%E2%80%93Kishi_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":698},{"m":699},{"m":700},{"m":701}],"t":"Wurtz reaction","fig":{"r":[702],"cond":["Na, dioxane, reflux"],"veil":true},"ex":"Sodium couples two alkyl halides into a C–C bond (2 R–X → R–R); intramolecularly it closes strained rings. Two different halides give a statistical mixture, so it suits symmetrical dimers and ring closures, like this bicyclo[1.1.0]butane.","mc":["Sodium transfers an electron to the C–Br bond; a second electron gives an organosodium (a carbanion).","The carbanion displaces chloride from the opposite ring carbon (SN2 across the ring).","This forms the central bond of bicyclo[1.1.0]butane."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Wurtz reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wurtz_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Wurtz reaction"},{"x":"Kulinkovich–de Meijere reaction"},{"x":"Kulinkovich–Szymoniak reaction"},{"x":"Directed ortho metalation"}],"fig":{"r":[702],"cond":["Na, dioxane, reflux"],"p":703},"ex":"Wurtz reaction: Sodium couples two alkyl halides into a C–C bond (2 R–X → R–R); intramolecularly it closes strained rings. Two different halides give a statistical mixture, so it suits symmetrical dimers and ring closures, like this bicyclo[1.1.0]butane.","mc":["Sodium transfers an electron to the C–Br bond; a second electron gives an organosodium (a carbanion).","The carbanion displaces chloride from the opposite ring carbon (SN2 across the ring).","This forms the central bond of bicyclo[1.1.0]butane."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Wurtz reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wurtz_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":704},{"m":705},{"m":706},{"m":707}],"t":"Wurtz–Fittig reaction","fig":{"r":[592,351],"cond":["Na, Et₂O, reflux"],"veil":true},"ex":"Sodium couples an aryl halide with an alkyl halide, giving an alkylarene. The alkyl halide forms the organosodium faster, and it then attacks the aryl halide. Octane and biphenyl form as side products but are easy to separate.","mc":["Sodium reduces butyl bromide to butylsodium.","Butylsodium reacts with bromobenzene, which takes the butyl group and loses bromide (radical and ionic paths are both discussed).","This gives butylbenzene and sodium bromide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Wurtz–Fittig reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wurtz%E2%80%93Fittig_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Wurtz–Fittig reaction"},{"x":"Kulinkovich–de Meijere reaction"},{"x":"Kulinkovich reaction"},{"x":"Kulinkovich–Szymoniak reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[592,351],"cond":["Na, Et₂O, reflux"],"p":708},"ex":"Wurtz–Fittig reaction: Sodium couples an aryl halide with an alkyl halide, giving an alkylarene. The alkyl halide forms the organosodium faster, and it then attacks the aryl halide. Octane and biphenyl form as side products but are easy to separate.","mc":["Sodium reduces butyl bromide to butylsodium.","Butylsodium reacts with bromobenzene, which takes the butyl group and loses bromide (radical and ionic paths are both discussed).","This gives butylbenzene and sodium bromide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Wurtz–Fittig reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wurtz%E2%80%93Fittig_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":709},{"m":710},{"m":711},{"m":712}],"t":"Directed ortho metalation","fig":{"r":[713],"cond":["s-BuLi, TMEDA, THF, −78 °C","I₂"],"veil":true},"ex":"A directing metalation group (amide, carbamate, OMe, …) guides an alkyllithium to the neighbouring ortho C–H; the aryllithium is then trapped by an electrophile. The group binds lithium and makes the ortho proton kinetically more acidic. Tertiary amides and O-carbamates are among the strongest directing groups; OMe is moderate.","mc":["s-BuLi, as its TMEDA complex, binds the amide oxygen.","This holds the base next to the ortho C–H, which it removes, giving the ortho-lithiated arene.","The aryllithium attacks iodine, giving the ortho-iodo amide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Directed ortho metalation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Directed_ortho_metalation"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Directed ortho metalation"},{"x":"Kulinkovich–Szymoniak reaction"},{"x":"Wurtz reaction"},{"x":"Kulinkovich reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[713],"cond":["s-BuLi, TMEDA, THF, −78 °C","I₂"],"p":714},"ex":"Directed ortho metalation: A directing metalation group (amide, carbamate, OMe, …) guides an alkyllithium to the neighbouring ortho C–H; the aryllithium is then trapped by an electrophile. The group binds lithium and makes the ortho proton kinetically more acidic. Tertiary amides and O-carbamates are among the strongest directing groups; OMe is moderate.","mc":["s-BuLi, as its TMEDA complex, binds the amide oxygen.","This holds the base next to the ortho C–H, which it removes, giving the ortho-lithiated arene.","The aryllithium attacks iodine, giving the ortho-iodo amide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Directed ortho metalation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Directed_ortho_metalation"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":715},{"m":716},{"m":717},{"m":718}],"t":"Swern oxidation","fig":{"r":[719],"cond":["(COCl)₂, DMSO, CH₂Cl₂, −78 °C","Et₃N"],"veil":true},"ex":"Activated DMSO oxidises primary alcohols to aldehydes and secondary alcohols to ketones. Goes through an alkoxysulfonium ylide; the by-product dimethyl sulfide smells strongly.","mc":["DMSO reacts with oxalyl chloride at −78 °C, giving the chlorodimethylsulfonium ion and releasing CO and CO₂.","The alcohol attacks sulfur, giving an alkoxysulfonium ion.","Et₃N deprotonates a methyl group, giving a sulfur ylide.","The ylide removes the α-hydrogen through a five-membered transition state, giving the aldehyde and dimethyl sulfide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Swern oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swern_oxidation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Swern oxidation"},{"x":"Anelli oxidation"},{"x":"Dess–Martin oxidation"},{"x":"Sharpless asymmetric dihydroxylation"}],"fig":{"r":[719],"cond":["(COCl)₂, DMSO, CH₂Cl₂, −78 °C","Et₃N"],"p":720},"ex":"Swern oxidation: Activated DMSO oxidises primary alcohols to aldehydes and secondary alcohols to ketones. Goes through an alkoxysulfonium ylide; the by-product dimethyl sulfide smells strongly.","mc":["DMSO reacts with oxalyl chloride at −78 °C, giving the chlorodimethylsulfonium ion and releasing CO and CO₂.","The alcohol attacks sulfur, giving an alkoxysulfonium ion.","Et₃N deprotonates a methyl group, giving a sulfur ylide.","The ylide removes the α-hydrogen through a five-membered transition state, giving the aldehyde and dimethyl sulfide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Swern oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swern_oxidation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":721},{"m":722},{"m":723},{"m":724}],"t":"Parikh–Doering oxidation","fig":{"r":[725],"cond":["SO₃·pyridine, DMSO, Et₃N, 0 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"DMSO activated by SO₃·pyridine oxidises alcohols at 0 °C to room temperature, with no cryogenic cooling.","mc":["DMSO attacks SO₃·pyridine, giving an activated DMSO–SO₃ adduct.","The alcohol attacks sulfur and displaces sulfate, giving an alkoxysulfonium ion.","Et₃N forms the sulfur ylide, which removes the α-hydrogen intramolecularly to give the ketone and Me₂S, as in the Swern."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Parikh–Doering oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parikh%E2%80%93Doering_oxidation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Parikh–Doering oxidation"},{"x":"Riley oxidation"},{"x":"Swern oxidation"},{"x":"Wacker oxidation"}],"fig":{"r":[725],"cond":["SO₃·pyridine, DMSO, Et₃N, 0 °C"],"p":726},"ex":"Parikh–Doering oxidation: DMSO activated by SO₃·pyridine oxidises alcohols at 0 °C to room temperature, with no cryogenic cooling.","mc":["DMSO attacks SO₃·pyridine, giving an activated DMSO–SO₃ adduct.","The alcohol attacks sulfur and displaces sulfate, giving an alkoxysulfonium ion.","Et₃N forms the sulfur ylide, which removes the α-hydrogen intramolecularly to give the ketone and Me₂S, as in the Swern."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Parikh–Doering oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parikh%E2%80%93Doering_oxidation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":727},{"m":435},{"m":319},{"m":728}],"t":"Ley–Griffith oxidation","fig":{"r":[729],"cond":["TPAP (cat.), NMO, 4 Å MS, CH₂Cl₂"],"veil":true},"ex":"Catalytic perruthenate (TPAP) with NMO oxidises alcohols to aldehydes and ketones. Molecular sieves remove the water that would otherwise lead on to the acid.","mc":["The alcohol binds to Ru(VII) in perruthenate, forming a ruthenate ester.","A hydride moves from the α-carbon to ruthenium, releasing the aldehyde and Ru(V).","NMO reoxidises ruthenium to Ru(VII), so TPAP is catalytic."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Tetrapropylammonium perruthenate","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tetrapropylammonium_perruthenate"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Ley–Griffith oxidation"},{"x":"Sharpless epoxidation"},{"x":"Oppenauer oxidation"},{"x":"Jacobsen epoxidation"}],"fig":{"r":[729],"cond":["TPAP (cat.), NMO, 4 Å MS, CH₂Cl₂"],"p":730},"ex":"Ley–Griffith oxidation: Catalytic perruthenate (TPAP) with NMO oxidises alcohols to aldehydes and ketones. Molecular sieves remove the water that would otherwise lead on to the acid.","mc":["The alcohol binds to Ru(VII) in perruthenate, forming a ruthenate ester.","A hydride moves from the α-carbon to ruthenium, releasing the aldehyde and Ru(V).","NMO reoxidises ruthenium to Ru(VII), so TPAP is catalytic."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Tetrapropylammonium perruthenate","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tetrapropylammonium_perruthenate"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":716},{"m":715},{"m":731},{"m":732}],"t":"Jones oxidation","fig":{"r":[719],"cond":["CrO₃, H₂SO₄, acetone, 0 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Chromic acid oxidises primary alcohols to carboxylic acids and secondary alcohols to ketones. Orange Cr(VI) turns green Cr(III) as it reacts.","mc":["CrO₃ in aqueous acid forms chromic acid, which esterifies the alcohol to a chromate ester.","Water removes the α-hydrogen as the O–Cr bond breaks (E2-like), giving the aldehyde and Cr(IV).","In water the aldehyde forms a hydrate, which is oxidised again in the same way to the carboxylic acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Jones oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jones_oxidation#Stoichiometry_and_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Jones oxidation"},{"x":"Lemieux–Johnson oxidation"},{"x":"Dess–Martin oxidation"},{"x":"Jacobsen epoxidation"}],"fig":{"r":[719],"cond":["CrO₃, H₂SO₄, acetone, 0 °C"],"p":733},"ex":"Jones oxidation: Chromic acid oxidises primary alcohols to carboxylic acids and secondary alcohols to ketones. Orange Cr(VI) turns green Cr(III) as it reacts.","mc":["CrO₃ in aqueous acid forms chromic acid, which esterifies the alcohol to a chromate ester.","Water removes the α-hydrogen as the O–Cr bond breaks (E2-like), giving the aldehyde and Cr(IV).","In water the aldehyde forms a hydrate, which is oxidised again in the same way to the carboxylic acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Jones oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jones_oxidation#Stoichiometry_and_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":734},{"m":735},{"m":736},{"m":737}],"t":"Pinnick oxidation","fig":{"r":[738],"cond":["NaClO₂, NaH₂PO₄, 2-methyl-2-butene, t-BuOH/H₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"Sodium chlorite oxidises aldehydes, even α,β-unsaturated ones, to carboxylic acids. 2-Methyl-2-butene scavenges the HOCl by-product.","mc":["Chlorous acid (HClO₂, from NaClO₂ at pH ~3.5) adds to the aldehyde carbonyl.","The adduct fragments in a pericyclic step: the aldehyde hydrogen is transferred and HOCl leaves, giving the carboxylic acid.","2-Methyl-2-butene traps the HOCl before it can destroy chlorite or chlorinate the substrate."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Pinnick oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinnick%E2%80%93Lindgren_oxidation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Pinnick oxidation"},{"x":"Corey–Kim oxidation"},{"x":"Swern oxidation"},{"x":"Rubottom oxidation"}],"fig":{"r":[738],"cond":["NaClO₂, NaH₂PO₄, 2-methyl-2-butene, t-BuOH/H₂O"],"p":739},"ex":"Pinnick oxidation: Sodium chlorite oxidises aldehydes, even α,β-unsaturated ones, to carboxylic acids. 2-Methyl-2-butene scavenges the HOCl by-product.","mc":["Chlorous acid (HClO₂, from NaClO₂ at pH ~3.5) adds to the aldehyde carbonyl.","The adduct fragments in a pericyclic step: the aldehyde hydrogen is transferred and HOCl leaves, giving the carboxylic acid.","2-Methyl-2-butene traps the HOCl before it can destroy chlorite or chlorinate the substrate."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Pinnick oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinnick%E2%80%93Lindgren_oxidation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":740},{"m":741},{"m":724},{"m":742}],"t":"Baeyer–Villiger oxidation","fig":{"r":[743],"cond":["mCPBA, CH₂Cl₂"],"veil":true},"ex":"A peracid inserts an oxygen next to a ketone carbonyl, giving an ester or lactone. The more substituted group migrates: tertiary > secondary ≈ phenyl > primary > methyl.","mc":["The peracid adds to the ketone carbonyl, giving a tetrahedral peroxy intermediate (the Criegee intermediate).","The more substituted group migrates from carbon to oxygen as the weak O–O bond breaks and m-chlorobenzoate leaves.","Loss of a proton gives the ester, here the seven-membered lactone; the migrating group keeps its configuration."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Baeyer–Villiger oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baeyer%E2%80%93Villiger_oxidation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Baeyer–Villiger oxidation"},{"x":"Ozonolysis"},{"x":"Parikh–Doering oxidation"},{"x":"Rubottom oxidation"}],"fig":{"r":[743],"cond":["mCPBA, CH₂Cl₂"],"p":744},"ex":"Baeyer–Villiger oxidation: A peracid inserts an oxygen next to a ketone carbonyl, giving an ester or lactone. The more substituted group migrates: tertiary > secondary ≈ phenyl > primary > methyl.","mc":["The peracid adds to the ketone carbonyl, giving a tetrahedral peroxy intermediate (the Criegee intermediate).","The more substituted group migrates from carbon to oxygen as the weak O–O bond breaks and m-chlorobenzoate leaves.","Loss of a proton gives the ester, here the seven-membered lactone; the migrating group keeps its configuration."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Baeyer–Villiger oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baeyer%E2%80%93Villiger_oxidation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":745},{"m":746},{"m":747},{"m":748}],"t":"Upjohn dihydroxylation","fig":{"r":[749],"cond":["OsO₄ (cat.), NMO, acetone/H₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"Catalytic OsO₄ with NMO as the reoxidant converts alkenes into cis-1,2-diols. Both oxygens come from the same osmate ester, so they add to the same face.","mc":["OsO₄ adds to the alkene in a concerted [3+2] cycloaddition, giving a cyclic osmate(VI) ester with both oxygens on one face.","Hydrolysis of the osmate ester releases the cis-diol.","NMO reoxidises Os(VI) to OsO₄, so only a catalytic amount of osmium is needed."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Upjohn dihydroxylation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Upjohn_dihydroxylation"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Upjohn dihydroxylation"},{"x":"Dakin oxidation"},{"x":"Baeyer–Villiger oxidation"},{"x":"Pinnick oxidation"}],"fig":{"r":[749],"cond":["OsO₄ (cat.), NMO, acetone/H₂O"],"p":750},"ex":"Upjohn dihydroxylation: Catalytic OsO₄ with NMO as the reoxidant converts alkenes into cis-1,2-diols. Both oxygens come from the same osmate ester, so they add to the same face.","mc":["OsO₄ adds to the alkene in a concerted [3+2] cycloaddition, giving a cyclic osmate(VI) ester with both oxygens on one face.","Hydrolysis of the osmate ester releases the cis-diol.","NMO reoxidises Os(VI) to OsO₄, so only a catalytic amount of osmium is needed."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Upjohn dihydroxylation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Upjohn_dihydroxylation"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":751},{"m":752},{"m":753},{"m":754}],"t":"Sharpless asymmetric dihydroxylation","fig":{"r":[755],"cond":["AD-mix-β, t-BuOH/H₂O, 0 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"OsO₄ with a chiral cinchona ligand (AD-mix) gives enantioenriched cis-diols from alkenes. AD-mix-β gives (R,R)-hydrobenzoin from trans-stilbene; AD-mix-α gives the (S,S) enantiomer.","mc":["The cinchona ligand in AD-mix-β, (DHQD)₂PHAL, binds OsO₄ and forms a chiral binding pocket.","The alkene adds to the osmium–ligand complex in a [3+2] cycloaddition on one preferred face.","Hydrolysis releases the diol; K₃Fe(CN)₆ in the mix reoxidises the osmium."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Sharpless asymmetric dihydroxylation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sharpless_asymmetric_dihydroxylation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Sharpless asymmetric dihydroxylation"},{"x":"Dess–Martin oxidation"},{"x":"Jones oxidation"},{"x":"Oppenauer oxidation"}],"fig":{"r":[755],"cond":["AD-mix-β, t-BuOH/H₂O, 0 °C"],"p":756},"ex":"Sharpless asymmetric dihydroxylation: OsO₄ with a chiral cinchona ligand (AD-mix) gives enantioenriched cis-diols from alkenes. AD-mix-β gives (R,R)-hydrobenzoin from trans-stilbene; AD-mix-α gives the (S,S) enantiomer.","mc":["The cinchona ligand in AD-mix-β, (DHQD)₂PHAL, binds OsO₄ and forms a chiral binding pocket.","The alkene adds to the osmium–ligand complex in a [3+2] cycloaddition on one preferred face.","Hydrolysis releases the diol; K₃Fe(CN)₆ in the mix reoxidises the osmium."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Sharpless asymmetric dihydroxylation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sharpless_asymmetric_dihydroxylation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":757},{"m":758},{"m":759},{"m":760}],"t":"Sharpless epoxidation","fig":{"r":[761],"cond":["Ti(Oi-Pr)₄, L-(+)-DET, t-BuOOH, 4 Å MS, CH₂Cl₂, −20 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Ti(Oi-Pr)₄, a chiral tartrate and t-BuOOH epoxidise allylic alcohols enantioselectively. The allylic OH binds titanium; the tartrate enantiomer decides which face is attacked.","mc":["Ti(Oi-Pr)₄ and diethyl tartrate form a chiral dimeric titanium complex.","The allylic alcohol and t-BuOOH exchange onto titanium, replacing isopropoxide ligands.","The peroxide oxygen is delivered to the face of the alkene set by the tartrate, giving the epoxy alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Sharpless epoxidation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sharpless_epoxidation"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Sharpless epoxidation"},{"x":"Wacker oxidation"},{"x":"Jacobsen epoxidation"},{"x":"Anelli oxidation"}],"fig":{"r":[761],"cond":["Ti(Oi-Pr)₄, L-(+)-DET, t-BuOOH, 4 Å MS, CH₂Cl₂, −20 °C"],"p":762},"ex":"Sharpless epoxidation: Ti(Oi-Pr)₄, a chiral tartrate and t-BuOOH epoxidise allylic alcohols enantioselectively. The allylic OH binds titanium; the tartrate enantiomer decides which face is attacked.","mc":["Ti(Oi-Pr)₄ and diethyl tartrate form a chiral dimeric titanium complex.","The allylic alcohol and t-BuOOH exchange onto titanium, replacing isopropoxide ligands.","The peroxide oxygen is delivered to the face of the alkene set by the tartrate, giving the epoxy alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Sharpless epoxidation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sharpless_epoxidation"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":763},{"m":764},{"m":765},{"m":766}],"t":"Jacobsen epoxidation","fig":{"r":[767],"cond":["(R,R)-Mn(salen) catalyst, NaOCl, CH₂Cl₂"],"veil":true},"ex":"A chiral Mn(salen) catalyst with bleach epoxidises unfunctionalised cis-alkenes enantioselectively. Complements the Sharpless epoxidation, which needs an allylic alcohol.","mc":["Bleach oxidises the Mn(III)–salen catalyst to an oxo-Mn(V) species.","The alkene approaches the Mn=O over the chiral salen ligand from one side.","Oxygen transfer, concerted or via a radical, gives the epoxide and regenerates Mn(III)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Jacobsen epoxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacobsen_epoxidation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Jacobsen epoxidation"},{"x":"Ley–Griffith oxidation"},{"x":"Upjohn dihydroxylation"},{"x":"Wacker oxidation"}],"fig":{"r":[767],"cond":["(R,R)-Mn(salen) catalyst, NaOCl, CH₂Cl₂"],"p":768},"ex":"Jacobsen epoxidation: A chiral Mn(salen) catalyst with bleach epoxidises unfunctionalised cis-alkenes enantioselectively. Complements the Sharpless epoxidation, which needs an allylic alcohol.","mc":["Bleach oxidises the Mn(III)–salen catalyst to an oxo-Mn(V) species.","The alkene approaches the Mn=O over the chiral salen ligand from one side.","Oxygen transfer, concerted or via a radical, gives the epoxide and regenerates Mn(III)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Jacobsen epoxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jacobsen_epoxidation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":769},{"m":770},{"m":771},{"m":772}],"t":"Wacker oxidation","fig":{"r":[773],"cond":["PdCl₂ (cat.), CuCl, O₂, DMF/H₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"Pd(II) with Cu/O₂ oxidises a terminal alkene to a methyl ketone. Water attacks the Pd-bound alkene at the more substituted carbon.","mc":["Pd(II) coordinates the terminal alkene.","Water attacks the more substituted alkene carbon (Markovnikov), giving a β-hydroxyalkyl palladium species.","β-Hydride elimination and tautomerisation give the methyl ketone and Pd(0).","Copper reoxidises Pd(0) to Pd(II), and O₂ reoxidises the copper."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Wacker oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wacker_process#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Wacker oxidation"},{"x":"Dess–Martin oxidation"},{"x":"Corey–Kim oxidation"},{"x":"Dakin oxidation"}],"fig":{"r":[773],"cond":["PdCl₂ (cat.), CuCl, O₂, DMF/H₂O"],"p":774},"ex":"Wacker oxidation: Pd(II) with Cu/O₂ oxidises a terminal alkene to a methyl ketone. Water attacks the Pd-bound alkene at the more substituted carbon.","mc":["Pd(II) coordinates the terminal alkene.","Water attacks the more substituted alkene carbon (Markovnikov), giving a β-hydroxyalkyl palladium species.","β-Hydride elimination and tautomerisation give the methyl ketone and Pd(0).","Copper reoxidises Pd(0) to Pd(II), and O₂ reoxidises the copper."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Wacker oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wacker_process#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":775},{"m":776},{"m":777},{"m":778}],"t":"Ozonolysis","fig":{"r":[779],"cond":["O₃, CH₂Cl₂, −78 °C","Me₂S"],"veil":true},"ex":"Ozone cleaves C=C bonds; a reductive work-up (Me₂S or PPh₃) gives aldehydes and ketones. An oxidative work-up (H₂O₂) gives carboxylic acids instead.","mc":["Ozone adds to the alkene in a 1,3-dipolar cycloaddition, giving the primary ozonide (molozonide).","The molozonide splits into a carbonyl compound and a carbonyl oxide (Criegee intermediate).","These recombine to the secondary ozonide (a 1,2,4-trioxolane).","Me₂S reduces the ozonide to the two carbonyl compounds, forming DMSO."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ozonolysis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ozonolysis#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Ozonolysis"},{"x":"Ley–Griffith oxidation"},{"x":"Sharpless asymmetric dihydroxylation"},{"x":"Sharpless epoxidation"}],"fig":{"r":[779],"cond":["O₃, CH₂Cl₂, −78 °C","Me₂S"],"p":780},"ex":"Ozonolysis: Ozone cleaves C=C bonds; a reductive work-up (Me₂S or PPh₃) gives aldehydes and ketones. An oxidative work-up (H₂O₂) gives carboxylic acids instead.","mc":["Ozone adds to the alkene in a 1,3-dipolar cycloaddition, giving the primary ozonide (molozonide).","The molozonide splits into a carbonyl compound and a carbonyl oxide (Criegee intermediate).","These recombine to the secondary ozonide (a 1,2,4-trioxolane).","Me₂S reduces the ozonide to the two carbonyl compounds, forming DMSO."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ozonolysis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ozonolysis#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":781},{"m":782},{"m":783},{"m":784}],"t":"Lemieux–Johnson oxidation","fig":{"r":[785],"cond":["OsO₄ (cat.), NaIO₄, dioxane/H₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"OsO₄ and NaIO₄ cleave an alkene to carbonyl compounds, like ozonolysis without ozone. NaIO₄ cleaves the diol and also reoxidises the osmium.","mc":["OsO₄ dihydroxylates the alkene via an osmate ester, giving a 1,2-diol.","Periodate cleaves the diol through a cyclic periodate ester, giving two carbonyl compounds.","Periodate also reoxidises Os(VI) to OsO₄, so osmium is catalytic."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Lemieux–Johnson oxidation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lemieux%E2%80%93Johnson_oxidation"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Lemieux–Johnson oxidation"},{"x":"Wacker oxidation"},{"x":"Riley oxidation"},{"x":"Baeyer–Villiger oxidation"}],"fig":{"r":[785],"cond":["OsO₄ (cat.), NaIO₄, dioxane/H₂O"],"p":786},"ex":"Lemieux–Johnson oxidation: OsO₄ and NaIO₄ cleave an alkene to carbonyl compounds, like ozonolysis without ozone. NaIO₄ cleaves the diol and also reoxidises the osmium.","mc":["OsO₄ dihydroxylates the alkene via an osmate ester, giving a 1,2-diol.","Periodate cleaves the diol through a cyclic periodate ester, giving two carbonyl compounds.","Periodate also reoxidises Os(VI) to OsO₄, so osmium is catalytic."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Lemieux–Johnson oxidation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lemieux%E2%80%93Johnson_oxidation"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":787},{"m":788},{"m":789},{"m":790}],"t":"Riley oxidation","fig":{"r":[791],"cond":["SeO₂, dioxane/H₂O, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"Selenium dioxide oxidises a CH₂ next to a carbonyl to a 1,2-dicarbonyl, or an allylic C–H to an allylic alcohol.","mc":["The enol of the ketone attacks selenium, forming a C–Se bond at the α-carbon.","A rearrangement moves oxygen from selenium onto the α-carbon, with loss of water.","Selenium is expelled as red elemental selenium, leaving the 1,2-dicarbonyl compound."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Riley oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riley_oxidation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Riley oxidation"},{"x":"Wacker oxidation"},{"x":"Baeyer–Villiger oxidation"},{"x":"Swern oxidation"}],"fig":{"r":[791],"cond":["SeO₂, dioxane/H₂O, heat"],"p":792},"ex":"Riley oxidation: Selenium dioxide oxidises a CH₂ next to a carbonyl to a 1,2-dicarbonyl, or an allylic C–H to an allylic alcohol.","mc":["The enol of the ketone attacks selenium, forming a C–Se bond at the α-carbon.","A rearrangement moves oxygen from selenium onto the α-carbon, with loss of water.","Selenium is expelled as red elemental selenium, leaving the 1,2-dicarbonyl compound."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Riley oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riley_oxidation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":793},{"m":794},{"m":795},{"m":796}],"t":"Anelli oxidation","fig":{"r":[797],"cond":["TEMPO (cat.), NaOCl, KBr, NaHCO₃, CH₂Cl₂/H₂O, 0 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Catalytic TEMPO with buffered bleach oxidises primary alcohols to aldehydes, and secondary alcohols to ketones, within minutes at 0 °C. Bromide gives hypobromite, the faster co-oxidant, and the bicarbonate buffer keeps the pH below 10. With more oxidant the aldehyde goes on to the carboxylic acid.","mc":["Hypobromite, formed from bleach and bromide, oxidises TEMPO to the oxoammonium ion (N⁺=O).","The alcohol adds to the oxoammonium ion and loses its α-hydrogen, giving the aldehyde and the hydroxylamine.","The hydroxylamine is oxidised back to the oxoammonium ion, so a catalytic amount of TEMPO is enough."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Anelli oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxoammonium-catalyzed_oxidation#Mechanism_and_stereochemistry"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Anelli oxidation"},{"x":"Jacobsen epoxidation"},{"x":"Ley–Griffith oxidation"},{"x":"Pinnick oxidation"}],"fig":{"r":[797],"cond":["TEMPO (cat.), NaOCl, KBr, NaHCO₃, CH₂Cl₂/H₂O, 0 °C"],"p":798},"ex":"Anelli oxidation: Catalytic TEMPO with buffered bleach oxidises primary alcohols to aldehydes, and secondary alcohols to ketones, within minutes at 0 °C. Bromide gives hypobromite, the faster co-oxidant, and the bicarbonate buffer keeps the pH below 10. With more oxidant the aldehyde goes on to the carboxylic acid.","mc":["Hypobromite, formed from bleach and bromide, oxidises TEMPO to the oxoammonium ion (N⁺=O).","The alcohol adds to the oxoammonium ion and loses its α-hydrogen, giving the aldehyde and the hydroxylamine.","The hydroxylamine is oxidised back to the oxoammonium ion, so a catalytic amount of TEMPO is enough."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Anelli oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxoammonium-catalyzed_oxidation#Mechanism_and_stereochemistry"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":799},{"m":800},{"m":721},{"m":801}],"t":"Rubottom oxidation","fig":{"r":[802],"cond":["mCPBA, CH₂Cl₂","TBAF or H₃O⁺"],"veil":true},"ex":"A silyl enol ether is epoxidised by a peracid and rearranges to an α-hydroxy ketone.","mc":["mCPBA epoxidises the electron-rich C=C of the silyl enol ether.","The siloxy epoxide opens to an oxocarbenium ion, and the silyl group migrates to the new oxygen, giving an α-silyloxy ketone.","Fluoride or acid removes the silyl group, giving the α-hydroxy ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Rubottom oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rubottom_oxidation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Rubottom oxidation"},{"x":"Dess–Martin oxidation"},{"x":"Ley–Griffith oxidation"},{"x":"Wacker oxidation"}],"fig":{"r":[802],"cond":["mCPBA, CH₂Cl₂","TBAF or H₃O⁺"],"p":803},"ex":"Rubottom oxidation: A silyl enol ether is epoxidised by a peracid and rearranges to an α-hydroxy ketone.","mc":["mCPBA epoxidises the electron-rich C=C of the silyl enol ether.","The siloxy epoxide opens to an oxocarbenium ion, and the silyl group migrates to the new oxygen, giving an α-silyloxy ketone.","Fluoride or acid removes the silyl group, giving the α-hydroxy ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Rubottom oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rubottom_oxidation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":804},{"m":805},{"m":806},{"m":807}],"t":"Oppenauer oxidation","fig":{"r":[808],"cond":["Al(Oi-Pr)₃, acetone (excess), heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"An aluminium alkoxide transfers hydride from a secondary alcohol to acetone, giving a ketone. The reverse of the Meerwein–Ponndorf–Verley reduction.","mc":["The alcohol exchanges onto aluminium, giving an aluminium alkoxide.","Acetone binds aluminium, and a hydride moves from the alkoxide carbon to acetone through a six-membered cyclic transition state.","The ketone is released; excess acetone pushes the equilibrium towards it."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Oppenauer oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oppenauer_oxidation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Oppenauer oxidation"},{"x":"Dess–Martin oxidation"},{"x":"Corey–Kim oxidation"},{"x":"Riley oxidation"}],"fig":{"r":[808],"cond":["Al(Oi-Pr)₃, acetone (excess), heat"],"p":809},"ex":"Oppenauer oxidation: An aluminium alkoxide transfers hydride from a secondary alcohol to acetone, giving a ketone. The reverse of the Meerwein–Ponndorf–Verley reduction.","mc":["The alcohol exchanges onto aluminium, giving an aluminium alkoxide.","Acetone binds aluminium, and a hydride moves from the alkoxide carbon to acetone through a six-membered cyclic transition state.","The ketone is released; excess acetone pushes the equilibrium towards it."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Oppenauer oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oppenauer_oxidation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":810},{"m":811},{"m":812},{"m":813}],"t":"Corey–Kim oxidation","fig":{"r":[814],"cond":["NCS, Me₂S, toluene, −25 °C","Et₃N"],"veil":true},"ex":"N-Chlorosuccinimide and dimethyl sulfide form a chlorodimethylsulfonium ion that oxidises alcohols to aldehydes and ketones, like a Swern oxidation. It runs at −25 °C rather than −78 °C. Allylic and benzylic alcohols can give chlorides instead if the base is added late.","mc":["Dimethyl sulfide attacks NCS, giving the chlorodimethylsulfonium ion.","The alcohol displaces chloride at sulfur, giving an alkoxysulfonium ion.","Triethylamine forms the sulfur ylide, which removes the α-hydrogen intramolecularly, giving the ketone and dimethyl sulfide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Corey–Kim oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corey%E2%80%93Kim_oxidation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Corey–Kim oxidation"},{"x":"Parikh–Doering oxidation"},{"x":"Baeyer–Villiger oxidation"},{"x":"Oppenauer oxidation"}],"fig":{"r":[814],"cond":["NCS, Me₂S, toluene, −25 °C","Et₃N"],"p":815},"ex":"Corey–Kim oxidation: N-Chlorosuccinimide and dimethyl sulfide form a chlorodimethylsulfonium ion that oxidises alcohols to aldehydes and ketones, like a Swern oxidation. It runs at −25 °C rather than −78 °C. Allylic and benzylic alcohols can give chlorides instead if the base is added late.","mc":["Dimethyl sulfide attacks NCS, giving the chlorodimethylsulfonium ion.","The alcohol displaces chloride at sulfur, giving an alkoxysulfonium ion.","Triethylamine forms the sulfur ylide, which removes the α-hydrogen intramolecularly, giving the ketone and dimethyl sulfide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Corey–Kim oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corey%E2%80%93Kim_oxidation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":816},{"m":817},{"m":818},{"m":819}],"t":"Dess–Martin oxidation","fig":{"r":[820],"cond":["DMP, CH₂Cl₂, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"Dess–Martin periodinane oxidises primary and secondary alcohols to aldehydes and ketones under neutral conditions at room temperature. Fast (0.5–2 h) and mild, so an epimerisable α-stereocentre, here from proline, survives. A little water speeds it up.","mc":["The alcohol displaces an acetate on iodine(V), giving an alkoxyperiodinane.","An acetate removes the α-hydrogen.","The C=O bond forms as iodine(V) is reduced to iodine(III), releasing the aldehyde and acetic acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Dess–Martin oxidation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dess%E2%80%93Martin_oxidation"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Dess–Martin oxidation"},{"x":"Dakin oxidation"},{"x":"Wacker oxidation"},{"x":"Corey–Kim oxidation"}],"fig":{"r":[820],"cond":["DMP, CH₂Cl₂, rt"],"p":821},"ex":"Dess–Martin oxidation: Dess–Martin periodinane oxidises primary and secondary alcohols to aldehydes and ketones under neutral conditions at room temperature. Fast (0.5–2 h) and mild, so an epimerisable α-stereocentre, here from proline, survives. A little water speeds it up.","mc":["The alcohol displaces an acetate on iodine(V), giving an alkoxyperiodinane.","An acetate removes the α-hydrogen.","The C=O bond forms as iodine(V) is reduced to iodine(III), releasing the aldehyde and acetic acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Dess–Martin oxidation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dess%E2%80%93Martin_oxidation"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":822},{"m":823},{"m":824},{"m":825}],"t":"Davis oxidation","fig":{"r":[826],"cond":["KHMDS, THF, −78 °C","Davis oxaziridine, −78 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"An enolate attacks the oxygen of an N-sulfonyloxaziridine (Davis reagent), giving the α-hydroxy carbonyl compound. The oxygen is transferred SN2-like; the hemiaminal formed falls apart to a sulfonylimine. Camphor-derived oxaziridines make it enantioselective.","mc":["KHMDS removes an α-proton, giving the ester enolate.","The enolate carbon attacks the oxaziridine oxygen (SN2 at oxygen), giving a hemiaminal.","The hemiaminal falls apart to the sulfonylimine and the α-alkoxide; work-up gives the α-hydroxy ester."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Davis oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Davis_oxidation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Davis oxidation"},{"x":"Swern oxidation"},{"x":"Pinnick oxidation"},{"x":"Rubottom oxidation"}],"fig":{"r":[826],"cond":["KHMDS, THF, −78 °C","Davis oxaziridine, −78 °C"],"p":827},"ex":"Davis oxidation: An enolate attacks the oxygen of an N-sulfonyloxaziridine (Davis reagent), giving the α-hydroxy carbonyl compound. The oxygen is transferred SN2-like; the hemiaminal formed falls apart to a sulfonylimine. Camphor-derived oxaziridines make it enantioselective.","mc":["KHMDS removes an α-proton, giving the ester enolate.","The enolate carbon attacks the oxaziridine oxygen (SN2 at oxygen), giving a hemiaminal.","The hemiaminal falls apart to the sulfonylimine and the α-alkoxide; work-up gives the α-hydroxy ester."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Davis oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Davis_oxidation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":828},{"m":829},{"m":830},{"m":831}],"t":"Dakin oxidation","fig":{"r":[832],"cond":["H₂O₂, NaOH (aq), rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"Hydrogen peroxide in base turns an ortho- or para-hydroxy (or amino) benzaldehyde into a phenol: the aryl group migrates from carbon to oxygen and the formate formed is hydrolysed. A Baeyer–Villiger-type oxidation in which the electron-rich aryl group migrates rather than hydrogen; without an OH or NH₂ ortho or para, the aldehyde is oxidised to the acid instead.","mc":["Hydroperoxide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl, giving a tetrahedral intermediate.","The electron-rich aryl group migrates from carbon to oxygen as hydroxide leaves (as in the Baeyer–Villiger oxidation), giving an aryl formate.","Hydroxide hydrolyses the formate, giving catechol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Dakin oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dakin_oxidation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Dakin oxidation"},{"x":"Davis oxidation"},{"x":"Ozonolysis"},{"x":"Rubottom oxidation"}],"fig":{"r":[832],"cond":["H₂O₂, NaOH (aq), rt"],"p":833},"ex":"Dakin oxidation: Hydrogen peroxide in base turns an ortho- or para-hydroxy (or amino) benzaldehyde into a phenol: the aryl group migrates from carbon to oxygen and the formate formed is hydrolysed. A Baeyer–Villiger-type oxidation in which the electron-rich aryl group migrates rather than hydrogen; without an OH or NH₂ ortho or para, the aldehyde is oxidised to the acid instead.","mc":["Hydroperoxide adds to the aldehyde carbonyl, giving a tetrahedral intermediate.","The electron-rich aryl group migrates from carbon to oxygen as hydroxide leaves (as in the Baeyer–Villiger oxidation), giving an aryl formate.","Hydroxide hydrolyses the formate, giving catechol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Dakin oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dakin_oxidation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":834},{"m":835},{"m":836},{"m":837}],"t":"Prilezhaev reaction","fig":{"r":[838],"cond":["mCPBA, CH₂Cl₂, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"A peracid transfers an oxygen atom to an alkene in one concerted step, giving an epoxide with the alkene geometry kept. In the 'butterfly' transition state the oxygen is delivered as the proton shifts. Electron-rich alkenes react fastest; mCPBA, MMPP and peracetic acid are common. cis-Stilbene gives the cis (meso) epoxide.","mc":["The peracid holds an intramolecular hydrogen bond, which makes its terminal oxygen electrophilic.","The alkene attacks that oxygen as the O–O bond breaks and the proton shifts to the carbonyl oxygen (butterfly transition state).","Both C–O bonds form on the same face at once, so cis-stilbene gives the cis epoxide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Prilezhaev reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prilezhaev_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Prilezhaev reaction"},{"x":"Shi epoxidation"},{"x":"Sharpless aminohydroxylation"},{"x":"Prévost reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[838],"cond":["mCPBA, CH₂Cl₂, rt"],"p":839},"ex":"Prilezhaev reaction: A peracid transfers an oxygen atom to an alkene in one concerted step, giving an epoxide with the alkene geometry kept. In the 'butterfly' transition state the oxygen is delivered as the proton shifts. Electron-rich alkenes react fastest; mCPBA, MMPP and peracetic acid are common. cis-Stilbene gives the cis (meso) epoxide.","mc":["The peracid holds an intramolecular hydrogen bond, which makes its terminal oxygen electrophilic.","The alkene attacks that oxygen as the O–O bond breaks and the proton shifts to the carbonyl oxygen (butterfly transition state).","Both C–O bonds form on the same face at once, so cis-stilbene gives the cis epoxide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Prilezhaev reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prilezhaev_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":840},{"m":841},{"m":842},{"m":843}],"t":"Shi epoxidation","fig":{"r":[755],"cond":["Shi catalyst (from D-fructose, 30 mol%), Oxone, K₂CO₃ (pH 10.5), MeCN/DMM, 0 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"A fructose-derived ketone and Oxone form a chiral dioxirane in situ, which epoxidises trans- and trisubstituted alkenes enantioselectively. Basic pH (about 10.5) speeds up dioxirane formation and suppresses the Baeyer–Villiger breakdown of the ketone, so 20–30 mol% is enough. The D-fructose catalyst gives (R,R)-epoxides from trans-alkenes.","mc":["Oxone (peroxymonosulfate) adds to the ketone; sulfate leaves as the dioxirane closes.","The chiral dioxirane transfers an oxygen to the alkene through a spiro transition state, on the face the catalyst leaves open.","The ketone is regenerated, so it works catalytically."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Shi epoxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shi_epoxidation#Epoxidation_mechanisms"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Shi epoxidation"},{"x":"Prévost reaction"},{"x":"Woodward cis-hydroxylation"},{"x":"Prilezhaev reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[755],"cond":["Shi catalyst (from D-fructose, 30 mol%), Oxone, K₂CO₃ (pH 10.5), MeCN/DMM, 0 °C"],"p":844},"ex":"Shi epoxidation: A fructose-derived ketone and Oxone form a chiral dioxirane in situ, which epoxidises trans- and trisubstituted alkenes enantioselectively. Basic pH (about 10.5) speeds up dioxirane formation and suppresses the Baeyer–Villiger breakdown of the ketone, so 20–30 mol% is enough. The D-fructose catalyst gives (R,R)-epoxides from trans-alkenes.","mc":["Oxone (peroxymonosulfate) adds to the ketone; sulfate leaves as the dioxirane closes.","The chiral dioxirane transfers an oxygen to the alkene through a spiro transition state, on the face the catalyst leaves open.","The ketone is regenerated, so it works catalytically."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Shi epoxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shi_epoxidation#Epoxidation_mechanisms"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":845},{"m":846},{"m":847},{"m":848}],"t":"Sharpless aminohydroxylation","fig":{"r":[849],"cond":["TsNClNa (chloramine-T), K₂OsO₂(OH)₄ (cat.), (DHQ)₂PHAL, t-BuOH/H₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"Osmium-catalysed syn addition of N and O across an alkene, giving a protected 1,2-amino alcohol; cinchona ligands make it enantioselective. With (DHQ)₂PHAL the nitrogen goes to the benzylic carbon and methyl cinnamate gives the (2R,3S) N-tosyl ester of the taxol side chain; anthraquinone-cored ligands reverse the regioselectivity.","mc":["Chloramine-T oxidises osmium to an imidotrioxoosmium(VIII) species, Os(=NTs)O₃, bound to the chiral ligand.","It adds syn to the alkene, probably as a [3+2] cycloaddition, giving an osmium(VI) azaglycolate.","Hydrolysis releases the amino alcohol, and chloramine-T reoxidises the osmium."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Sharpless aminohydroxylation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sharpless_oxyamination#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Sharpless aminohydroxylation"},{"x":"Woodward cis-hydroxylation"},{"x":"Prévost reaction"},{"x":"Shi epoxidation"}],"fig":{"r":[849],"cond":["TsNClNa (chloramine-T), K₂OsO₂(OH)₄ (cat.), (DHQ)₂PHAL, t-BuOH/H₂O"],"p":850},"ex":"Sharpless aminohydroxylation: Osmium-catalysed syn addition of N and O across an alkene, giving a protected 1,2-amino alcohol; cinchona ligands make it enantioselective. With (DHQ)₂PHAL the nitrogen goes to the benzylic carbon and methyl cinnamate gives the (2R,3S) N-tosyl ester of the taxol side chain; anthraquinone-cored ligands reverse the regioselectivity.","mc":["Chloramine-T oxidises osmium to an imidotrioxoosmium(VIII) species, Os(=NTs)O₃, bound to the chiral ligand.","It adds syn to the alkene, probably as a [3+2] cycloaddition, giving an osmium(VI) azaglycolate.","Hydrolysis releases the amino alcohol, and chloramine-T reoxidises the osmium."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Sharpless aminohydroxylation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sharpless_oxyamination#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":753},{"m":752},{"m":851},{"m":852}],"t":"Prévost reaction","fig":{"r":[755],"cond":["I₂, PhCO₂Ag (2 equiv), benzene, reflux (dry)","KOH, MeOH"],"veil":true},"ex":"Iodine and silver benzoate, with no water, add two benzoates anti across an alkene; hydrolysis gives the anti diol. The neighbouring benzoate traps the cation as a cyclic benzoxonium ion, which a second benzoate opens from the back. With water present (Woodward's version) the same intermediate gives the syn diol. Here trans-stilbene gives the meso diol.","mc":["Iodine adds to the alkene, giving a cyclic iodonium ion, which benzoate opens anti.","The neighbouring benzoate displaces iodide, giving a cyclic benzoxonium ion.","A second benzoate opens it from the back, giving the anti dibenzoate; hydrolysis gives the diol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Prévost reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pr%C3%A9vost_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Prévost reaction"},{"x":"Shi epoxidation"},{"x":"Prilezhaev reaction"},{"x":"Woodward cis-hydroxylation"}],"fig":{"r":[755],"cond":["I₂, PhCO₂Ag (2 equiv), benzene, reflux (dry)","KOH, MeOH"],"p":853},"ex":"Prévost reaction: Iodine and silver benzoate, with no water, add two benzoates anti across an alkene; hydrolysis gives the anti diol. The neighbouring benzoate traps the cation as a cyclic benzoxonium ion, which a second benzoate opens from the back. With water present (Woodward's version) the same intermediate gives the syn diol. Here trans-stilbene gives the meso diol.","mc":["Iodine adds to the alkene, giving a cyclic iodonium ion, which benzoate opens anti.","The neighbouring benzoate displaces iodide, giving a cyclic benzoxonium ion.","A second benzoate opens it from the back, giving the anti dibenzoate; hydrolysis gives the diol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Prévost reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pr%C3%A9vost_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":854},{"m":855},{"m":856},{"m":857}],"t":"Woodward cis-hydroxylation","fig":{"r":[838],"cond":["I₂, AgOAc, AcOH/H₂O, heat","KOH, MeOH"],"veil":true},"ex":"Iodine and silver acetate in wet acetic acid give the syn diol: water opens the cyclic acetoxonium ion to an orthoester, which collapses to a monoacetate. Iodine attacks the less hindered face and the oxygens end up on the other face, so in rigid molecules (steroids) the cis diol forms on the face opposite to the one OsO₄ attacks. Here cis-stilbene gives the meso diol.","mc":["Iodine adds to the alkene, giving a cyclic iodonium ion, which acetate opens anti.","The neighbouring acetate displaces iodide, giving a cyclic acetoxonium ion.","Water adds to its central carbon; the orthoester collapses to a monoacetate with both oxygens on the same face, and hydrolysis gives the syn diol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Woodward cis-hydroxylation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodward_cis-hydroxylation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Woodward cis-hydroxylation"},{"x":"Prilezhaev reaction"},{"x":"Shi epoxidation"},{"x":"Sharpless aminohydroxylation"}],"fig":{"r":[838],"cond":["I₂, AgOAc, AcOH/H₂O, heat","KOH, MeOH"],"p":858},"ex":"Woodward cis-hydroxylation: Iodine and silver acetate in wet acetic acid give the syn diol: water opens the cyclic acetoxonium ion to an orthoester, which collapses to a monoacetate. Iodine attacks the less hindered face and the oxygens end up on the other face, so in rigid molecules (steroids) the cis diol forms on the face opposite to the one OsO₄ attacks. Here cis-stilbene gives the meso diol.","mc":["Iodine adds to the alkene, giving a cyclic iodonium ion, which acetate opens anti.","The neighbouring acetate displaces iodide, giving a cyclic acetoxonium ion.","Water adds to its central carbon; the orthoester collapses to a monoacetate with both oxygens on the same face, and hydrolysis gives the syn diol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Woodward cis-hydroxylation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodward_cis-hydroxylation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":859},{"m":860},{"m":861},{"m":862}],"t":"Birch reduction","fig":{"r":[863],"cond":["Na, NH₃(l), EtOH, −33 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Dissolving metal in liquid ammonia reduces arenes to 1,4-cyclohexadienes. Electron-donating groups end up on a double bond; electron-withdrawing groups on an sp³ carbon.","mc":["Sodium gives an electron to the ring, forming a radical anion.","Ethanol protonates the radical anion ortho to the methoxy group, giving a radical.","A second electron gives a carbanion, which is protonated para to the first site, giving the 1,4-diene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Birch reduction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birch_reduction#Reaction_mechanism_and_regioselectivity"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Birch reduction"},{"x":"Rosenmund reduction"},{"x":"Staudinger reduction"},{"x":"Noyori asymmetric hydrogenation"}],"fig":{"r":[863],"cond":["Na, NH₃(l), EtOH, −33 °C"],"p":864},"ex":"Birch reduction: Dissolving metal in liquid ammonia reduces arenes to 1,4-cyclohexadienes. Electron-donating groups end up on a double bond; electron-withdrawing groups on an sp³ carbon.","mc":["Sodium gives an electron to the ring, forming a radical anion.","Ethanol protonates the radical anion ortho to the methoxy group, giving a radical.","A second electron gives a carbanion, which is protonated para to the first site, giving the 1,4-diene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Birch reduction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birch_reduction#Reaction_mechanism_and_regioselectivity"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":865},{"m":866},{"m":867},{"m":868}],"t":"Wolff–Kishner reduction","fig":{"r":[869],"cond":["N₂H₄, KOH, ethylene glycol, 200 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Hydrazine and strong base at high temperature reduce a ketone or aldehyde C=O to CH₂. Loss of N₂ drives the reaction. Use it for acid-sensitive substrates.","mc":["Hydrazine condenses with the ketone to the hydrazone.","Strong base removes an N–H proton; reprotonation at carbon gives a diimide anion.","A second deprotonation leads to loss of N₂ and a carbanion, which the solvent protonates to the CH₂."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Wolff–Kishner reduction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wolff%E2%80%93Kishner_reduction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Wolff–Kishner reduction"},{"x":"Rosenmund reduction"},{"x":"Fukuyama reduction"},{"x":"Barton–McCombie deoxygenation"}],"fig":{"r":[869],"cond":["N₂H₄, KOH, ethylene glycol, 200 °C"],"p":870},"ex":"Wolff–Kishner reduction: Hydrazine and strong base at high temperature reduce a ketone or aldehyde C=O to CH₂. Loss of N₂ drives the reaction. Use it for acid-sensitive substrates.","mc":["Hydrazine condenses with the ketone to the hydrazone.","Strong base removes an N–H proton; reprotonation at carbon gives a diimide anion.","A second deprotonation leads to loss of N₂ and a carbanion, which the solvent protonates to the CH₂."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Wolff–Kishner reduction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wolff%E2%80%93Kishner_reduction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":871},{"m":872},{"m":873},{"m":874}],"t":"Clemmensen reduction","fig":{"r":[875],"cond":["Zn(Hg), HCl, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"Zinc amalgam in hot HCl reduces aryl ketones to CH₂. The acidic counterpart of the Wolff–Kishner reduction.","mc":["The protonated carbonyl takes electrons from the zinc surface.","Zinc-bound carbenoid-type intermediates form as the oxygen leaves as water.","Further reduction and protonation give the CH₂ group; the details of the surface mechanism are still debated."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Clemmensen reduction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clemmensen_reduction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Clemmensen reduction"},{"x":"Birch reduction"},{"x":"Wolff–Kishner reduction"},{"x":"Noyori asymmetric hydrogenation"}],"fig":{"r":[875],"cond":["Zn(Hg), HCl, heat"],"p":876},"ex":"Clemmensen reduction: Zinc amalgam in hot HCl reduces aryl ketones to CH₂. The acidic counterpart of the Wolff–Kishner reduction.","mc":["The protonated carbonyl takes electrons from the zinc surface.","Zinc-bound carbenoid-type intermediates form as the oxygen leaves as water.","Further reduction and protonation give the CH₂ group; the details of the surface mechanism are still debated."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Clemmensen reduction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clemmensen_reduction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":877},{"m":878},{"m":468},{"m":879}],"t":"Luche reduction","fig":{"r":[880],"cond":["NaBH₄, CeCl₃·7H₂O, MeOH"],"veil":true},"ex":"NaBH₄ with CeCl₃ reduces enones at the carbonyl (1,2-reduction) to allylic alcohols. Cerium makes the borohydride harder, so it attacks the carbonyl carbon, not the β-carbon.","mc":["CeCl₃ catalyses the formation of methoxyborohydrides from NaBH₄ and methanol; these are harder hydride donors.","Ce³⁺ coordinates to the carbonyl oxygen, making the carbonyl carbon the harder electrophilic site.","Hard–hard interaction favours hydride attack at the carbonyl carbon (1,2) over the β-carbon (1,4), giving the allylic alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Luche reduction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luche_reduction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Luche reduction"},{"x":"Clemmensen reduction"},{"x":"Staudinger reduction"},{"x":"Birch reduction"}],"fig":{"r":[880],"cond":["NaBH₄, CeCl₃·7H₂O, MeOH"],"p":881},"ex":"Luche reduction: NaBH₄ with CeCl₃ reduces enones at the carbonyl (1,2-reduction) to allylic alcohols. Cerium makes the borohydride harder, so it attacks the carbonyl carbon, not the β-carbon.","mc":["CeCl₃ catalyses the formation of methoxyborohydrides from NaBH₄ and methanol; these are harder hydride donors.","Ce³⁺ coordinates to the carbonyl oxygen, making the carbonyl carbon the harder electrophilic site.","Hard–hard interaction favours hydride attack at the carbonyl carbon (1,2) over the β-carbon (1,4), giving the allylic alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Luche reduction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luche_reduction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":882},{"m":883},{"m":865},{"m":884}],"t":"Corey–Bakshi–Shibata reduction","fig":{"r":[869],"cond":["(S)-Me-CBS catalyst (cat.), BH₃·THF"],"veil":true},"ex":"A chiral oxazaborolidine catalyst and borane reduce prochiral ketones enantioselectively. The (S)-catalyst gives (R)-1-phenylethanol from acetophenone.","mc":["BH₃ binds to the nitrogen of the oxazaborolidine, activating it as a hydride donor and making the ring boron more Lewis acidic.","The ketone binds to the ring boron through the lone pair on the side of its smaller substituent.","Hydride moves from the nitrogen-bound BH₃ to the carbonyl carbon through a six-membered transition state, setting the configuration.","The alkoxyborane is released and hydrolysed on work-up to the alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Corey–Bakshi–Shibata reduction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corey%E2%80%93Itsuno_reduction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Corey–Bakshi–Shibata reduction"},{"x":"Barton–McCombie deoxygenation"},{"x":"Rosenmund reduction"},{"x":"Noyori asymmetric hydrogenation"}],"fig":{"r":[869],"cond":["(S)-Me-CBS catalyst (cat.), BH₃·THF"],"p":885},"ex":"Corey–Bakshi–Shibata reduction: A chiral oxazaborolidine catalyst and borane reduce prochiral ketones enantioselectively. The (S)-catalyst gives (R)-1-phenylethanol from acetophenone.","mc":["BH₃ binds to the nitrogen of the oxazaborolidine, activating it as a hydride donor and making the ring boron more Lewis acidic.","The ketone binds to the ring boron through the lone pair on the side of its smaller substituent.","Hydride moves from the nitrogen-bound BH₃ to the carbonyl carbon through a six-membered transition state, setting the configuration.","The alkoxyborane is released and hydrolysed on work-up to the alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Corey–Bakshi–Shibata reduction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corey%E2%80%93Itsuno_reduction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":468},{"m":481},{"m":886},{"m":887}],"t":"Meerwein–Ponndorf–Verley reduction","fig":{"r":[263],"cond":["Al(Oi-Pr)₃, i-PrOH, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"An aluminium isopropoxide transfers hydride from isopropanol to a ketone or aldehyde. Reversible; distilling off acetone drives it. The reverse is the Oppenauer oxidation.","mc":["The ketone coordinates to aluminium isopropoxide.","A hydride moves from an isopropoxide ligand to the carbonyl carbon through a six-membered cyclic transition state, releasing acetone.","Exchange with isopropanol frees the product alcohol and regenerates the catalyst."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Meerwein–Ponndorf–Verley reduction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meerwein%E2%80%93Ponndorf%E2%80%93Verley_reduction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Meerwein–Ponndorf–Verley reduction"},{"x":"Noyori asymmetric hydrogenation"},{"x":"Birch reduction"},{"x":"Wolff–Kishner reduction"}],"fig":{"r":[263],"cond":["Al(Oi-Pr)₃, i-PrOH, heat"],"p":888},"ex":"Meerwein–Ponndorf–Verley reduction: An aluminium isopropoxide transfers hydride from isopropanol to a ketone or aldehyde. Reversible; distilling off acetone drives it. The reverse is the Oppenauer oxidation.","mc":["The ketone coordinates to aluminium isopropoxide.","A hydride moves from an isopropoxide ligand to the carbonyl carbon through a six-membered cyclic transition state, releasing acetone.","Exchange with isopropanol frees the product alcohol and regenerates the catalyst."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Meerwein–Ponndorf–Verley reduction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meerwein%E2%80%93Ponndorf%E2%80%93Verley_reduction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":889},{"m":890},{"m":891},{"m":892}],"t":"Noyori asymmetric hydrogenation","fig":{"r":[893],"cond":["H₂ (100 atm), RuCl₂[(R)-BINAP] (cat.), MeOH"],"veil":true},"ex":"Ru–BINAP catalysts hydrogenate ketones, especially β-keto esters, with high enantioselectivity. Ryōji Noyori shared the 2001 Nobel Prize for asymmetric hydrogenation.","mc":["H₂ reacts with the Ru–BINAP complex, giving a ruthenium hydride.","The β-keto ester chelates ruthenium through both carbonyl oxygens, inside the chiral pocket formed by BINAP.","Hydride transfer to one face of the ketone and protonation give the (R)-hydroxy ester."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Noyori asymmetric hydrogenation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asymmetric_hydrogenation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Noyori asymmetric hydrogenation"},{"x":"Barton–McCombie deoxygenation"},{"x":"Luche reduction"},{"x":"Corey–Bakshi–Shibata reduction"}],"fig":{"r":[893],"cond":["H₂ (100 atm), RuCl₂[(R)-BINAP] (cat.), MeOH"],"p":894},"ex":"Noyori asymmetric hydrogenation: Ru–BINAP catalysts hydrogenate ketones, especially β-keto esters, with high enantioselectivity. Ryōji Noyori shared the 2001 Nobel Prize for asymmetric hydrogenation.","mc":["H₂ reacts with the Ru–BINAP complex, giving a ruthenium hydride.","The β-keto ester chelates ruthenium through both carbonyl oxygens, inside the chiral pocket formed by BINAP.","Hydride transfer to one face of the ketone and protonation give the (R)-hydroxy ester."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Noyori asymmetric hydrogenation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asymmetric_hydrogenation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":895},{"m":896},{"m":897},{"m":898}],"t":"Rosenmund reduction","fig":{"r":[899],"cond":["H₂, Pd/BaSO₄ (poisoned)"],"veil":true},"ex":"Hydrogenation over a poisoned Pd catalyst reduces an acyl chloride to an aldehyde. The poisoned catalyst stops the aldehyde from being reduced further.","mc":["H₂ is activated on the palladium surface.","The C–Cl bond of the acyl chloride is hydrogenolysed, giving the aldehyde and HCl.","The poisoned catalyst (Pd/BaSO₄, often with quinoline–sulfur) is too weak to reduce the aldehyde further."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Rosenmund reduction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosenmund_reduction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Rosenmund reduction"},{"x":"Staudinger reduction"},{"x":"Noyori asymmetric hydrogenation"},{"x":"Pinacol coupling"}],"fig":{"r":[899],"cond":["H₂, Pd/BaSO₄ (poisoned)"],"p":900},"ex":"Rosenmund reduction: Hydrogenation over a poisoned Pd catalyst reduces an acyl chloride to an aldehyde. The poisoned catalyst stops the aldehyde from being reduced further.","mc":["H₂ is activated on the palladium surface.","The C–Cl bond of the acyl chloride is hydrogenolysed, giving the aldehyde and HCl.","The poisoned catalyst (Pd/BaSO₄, often with quinoline–sulfur) is too weak to reduce the aldehyde further."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Rosenmund reduction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosenmund_reduction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":901},{"m":805},{"m":902},{"m":804}],"t":"Barton–McCombie deoxygenation","fig":{"r":[808],"cond":["NaH, CS₂, MeI","Bu₃SnH, AIBN, toluene, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"An alcohol is converted into a xanthate, then cleaved by tin hydride radicals, replacing OH with H. The strong C=O bond formed in the by-product pays for breaking the C–O bond.","mc":["NaH, CS₂ and MeI convert the alcohol into an O-xanthate.","AIBN starts a radical chain: Bu₃Sn• adds to the thiocarbonyl sulfur.","The adduct fragments into an alkyl radical and a tin-bound S–C(=O)SMe species with a strong C=O bond.","The alkyl radical abstracts H from Bu₃SnH, giving the alkane and a new Bu₃Sn•."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Barton–McCombie deoxygenation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barton%E2%80%93McCombie_deoxygenation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Barton–McCombie deoxygenation"},{"x":"Noyori asymmetric hydrogenation"},{"x":"Bouveault–Blanc reduction"},{"x":"Luche reduction"}],"fig":{"r":[808],"cond":["NaH, CS₂, MeI","Bu₃SnH, AIBN, toluene, heat"],"p":903},"ex":"Barton–McCombie deoxygenation: An alcohol is converted into a xanthate, then cleaved by tin hydride radicals, replacing OH with H. The strong C=O bond formed in the by-product pays for breaking the C–O bond.","mc":["NaH, CS₂ and MeI convert the alcohol into an O-xanthate.","AIBN starts a radical chain: Bu₃Sn• adds to the thiocarbonyl sulfur.","The adduct fragments into an alkyl radical and a tin-bound S–C(=O)SMe species with a strong C=O bond.","The alkyl radical abstracts H from Bu₃SnH, giving the alkane and a new Bu₃Sn•."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Barton–McCombie deoxygenation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barton%E2%80%93McCombie_deoxygenation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":904},{"m":905},{"m":906},{"m":907}],"t":"Staudinger reduction","fig":{"r":[908],"cond":["PPh₃, THF","H₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"Triphenylphosphine reduces an azide to a primary amine via an iminophosphorane. Mild enough to leave most other groups untouched; N₂ and Ph₃PO are the by-products.","mc":["PPh₃ attacks the terminal nitrogen of the azide, giving a phosphazide.","Loss of N₂ through a four-membered transition state gives an iminophosphorane (aza-ylide).","Water hydrolyses the iminophosphorane to the amine and Ph₃P=O."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Staudinger reduction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Staudinger_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Staudinger reduction"},{"x":"Bouveault–Blanc reduction"},{"x":"Pinacol coupling"},{"x":"Wolff–Kishner reduction"}],"fig":{"r":[908],"cond":["PPh₃, THF","H₂O"],"p":909},"ex":"Staudinger reduction: Triphenylphosphine reduces an azide to a primary amine via an iminophosphorane. Mild enough to leave most other groups untouched; N₂ and Ph₃PO are the by-products.","mc":["PPh₃ attacks the terminal nitrogen of the azide, giving a phosphazide.","Loss of N₂ through a four-membered transition state gives an iminophosphorane (aza-ylide).","Water hydrolyses the iminophosphorane to the amine and Ph₃P=O."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Staudinger reduction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Staudinger_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":910},{"m":911},{"m":912},{"m":913}],"t":"Pinacol coupling","fig":{"r":[914,914],"cond":["Mg (or SmI₂), then H₃O⁺"],"veil":true},"ex":"Single-electron reduction couples two ketones into a 1,2-diol. Two ketyl radicals join to form the new C–C bond.","mc":["Magnesium transfers one electron to each ketone, giving ketyl radical anions.","Two ketyls couple at carbon, forming the C–C bond and a magnesium pinacolate.","Acid work-up protonates the alkoxides to the 1,2-diol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Pinacol coupling: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinacol_coupling_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Pinacol coupling"},{"x":"Bouveault–Blanc reduction"},{"x":"Corey–Bakshi–Shibata reduction"},{"x":"Meerwein–Ponndorf–Verley reduction"}],"fig":{"r":[914,914],"cond":["Mg (or SmI₂), then H₃O⁺"],"p":915},"ex":"Pinacol coupling: Single-electron reduction couples two ketones into a 1,2-diol. Two ketyl radicals join to form the new C–C bond.","mc":["Magnesium transfers one electron to each ketone, giving ketyl radical anions.","Two ketyls couple at carbon, forming the C–C bond and a magnesium pinacolate.","Acid work-up protonates the alkoxides to the 1,2-diol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Pinacol coupling: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinacol_coupling_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":916},{"m":917},{"m":918},{"m":919}],"t":"Bouveault–Blanc reduction","fig":{"r":[920],"cond":["Na, EtOH (absolute), reflux"],"veil":true},"ex":"Sodium in ethanol reduces an ester to two alcohols: the primary alcohol of the acyl part and the alcohol of the alkoxy part. A dissolving-metal reduction: electrons from sodium, protons from ethanol. Without the alcohol the radical anions couple instead (acyloin condensation). It was the cheap industrial alternative to LiAlH₄.","mc":["Sodium gives an electron to the ester carbonyl; ethanol protonates the radical anion.","A second electron and loss of ethoxide give the aldehyde.","The aldehyde is reduced the same way (two electrons, two protons) to the primary alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Bouveault–Blanc reduction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bouveault%E2%80%93Blanc_reduction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Bouveault–Blanc reduction"},{"x":"Noyori asymmetric hydrogenation"},{"x":"Fukuyama reduction"},{"x":"Meerwein–Ponndorf–Verley reduction"}],"fig":{"r":[920],"cond":["Na, EtOH (absolute), reflux"],"p":921},"ex":"Bouveault–Blanc reduction: Sodium in ethanol reduces an ester to two alcohols: the primary alcohol of the acyl part and the alcohol of the alkoxy part. A dissolving-metal reduction: electrons from sodium, protons from ethanol. Without the alcohol the radical anions couple instead (acyloin condensation). It was the cheap industrial alternative to LiAlH₄.","mc":["Sodium gives an electron to the ester carbonyl; ethanol protonates the radical anion.","A second electron and loss of ethoxide give the aldehyde.","The aldehyde is reduced the same way (two electrons, two protons) to the primary alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Bouveault–Blanc reduction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bouveault%E2%80%93Blanc_reduction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":652},{"m":922},{"m":653},{"m":923}],"t":"Fukuyama reduction","fig":{"r":[654],"cond":["Et₃SiH, Pd/C (cat.), acetone, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"Palladium and triethylsilane reduce a thioester to the aldehyde, and stop there. Mild and selective: ketones, esters and many other groups survive, unlike with DIBAL-H, and the thioester is easily made from the acid. With an organozinc instead of the silane it gives ketones (Fukuyama coupling).","mc":["Pd(0) inserts into the acyl C–S bond (oxidative addition), giving an acylpalladium thiolate.","Et₃SiH exchanges with the thiolate, giving an acylpalladium hydride and Et₃SiSEt.","Reductive elimination releases the aldehyde and Pd(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Fukuyama reduction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fukuyama_reduction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Fukuyama reduction"},{"x":"Bouveault–Blanc reduction"},{"x":"Clemmensen reduction"},{"x":"Pinacol coupling"}],"fig":{"r":[654],"cond":["Et₃SiH, Pd/C (cat.), acetone, rt"],"p":924},"ex":"Fukuyama reduction: Palladium and triethylsilane reduce a thioester to the aldehyde, and stop there. Mild and selective: ketones, esters and many other groups survive, unlike with DIBAL-H, and the thioester is easily made from the acid. With an organozinc instead of the silane it gives ketones (Fukuyama coupling).","mc":["Pd(0) inserts into the acyl C–S bond (oxidative addition), giving an acylpalladium thiolate.","Et₃SiH exchanges with the thiolate, giving an acylpalladium hydride and Et₃SiSEt.","Reductive elimination releases the aldehyde and Pd(0)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Fukuyama reduction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fukuyama_reduction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":925},{"m":926},{"m":927},{"m":928}],"t":"Claisen rearrangement","fig":{"r":[929],"cond":["heat (~200 °C)"],"veil":true},"ex":"A [3,3]-sigmatropic shift turns an allyl vinyl ether into a γ,δ-unsaturated carbonyl compound. The strong C=O bond formed makes it irreversible.","mc":["The allyl vinyl ether adopts a chair-like six-membered transition state.","In one concerted [3,3]-sigmatropic step, the C–O bond breaks, a new C–C bond forms and both π bonds shift.","The γ,δ-unsaturated aldehyde forms; the new C=O makes the reaction irreversible."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Claisen rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claisen_rearrangement#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Claisen rearrangement"},{"x":"Hofmann rearrangement"},{"x":"Ireland–Claisen rearrangement"},{"x":"Cope rearrangement"}],"fig":{"r":[929],"cond":["heat (~200 °C)"],"p":930},"ex":"Claisen rearrangement: A [3,3]-sigmatropic shift turns an allyl vinyl ether into a γ,δ-unsaturated carbonyl compound. The strong C=O bond formed makes it irreversible.","mc":["The allyl vinyl ether adopts a chair-like six-membered transition state.","In one concerted [3,3]-sigmatropic step, the C–O bond breaks, a new C–C bond forms and both π bonds shift.","The γ,δ-unsaturated aldehyde forms; the new C=O makes the reaction irreversible."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Claisen rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claisen_rearrangement#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":931},{"m":932},{"m":933},{"m":934}],"t":"Cope rearrangement","fig":{"r":[935],"cond":["heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"A [3,3]-sigmatropic shift of a 1,5-diene. Goes through a chair-like transition state and is often reversible.","mc":["The 1,5-diene adopts a chair-like transition state.","In one concerted [3,3] step, the central σ bond breaks, a new σ bond forms between the ends, and both π bonds shift.","The equilibrium favours the more substituted, more stable diene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Cope rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cope_rearrangement#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Cope rearrangement"},{"x":"Oxy-Cope rearrangement"},{"x":"Wolff rearrangement"},{"x":"Beckmann rearrangement"}],"fig":{"r":[935],"cond":["heat"],"p":936},"ex":"Cope rearrangement: A [3,3]-sigmatropic shift of a 1,5-diene. Goes through a chair-like transition state and is often reversible.","mc":["The 1,5-diene adopts a chair-like transition state.","In one concerted [3,3] step, the central σ bond breaks, a new σ bond forms between the ends, and both π bonds shift.","The equilibrium favours the more substituted, more stable diene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Cope rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cope_rearrangement#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":937},{"m":938},{"m":939},{"m":940}],"t":"Oxy-Cope rearrangement","fig":{"r":[941],"cond":["KH, 18-crown-6, THF","H₃O⁺"],"veil":true},"ex":"A 3-hydroxy-1,5-diene shifts to an enol that tautomerises to a carbonyl compound. As the potassium alkoxide (anionic oxy-Cope) it is up to 10¹⁷ times faster.","mc":["KH deprotonates the alcohol; 18-crown-6 pulls potassium away from the alkoxide.","The alkoxide-substituted 1,5-diene undergoes a very fast [3,3] shift, because the negative charge weakens the breaking C–C bond.","The product is an enolate, which is protonated on work-up and tautomerises to the ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Oxy-Cope rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxy-Cope_rearrangement#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Oxy-Cope rearrangement"},{"x":"Ireland–Claisen rearrangement"},{"x":"Benzilic acid rearrangement"},{"x":"Fries rearrangement"}],"fig":{"r":[941],"cond":["KH, 18-crown-6, THF","H₃O⁺"],"p":942},"ex":"Oxy-Cope rearrangement: A 3-hydroxy-1,5-diene shifts to an enol that tautomerises to a carbonyl compound. As the potassium alkoxide (anionic oxy-Cope) it is up to 10¹⁷ times faster.","mc":["KH deprotonates the alcohol; 18-crown-6 pulls potassium away from the alkoxide.","The alkoxide-substituted 1,5-diene undergoes a very fast [3,3] shift, because the negative charge weakens the breaking C–C bond.","The product is an enolate, which is protonated on work-up and tautomerises to the ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Oxy-Cope rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxy-Cope_rearrangement#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":943},{"m":944},{"m":945},{"m":946}],"t":"Ireland–Claisen rearrangement","fig":{"r":[947],"cond":["LDA, TMSCl, THF, −78 °C → rt","H₃O⁺"],"veil":true},"ex":"The silyl ketene acetal of an allyl ester undergoes a [3,3] shift to a γ,δ-unsaturated carboxylic acid. Runs at room temperature instead of the ~200 °C of the classic Claisen.","mc":["LDA forms the ester enolate, and TMSCl traps it on oxygen as a silyl ketene acetal.","On warming, the silyl ketene acetal undergoes a [3,3] Claisen shift through a chair transition state.","Work-up hydrolyses the silyl ester to the γ,δ-unsaturated acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ireland–Claisen rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claisen_rearrangement#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Ireland–Claisen rearrangement"},{"x":"Benzilic acid rearrangement"},{"x":"Pinacol rearrangement"},{"x":"Fries rearrangement"}],"fig":{"r":[947],"cond":["LDA, TMSCl, THF, −78 °C → rt","H₃O⁺"],"p":948},"ex":"Ireland–Claisen rearrangement: The silyl ketene acetal of an allyl ester undergoes a [3,3] shift to a γ,δ-unsaturated carboxylic acid. Runs at room temperature instead of the ~200 °C of the classic Claisen.","mc":["LDA forms the ester enolate, and TMSCl traps it on oxygen as a silyl ketene acetal.","On warming, the silyl ketene acetal undergoes a [3,3] Claisen shift through a chair transition state.","Work-up hydrolyses the silyl ester to the γ,δ-unsaturated acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ireland–Claisen rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claisen_rearrangement#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":949},{"m":950},{"m":951},{"m":952}],"t":"Beckmann rearrangement","fig":{"r":[953],"cond":["H₂SO₄ (or TsCl, then heat)"],"veil":true},"ex":"An oxime rearranges under acid to an amide; the group anti to the OH migrates to nitrogen. The industrial route to caprolactam, the monomer of nylon-6.","mc":["Acid protonates the oxime OH, turning it into a good leaving group.","The group anti to the leaving group migrates to nitrogen as water leaves, giving a nitrilium ion.","Water attacks the nitrilium carbon; tautomerisation of the imidic acid gives the amide, here the lactam."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Beckmann rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beckmann_rearrangement#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Beckmann rearrangement"},{"x":"Hofmann rearrangement"},{"x":"Claisen rearrangement"},{"x":"Pinacol rearrangement"}],"fig":{"r":[953],"cond":["H₂SO₄ (or TsCl, then heat)"],"p":954},"ex":"Beckmann rearrangement: An oxime rearranges under acid to an amide; the group anti to the OH migrates to nitrogen. The industrial route to caprolactam, the monomer of nylon-6.","mc":["Acid protonates the oxime OH, turning it into a good leaving group.","The group anti to the leaving group migrates to nitrogen as water leaves, giving a nitrilium ion.","Water attacks the nitrilium carbon; tautomerisation of the imidic acid gives the amide, here the lactam."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Beckmann rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beckmann_rearrangement#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":955},{"m":956},{"m":957},{"m":958}],"t":"Curtius rearrangement","fig":{"r":[959],"cond":["DPPA, Et₃N, toluene, heat","t-BuOH"],"veil":true},"ex":"An acyl azide loses N₂ on heating and rearranges to an isocyanate, trapped here as a Boc carbamate. The migrating group keeps its configuration.","mc":["DPPA converts the carboxylic acid into an acyl azide.","On heating, the acyl azide loses N₂ while R migrates from carbon to nitrogen in one step, giving an isocyanate.","tert-Butanol adds to the isocyanate, giving the Boc carbamate."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Curtius rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curtius_rearrangement#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Curtius rearrangement"},{"x":"Pinacol rearrangement"},{"x":"Overman rearrangement"},{"x":"Benzilic acid rearrangement"}],"fig":{"r":[959],"cond":["DPPA, Et₃N, toluene, heat","t-BuOH"],"p":960},"ex":"Curtius rearrangement: An acyl azide loses N₂ on heating and rearranges to an isocyanate, trapped here as a Boc carbamate. The migrating group keeps its configuration.","mc":["DPPA converts the carboxylic acid into an acyl azide.","On heating, the acyl azide loses N₂ while R migrates from carbon to nitrogen in one step, giving an isocyanate.","tert-Butanol adds to the isocyanate, giving the Boc carbamate."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Curtius rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curtius_rearrangement#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":961},{"m":962},{"m":963},{"m":964}],"t":"Hofmann rearrangement","fig":{"r":[965],"cond":["Br₂, NaOH, H₂O, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"A primary amide gives an amine with one carbon fewer, via an isocyanate. The lost carbon leaves as CO₂.","mc":["Base and Br₂ convert the amide into an N-bromoamide.","Deprotonation gives an anion that loses bromide as the aryl group migrates to nitrogen, giving an isocyanate.","Water adds to the isocyanate; the carbamic acid loses CO₂ to give the amine."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Hofmann rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hofmann_rearrangement#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Hofmann rearrangement"},{"x":"[2,3]-Wittig rearrangement"},{"x":"Benzilic acid rearrangement"},{"x":"Wolff rearrangement"}],"fig":{"r":[965],"cond":["Br₂, NaOH, H₂O, heat"],"p":966},"ex":"Hofmann rearrangement: A primary amide gives an amine with one carbon fewer, via an isocyanate. The lost carbon leaves as CO₂.","mc":["Base and Br₂ convert the amide into an N-bromoamide.","Deprotonation gives an anion that loses bromide as the aryl group migrates to nitrogen, giving an isocyanate.","Water adds to the isocyanate; the carbamic acid loses CO₂ to give the amine."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Hofmann rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hofmann_rearrangement#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":967},{"m":968},{"m":961},{"m":969}],"t":"Schmidt reaction","fig":{"r":[970],"cond":["HN₃, H₂SO₄"],"veil":true},"ex":"Hydrazoic acid turns ketones into amides (and carboxylic acids into amines) with loss of N₂. Like the Beckmann rearrangement, but straight from the ketone.","mc":["Acid activates the ketone, and hydrazoic acid adds to the carbonyl carbon.","Loss of water gives an iminodiazonium ion.","The phenyl group migrates to nitrogen as N₂ leaves, giving a nitrilium ion, which water converts into the amide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Schmidt reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schmidt_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Schmidt reaction"},{"x":"Hofmann rearrangement"},{"x":"Pinacol rearrangement"},{"x":"Beckmann rearrangement"}],"fig":{"r":[970],"cond":["HN₃, H₂SO₄"],"p":971},"ex":"Schmidt reaction: Hydrazoic acid turns ketones into amides (and carboxylic acids into amines) with loss of N₂. Like the Beckmann rearrangement, but straight from the ketone.","mc":["Acid activates the ketone, and hydrazoic acid adds to the carbonyl carbon.","Loss of water gives an iminodiazonium ion.","The phenyl group migrates to nitrogen as N₂ leaves, giving a nitrilium ion, which water converts into the amide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Schmidt reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schmidt_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":972},{"m":973},{"m":974},{"m":975}],"t":"Wolff rearrangement","fig":{"r":[976],"cond":["Ag₂O, H₂O (or hν)"],"veil":true},"ex":"An α-diazoketone loses N₂ and rearranges to a ketene, which water or alcohols trap as acids or esters. The key step of the Arndt–Eistert homologation.","mc":["Silver(I) or light promotes loss of N₂ from the α-diazoketone.","R shifts from the carbonyl carbon to the neighbouring carbon, together with N₂ loss or via a carbene, giving a ketene.","Water adds to the ketene, giving the carboxylic acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Wolff rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wolff_rearrangement#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Wolff rearrangement"},{"x":"Pinacol rearrangement"},{"x":"Hofmann rearrangement"},{"x":"[1,2]-Wittig rearrangement"}],"fig":{"r":[976],"cond":["Ag₂O, H₂O (or hν)"],"p":977},"ex":"Wolff rearrangement: An α-diazoketone loses N₂ and rearranges to a ketene, which water or alcohols trap as acids or esters. The key step of the Arndt–Eistert homologation.","mc":["Silver(I) or light promotes loss of N₂ from the α-diazoketone.","R shifts from the carbonyl carbon to the neighbouring carbon, together with N₂ loss or via a carbene, giving a ketene.","Water adds to the ketene, giving the carboxylic acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Wolff rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wolff_rearrangement#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":978},{"m":979},{"m":980},{"m":981}],"t":"Pinacol rearrangement","fig":{"r":[982],"cond":["H₂SO₄, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"Acid converts a 1,2-diol into a ketone (or aldehyde) by a 1,2-shift. A carbocation forms first; the group that migrates is the one best at stabilising positive charge.","mc":["Acid protonates one OH, which leaves as water, giving a tertiary carbocation.","A methyl group on the neighbouring carbon shifts to the cationic centre (1,2-shift).","The new cation is an oxocarbenium ion; loss of a proton gives the ketone, pinacolone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Pinacol rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinacol_rearrangement#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Pinacol rearrangement"},{"x":"[2,3]-Wittig rearrangement"},{"x":"Beckmann rearrangement"},{"x":"Oxy-Cope rearrangement"}],"fig":{"r":[982],"cond":["H₂SO₄, heat"],"p":983},"ex":"Pinacol rearrangement: Acid converts a 1,2-diol into a ketone (or aldehyde) by a 1,2-shift. A carbocation forms first; the group that migrates is the one best at stabilising positive charge.","mc":["Acid protonates one OH, which leaves as water, giving a tertiary carbocation.","A methyl group on the neighbouring carbon shifts to the cationic centre (1,2-shift).","The new cation is an oxocarbenium ion; loss of a proton gives the ketone, pinacolone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Pinacol rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinacol_rearrangement#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":984},{"m":985},{"m":986},{"m":987}],"t":"Favorskii rearrangement","fig":{"r":[988],"cond":["NaOMe, MeOH"],"veil":true},"ex":"An α-halo ketone and alkoxide give a rearranged ester via a cyclopropanone; cyclic ketones contract by one carbon.","mc":["Methoxide removes an α'-proton (on the side away from chlorine), giving an enolate.","The enolate displaces chloride intramolecularly, forming a cyclopropanone.","Methoxide adds to the cyclopropanone carbonyl, and the ring opens towards the more stable carbanion.","Protonation gives the ring-contracted methyl ester."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Favorskii rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Favorskii_rearrangement#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Favorskii rearrangement"},{"x":"Benzilic acid rearrangement"},{"x":"Pinacol rearrangement"},{"x":"Curtius rearrangement"}],"fig":{"r":[988],"cond":["NaOMe, MeOH"],"p":989},"ex":"Favorskii rearrangement: An α-halo ketone and alkoxide give a rearranged ester via a cyclopropanone; cyclic ketones contract by one carbon.","mc":["Methoxide removes an α'-proton (on the side away from chlorine), giving an enolate.","The enolate displaces chloride intramolecularly, forming a cyclopropanone.","Methoxide adds to the cyclopropanone carbonyl, and the ring opens towards the more stable carbanion.","Protonation gives the ring-contracted methyl ester."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Favorskii rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Favorskii_rearrangement#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product under these conditions?","o":[{"m":990},{"m":991},{"m":992},{"m":993}],"t":"Fries rearrangement","fig":{"r":[994],"cond":["AlCl₃, nitrobenzene, 25 °C"],"veil":true,"key":["25 °C"]},"ex":"A Lewis acid moves the acyl group of a phenyl ester onto the ring, giving a hydroxyaryl ketone. At 25 °C in nitrobenzene the para ketone wins (kinetic control). Heated above about 160 °C, neat or in a non-polar solvent, the ortho ketone takes over: chelated to aluminium through both oxygens, it is the more stable product.","mc":["AlCl₃ coordinates to the ester carbonyl oxygen.","The acyl–O bond breaks, giving an acylium ion and an aluminium phenoxide.","The acylium ion attacks the ring ortho or para (electrophilic aromatic substitution); work-up gives the hydroxyaryl ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Fries rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fries_rearrangement#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product under these conditions?","o":[{"m":991},{"m":990},{"m":992},{"m":995}],"t":"Fries rearrangement","fig":{"r":[994],"cond":["AlCl₃, neat, 165 °C"],"veil":true,"key":["165 °C"]},"ex":"A Lewis acid moves the acyl group of a phenyl ester onto the ring, giving a hydroxyaryl ketone. Heated above about 160 °C without solvent, the ortho ketone wins: chelated to aluminium through both oxygens, it is the more stable product (thermodynamic control). At 25 °C in nitrobenzene the para ketone forms instead.","mc":["AlCl₃ coordinates to the ester carbonyl oxygen.","The acyl–O bond breaks, giving an acylium ion and an aluminium phenoxide.","The acylium ion attacks the ring ortho or para (electrophilic aromatic substitution); work-up gives the hydroxyaryl ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Fries rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fries_rearrangement#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Fries rearrangement"},{"x":"Overman rearrangement"},{"x":"Oxy-Cope rearrangement"},{"x":"Benzilic acid rearrangement"}],"fig":{"r":[994],"cond":["AlCl₃, nitrobenzene, 25 °C"],"p":996,"key":["25 °C"]},"ex":"Fries rearrangement: A Lewis acid moves the acyl group of a phenyl ester onto the ring, giving a hydroxyaryl ketone. At 25 °C in nitrobenzene the para ketone wins (kinetic control). Heated above about 160 °C, neat or in a non-polar solvent, the ortho ketone takes over: chelated to aluminium through both oxygens, it is the more stable product.","mc":["AlCl₃ coordinates to the ester carbonyl oxygen.","The acyl–O bond breaks, giving an acylium ion and an aluminium phenoxide.","The acylium ion attacks the ring ortho or para (electrophilic aromatic substitution); work-up gives the hydroxyaryl ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Fries rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fries_rearrangement#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":997},{"m":998},{"m":999},{"m":1000}],"t":"[2,3]-Wittig rearrangement","fig":{"r":[1001],"cond":["n-BuLi, THF, −78 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"An α-allyloxy carbanion rearranges through a five-membered transition state to a homoallylic alcohol. The [1,2]-Wittig rearrangement is a radical process that competes at higher temperature.","mc":["n-BuLi removes the propargylic proton next to oxygen, giving a carbanion.","The carbanion bonds to the far end of the allyl group through a five-membered envelope transition state as the C–O bond breaks.","The resulting homoallylic alkoxide is protonated on work-up to the alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["[2,3]-Wittig rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2%2C3-Wittig_rearrangement#Mechanism_and_stereochemistry"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"[2,3]-Wittig rearrangement"},{"x":"Wolff rearrangement"},{"x":"Claisen rearrangement"},{"x":"Beckmann rearrangement"}],"fig":{"r":[1001],"cond":["n-BuLi, THF, −78 °C"],"p":1002},"ex":"[2,3]-Wittig rearrangement: An α-allyloxy carbanion rearranges through a five-membered transition state to a homoallylic alcohol. The [1,2]-Wittig rearrangement is a radical process that competes at higher temperature.","mc":["n-BuLi removes the propargylic proton next to oxygen, giving a carbanion.","The carbanion bonds to the far end of the allyl group through a five-membered envelope transition state as the C–O bond breaks.","The resulting homoallylic alkoxide is protonated on work-up to the alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["[2,3]-Wittig rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2%2C3-Wittig_rearrangement#Mechanism_and_stereochemistry"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1003},{"m":1004},{"m":1005},{"m":1006}],"t":"Benzilic acid rearrangement","fig":{"r":[1007],"cond":["KOH, H₂O, heat","H₃O⁺"],"veil":true},"ex":"A 1,2-diketone and hydroxide rearrange to an α-hydroxy carboxylic acid. One of the oldest known rearrangements (Liebig, 1838).","mc":["Hydroxide adds to one carbonyl of benzil, giving a tetrahedral alkoxide.","The phenyl group on that carbon migrates to the neighbouring carbonyl carbon as the C=O reforms (1,2-shift).","Proton transfer gives the carboxylate of the α-hydroxy acid; acid work-up gives benzilic acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Benzilic acid rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benzilic_acid_rearrangement#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Benzilic acid rearrangement"},{"x":"Favorskii rearrangement"},{"x":"Ireland–Claisen rearrangement"},{"x":"Oxy-Cope rearrangement"}],"fig":{"r":[1007],"cond":["KOH, H₂O, heat","H₃O⁺"],"p":1008},"ex":"Benzilic acid rearrangement: A 1,2-diketone and hydroxide rearrange to an α-hydroxy carboxylic acid. One of the oldest known rearrangements (Liebig, 1838).","mc":["Hydroxide adds to one carbonyl of benzil, giving a tetrahedral alkoxide.","The phenyl group on that carbon migrates to the neighbouring carbonyl carbon as the C=O reforms (1,2-shift).","Proton transfer gives the carboxylate of the α-hydroxy acid; acid work-up gives benzilic acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Benzilic acid rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benzilic_acid_rearrangement#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1009},{"m":1010},{"m":1011},{"m":1012}],"t":"Overman rearrangement","fig":{"r":[1013],"cond":["Cl₃CCN, DBU (cat.), CH₂Cl₂, 0 °C","xylene, 140 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"An allylic alcohol becomes an allylic amine: its trichloroacetimidate rearranges ([3,3]-sigmatropic) to an allylic trichloroacetamide with the double bond shifted. The rearrangement goes through a chair transition state; Pd(II) or Hg(II) salts let it run near room temperature, and chiral Pd(II) catalysts make it enantioselective.","mc":["The alkoxide (from catalytic base) adds to trichloroacetonitrile, giving the allylic trichloroacetimidate.","On heating a [3,3]-sigmatropic shift through a chair transition state moves the nitrogen to the far alkene carbon and shifts the double bond.","The new amide C=O drives it, giving the allylic trichloroacetamide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Overman rearrangement","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Overman_rearrangement"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Overman rearrangement"},{"x":"[2,3]-Wittig rearrangement"},{"x":"Cope rearrangement"},{"x":"Ireland–Claisen rearrangement"}],"fig":{"r":[1013],"cond":["Cl₃CCN, DBU (cat.), CH₂Cl₂, 0 °C","xylene, 140 °C"],"p":1014},"ex":"Overman rearrangement: An allylic alcohol becomes an allylic amine: its trichloroacetimidate rearranges ([3,3]-sigmatropic) to an allylic trichloroacetamide with the double bond shifted. The rearrangement goes through a chair transition state; Pd(II) or Hg(II) salts let it run near room temperature, and chiral Pd(II) catalysts make it enantioselective.","mc":["The alkoxide (from catalytic base) adds to trichloroacetonitrile, giving the allylic trichloroacetimidate.","On heating a [3,3]-sigmatropic shift through a chair transition state moves the nitrogen to the far alkene carbon and shifts the double bond.","The new amide C=O drives it, giving the allylic trichloroacetamide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Overman rearrangement","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Overman_rearrangement"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1015},{"m":1016},{"m":1017},{"m":1018}],"t":"[1,2]-Wittig rearrangement","fig":{"r":[1019],"cond":["n-BuLi, THF, −78 °C → rt","H₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"A strong base deprotonates an ether next to oxygen; the other alkyl group then migrates from oxygen to that carbon, giving an alcohol. It goes through radical dissociation and fast recombination in the solvent cage, so groups that form stable radicals (benzyl, allyl, tertiary alkyl) migrate best, largely with retention.","mc":["n-BuLi removes a benzylic proton next to oxygen, giving an α-lithio ether.","The O–CH₂Ph bond breaks homolytically, giving a ketyl radical anion and a benzyl radical held in the solvent cage.","The radicals recombine at carbon, giving the lithium alkoxide; work-up gives 1,2-diphenylethanol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["[1,2]-Wittig rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1%2C2-Wittig_rearrangement#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"[1,2]-Wittig rearrangement"},{"x":"Wolff rearrangement"},{"x":"Cope rearrangement"},{"x":"Pinacol rearrangement"}],"fig":{"r":[1019],"cond":["n-BuLi, THF, −78 °C → rt","H₂O"],"p":1020},"ex":"[1,2]-Wittig rearrangement: A strong base deprotonates an ether next to oxygen; the other alkyl group then migrates from oxygen to that carbon, giving an alcohol. It goes through radical dissociation and fast recombination in the solvent cage, so groups that form stable radicals (benzyl, allyl, tertiary alkyl) migrate best, largely with retention.","mc":["n-BuLi removes a benzylic proton next to oxygen, giving an α-lithio ether.","The O–CH₂Ph bond breaks homolytically, giving a ketyl radical anion and a benzyl radical held in the solvent cage.","The radicals recombine at carbon, giving the lithium alkoxide; work-up gives 1,2-diphenylethanol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["[1,2]-Wittig rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1%2C2-Wittig_rearrangement#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1021},{"m":1022},{"m":1023},{"m":1024}],"t":"Diels–Alder reaction","fig":{"r":[1025,1026],"cond":["toluene, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"A conjugated diene and an alkene (the dienophile) combine in a [4+2] cycloaddition to a cyclohexene. Concerted and stereospecific; endo products are favoured and electron-poor dienophiles react fastest.","mc":["The diene adopts its s-cis conformation.","The diene's HOMO and the dienophile's LUMO overlap on the same face of each component (suprafacial).","Two new σ bonds and a new π bond form in one concerted step; secondary orbital overlap favours the endo transition state."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Diels–Alder reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diels%E2%80%93Alder_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Diels–Alder reaction"},{"x":"Paternò–Büchi reaction"},{"x":"Staudinger synthesis"},{"x":"Nazarov cyclization"}],"fig":{"r":[1025,1026],"cond":["toluene, heat"],"p":1027},"ex":"Diels–Alder reaction: A conjugated diene and an alkene (the dienophile) combine in a [4+2] cycloaddition to a cyclohexene. Concerted and stereospecific; endo products are favoured and electron-poor dienophiles react fastest.","mc":["The diene adopts its s-cis conformation.","The diene's HOMO and the dienophile's LUMO overlap on the same face of each component (suprafacial).","Two new σ bonds and a new π bond form in one concerted step; secondary orbital overlap favours the endo transition state."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Diels–Alder reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diels%E2%80%93Alder_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1028},{"m":1029},{"m":1030},{"m":1031}],"t":"1,3-Dipolar cycloaddition","fig":{"r":[1032,1033],"cond":["toluene, reflux"],"veil":true},"ex":"A 1,3-dipole (here an azide) and a dipolarophile (an alkene or alkyne) combine in a concerted [3+2] cycloaddition to a five-membered heterocycle. Without copper the azide–alkyne reaction needs heat, and a terminal alkyne gives both the 1,4- and the 1,5-triazole; a symmetrical alkyne gives one product. Nitrile oxides, nitrones and diazo compounds react the same way.","mc":["The azide is a 1,3-dipole: four π electrons spread over three nitrogens, with reactive ends at N1 and N3.","On heating both new bonds form at once (a concerted [3+2] cycloaddition) across the alkyne.","The product is an aromatic 1,2,3-triazole; with this symmetrical alkyne only one regioisomer is possible."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["1,3-Dipolar cycloaddition: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1%2C3-Dipolar_cycloaddition#Pericyclic_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"1,3-Dipolar cycloaddition"},{"x":"Nazarov cyclization"},{"x":"Simmons–Smith reaction"},{"x":"Staudinger synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[1032,1033],"cond":["toluene, reflux"],"p":1034},"ex":"1,3-Dipolar cycloaddition: A 1,3-dipole (here an azide) and a dipolarophile (an alkene or alkyne) combine in a concerted [3+2] cycloaddition to a five-membered heterocycle. Without copper the azide–alkyne reaction needs heat, and a terminal alkyne gives both the 1,4- and the 1,5-triazole; a symmetrical alkyne gives one product. Nitrile oxides, nitrones and diazo compounds react the same way.","mc":["The azide is a 1,3-dipole: four π electrons spread over three nitrogens, with reactive ends at N1 and N3.","On heating both new bonds form at once (a concerted [3+2] cycloaddition) across the alkyne.","The product is an aromatic 1,2,3-triazole; with this symmetrical alkyne only one regioisomer is possible."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["1,3-Dipolar cycloaddition: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1%2C3-Dipolar_cycloaddition#Pericyclic_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1035},{"m":1036},{"m":1037},{"m":1038}],"t":"Copper-catalysed azide–alkyne cycloaddition","fig":{"r":[1032,1039],"cond":["CuSO₄, sodium ascorbate, t-BuOH/H₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"Cu(I) catalyses the [3+2] cycloaddition of an azide and a terminal alkyne to a 1,4-disubstituted 1,2,3-triazole. The best-known click reaction. Without copper (Huisgen) it needs heat and gives both regioisomers.","mc":["Ascorbate reduces Cu(II) to Cu(I), which forms a copper acetylide with the terminal alkyne.","The azide binds copper; its terminal nitrogen attacks the acetylide carbon, forming a six-membered copper metallacycle.","Ring contraction gives a copper triazolide, which is protonated to the 1,4-triazole and releases Cu(I)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Copper-catalysed azide–alkyne cycloaddition: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Azide-alkyne_Huisgen_cycloaddition#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Copper-catalysed azide–alkyne cycloaddition"},{"x":"1,3-Dipolar cycloaddition"},{"x":"Simmons–Smith reaction"},{"x":"Paternò–Büchi reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1032,1039],"cond":["CuSO₄, sodium ascorbate, t-BuOH/H₂O"],"p":1040},"ex":"Copper-catalysed azide–alkyne cycloaddition: Cu(I) catalyses the [3+2] cycloaddition of an azide and a terminal alkyne to a 1,4-disubstituted 1,2,3-triazole. The best-known click reaction. Without copper (Huisgen) it needs heat and gives both regioisomers.","mc":["Ascorbate reduces Cu(II) to Cu(I), which forms a copper acetylide with the terminal alkyne.","The azide binds copper; its terminal nitrogen attacks the acetylide carbon, forming a six-membered copper metallacycle.","Ring contraction gives a copper triazolide, which is protonated to the 1,4-triazole and releases Cu(I)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Copper-catalysed azide–alkyne cycloaddition: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Azide-alkyne_Huisgen_cycloaddition#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1041},{"m":318},{"m":980},{"m":1042}],"t":"Paternò–Büchi reaction","fig":{"r":[285,1043],"cond":["hν"],"veil":true},"ex":"Light drives a [2+2] cycloaddition of a carbonyl compound and an alkene to an oxetane. The carbonyl reacts from its excited (n,π*) state.","mc":["Light excites benzaldehyde to its n,π* state.","The excited carbonyl oxygen adds to the alkene, giving the more stable 1,4-biradical.","The two radical centres combine, closing the oxetane ring."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Paternò–Büchi reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patern%C3%B2%E2%80%93B%C3%BCchi_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Paternò–Büchi reaction"},{"x":"Nazarov cyclization"},{"x":"Copper-catalysed azide–alkyne cycloaddition"},{"x":"Pauson–Khand reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[285,1043],"cond":["hν"],"p":1044},"ex":"Paternò–Büchi reaction: Light drives a [2+2] cycloaddition of a carbonyl compound and an alkene to an oxetane. The carbonyl reacts from its excited (n,π*) state.","mc":["Light excites benzaldehyde to its n,π* state.","The excited carbonyl oxygen adds to the alkene, giving the more stable 1,4-biradical.","The two radical centres combine, closing the oxetane ring."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Paternò–Büchi reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patern%C3%B2%E2%80%93B%C3%BCchi_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1045},{"m":1046},{"m":1047},{"m":1048}],"t":"Ene reaction","fig":{"r":[1049,1026],"cond":["heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"An alkene with an allylic hydrogen (the ene) adds to an enophile, moving the double bond and the hydrogen.","mc":["Propene (the ene) and maleic anhydride (the enophile) approach through an envelope-shaped six-membered transition state.","In one concerted step the allylic hydrogen moves to the enophile, a new C–C bond forms, and the ene's double bond shifts."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ene reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ene_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Ene reaction"},{"x":"Paternò–Büchi reaction"},{"x":"Pauson–Khand reaction"},{"x":"Staudinger synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[1049,1026],"cond":["heat"],"p":1050},"ex":"Ene reaction: An alkene with an allylic hydrogen (the ene) adds to an enophile, moving the double bond and the hydrogen.","mc":["Propene (the ene) and maleic anhydride (the enophile) approach through an envelope-shaped six-membered transition state.","In one concerted step the allylic hydrogen moves to the enophile, a new C–C bond forms, and the ene's double bond shifts."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ene reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ene_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1051},{"m":1052},{"m":1053},{"m":248}],"t":"Nazarov cyclization","fig":{"r":[1054],"cond":["BF₃·OEt₂ (or another Lewis acid)"],"veil":true},"ex":"Acid triggers a 4π-electrocyclic ring closure of a divinyl ketone to a cyclopentenone. The ring closure is conrotatory, as the Woodward–Hoffmann rules predict for 4π electrons.","mc":["The Lewis acid coordinates to the carbonyl oxygen, giving a pentadienyl cation.","A 4π conrotatory electrocyclic ring closure forms the C–C bond, giving an oxyallyl cation.","Loss of a proton gives a dienolate, which is protonated (tautomerises) to the cyclopentenone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Nazarov cyclization: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nazarov_cyclization#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Nazarov cyclization"},{"x":"Diels–Alder reaction"},{"x":"Staudinger synthesis"},{"x":"Pauson–Khand reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1054],"cond":["BF₃·OEt₂ (or another Lewis acid)"],"p":1055},"ex":"Nazarov cyclization: Acid triggers a 4π-electrocyclic ring closure of a divinyl ketone to a cyclopentenone. The ring closure is conrotatory, as the Woodward–Hoffmann rules predict for 4π electrons.","mc":["The Lewis acid coordinates to the carbonyl oxygen, giving a pentadienyl cation.","A 4π conrotatory electrocyclic ring closure forms the C–C bond, giving an oxyallyl cation.","Loss of a proton gives a dienolate, which is protonated (tautomerises) to the cyclopentenone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Nazarov cyclization: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nazarov_cyclization#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1056},{"m":1057},{"m":1058},{"m":1059}],"t":"Pauson–Khand reaction","fig":{"r":[1060],"cond":["Co₂(CO)₈, then NMO (or heat)"],"veil":true},"ex":"An alkyne, an alkene and CO combine on a cobalt carbonyl complex to a cyclopentenone. A formal [2+2+1] cycloaddition.","mc":["The alkyne binds Co₂(CO)₈, losing two CO, to form a dicobalt hexacarbonyl alkyne complex.","NMO removes a CO ligand, and the alkene coordinates and inserts into a Co–C bond.","CO inserts into the Co–C bond, and reductive elimination closes the cyclopentenone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Pauson–Khand reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pauson%E2%80%93Khand_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Pauson–Khand reaction"},{"x":"Diels–Alder reaction"},{"x":"Paternò–Büchi reaction"},{"x":"1,3-Dipolar cycloaddition"}],"fig":{"r":[1060],"cond":["Co₂(CO)₈, then NMO (or heat)"],"p":1061},"ex":"Pauson–Khand reaction: An alkyne, an alkene and CO combine on a cobalt carbonyl complex to a cyclopentenone. A formal [2+2+1] cycloaddition.","mc":["The alkyne binds Co₂(CO)₈, losing two CO, to form a dicobalt hexacarbonyl alkyne complex.","NMO removes a CO ligand, and the alkene coordinates and inserts into a Co–C bond.","CO inserts into the Co–C bond, and reductive elimination closes the cyclopentenone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Pauson–Khand reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pauson%E2%80%93Khand_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1062},{"m":1063},{"m":1064},{"m":1065}],"t":"Simmons–Smith reaction","fig":{"r":[749],"cond":["CH₂I₂, Zn(Cu), Et₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"A zinc carbenoid made from CH₂I₂ adds CH₂ to an alkene stereospecifically, giving a cyclopropane. A nearby OH group directs the reagent to its own face.","mc":["Zn(Cu) inserts into a C–I bond of CH₂I₂, giving the carbenoid ICH₂ZnI.","The carbenoid delivers CH₂ to the alkene through a concerted 'butterfly' transition state.","Both new C–C bonds form on the same face, so the alkene geometry is kept; ZnI₂ is released."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Simmons–Smith reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simmons%E2%80%93Smith_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Simmons–Smith reaction"},{"x":"Pauson–Khand reaction"},{"x":"Diels–Alder reaction"},{"x":"Nazarov cyclization"}],"fig":{"r":[749],"cond":["CH₂I₂, Zn(Cu), Et₂O"],"p":1066},"ex":"Simmons–Smith reaction: A zinc carbenoid made from CH₂I₂ adds CH₂ to an alkene stereospecifically, giving a cyclopropane. A nearby OH group directs the reagent to its own face.","mc":["Zn(Cu) inserts into a C–I bond of CH₂I₂, giving the carbenoid ICH₂ZnI.","The carbenoid delivers CH₂ to the alkene through a concerted 'butterfly' transition state.","Both new C–C bonds form on the same face, so the alkene geometry is kept; ZnI₂ is released."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Simmons–Smith reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simmons%E2%80%93Smith_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1067},{"m":1068},{"m":1069},{"m":1070}],"t":"Staudinger synthesis","fig":{"r":[1071,1072],"cond":["Et₃N, CH₂Cl₂ (makes the ketene)"],"veil":true},"ex":"A ketene and an imine undergo a [2+2] cycloaddition to a β-lactam. Not to be confused with the Staudinger reduction of azides.","mc":["Et₃N removes HCl from acetyl chloride, giving the ketene H₂C=C=O.","The imine nitrogen attacks the central ketene carbon, giving a zwitterionic enolate–iminium intermediate.","Conrotatory ring closure of the zwitterion forms the β-lactam."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Staudinger synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Staudinger_synthesis#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Staudinger synthesis"},{"x":"Diels–Alder reaction"},{"x":"Pauson–Khand reaction"},{"x":"Paternò–Büchi reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1071,1072],"cond":["Et₃N, CH₂Cl₂ (makes the ketene)"],"p":1073},"ex":"Staudinger synthesis: A ketene and an imine undergo a [2+2] cycloaddition to a β-lactam. Not to be confused with the Staudinger reduction of azides.","mc":["Et₃N removes HCl from acetyl chloride, giving the ketene H₂C=C=O.","The imine nitrogen attacks the central ketene carbon, giving a zwitterionic enolate–iminium intermediate.","Conrotatory ring closure of the zwitterion forms the β-lactam."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Staudinger synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Staudinger_synthesis#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1074},{"m":1075},{"m":811},{"m":1076}],"t":"Mitsunobu reaction","fig":{"r":[1077,1078],"cond":["PPh₃, DIAD, THF, 0 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"PPh₃ and DEAD/DIAD activate an alcohol so an acidic nucleophile replaces it with inversion of configuration. The nucleophile needs a pKa below about 13.","mc":["PPh₃ attacks DIAD, giving a phosphonium betaine.","The betaine deprotonates the carboxylic acid; the alcohol then attacks phosphorus, giving an alkoxyphosphonium ion.","The carboxylate displaces Ph₃P=O by SN2 at carbon, inverting the stereocentre."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Mitsunobu reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mitsunobu_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Mitsunobu reaction"},{"x":"Sandmeyer reaction"},{"x":"Arndt–Eistert homologation"},{"x":"Strecker synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[1077,1078],"cond":["PPh₃, DIAD, THF, 0 °C"],"p":1079},"ex":"Mitsunobu reaction: PPh₃ and DEAD/DIAD activate an alcohol so an acidic nucleophile replaces it with inversion of configuration. The nucleophile needs a pKa below about 13.","mc":["PPh₃ attacks DIAD, giving a phosphonium betaine.","The betaine deprotonates the carboxylic acid; the alcohol then attacks phosphorus, giving an alkoxyphosphonium ion.","The carboxylate displaces Ph₃P=O by SN2 at carbon, inverting the stereocentre."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Mitsunobu reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mitsunobu_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1080},{"m":1081},{"m":715},{"m":1082}],"t":"Appel reaction","fig":{"r":[719],"cond":["CBr₄, PPh₃, CH₂Cl₂"],"veil":true},"ex":"PPh₃ and CBr₄ (or CCl₄) turn an alcohol into an alkyl bromide (or chloride). Proceeds by SN2, so stereocentres are inverted. Ph₃PO is the driving force.","mc":["PPh₃ attacks a bromine of CBr₄, giving Ph₃P⁺–Br and CBr₃⁻.","CBr₃⁻ deprotonates the alcohol, and the alkoxide attacks phosphorus, giving an alkoxyphosphonium ion.","Bromide displaces Ph₃P=O by SN2 at carbon."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Appel reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Appel_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Appel reaction"},{"x":"Hell–Volhard–Zelinsky reaction"},{"x":"Strecker synthesis"},{"x":"Eschweiler–Clarke reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[719],"cond":["CBr₄, PPh₃, CH₂Cl₂"],"p":1083},"ex":"Appel reaction: PPh₃ and CBr₄ (or CCl₄) turn an alcohol into an alkyl bromide (or chloride). Proceeds by SN2, so stereocentres are inverted. Ph₃PO is the driving force.","mc":["PPh₃ attacks a bromine of CBr₄, giving Ph₃P⁺–Br and CBr₃⁻.","CBr₃⁻ deprotonates the alcohol, and the alkoxide attacks phosphorus, giving an alkoxyphosphonium ion.","Bromide displaces Ph₃P=O by SN2 at carbon."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Appel reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Appel_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1084},{"m":1085},{"m":1086},{"m":1087}],"t":"Williamson ether synthesis","fig":{"r":[639,1088],"cond":["K₂CO₃, acetone (or NaH, THF)"],"veil":true},"ex":"An alkoxide or phenoxide displaces a primary alkyl halide by SN2 to form an ether. Secondary and tertiary halides eliminate instead.","mc":["The base deprotonates phenol to phenoxide.","Phenoxide attacks methyl iodide in an SN2 reaction, displacing iodide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Williamson ether synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Williamson_ether_synthesis#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Williamson ether synthesis"},{"x":"Fischer esterification"},{"x":"Hell–Volhard–Zelinsky reaction"},{"x":"Strecker synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[639,1088],"cond":["K₂CO₃, acetone (or NaH, THF)"],"p":1089},"ex":"Williamson ether synthesis: An alkoxide or phenoxide displaces a primary alkyl halide by SN2 to form an ether. Secondary and tertiary halides eliminate instead.","mc":["The base deprotonates phenol to phenoxide.","Phenoxide attacks methyl iodide in an SN2 reaction, displacing iodide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Williamson ether synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Williamson_ether_synthesis#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1090},{"m":1091},{"m":1092},{"m":1093}],"t":"Fischer esterification","fig":{"r":[1094,1095],"cond":["H₂SO₄ (cat.), heat (remove water)"],"veil":true},"ex":"An acid catalyst and excess alcohol, or removal of water, convert a carboxylic acid into an ester. ¹⁸O labelling shows the ester oxygen comes from the alcohol.","mc":["Acid protonates the carbonyl oxygen of acetic acid.","Ethanol adds to the carbonyl carbon, giving a tetrahedral intermediate.","Proton transfer turns one OH into OH₂⁺, which leaves as water.","Loss of a proton gives the ester; every step is reversible."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Fischer–Speier esterification","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fischer%E2%80%93Speier_esterification"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Fischer esterification"},{"x":"Gabriel synthesis"},{"x":"Steglich esterification"},{"x":"Williamson ether synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[1094,1095],"cond":["H₂SO₄ (cat.), heat (remove water)"],"p":1096},"ex":"Fischer esterification: An acid catalyst and excess alcohol, or removal of water, convert a carboxylic acid into an ester. ¹⁸O labelling shows the ester oxygen comes from the alcohol.","mc":["Acid protonates the carbonyl oxygen of acetic acid.","Ethanol adds to the carbonyl carbon, giving a tetrahedral intermediate.","Proton transfer turns one OH into OH₂⁺, which leaves as water.","Loss of a proton gives the ester; every step is reversible."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Fischer–Speier esterification","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fischer%E2%80%93Speier_esterification"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1097},{"m":1098},{"m":1099},{"m":1100}],"t":"Steglich esterification","fig":{"r":[1101,1102],"cond":["DCC, DMAP (cat.), CH₂Cl₂"],"veil":true},"ex":"DCC activates a carboxylic acid and DMAP catalyses acyl transfer to the alcohol, under mild conditions. Works for hindered alcohols such as tert-butanol; the by-product is insoluble dicyclohexylurea.","mc":["The carboxylic acid adds to DCC, giving an O-acylisourea.","DMAP attacks the O-acylisourea, giving an acylpyridinium ion and dicyclohexylurea.","The alcohol attacks the acylpyridinium ion, giving the ester and releasing DMAP."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Steglich esterification: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steglich_esterification#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Steglich esterification"},{"x":"Williamson ether synthesis"},{"x":"Mitsunobu reaction"},{"x":"Hell–Volhard–Zelinsky reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1101,1102],"cond":["DCC, DMAP (cat.), CH₂Cl₂"],"p":1103},"ex":"Steglich esterification: DCC activates a carboxylic acid and DMAP catalyses acyl transfer to the alcohol, under mild conditions. Works for hindered alcohols such as tert-butanol; the by-product is insoluble dicyclohexylurea.","mc":["The carboxylic acid adds to DCC, giving an O-acylisourea.","DMAP attacks the O-acylisourea, giving an acylpyridinium ion and dicyclohexylurea.","The alcohol attacks the acylpyridinium ion, giving the ester and releasing DMAP."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Steglich esterification: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steglich_esterification#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1104},{"m":1105},{"m":1106},{"m":1107}],"t":"Gabriel synthesis","fig":{"r":[1108],"cond":["potassium phthalimide, DMF","N₂H₄, EtOH (Ing–Manske)"],"veil":true},"ex":"Potassium phthalimide alkylates a primary halide; hydrazine then releases the primary amine. Stops at the primary amine, with no over-alkylation.","mc":["The phthalimide anion attacks the alkyl bromide (SN2), giving the N-alkylphthalimide.","Hydrazine attacks both imide carbonyls, releasing the primary amine and phthalhydrazide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Gabriel synthesis","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gabriel_synthesis"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Gabriel synthesis"},{"x":"Ritter reaction"},{"x":"Reductive amination"},{"x":"Eschweiler–Clarke reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1108],"cond":["potassium phthalimide, DMF","N₂H₄, EtOH (Ing–Manske)"],"p":1109},"ex":"Gabriel synthesis: Potassium phthalimide alkylates a primary halide; hydrazine then releases the primary amine. Stops at the primary amine, with no over-alkylation.","mc":["The phthalimide anion attacks the alkyl bromide (SN2), giving the N-alkylphthalimide.","Hydrazine attacks both imide carbonyls, releasing the primary amine and phthalhydrazide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Gabriel synthesis","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gabriel_synthesis"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1110},{"m":1111},{"m":1112},{"m":1113}],"t":"Strecker synthesis","fig":{"r":[278],"cond":["NH₄Cl, KCN, H₂O","H₃O⁺, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"An aldehyde, ammonia and cyanide give an α-amino nitrile, which is hydrolysed to an α-amino acid. Gives racemic amino acids.","mc":["The aldehyde and ammonia condense to an imine, protonated to an iminium ion.","Cyanide adds to the iminium carbon, giving the α-amino nitrile.","Acid hydrolysis converts the nitrile, via the amide, into the carboxylic acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Strecker synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strecker_amino_acid_synthesis#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Strecker synthesis"},{"x":"Mitsunobu reaction"},{"x":"Fischer esterification"},{"x":"Eschweiler–Clarke reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[278],"cond":["NH₄Cl, KCN, H₂O","H₃O⁺, heat"],"p":1114},"ex":"Strecker synthesis: An aldehyde, ammonia and cyanide give an α-amino nitrile, which is hydrolysed to an α-amino acid. Gives racemic amino acids.","mc":["The aldehyde and ammonia condense to an imine, protonated to an iminium ion.","Cyanide adds to the iminium carbon, giving the α-amino nitrile.","Acid hydrolysis converts the nitrile, via the amide, into the carboxylic acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Strecker synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strecker_amino_acid_synthesis#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1115},{"m":1116},{"m":1117},{"m":1118}],"t":"Reductive amination","fig":{"r":[263,1119],"cond":["NaBH₃CN, MeOH, pH ~6"],"veil":true},"ex":"A carbonyl compound and an amine form an imine or iminium ion that is reduced in situ to an amine. Avoids the over-alkylation of direct N-alkylation with alkyl halides.","mc":["Benzylamine adds to the ketone, and loss of water gives an imine, protonated to an iminium ion at pH ~6.","NaBH₃CN delivers hydride to the iminium ion much faster than to the ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Reductive amination","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reductive_amination"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Reductive amination"},{"x":"Mitsunobu reaction"},{"x":"Williamson ether synthesis"},{"x":"Ritter reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[263,1119],"cond":["NaBH₃CN, MeOH, pH ~6"],"p":1120},"ex":"Reductive amination: A carbonyl compound and an amine form an imine or iminium ion that is reduced in situ to an amine. Avoids the over-alkylation of direct N-alkylation with alkyl halides.","mc":["Benzylamine adds to the ketone, and loss of water gives an imine, protonated to an iminium ion at pH ~6.","NaBH₃CN delivers hydride to the iminium ion much faster than to the ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Reductive amination","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reductive_amination"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1121},{"m":1122},{"m":1123},{"m":1124}],"t":"Eschweiler–Clarke reaction","fig":{"r":[1119],"cond":["HCHO, HCOOH, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"Formaldehyde and formic acid dimethylate a primary or secondary amine without forming a quaternary salt. Formic acid is the hydride donor and leaves as CO₂.","mc":["The amine and formaldehyde form an iminium ion.","Formate transfers a hydride to the iminium ion, releasing CO₂ and giving the N-methyl amine.","The cycle repeats on the secondary amine; a tertiary amine cannot form an iminium ion, so it stops there."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Eschweiler–Clarke reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eschweiler%E2%80%93Clarke_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Eschweiler–Clarke reaction"},{"x":"Reductive amination"},{"x":"Gabriel synthesis"},{"x":"Finkelstein reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1119],"cond":["HCHO, HCOOH, heat"],"p":1125},"ex":"Eschweiler–Clarke reaction: Formaldehyde and formic acid dimethylate a primary or secondary amine without forming a quaternary salt. Formic acid is the hydride donor and leaves as CO₂.","mc":["The amine and formaldehyde form an iminium ion.","Formate transfers a hydride to the iminium ion, releasing CO₂ and giving the N-methyl amine.","The cycle repeats on the secondary amine; a tertiary amine cannot form an iminium ion, so it stops there."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Eschweiler–Clarke reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eschweiler%E2%80%93Clarke_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1126},{"m":1127},{"m":1128},{"m":1129}],"t":"Sandmeyer reaction","fig":{"r":[1130],"cond":["NaNO₂, HCl, 0 °C","CuBr"],"veil":true},"ex":"An aryl diazonium salt is converted by a copper(I) salt into an aryl halide or nitrile. A radical mechanism: copper(I) gives an electron to the diazonium ion.","mc":["Nitrous acid (NaNO₂ and HCl) converts the aniline into the aryl diazonium ion.","Cu(I) transfers one electron to the diazonium ion, which loses N₂, giving an aryl radical.","The aryl radical abstracts bromine from Cu(II), giving bromobenzene and regenerating Cu(I)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Sandmeyer reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandmeyer_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Sandmeyer reaction"},{"x":"Gabriel synthesis"},{"x":"Strecker synthesis"},{"x":"Ritter reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1130],"cond":["NaNO₂, HCl, 0 °C","CuBr"],"p":1131},"ex":"Sandmeyer reaction: An aryl diazonium salt is converted by a copper(I) salt into an aryl halide or nitrile. A radical mechanism: copper(I) gives an electron to the diazonium ion.","mc":["Nitrous acid (NaNO₂ and HCl) converts the aniline into the aryl diazonium ion.","Cu(I) transfers one electron to the diazonium ion, which loses N₂, giving an aryl radical.","The aryl radical abstracts bromine from Cu(II), giving bromobenzene and regenerating Cu(I)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Sandmeyer reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sandmeyer_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1132},{"m":1107},{"m":1133},{"m":1134}],"t":"Finkelstein reaction","fig":{"r":[1135],"cond":["NaI, acetone"],"veil":true},"ex":"Halide exchange by SN2; NaCl or NaBr precipitates from acetone and drives the reaction.","mc":["Iodide attacks the carbon bearing chlorine from the back (SN2).","Chloride leaves and precipitates as NaCl, which does not dissolve in acetone, so the equilibrium is pulled forward."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Finkelstein reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finkelstein_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Finkelstein reaction"},{"x":"Strecker synthesis"},{"x":"Gabriel synthesis"},{"x":"Steglich esterification"}],"fig":{"r":[1135],"cond":["NaI, acetone"],"p":1136},"ex":"Finkelstein reaction: Halide exchange by SN2; NaCl or NaBr precipitates from acetone and drives the reaction.","mc":["Iodide attacks the carbon bearing chlorine from the back (SN2).","Chloride leaves and precipitates as NaCl, which does not dissolve in acetone, so the equilibrium is pulled forward."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Finkelstein reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finkelstein_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1137},{"m":1138},{"m":1139},{"m":1140}],"t":"Hell–Volhard–Zelinsky reaction","fig":{"r":[1141],"cond":["Br₂, PBr₃ (cat.)","H₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"PBr₃ and Br₂ brominate a carboxylic acid at the α-position, via the enol of its acyl bromide.","mc":["PBr₃ converts the acid into the acyl bromide.","The acyl bromide enolises far more than the acid; the enol attacks Br₂ at the α-carbon.","Water hydrolyses the α-bromo acyl bromide to the α-bromo acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Hell–Volhard–Zelinsky reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hell%E2%80%93Volhard%E2%80%93Zelinsky_halogenation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Hell–Volhard–Zelinsky reaction"},{"x":"Appel reaction"},{"x":"Ritter reaction"},{"x":"Sandmeyer reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1141],"cond":["Br₂, PBr₃ (cat.)","H₂O"],"p":1142},"ex":"Hell–Volhard–Zelinsky reaction: PBr₃ and Br₂ brominate a carboxylic acid at the α-position, via the enol of its acyl bromide.","mc":["PBr₃ converts the acid into the acyl bromide.","The acyl bromide enolises far more than the acid; the enol attacks Br₂ at the α-carbon.","Water hydrolyses the α-bromo acyl bromide to the α-bromo acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Hell–Volhard–Zelinsky reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hell%E2%80%93Volhard%E2%80%93Zelinsky_halogenation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1143},{"m":1144},{"m":1145},{"m":1146}],"t":"Arndt–Eistert homologation","fig":{"r":[959],"cond":["(COCl)₂","CH₂N₂","Ag₂O, H₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"A carboxylic acid gains one CH₂ via a diazoketone and a Wolff rearrangement. TMSCHN₂ is a safer substitute for diazomethane.","mc":["Oxalyl chloride converts the acid into the acid chloride.","Diazomethane attacks the acid chloride, giving the α-diazoketone; a second equivalent mops up HCl.","Ag₂O promotes a Wolff rearrangement to a ketene, which water converts into the acid with one more carbon."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Arndt–Eistert homologation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arndt%E2%80%93Eistert_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Arndt–Eistert homologation"},{"x":"Strecker synthesis"},{"x":"Eschweiler–Clarke reaction"},{"x":"Fischer esterification"}],"fig":{"r":[959],"cond":["(COCl)₂","CH₂N₂","Ag₂O, H₂O"],"p":1147},"ex":"Arndt–Eistert homologation: A carboxylic acid gains one CH₂ via a diazoketone and a Wolff rearrangement. TMSCHN₂ is a safer substitute for diazomethane.","mc":["Oxalyl chloride converts the acid into the acid chloride.","Diazomethane attacks the acid chloride, giving the α-diazoketone; a second equivalent mops up HCl.","Ag₂O promotes a Wolff rearrangement to a ketene, which water converts into the acid with one more carbon."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Arndt–Eistert homologation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arndt%E2%80%93Eistert_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1148},{"m":1149},{"m":1150},{"m":1151}],"t":"Ritter reaction","fig":{"r":[1152,1153],"cond":["H₂SO₄"],"veil":true},"ex":"A carbocation from an alcohol or alkene in strong acid adds to a nitrile, giving an N-alkyl amide.","mc":["Sulfuric acid protonates tert-butanol, which loses water, giving the tert-butyl cation.","The nitrile nitrogen attacks the carbocation, giving a nitrilium ion.","Water adds to the nitrilium carbon; tautomerisation gives the N-tert-butyl amide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ritter reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ritter_reaction#Mechanism_and_scope"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Ritter reaction"},{"x":"Gabriel synthesis"},{"x":"Appel reaction"},{"x":"Eschweiler–Clarke reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1152,1153],"cond":["H₂SO₄"],"p":1154},"ex":"Ritter reaction: A carbocation from an alcohol or alkene in strong acid adds to a nitrile, giving an N-alkyl amide.","mc":["Sulfuric acid protonates tert-butanol, which loses water, giving the tert-butyl cation.","The nitrile nitrogen attacks the carbocation, giving a nitrilium ion.","Water adds to the nitrilium carbon; tautomerisation gives the N-tert-butyl amide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ritter reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ritter_reaction#Mechanism_and_scope"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1155},{"m":1156},{"m":1157},{"m":1158}],"t":"Hydroboration–oxidation","fig":{"r":[1159],"cond":["BH₃·THF","H₂O₂, NaOH"],"veil":true},"ex":"Borane adds across an alkene (anti-Markovnikov, syn); oxidation then replaces boron with OH. Boron goes to the less hindered carbon, so the OH does too.","mc":["BH₃ adds across the alkene in one four-centred step: boron to the less hindered carbon, H to the other, on the same face.","Hydroperoxide adds to boron, giving a borate.","The alkyl group migrates from boron to oxygen with retention, displacing hydroxide.","Hydrolysis of the borate ester releases the alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Hydroboration–oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydroboration%E2%80%93oxidation_reaction#Mechanism_and_scope"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Hydroboration–oxidation"},{"x":"Steglich esterification"},{"x":"Gabriel synthesis"},{"x":"Mitsunobu reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1159],"cond":["BH₃·THF","H₂O₂, NaOH"],"p":1160},"ex":"Hydroboration–oxidation: Borane adds across an alkene (anti-Markovnikov, syn); oxidation then replaces boron with OH. Boron goes to the less hindered carbon, so the OH does too.","mc":["BH₃ adds across the alkene in one four-centred step: boron to the less hindered carbon, H to the other, on the same face.","Hydroperoxide adds to boron, giving a borate.","The alkyl group migrates from boron to oxygen with retention, displacing hydroxide.","Hydrolysis of the borate ester releases the alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Hydroboration–oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydroboration%E2%80%93oxidation_reaction#Mechanism_and_scope"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1161},{"m":1162},{"m":1163},{"m":1164}],"t":"Weinreb ketone synthesis","fig":{"r":[1165],"cond":["PhMgBr, THF, 0 °C","H₃O⁺"],"veil":true},"ex":"A Grignard or organolithium reagent adds once to an N-methoxy-N-methylamide (Weinreb amide), giving a ketone with no over-addition. The tetrahedral adduct is held as a stable five-membered chelate and only collapses to the ketone on work-up, after the excess organometallic has been destroyed. LiAlH₄ or DIBAL-H give the aldehyde instead.","mc":["The phenyl group adds to the amide carbonyl, giving a tetrahedral alkoxide.","Magnesium binds both the alkoxide and the N-methoxy oxygen, giving a stable five-membered chelate that does not collapse.","Acidic work-up breaks the chelate; the hemiaminal loses N,O-dimethylhydroxylamine, giving the ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Weinreb ketone synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weinreb_ketone_synthesis#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Weinreb ketone synthesis"},{"x":"Haloform reaction"},{"x":"Yamaguchi esterification"},{"x":"Jocic reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1165],"cond":["PhMgBr, THF, 0 °C","H₃O⁺"],"p":1166},"ex":"Weinreb ketone synthesis: A Grignard or organolithium reagent adds once to an N-methoxy-N-methylamide (Weinreb amide), giving a ketone with no over-addition. The tetrahedral adduct is held as a stable five-membered chelate and only collapses to the ketone on work-up, after the excess organometallic has been destroyed. LiAlH₄ or DIBAL-H give the aldehyde instead.","mc":["The phenyl group adds to the amide carbonyl, giving a tetrahedral alkoxide.","Magnesium binds both the alkoxide and the N-methoxy oxygen, giving a stable five-membered chelate that does not collapse.","Acidic work-up breaks the chelate; the hemiaminal loses N,O-dimethylhydroxylamine, giving the ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Weinreb ketone synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weinreb_ketone_synthesis#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1167},{"m":1168},{"m":1169},{"m":1170}],"t":"Schotten–Baumann reaction","fig":{"r":[1171,899],"cond":["NaOH (aq), CH₂Cl₂, 0 °C → rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"An amine (or alcohol) is acylated by an acid chloride in a two-phase system with aqueous base, which neutralises the HCl formed. Without the base the HCl would protonate half of the amine and stop it reacting. The acid chloride stays in the organic layer, so little of it is hydrolysed.","mc":["The amine adds to the acid chloride carbonyl, giving a tetrahedral intermediate.","Chloride leaves, giving the protonated amide.","Hydroxide in the water layer removes the proton and neutralises the HCl, so the amine stays free to react."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Schotten–Baumann reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schotten%E2%80%93Baumann_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Schotten–Baumann reaction"},{"x":"Pinner reaction"},{"x":"Jocic reaction"},{"x":"Haloform reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1171,899],"cond":["NaOH (aq), CH₂Cl₂, 0 °C → rt"],"p":1172},"ex":"Schotten–Baumann reaction: An amine (or alcohol) is acylated by an acid chloride in a two-phase system with aqueous base, which neutralises the HCl formed. Without the base the HCl would protonate half of the amine and stop it reacting. The acid chloride stays in the organic layer, so little of it is hydrolysed.","mc":["The amine adds to the acid chloride carbonyl, giving a tetrahedral intermediate.","Chloride leaves, giving the protonated amide.","Hydroxide in the water layer removes the proton and neutralises the HCl, so the amine stays free to react."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Schotten–Baumann reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schotten%E2%80%93Baumann_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1173},{"m":1174},{"m":1175},{"m":722}],"t":"Yamaguchi esterification","fig":{"r":[1176,1177],"cond":["2,4,6-Cl₃C₆H₂COCl, Et₃N, THF","DMAP (1 equiv), toluene"],"veil":true},"ex":"The acid is activated as a mixed anhydride with 2,4,6-trichlorobenzoyl chloride; DMAP then transfers the acyl group to the alcohol under mild conditions. DMAP attacks the less hindered carbonyl, so the bulky trichlorobenzoyl group stays behind. Widely used for macrolactonisation at high dilution.","mc":["The carboxylate attacks 2,4,6-trichlorobenzoyl chloride, giving the mixed anhydride.","DMAP attacks the less hindered carbonyl (the hexanoyl one), giving an acylpyridinium ion.","The alcohol attacks the acylpyridinium ion, giving the ester and releasing DMAP."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Yamaguchi esterification: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yamaguchi_esterification#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Yamaguchi esterification"},{"x":"Delépine reaction"},{"x":"Kolbe nitrile synthesis"},{"x":"Jocic reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1176,1177],"cond":["2,4,6-Cl₃C₆H₂COCl, Et₃N, THF","DMAP (1 equiv), toluene"],"p":1178},"ex":"Yamaguchi esterification: The acid is activated as a mixed anhydride with 2,4,6-trichlorobenzoyl chloride; DMAP then transfers the acyl group to the alcohol under mild conditions. DMAP attacks the less hindered carbonyl, so the bulky trichlorobenzoyl group stays behind. Widely used for macrolactonisation at high dilution.","mc":["The carboxylate attacks 2,4,6-trichlorobenzoyl chloride, giving the mixed anhydride.","DMAP attacks the less hindered carbonyl (the hexanoyl one), giving an acylpyridinium ion.","The alcohol attacks the acylpyridinium ion, giving the ester and releasing DMAP."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Yamaguchi esterification: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yamaguchi_esterification#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1179},{"m":1180},{"m":374},{"m":1181}],"t":"Pinner reaction","fig":{"r":[691,1095],"cond":["HCl (g), EtOH, CHCl₃, 0 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"A nitrile and an alcohol with dry HCl give an imino ester hydrochloride (a Pinner salt), which can be turned into esters, amidines or orthoesters. Water must be kept out, or the salt hydrolyses to the ester. Ammonia or amines turn the Pinner salt into amidines; excess alcohol gives an orthoester.","mc":["HCl protonates the nitrile nitrogen, giving a very electrophilic nitrilium ion.","Ethanol adds to the nitrilium carbon.","Proton transfer gives the imino ester, which crystallises as its hydrochloride (the Pinner salt)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Pinner reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinner_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Pinner reaction"},{"x":"Weinreb ketone synthesis"},{"x":"Delépine reaction"},{"x":"Kolbe nitrile synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[691,1095],"cond":["HCl (g), EtOH, CHCl₃, 0 °C"],"p":1182},"ex":"Pinner reaction: A nitrile and an alcohol with dry HCl give an imino ester hydrochloride (a Pinner salt), which can be turned into esters, amidines or orthoesters. Water must be kept out, or the salt hydrolyses to the ester. Ammonia or amines turn the Pinner salt into amidines; excess alcohol gives an orthoester.","mc":["HCl protonates the nitrile nitrogen, giving a very electrophilic nitrilium ion.","Ethanol adds to the nitrilium carbon.","Proton transfer gives the imino ester, which crystallises as its hydrochloride (the Pinner salt)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Pinner reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinner_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1183},{"m":1184},{"m":1185},{"m":1186}],"t":"Delépine reaction","fig":{"r":[358],"cond":["hexamethylenetetramine, CHCl₃, reflux","HCl (conc.), EtOH, reflux"],"veil":true},"ex":"An alkyl halide alkylates hexamethylenetetramine (urotropine); acidic ethanolysis of the quaternary salt then releases the primary amine. Like the Gabriel synthesis it stops at the primary amine. The salt crystallises from chloroform; formaldehyde (as its diethyl acetal) and ammonium chloride are the by-products.","mc":["A nitrogen of hexamethylenetetramine displaces bromide (SN2), giving a quaternary ammonium salt.","Acid and ethanol break down the cage through hemiaminals, releasing formaldehyde (as its diethyl acetal) and ammonium chloride.","This leaves benzylamine as its hydrochloride."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Delépine reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Del%C3%A9pine_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Delépine reaction"},{"x":"Schotten–Baumann reaction"},{"x":"Jocic reaction"},{"x":"Yamaguchi esterification"}],"fig":{"r":[358],"cond":["hexamethylenetetramine, CHCl₃, reflux","HCl (conc.), EtOH, reflux"],"p":1187},"ex":"Delépine reaction: An alkyl halide alkylates hexamethylenetetramine (urotropine); acidic ethanolysis of the quaternary salt then releases the primary amine. Like the Gabriel synthesis it stops at the primary amine. The salt crystallises from chloroform; formaldehyde (as its diethyl acetal) and ammonium chloride are the by-products.","mc":["A nitrogen of hexamethylenetetramine displaces bromide (SN2), giving a quaternary ammonium salt.","Acid and ethanol break down the cage through hemiaminals, releasing formaldehyde (as its diethyl acetal) and ammonium chloride.","This leaves benzylamine as its hydrochloride."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Delépine reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Del%C3%A9pine_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1188},{"m":1189},{"m":1190},{"m":1191}],"t":"Kolbe nitrile synthesis","fig":{"r":[1192],"cond":["NaCN, DMSO, 60 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"An alkyl halide and an alkali metal cyanide give a nitrile with one more carbon, by SN2. Polar aprotic solvents such as DMSO work best; primary halides react well and tertiary ones mostly eliminate. Cyanide is ambident: covalent cyanides (AgCN, CuCN) and protic solvents favour the isonitrile.","mc":["Cyanide, poorly solvated in DMSO, attacks the carbon bonded to bromine from the back (SN2).","The C–C bond forms as bromide leaves, in one step.","The carbon end of cyanide reacts, giving the nitrile."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Kolbe nitrile synthesis","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kolbe_nitrile_synthesis"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Kolbe nitrile synthesis"},{"x":"Weinreb ketone synthesis"},{"x":"Haloform reaction"},{"x":"Schotten–Baumann reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1192],"cond":["NaCN, DMSO, 60 °C"],"p":1193},"ex":"Kolbe nitrile synthesis: An alkyl halide and an alkali metal cyanide give a nitrile with one more carbon, by SN2. Polar aprotic solvents such as DMSO work best; primary halides react well and tertiary ones mostly eliminate. Cyanide is ambident: covalent cyanides (AgCN, CuCN) and protic solvents favour the isonitrile.","mc":["Cyanide, poorly solvated in DMSO, attacks the carbon bonded to bromine from the back (SN2).","The C–C bond forms as bromide leaves, in one step.","The carbon end of cyanide reacts, giving the nitrile."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Kolbe nitrile synthesis","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kolbe_nitrile_synthesis"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product under these conditions?","o":[{"m":1194},{"m":1195},{"m":1196},{"m":1197}],"t":"Nef reaction","fig":{"r":[1198],"cond":["NaOH, EtOH","H₂SO₄ (aq), 0 °C, pH < 1"],"veil":true,"key":["pH < 1"]},"ex":"A nitroalkane is turned into a ketone or aldehyde: its nitronate salt is hydrolysed in strong acid, releasing N₂O. The acid must be strong (pH < 1); weaker acid gives oximes and other side products. Oxidative (Oxone) and reductive (TiCl₃, through the oxime) variants exist.","mc":["Base removes the α-proton, giving the nitronate salt.","Strong acid protonates it to the nitronic acid and further; water adds to its carbon.","Loss of HNO (which gives N₂O and water) leaves the ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Nef reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nef_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Nef reaction"},{"x":"Weinreb ketone synthesis"},{"x":"Haloform reaction"},{"x":"Pinner reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1198],"cond":["NaOH, EtOH","H₂SO₄ (aq), 0 °C, pH < 1"],"p":1199,"key":["pH < 1"]},"ex":"Nef reaction: A nitroalkane is turned into a ketone or aldehyde: its nitronate salt is hydrolysed in strong acid, releasing N₂O. The acid must be strong (pH < 1); weaker acid gives oximes and other side products. Oxidative (Oxone) and reductive (TiCl₃, through the oxime) variants exist.","mc":["Base removes the α-proton, giving the nitronate salt.","Strong acid protonates it to the nitronic acid and further; water adds to its carbon.","Loss of HNO (which gives N₂O and water) leaves the ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Nef reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nef_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1200},{"m":1201},{"m":1202},{"m":1203}],"t":"Haloform reaction","fig":{"r":[1204],"cond":["NaOCl (excess), NaOH, H₂O, 60 °C","H₃O⁺"],"veil":true},"ex":"A methyl ketone is halogenated three times at the methyl group in base; hydroxide then cleaves off CX₃⁻ (leaving as the haloform), giving the carboxylic acid. Each halogen makes the next deprotonation easier, so it runs to CX₃. With iodine, yellow iodoform precipitates: the classic test for methyl ketones (and for alcohols that oxidise to them).","mc":["Hydroxide forms the enolate, which takes Cl⁺ from hypochlorite; this repeats until the methyl group is CCl₃.","Hydroxide adds to the carbonyl; the tetrahedral intermediate expels CCl₃⁻, a good leaving group.","Proton transfer gives chloroform and the carboxylate; acid work-up gives benzoic acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Haloform reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haloform_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Haloform reaction"},{"x":"Yamaguchi esterification"},{"x":"Weinreb ketone synthesis"},{"x":"Jocic reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1204],"cond":["NaOCl (excess), NaOH, H₂O, 60 °C","H₃O⁺"],"p":1205},"ex":"Haloform reaction: A methyl ketone is halogenated three times at the methyl group in base; hydroxide then cleaves off CX₃⁻ (leaving as the haloform), giving the carboxylic acid. Each halogen makes the next deprotonation easier, so it runs to CX₃. With iodine, yellow iodoform precipitates: the classic test for methyl ketones (and for alcohols that oxidise to them).","mc":["Hydroxide forms the enolate, which takes Cl⁺ from hypochlorite; this repeats until the methyl group is CCl₃.","Hydroxide adds to the carbonyl; the tetrahedral intermediate expels CCl₃⁻, a good leaving group.","Proton transfer gives chloroform and the carboxylate; acid work-up gives benzoic acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Haloform reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haloform_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1206},{"m":1207},{"m":1208},{"m":1209}],"t":"Jocic reaction","fig":{"r":[1210],"cond":["NaN₃, NaOH, MeOH/H₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"A trichloromethyl carbinol and a nucleophile in aqueous base give an α-substituted carboxylic acid, through a gem-dichloro epoxide. The nucleophile opens the epoxide by SN2 with inversion, so an enantioenriched carbinol (from a CBS reduction of the trichloromethyl ketone) gives an enantioenriched α-azido acid, and then an amino acid.","mc":["Hydroxide deprotonates the alcohol; the alkoxide displaces a chloride from the CCl₃ group, giving a gem-dichloro epoxide.","Azide opens the epoxide at the carbon bearing the cyclohexyl group (SN2, with inversion), giving an acyl chloride.","Hydroxide hydrolyses the acyl chloride, giving the α-azido acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Jocic reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jocic_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Jocic reaction"},{"x":"Delépine reaction"},{"x":"Kolbe nitrile synthesis"},{"x":"Nef reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1210],"cond":["NaN₃, NaOH, MeOH/H₂O"],"p":1211},"ex":"Jocic reaction: A trichloromethyl carbinol and a nucleophile in aqueous base give an α-substituted carboxylic acid, through a gem-dichloro epoxide. The nucleophile opens the epoxide by SN2 with inversion, so an enantioenriched carbinol (from a CBS reduction of the trichloromethyl ketone) gives an enantioenriched α-azido acid, and then an amino acid.","mc":["Hydroxide deprotonates the alcohol; the alkoxide displaces a chloride from the CCl₃ group, giving a gem-dichloro epoxide.","Azide opens the epoxide at the carbon bearing the cyclohexyl group (SN2, with inversion), giving an acyl chloride.","Hydroxide hydrolyses the acyl chloride, giving the α-azido acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Jocic reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jocic_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1212},{"m":1213},{"m":1214},{"m":1215}],"t":"Friedel–Crafts acylation","fig":{"r":[613,1071],"cond":["AlCl₃, CH₂Cl₂"],"veil":true},"ex":"An acyl chloride and AlCl₃ form an acylium ion that acylates an arene, giving an aryl ketone. The product ketone deactivates the ring, so the reaction stops after one acylation.","mc":["AlCl₃ takes chloride from acetyl chloride, giving the acylium ion CH₃C≡O⁺.","The arene attacks the acylium ion, giving a σ-complex (Wheland intermediate).","Loss of a proton restores aromaticity; the ketone stays complexed to AlCl₃ until work-up."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Friedel–Crafts acylation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friedel%E2%80%93Crafts_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Friedel–Crafts acylation"},{"x":"Blanc chloromethylation"},{"x":"Diazotisation"},{"x":"Vicarious nucleophilic substitution"}],"fig":{"r":[613,1071],"cond":["AlCl₃, CH₂Cl₂"],"p":1216},"ex":"Friedel–Crafts acylation: An acyl chloride and AlCl₃ form an acylium ion that acylates an arene, giving an aryl ketone. The product ketone deactivates the ring, so the reaction stops after one acylation.","mc":["AlCl₃ takes chloride from acetyl chloride, giving the acylium ion CH₃C≡O⁺.","The arene attacks the acylium ion, giving a σ-complex (Wheland intermediate).","Loss of a proton restores aromaticity; the ketone stays complexed to AlCl₃ until work-up."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Friedel–Crafts acylation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friedel%E2%80%93Crafts_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product under these conditions?","o":[{"m":1217},{"m":1218},{"m":1219},{"m":1220}],"t":"Friedel–Crafts alkylation","fig":{"r":[613,1221],"cond":["AlCl₃, excess benzene"],"veil":true,"key":["excess benzene"]},"ex":"A carbocation from an alkyl halide and a Lewis acid alkylates an arene. The tert-butyl group makes the product more nucleophilic than benzene, so a second alkylation competes; only a large excess of benzene stops it at one. With more t-BuCl, 1,4-di-tert-butylbenzene forms. Unlike the acylation, the alkylation is also prone to carbocation rearrangements.","mc":["AlCl₃ takes chloride from tert-butyl chloride, giving the tert-butyl cation.","The arene attacks the carbocation, giving a σ-complex.","Loss of a proton restores aromaticity and regenerates AlCl₃, releasing HCl."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Friedel–Crafts alkylation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friedel%E2%80%93Crafts_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product under these conditions?","o":[{"m":1222},{"m":1217},{"m":1223},{"m":1224}],"t":"Friedel–Crafts alkylation","fig":{"r":[613,1221],"cond":["AlCl₃, excess t-BuCl, 0 °C"],"veil":true,"key":["excess t-BuCl"]},"ex":"A carbocation from an alkyl halide and a Lewis acid alkylates an arene. The first tert-butyl group makes the ring more nucleophilic than benzene, so with t-BuCl in excess a second one adds, para to the first: next to it is too crowded. With benzene in excess instead, the reaction stops at tert-butylbenzene.","mc":["AlCl₃ takes chloride from tert-butyl chloride, giving the tert-butyl cation.","The arene attacks the carbocation, giving a σ-complex.","Loss of a proton restores aromaticity and regenerates AlCl₃, releasing HCl."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Friedel–Crafts alkylation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friedel%E2%80%93Crafts_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Friedel–Crafts alkylation"},{"x":"Friedel–Crafts acylation"},{"x":"Vilsmeier–Haack reaction"},{"x":"Kolbe–Schmitt reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[613,1221],"cond":["AlCl₃, excess benzene"],"p":1225,"key":["excess benzene"]},"ex":"Friedel–Crafts alkylation: A carbocation from an alkyl halide and a Lewis acid alkylates an arene. The tert-butyl group makes the product more nucleophilic than benzene, so a second alkylation competes; only a large excess of benzene stops it at one. With more t-BuCl, 1,4-di-tert-butylbenzene forms. Unlike the acylation, the alkylation is also prone to carbocation rearrangements.","mc":["AlCl₃ takes chloride from tert-butyl chloride, giving the tert-butyl cation.","The arene attacks the carbocation, giving a σ-complex.","Loss of a proton restores aromaticity and regenerates AlCl₃, releasing HCl."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Friedel–Crafts alkylation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friedel%E2%80%93Crafts_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1226},{"m":1227},{"m":1228},{"m":1229}],"t":"Vilsmeier–Haack reaction","fig":{"r":[1230],"cond":["POCl₃, DMF","H₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"DMF and POCl₃ form a chloroiminium ion that formylates electron-rich arenes. Hydrolysis of the iminium ion at the end gives the aldehyde.","mc":["DMF attacks POCl₃; loss of a phosphate gives the chloroiminium ion (the Vilsmeier reagent).","The electron-rich arene attacks the chloroiminium carbon para to NMe₂, and loss of a proton restores aromaticity.","Loss of chloride gives an aryl iminium ion, which water hydrolyses to the aldehyde."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Vilsmeier–Haack reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vilsmeier%E2%80%93Haack_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Vilsmeier–Haack reaction"},{"x":"Skraup synthesis"},{"x":"Reimer–Tiemann reaction"},{"x":"Blanc chloromethylation"}],"fig":{"r":[1230],"cond":["POCl₃, DMF","H₂O"],"p":1231},"ex":"Vilsmeier–Haack reaction: DMF and POCl₃ form a chloroiminium ion that formylates electron-rich arenes. Hydrolysis of the iminium ion at the end gives the aldehyde.","mc":["DMF attacks POCl₃; loss of a phosphate gives the chloroiminium ion (the Vilsmeier reagent).","The electron-rich arene attacks the chloroiminium carbon para to NMe₂, and loss of a proton restores aromaticity.","Loss of chloride gives an aryl iminium ion, which water hydrolyses to the aldehyde."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Vilsmeier–Haack reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vilsmeier%E2%80%93Haack_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1232},{"m":1233},{"m":1234},{"m":1235}],"t":"Fischer indole synthesis","fig":{"r":[484,1236],"cond":["phenylhydrazine (forms the hydrazone)","ZnCl₂ or H⁺, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"The aryl hydrazone of a ketone cyclises under acid, with loss of NH₃, to an indole. The key step is a [3,3]-sigmatropic shift of the ene-hydrazine.","mc":["Phenylhydrazine condenses with the ketone to the phenylhydrazone, which tautomerises to the ene-hydrazine.","A [3,3]-sigmatropic rearrangement breaks the N–N bond and forms the C–C bond to the ring.","The ring rearomatises, and the aniline nitrogen attacks the imine, giving a cyclic aminal.","Loss of NH₃ under acid gives the aromatic indole."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Fischer indole synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fischer_indole_synthesis#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Fischer indole synthesis"},{"x":"Bischler–Napieralski reaction"},{"x":"Vicarious nucleophilic substitution"},{"x":"Diazotisation"}],"fig":{"r":[484,1236],"cond":["phenylhydrazine (forms the hydrazone)","ZnCl₂ or H⁺, heat"],"p":1237},"ex":"Fischer indole synthesis: The aryl hydrazone of a ketone cyclises under acid, with loss of NH₃, to an indole. The key step is a [3,3]-sigmatropic shift of the ene-hydrazine.","mc":["Phenylhydrazine condenses with the ketone to the phenylhydrazone, which tautomerises to the ene-hydrazine.","A [3,3]-sigmatropic rearrangement breaks the N–N bond and forms the C–C bond to the ring.","The ring rearomatises, and the aniline nitrogen attacks the imine, giving a cyclic aminal.","Loss of NH₃ under acid gives the aromatic indole."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Fischer indole synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fischer_indole_synthesis#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1238},{"m":1239},{"m":1240},{"m":1241}],"t":"Paal–Knorr synthesis","fig":{"r":[1242,1171],"cond":["AcOH, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"A 1,4-diketone condenses with a primary amine to a pyrrole (or with acid alone to a furan).","mc":["The amine attacks one ketone, giving a hemiaminal.","The nitrogen attacks the second ketone, closing the five-membered ring.","Loss of two molecules of water makes the ring aromatic: the pyrrole."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Paal–Knorr synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paal%E2%80%93Knorr_synthesis#Mechanisms"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Paal–Knorr synthesis"},{"x":"Fischer indole synthesis"},{"x":"Azo coupling"},{"x":"Balz–Schiemann reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1242,1171],"cond":["AcOH, heat"],"p":1243},"ex":"Paal–Knorr synthesis: A 1,4-diketone condenses with a primary amine to a pyrrole (or with acid alone to a furan).","mc":["The amine attacks one ketone, giving a hemiaminal.","The nitrogen attacks the second ketone, closing the five-membered ring.","Loss of two molecules of water makes the ring aromatic: the pyrrole."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Paal–Knorr synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paal%E2%80%93Knorr_synthesis#Mechanisms"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1244},{"m":1245},{"m":1246},{"m":1247}],"t":"Hantzsch pyridine synthesis","fig":{"r":[1248,1248,278],"cond":["NH₄OAc, EtOH, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"An aldehyde, two equivalents of a β-keto ester and ammonia give a 1,4-dihydropyridine, oxidised to a pyridine. The dihydropyridine itself is the core of drugs such as nifedipine.","mc":["One keto ester condenses with the aldehyde (Knoevenagel), giving an alkylidene keto ester.","The other keto ester forms an enamine with ammonia.","The enamine adds 1,4 to the Knoevenagel product (Michael addition).","The nitrogen closes onto the ketone, and loss of water gives the 1,4-dihydropyridine."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Hantzsch pyridine synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hantzsch_pyridine_synthesis#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Hantzsch pyridine synthesis"},{"x":"Pictet–Spengler reaction"},{"x":"Fischer indole synthesis"},{"x":"Chichibabin reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1248,1248,278],"cond":["NH₄OAc, EtOH, heat"],"p":1249},"ex":"Hantzsch pyridine synthesis: An aldehyde, two equivalents of a β-keto ester and ammonia give a 1,4-dihydropyridine, oxidised to a pyridine. The dihydropyridine itself is the core of drugs such as nifedipine.","mc":["One keto ester condenses with the aldehyde (Knoevenagel), giving an alkylidene keto ester.","The other keto ester forms an enamine with ammonia.","The enamine adds 1,4 to the Knoevenagel product (Michael addition).","The nitrogen closes onto the ketone, and loss of water gives the 1,4-dihydropyridine."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Hantzsch pyridine synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hantzsch_pyridine_synthesis#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1250},{"m":1251},{"m":1252},{"m":1253}],"t":"Pictet–Spengler reaction","fig":{"r":[1254,1255],"cond":["TFA, CH₂Cl₂"],"veil":true},"ex":"A β-arylethylamine condenses with an aldehyde and the iminium ion cyclises onto the ring. Builds tetrahydroisoquinolines and tetrahydro-β-carbolines, as in many alkaloids.","mc":["The amine and the aldehyde condense to an imine, which TFA protonates to an iminium ion.","The electron-rich indole attacks the iminium carbon, closing the six-membered ring.","Loss of a proton restores aromaticity, giving the tetrahydro-β-carboline."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Pictet–Spengler reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pictet%E2%80%93Spengler_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Pictet–Spengler reaction"},{"x":"Azo coupling"},{"x":"Friedel–Crafts alkylation"},{"x":"Chichibabin reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1254,1255],"cond":["TFA, CH₂Cl₂"],"p":1256},"ex":"Pictet–Spengler reaction: A β-arylethylamine condenses with an aldehyde and the iminium ion cyclises onto the ring. Builds tetrahydroisoquinolines and tetrahydro-β-carbolines, as in many alkaloids.","mc":["The amine and the aldehyde condense to an imine, which TFA protonates to an iminium ion.","The electron-rich indole attacks the iminium carbon, closing the six-membered ring.","Loss of a proton restores aromaticity, giving the tetrahydro-β-carboline."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Pictet–Spengler reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pictet%E2%80%93Spengler_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1257},{"m":1258},{"m":1259},{"m":1260}],"t":"Bischler–Napieralski reaction","fig":{"r":[1261],"cond":["POCl₃, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"A β-arylethylamide cyclises with POCl₃ to a 3,4-dihydroisoquinoline. Electron-donating groups on the ring speed up the cyclisation.","mc":["POCl₃ activates the amide oxygen, forming an imidoyl phosphate.","Loss of the phosphate gives a nitrilium ion.","The arene attacks the nitrilium carbon intramolecularly; loss of a proton gives the 3,4-dihydroisoquinoline."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Bischler–Napieralski reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bischler%E2%80%93Napieralski_reaction#Mechanisms"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Bischler–Napieralski reaction"},{"x":"Paal–Knorr synthesis"},{"x":"Diazotisation"},{"x":"Pictet–Spengler reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1261],"cond":["POCl₃, heat"],"p":1262},"ex":"Bischler–Napieralski reaction: A β-arylethylamide cyclises with POCl₃ to a 3,4-dihydroisoquinoline. Electron-donating groups on the ring speed up the cyclisation.","mc":["POCl₃ activates the amide oxygen, forming an imidoyl phosphate.","Loss of the phosphate gives a nitrilium ion.","The arene attacks the nitrilium carbon intramolecularly; loss of a proton gives the 3,4-dihydroisoquinoline."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Bischler–Napieralski reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bischler%E2%80%93Napieralski_reaction#Mechanisms"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1263},{"m":1264},{"m":1265},{"m":1266}],"t":"Skraup synthesis","fig":{"r":[1130],"cond":["glycerol, H₂SO₄, PhNO₂, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"Aniline heated with glycerol, sulfuric acid and an oxidant gives quinoline. Glycerol dehydrates to acrolein, which reacts with the aniline.","mc":["Hot sulfuric acid dehydrates glycerol to acrolein.","Aniline adds 1,4 to acrolein (Michael addition).","Acid closes the aldehyde onto the ring, and loss of water gives 1,2-dihydroquinoline.","Nitrobenzene oxidises it to quinoline."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Skraup synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skraup_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Skraup synthesis"},{"x":"Chichibabin reaction"},{"x":"Blanc chloromethylation"},{"x":"Pictet–Spengler reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1130],"cond":["glycerol, H₂SO₄, PhNO₂, heat"],"p":1267},"ex":"Skraup synthesis: Aniline heated with glycerol, sulfuric acid and an oxidant gives quinoline. Glycerol dehydrates to acrolein, which reacts with the aniline.","mc":["Hot sulfuric acid dehydrates glycerol to acrolein.","Aniline adds 1,4 to acrolein (Michael addition).","Acid closes the aldehyde onto the ring, and loss of water gives 1,2-dihydroquinoline.","Nitrobenzene oxidises it to quinoline."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Skraup synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skraup_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1268},{"m":1127},{"m":1269},{"m":613}],"t":"Balz–Schiemann reaction","fig":{"r":[1130],"cond":["NaNO₂, HBF₄, 0 °C","heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"An aryl diazonium tetrafluoroborate decomposes on heating to an aryl fluoride.","mc":["NaNO₂ and HBF₄ convert the aniline into the aryl diazonium tetrafluoroborate, which can be isolated.","On heating, N₂ leaves, giving an aryl cation.","The aryl cation takes fluoride from BF₄⁻, giving the aryl fluoride and BF₃."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Balz–Schiemann reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balz%E2%80%93Schiemann_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Balz–Schiemann reaction"},{"x":"Vilsmeier–Haack reaction"},{"x":"Pschorr reaction"},{"x":"Hantzsch pyridine synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[1130],"cond":["NaNO₂, HBF₄, 0 °C","heat"],"p":1270},"ex":"Balz–Schiemann reaction: An aryl diazonium tetrafluoroborate decomposes on heating to an aryl fluoride.","mc":["NaNO₂ and HBF₄ convert the aniline into the aryl diazonium tetrafluoroborate, which can be isolated.","On heating, N₂ leaves, giving an aryl cation.","The aryl cation takes fluoride from BF₄⁻, giving the aryl fluoride and BF₃."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Balz–Schiemann reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balz%E2%80%93Schiemann_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product under these conditions?","o":[{"m":1271},{"m":1272},{"m":1273},{"m":1274}],"t":"Kolbe–Schmitt reaction","fig":{"r":[639],"cond":["NaOH","CO₂ (100 atm), 125 °C","H₃O⁺"],"veil":true,"key":["NaOH"]},"ex":"A phenoxide is carboxylated by CO₂ under pressure, ortho to the oxygen, giving salicylic acid. Sodium phenoxide gives the ortho acid. Larger cations push the carboxylation para: potassium phenoxide gives 4-hydroxybenzoic acid.","mc":["NaOH converts phenol into sodium phenoxide.","The phenoxide ring attacks CO₂ ortho to the oxygen; sodium bridges the phenoxide oxygen and CO₂, which favours ortho.","Tautomerisation restores aromaticity, giving sodium salicylate; acid gives salicylic acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Kolbe–Schmitt reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kolbe%E2%80%93Schmitt_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product under these conditions?","o":[{"m":1275},{"m":1276},{"m":1277},{"m":1273}],"t":"Kolbe–Schmitt reaction","fig":{"r":[639],"cond":["KOH","CO₂, 230 °C","H₃O⁺"],"veil":true,"key":["KOH"]},"ex":"A phenoxide is carboxylated by CO₂ under pressure, ortho to the oxygen, giving salicylic acid. Larger cations push the carboxylation para: potassium phenoxide and CO₂ are how 4-hydroxybenzoic acid, the parent acid of the parabens, is made commercially, and rubidium and caesium salts are more para-selective still. Sodium phenoxide gives salicylic acid instead.","mc":["NaOH converts phenol into sodium phenoxide.","The phenoxide ring attacks CO₂ ortho to the oxygen; sodium bridges the phenoxide oxygen and CO₂, which favours ortho.","Tautomerisation restores aromaticity, giving sodium salicylate; acid gives salicylic acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Kolbe–Schmitt reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kolbe%E2%80%93Schmitt_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Kolbe–Schmitt reaction"},{"x":"Chichibabin reaction"},{"x":"Diazotisation"},{"x":"Skraup synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[639],"cond":["NaOH","CO₂ (100 atm), 125 °C","H₃O⁺"],"p":1278,"key":["NaOH"]},"ex":"Kolbe–Schmitt reaction: A phenoxide is carboxylated by CO₂ under pressure, ortho to the oxygen, giving salicylic acid. Sodium phenoxide gives the ortho acid. Larger cations push the carboxylation para: potassium phenoxide gives 4-hydroxybenzoic acid.","mc":["NaOH converts phenol into sodium phenoxide.","The phenoxide ring attacks CO₂ ortho to the oxygen; sodium bridges the phenoxide oxygen and CO₂, which favours ortho.","Tautomerisation restores aromaticity, giving sodium salicylate; acid gives salicylic acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Kolbe–Schmitt reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kolbe%E2%80%93Schmitt_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1279},{"m":1280},{"m":1281},{"m":1282}],"t":"Reimer–Tiemann reaction","fig":{"r":[639],"cond":["CHCl₃, NaOH, H₂O, 60 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Dichlorocarbene made from chloroform and base ortho-formylates phenols.","mc":["Hydroxide deprotonates chloroform; CCl₃⁻ loses chloride, giving dichlorocarbene.","The phenoxide ring attacks the carbene at the ortho position.","Hydroxide hydrolyses the dichloromethyl group to the aldehyde."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Reimer–Tiemann reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reimer%E2%80%93Tiemann_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Reimer–Tiemann reaction"},{"x":"Vilsmeier–Haack reaction"},{"x":"Chichibabin reaction"},{"x":"Fischer indole synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[639],"cond":["CHCl₃, NaOH, H₂O, 60 °C"],"p":1283},"ex":"Reimer–Tiemann reaction: Dichlorocarbene made from chloroform and base ortho-formylates phenols.","mc":["Hydroxide deprotonates chloroform; CCl₃⁻ loses chloride, giving dichlorocarbene.","The phenoxide ring attacks the carbene at the ortho position.","Hydroxide hydrolyses the dichloromethyl group to the aldehyde."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Reimer–Tiemann reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reimer%E2%80%93Tiemann_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1284},{"m":1285},{"m":1286},{"m":1287}],"t":"Chichibabin reaction","fig":{"r":[1288],"cond":["NaNH₂, toluene, heat","H₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"Sodium amide aminates pyridine at C2, with loss of hydride.","mc":["The amide ion adds to C2 of pyridine, giving an anionic σ-adduct.","Hydride is lost from C2 (reacting with NH₂ or the product N–H to give H₂), restoring aromaticity.","Water protonates the sodium salt, giving 2-aminopyridine."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Chichibabin reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chichibabin_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Chichibabin reaction"},{"x":"Bischler–Napieralski reaction"},{"x":"Friedel–Crafts acylation"},{"x":"Vilsmeier–Haack reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1288],"cond":["NaNH₂, toluene, heat","H₂O"],"p":1289},"ex":"Chichibabin reaction: Sodium amide aminates pyridine at C2, with loss of hydride.","mc":["The amide ion adds to C2 of pyridine, giving an anionic σ-adduct.","Hydride is lost from C2 (reacting with NH₂ or the product N–H to give H₂), restoring aromaticity.","Water protonates the sodium salt, giving 2-aminopyridine."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Chichibabin reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chichibabin_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1290},{"m":1291},{"m":1292},{"m":1293}],"t":"Azo coupling","fig":{"r":[1294,639],"cond":["NaOH (aq, pH about 9), 0 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"An aryl diazonium ion, a weak electrophile, attacks an activated arene (a phenol or an aniline), usually para, giving an azo compound. The basis of azo dyes. Phenols couple as phenoxides in mildly basic solution, and in strong acid nothing happens. If the para position is taken, coupling goes ortho.","mc":["Hydroxide turns phenol into the more nucleophilic phenoxide.","The para carbon attacks the terminal nitrogen of the diazonium ion (electrophilic aromatic substitution).","Loss of the para proton restores aromaticity, giving the (E)-azo compound."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Azo coupling","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Azo_coupling"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Azo coupling"},{"x":"Fischer indole synthesis"},{"x":"Diazotisation"},{"x":"Reimer–Tiemann reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1294,639],"cond":["NaOH (aq, pH about 9), 0 °C"],"p":1295},"ex":"Azo coupling: An aryl diazonium ion, a weak electrophile, attacks an activated arene (a phenol or an aniline), usually para, giving an azo compound. The basis of azo dyes. Phenols couple as phenoxides in mildly basic solution, and in strong acid nothing happens. If the para position is taken, coupling goes ortho.","mc":["Hydroxide turns phenol into the more nucleophilic phenoxide.","The para carbon attacks the terminal nitrogen of the diazonium ion (electrophilic aromatic substitution).","Loss of the para proton restores aromaticity, giving the (E)-azo compound."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Azo coupling","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Azo_coupling"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1296},{"m":1297},{"m":1220},{"m":1298}],"t":"Blanc chloromethylation","fig":{"r":[613],"cond":["HCHO, HCl (g), ZnCl₂, 60 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Formaldehyde and HCl with ZnCl₂ chloromethylate an arene (Ar–H → Ar–CH₂Cl), a Friedel–Crafts-type substitution. Used to make chloromethylated polystyrene (Merrifield resin). Highly carcinogenic bis(chloromethyl) ether can form from formaldehyde and HCl.","mc":["ZnCl₂ and HCl activate formaldehyde, giving a hydroxymethyl (or chloromethyl) cation equivalent.","The arene attacks it; loss of a proton restores aromaticity, giving benzyl alcohol (bound to zinc).","HCl turns the benzylic alcohol into benzyl chloride."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Blanc chloromethylation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blanc_chloromethylation#Mechanism_and_scope"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Blanc chloromethylation"},{"x":"Vilsmeier–Haack reaction"},{"x":"Skraup synthesis"},{"x":"Fischer indole synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[613],"cond":["HCHO, HCl (g), ZnCl₂, 60 °C"],"p":1299},"ex":"Blanc chloromethylation: Formaldehyde and HCl with ZnCl₂ chloromethylate an arene (Ar–H → Ar–CH₂Cl), a Friedel–Crafts-type substitution. Used to make chloromethylated polystyrene (Merrifield resin). Highly carcinogenic bis(chloromethyl) ether can form from formaldehyde and HCl.","mc":["ZnCl₂ and HCl activate formaldehyde, giving a hydroxymethyl (or chloromethyl) cation equivalent.","The arene attacks it; loss of a proton restores aromaticity, giving benzyl alcohol (bound to zinc).","HCl turns the benzylic alcohol into benzyl chloride."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Blanc chloromethylation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blanc_chloromethylation#Mechanism_and_scope"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1300},{"m":1301},{"m":1302},{"m":1303}],"t":"Vicarious nucleophilic substitution","fig":{"r":[1304,1305],"cond":["PhSO₂CH₂Cl, KOt-Bu, DMF, −40 °C","H₃O⁺"],"veil":true},"ex":"A carbanion that carries a leaving group on its own carbon replaces a hydrogen, not a halogen, of a nitroarene, ortho or para to the nitro group. Addition at C–H is faster than at C–Cl, so hydrogen is replaced even in a chloronitrobenzene; base then removes HCl from the σ-adduct, restoring aromaticity. Developed by Mąkosza.","mc":["Base removes a proton from chloromethyl phenyl sulfone, giving a carbanion that carries chlorine.","The carbanion adds ortho to the nitro group, at a C–H, giving a σ-adduct.","Base removes that ring hydrogen as chloride leaves the side chain (β-elimination), restoring aromaticity; acid work-up gives the product."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Vicarious nucleophilic substitution","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vicarious_nucleophilic_substitution"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Vicarious nucleophilic substitution"},{"x":"Blanc chloromethylation"},{"x":"Reimer–Tiemann reaction"},{"x":"Diazotisation"}],"fig":{"r":[1304,1305],"cond":["PhSO₂CH₂Cl, KOt-Bu, DMF, −40 °C","H₃O⁺"],"p":1306},"ex":"Vicarious nucleophilic substitution: A carbanion that carries a leaving group on its own carbon replaces a hydrogen, not a halogen, of a nitroarene, ortho or para to the nitro group. Addition at C–H is faster than at C–Cl, so hydrogen is replaced even in a chloronitrobenzene; base then removes HCl from the σ-adduct, restoring aromaticity. Developed by Mąkosza.","mc":["Base removes a proton from chloromethyl phenyl sulfone, giving a carbanion that carries chlorine.","The carbanion adds ortho to the nitro group, at a C–H, giving a σ-adduct.","Base removes that ring hydrogen as chloride leaves the side chain (β-elimination), restoring aromaticity; acid work-up gives the product."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Vicarious nucleophilic substitution","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vicarious_nucleophilic_substitution"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1307},{"m":1308},{"m":1309},{"m":1310}],"t":"Diazotisation","fig":{"r":[1311],"cond":["NaNO₂, HBF₄ (aq), 0 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Nitrous acid (from NaNO₂ and a strong acid) nitrosates a primary aromatic amine, giving an aryl diazonium salt. Kept at 0–5 °C; with a non-nucleophilic counterion such as BF₄⁻ the salt can be isolated. Aliphatic diazonium ions lose N₂ at once, giving carbocations.","mc":["Acid turns nitrous acid into the nitrosonium ion, NO⁺.","The amine nitrogen attacks NO⁺, giving an N-nitrosamine after loss of a proton.","Tautomerisation to the diazohydroxide, protonation and loss of water give the diazonium ion."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Diazonium compound","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diazonium_compound"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Diazotisation"},{"x":"Vicarious nucleophilic substitution"},{"x":"Reimer–Tiemann reaction"},{"x":"Kolbe–Schmitt reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1311],"cond":["NaNO₂, HBF₄ (aq), 0 °C"],"p":1312},"ex":"Diazotisation: Nitrous acid (from NaNO₂ and a strong acid) nitrosates a primary aromatic amine, giving an aryl diazonium salt. Kept at 0–5 °C; with a non-nucleophilic counterion such as BF₄⁻ the salt can be isolated. Aliphatic diazonium ions lose N₂ at once, giving carbocations.","mc":["Acid turns nitrous acid into the nitrosonium ion, NO⁺.","The amine nitrogen attacks NO⁺, giving an N-nitrosamine after loss of a proton.","Tautomerisation to the diazohydroxide, protonation and loss of water give the diazonium ion."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Diazonium compound","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diazonium_compound"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1313},{"m":1314},{"m":1315},{"m":1316}],"t":"Pschorr reaction","fig":{"r":[1317],"cond":["NaNO₂, H₂SO₄ (aq), 0 °C","Cu powder, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"An aryl diazonium salt loses N₂ with copper, and the aryl radical formed attacks a neighbouring arene intramolecularly, giving a fused tricycle. A classic route to phenanthrenes, fluorenones, dibenzofurans and carbazoles. Yields are often moderate even with excess copper; soluble one-electron donors do better.","mc":["Nitrous acid turns the amine into the aryl diazonium ion.","Copper gives it an electron; N₂ is lost, giving an aryl radical.","The radical adds to the other ring; oxidation and loss of a proton rearomatise it, giving dibenzofuran."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Pschorr reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pschorr_cyclization#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Pschorr reaction"},{"x":"Pictet–Spengler reaction"},{"x":"Hantzsch pyridine synthesis"},{"x":"Skraup synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[1317],"cond":["NaNO₂, H₂SO₄ (aq), 0 °C","Cu powder, heat"],"p":1318},"ex":"Pschorr reaction: An aryl diazonium salt loses N₂ with copper, and the aryl radical formed attacks a neighbouring arene intramolecularly, giving a fused tricycle. A classic route to phenanthrenes, fluorenones, dibenzofurans and carbazoles. Yields are often moderate even with excess copper; soluble one-electron donors do better.","mc":["Nitrous acid turns the amine into the aryl diazonium ion.","Copper gives it an electron; N₂ is lost, giving an aryl radical.","The radical adds to the other ring; oxidation and loss of a proton rearomatise it, giving dibenzofuran."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Pschorr reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pschorr_cyclization#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1319},{"m":1320},{"m":1321},{"m":1322}],"t":"Biginelli reaction","fig":{"r":[285,1248,1323],"cond":["HCl (cat.), EtOH, reflux"],"veil":true},"ex":"An aldehyde, a β-keto ester and urea condense in one pot, with acid, to a 3,4-dihydropyrimidin-2(1H)-one. The aldehyde and urea first give an N-acyliminium ion, as in a Mannich reaction; the enol of the keto ester attacks it, and ring closure onto the ketone follows. Monastrol is made this way.","mc":["Benzaldehyde and urea condense, with acid, to an N-acyliminium ion.","The enol of ethyl acetoacetate adds to the iminium ion (Mannich-type).","The free urea NH₂ attacks the ketone; loss of water gives the dihydropyrimidinone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Biginelli reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biginelli_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Biginelli reaction"},{"x":"Van Leusen imidazole synthesis"},{"x":"Ugi reaction"},{"x":"Bucherer–Bergs reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[285,1248,1323],"cond":["HCl (cat.), EtOH, reflux"],"p":1324},"ex":"Biginelli reaction: An aldehyde, a β-keto ester and urea condense in one pot, with acid, to a 3,4-dihydropyrimidin-2(1H)-one. The aldehyde and urea first give an N-acyliminium ion, as in a Mannich reaction; the enol of the keto ester attacks it, and ring closure onto the ketone follows. Monastrol is made this way.","mc":["Benzaldehyde and urea condense, with acid, to an N-acyliminium ion.","The enol of ethyl acetoacetate adds to the iminium ion (Mannich-type).","The free urea NH₂ attacks the ketone; loss of water gives the dihydropyrimidinone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Biginelli reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biginelli_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1325},{"m":1326},{"m":1327},{"m":1328}],"t":"Passerini reaction","fig":{"r":[1329,1094,1330],"cond":["CH₂Cl₂, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"A carboxylic acid, an aldehyde (or ketone) and an isocyanide combine at room temperature to an α-acyloxy amide. It is fastest in aprotic solvents at high concentration, which points to a non-ionic, hydrogen-bonded cyclic transition state. With an amine as well it becomes the Ugi reaction.","mc":["The acid hydrogen-bonds to the aldehyde; the isocyanide carbon bonds to the carbonyl carbon and to a carboxylate oxygen (α-addition), giving an imidate.","The acyl group moves from the imidate oxygen to the alkoxide oxygen (Mumm rearrangement).","This gives the α-acyloxy amide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Passerini reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Passerini_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Passerini reaction"},{"x":"Petasis reaction"},{"x":"Van Leusen imidazole synthesis"},{"x":"Biginelli reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1329,1094,1330],"cond":["CH₂Cl₂, rt"],"p":1331},"ex":"Passerini reaction: A carboxylic acid, an aldehyde (or ketone) and an isocyanide combine at room temperature to an α-acyloxy amide. It is fastest in aprotic solvents at high concentration, which points to a non-ionic, hydrogen-bonded cyclic transition state. With an amine as well it becomes the Ugi reaction.","mc":["The acid hydrogen-bonds to the aldehyde; the isocyanide carbon bonds to the carbonyl carbon and to a carboxylate oxygen (α-addition), giving an imidate.","The acyl group moves from the imidate oxygen to the alkoxide oxygen (Mumm rearrangement).","This gives the α-acyloxy amide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Passerini reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Passerini_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1332},{"m":1333},{"m":1334},{"m":1335}],"t":"Ugi reaction","fig":{"r":[1329,1336,1094,1330],"cond":["MeOH, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"An aldehyde, an amine, a carboxylic acid and an isocyanide combine in one pot to an α-acylamino amide, a peptide-like bis-amide. The final acyl shift (Mumm rearrangement) is irreversible and pulls all the earlier equilibria through. Widely used to build compound libraries.","mc":["The amine and the aldehyde condense to an imine, which the acid protonates to an iminium ion.","The isocyanide adds to the iminium carbon; the carboxylate traps the nitrilium ion, giving an imidate.","The acyl group moves from oxygen to the amine nitrogen (Mumm rearrangement), giving the bis-amide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ugi reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ugi_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Ugi reaction"},{"x":"Passerini reaction"},{"x":"Van Leusen imidazole synthesis"},{"x":"Petasis reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1329,1336,1094,1330],"cond":["MeOH, rt"],"p":1337},"ex":"Ugi reaction: An aldehyde, an amine, a carboxylic acid and an isocyanide combine in one pot to an α-acylamino amide, a peptide-like bis-amide. The final acyl shift (Mumm rearrangement) is irreversible and pulls all the earlier equilibria through. Widely used to build compound libraries.","mc":["The amine and the aldehyde condense to an imine, which the acid protonates to an iminium ion.","The isocyanide adds to the iminium carbon; the carboxylate traps the nitrilium ion, giving an imidate.","The acyl group moves from oxygen to the amine nitrogen (Mumm rearrangement), giving the bis-amide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ugi reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ugi_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1338},{"m":1339},{"m":1340},{"m":1341}],"t":"Petasis reaction","fig":{"r":[1342,1343,1344],"cond":["CH₂Cl₂, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"An amine, an aldehyde and a vinyl or aryl boronic acid give an amine in one step; with glyoxylic acid, an α-amino acid. Also called the boronic acid Mannich reaction. The vinyl group moves from boron to the iminium carbon, probably from a boronate bound to the neighbouring oxygen, and this step is irreversible, unlike the last step of a Mannich reaction.","mc":["Dibenzylamine and glyoxylic acid form an iminium ion.","The boronic acid binds the neighbouring carboxylate oxygen, giving a tetracoordinate boronate.","The vinyl group moves from boron to the iminium carbon, irreversibly, giving the α-amino acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Petasis reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petasis_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Petasis reaction"},{"x":"Biginelli reaction"},{"x":"Ugi reaction"},{"x":"Van Leusen imidazole synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[1342,1343,1344],"cond":["CH₂Cl₂, rt"],"p":1345},"ex":"Petasis reaction: An amine, an aldehyde and a vinyl or aryl boronic acid give an amine in one step; with glyoxylic acid, an α-amino acid. Also called the boronic acid Mannich reaction. The vinyl group moves from boron to the iminium carbon, probably from a boronate bound to the neighbouring oxygen, and this step is irreversible, unlike the last step of a Mannich reaction.","mc":["Dibenzylamine and glyoxylic acid form an iminium ion.","The boronic acid binds the neighbouring carboxylate oxygen, giving a tetracoordinate boronate.","The vinyl group moves from boron to the iminium carbon, irreversibly, giving the α-amino acid."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Petasis reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petasis_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1346},{"m":1347},{"m":1348},{"m":1349}],"t":"Bucherer–Bergs reaction","fig":{"r":[263],"cond":["KCN, (NH₄)₂CO₃, EtOH/H₂O, 60 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"A ketone or aldehyde, cyanide and ammonium carbonate give a hydantoin in one pot: a Strecker synthesis with an extra CO₂. Hydrolysing the hydantoin gives the α-amino acid. A cyanohydrin can replace the ketone and the cyanide.","mc":["The ketone and ammonia give an imine; cyanide adds, giving the α-amino nitrile (as in the Strecker synthesis).","The amine adds to CO₂, giving a carbamic acid, whose oxygen attacks the nitrile and closes a ring.","The cyclic imino carbamate rearranges, through an isocyanate, to the hydantoin."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Bucherer–Bergs reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bucherer%E2%80%93Bergs_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Bucherer–Bergs reaction"},{"x":"Passerini reaction"},{"x":"Petasis reaction"},{"x":"Biginelli reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[263],"cond":["KCN, (NH₄)₂CO₃, EtOH/H₂O, 60 °C"],"p":1350},"ex":"Bucherer–Bergs reaction: A ketone or aldehyde, cyanide and ammonium carbonate give a hydantoin in one pot: a Strecker synthesis with an extra CO₂. Hydrolysing the hydantoin gives the α-amino acid. A cyanohydrin can replace the ketone and the cyanide.","mc":["The ketone and ammonia give an imine; cyanide adds, giving the α-amino nitrile (as in the Strecker synthesis).","The amine adds to CO₂, giving a carbamic acid, whose oxygen attacks the nitrile and closes a ring.","The cyclic imino carbamate rearranges, through an isocyanate, to the hydantoin."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Bucherer–Bergs reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bucherer%E2%80%93Bergs_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1351},{"m":1352},{"m":1353},{"m":1354}],"t":"Van Leusen imidazole synthesis","fig":{"r":[1355,1356],"cond":["TosMIC, K₂CO₃, MeOH/DME, reflux"],"veil":true},"ex":"TosMIC adds to an aldimine in a stepwise [3+2] cycloaddition; loss of toluenesulfinic acid gives a 1,5-disubstituted imidazole. The imine can be made in situ from the aldehyde and the amine (the Van Leusen three-component reaction); without the amine, the aldehyde gives an oxazole instead.","mc":["Base deprotonates TosMIC between the sulfonyl and the isocyanide groups.","The anion adds to the imine carbon; the nitrogen anion attacks the isocyanide carbon, giving a 4-tosyl-2-imidazoline.","Elimination of toluenesulfinic acid aromatises the ring to the imidazole."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Van Leusen imidazole synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Van_Leusen_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Van Leusen imidazole synthesis"},{"x":"Bucherer–Bergs reaction"},{"x":"Biginelli reaction"},{"x":"Passerini reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1355,1356],"cond":["TosMIC, K₂CO₃, MeOH/DME, reflux"],"p":1357},"ex":"Van Leusen imidazole synthesis: TosMIC adds to an aldimine in a stepwise [3+2] cycloaddition; loss of toluenesulfinic acid gives a 1,5-disubstituted imidazole. The imine can be made in situ from the aldehyde and the amine (the Van Leusen three-component reaction); without the amine, the aldehyde gives an oxazole instead.","mc":["Base deprotonates TosMIC between the sulfonyl and the isocyanide groups.","The anion adds to the imine carbon; the nitrogen anion attacks the isocyanide carbon, giving a 4-tosyl-2-imidazoline.","Elimination of toluenesulfinic acid aromatises the ring to the imidazole."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Van Leusen imidazole synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Van_Leusen_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1358},{"m":1359},{"m":1360},{"m":1361}],"t":"Friedländer synthesis","fig":{"r":[1362,1363],"cond":["NaOH (cat.), EtOH, reflux"],"veil":true},"ex":"A 2-aminoaryl aldehyde or ketone condenses with a ketone that has an α-CH₂, giving a quinoline. Acid or base catalyses it; condensation and cyclodehydration can happen in either order.","mc":["Base forms the enolate of acetophenone, which adds to the aldehyde; loss of water gives the enone.","The aniline nitrogen attacks the ketone carbonyl intramolecularly.","Loss of water gives the aromatic quinoline."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Friedländer synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friedl%C3%A4nder_synthesis#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Friedländer synthesis"},{"x":"Gewald reaction"},{"x":"Paal–Knorr thiophene synthesis"},{"x":"Paal–Knorr furan synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[1362,1363],"cond":["NaOH (cat.), EtOH, reflux"],"p":1364},"ex":"Friedländer synthesis: A 2-aminoaryl aldehyde or ketone condenses with a ketone that has an α-CH₂, giving a quinoline. Acid or base catalyses it; condensation and cyclodehydration can happen in either order.","mc":["Base forms the enolate of acetophenone, which adds to the aldehyde; loss of water gives the enone.","The aniline nitrogen attacks the ketone carbonyl intramolecularly.","Loss of water gives the aromatic quinoline."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Friedländer synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friedl%C3%A4nder_synthesis#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1365},{"m":1366},{"m":1367},{"m":1368}],"t":"Gewald reaction","fig":{"r":[484,1369],"cond":["S₈, morpholine, EtOH, 50 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"A ketone with an α-CH₂, an α-cyano ester and elemental sulfur give a 2-aminothiophene in one pot, with an amine base. The first step is a Knoevenagel condensation; how the sulfur is then taken up is not known in detail.","mc":["Morpholine catalyses a Knoevenagel condensation of the ketone with ethyl cyanoacetate.","Sulfur is taken up at the allylic CH₂ of the condensation product (the exact steps are not known).","The thiolate attacks the nitrile; tautomerisation gives the aromatic 2-aminothiophene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Gewald reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gewald_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Gewald reaction"},{"x":"Friedländer synthesis"},{"x":"Van Leusen oxazole synthesis"},{"x":"Paal–Knorr thiophene synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[484,1369],"cond":["S₈, morpholine, EtOH, 50 °C"],"p":1370},"ex":"Gewald reaction: A ketone with an α-CH₂, an α-cyano ester and elemental sulfur give a 2-aminothiophene in one pot, with an amine base. The first step is a Knoevenagel condensation; how the sulfur is then taken up is not known in detail.","mc":["Morpholine catalyses a Knoevenagel condensation of the ketone with ethyl cyanoacetate.","Sulfur is taken up at the allylic CH₂ of the condensation product (the exact steps are not known).","The thiolate attacks the nitrile; tautomerisation gives the aromatic 2-aminothiophene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Gewald reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gewald_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1371},{"m":1372},{"m":1373},{"m":1374}],"t":"Paal–Knorr furan synthesis","fig":{"r":[1375],"cond":["TsOH (cat.), toluene, reflux"],"veil":true},"ex":"Acid cyclises a 1,4-dicarbonyl compound to a furan, with loss of water. meso- and dl-3,4-disubstituted 1,4-diones cyclise at different rates, so the ring closure, not a fast enolisation, decides the rate. Amines give pyrroles instead, and P₄S₁₀ or Lawesson's reagent thiophenes.","mc":["Acid protonates one carbonyl group.","The other carbonyl oxygen (through its enol) attacks it and closes the five-membered ring; this ring closure decides the rate.","Loss of water gives the aromatic furan."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Paal–Knorr furan synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paal%E2%80%93Knorr_synthesis#Mechanisms"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Paal–Knorr furan synthesis"},{"x":"Gewald reaction"},{"x":"Pechmann condensation"},{"x":"Friedländer synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[1375],"cond":["TsOH (cat.), toluene, reflux"],"p":1376},"ex":"Paal–Knorr furan synthesis: Acid cyclises a 1,4-dicarbonyl compound to a furan, with loss of water. meso- and dl-3,4-disubstituted 1,4-diones cyclise at different rates, so the ring closure, not a fast enolisation, decides the rate. Amines give pyrroles instead, and P₄S₁₀ or Lawesson's reagent thiophenes.","mc":["Acid protonates one carbonyl group.","The other carbonyl oxygen (through its enol) attacks it and closes the five-membered ring; this ring closure decides the rate.","Loss of water gives the aromatic furan."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Paal–Knorr furan synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paal%E2%80%93Knorr_synthesis#Mechanisms"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1377},{"m":1378},{"m":1379},{"m":1380}],"t":"Paal–Knorr thiophene synthesis","fig":{"r":[1381],"cond":["Lawesson's reagent, toluene, reflux"],"veil":true},"ex":"A 1,4-dicarbonyl compound and a sulfurising agent (P₄S₁₀ or Lawesson's reagent) give a thiophene. The reagent both sulfurises and dehydrates. The furan is not the main intermediate: 2,5-dimethylfuran gives less thiophene than the dione under the same conditions. Toxic H₂S is released.","mc":["Lawesson's reagent turns a carbonyl group into a thiocarbonyl (C=S).","The sulfur, as its enethiol, attacks the other carbonyl and closes the five-membered ring.","Loss of water, helped by the phosphorus reagent, gives the aromatic thiophene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Paal–Knorr thiophene synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paal%E2%80%93Knorr_synthesis#Mechanisms"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Paal–Knorr thiophene synthesis"},{"x":"Gewald reaction"},{"x":"Pechmann condensation"},{"x":"Friedländer synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[1381],"cond":["Lawesson's reagent, toluene, reflux"],"p":1382},"ex":"Paal–Knorr thiophene synthesis: A 1,4-dicarbonyl compound and a sulfurising agent (P₄S₁₀ or Lawesson's reagent) give a thiophene. The reagent both sulfurises and dehydrates. The furan is not the main intermediate: 2,5-dimethylfuran gives less thiophene than the dione under the same conditions. Toxic H₂S is released.","mc":["Lawesson's reagent turns a carbonyl group into a thiocarbonyl (C=S).","The sulfur, as its enethiol, attacks the other carbonyl and closes the five-membered ring.","Loss of water, helped by the phosphorus reagent, gives the aromatic thiophene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Paal–Knorr thiophene synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paal%E2%80%93Knorr_synthesis#Mechanisms"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1383},{"m":1384},{"m":1385},{"m":1386}],"t":"Pechmann condensation","fig":{"r":[1387,1388],"cond":["H₂SO₄ (conc.), 0 °C → rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"A phenol and a β-keto ester condense with acid to a coumarin: transesterification, ring closure onto the arene and dehydration. Electron-rich phenols such as resorcinol react under mild conditions. Strong Brønsted acids (H₂SO₄, MsOH) or Lewis acids (AlCl₃) catalyse it.","mc":["Acid catalyses transesterification of the keto ester with a phenol oxygen.","The protonated ketone is attacked by the ring carbon ortho to that oxygen (intramolecular electrophilic substitution); rearomatisation follows.","Acid-promoted loss of water gives the coumarin."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Pechmann condensation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pechmann_condensation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Pechmann condensation"},{"x":"Gewald reaction"},{"x":"Friedländer synthesis"},{"x":"Wenker synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[1387,1388],"cond":["H₂SO₄ (conc.), 0 °C → rt"],"p":1389},"ex":"Pechmann condensation: A phenol and a β-keto ester condense with acid to a coumarin: transesterification, ring closure onto the arene and dehydration. Electron-rich phenols such as resorcinol react under mild conditions. Strong Brønsted acids (H₂SO₄, MsOH) or Lewis acids (AlCl₃) catalyse it.","mc":["Acid catalyses transesterification of the keto ester with a phenol oxygen.","The protonated ketone is attacked by the ring carbon ortho to that oxygen (intramolecular electrophilic substitution); rearomatisation follows.","Acid-promoted loss of water gives the coumarin."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Pechmann condensation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pechmann_condensation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1352},{"m":1390},{"m":1391},{"m":1392}],"t":"Van Leusen oxazole synthesis","fig":{"r":[285,1356],"cond":["TosMIC, K₂CO₃, MeOH, reflux"],"veil":true},"ex":"TosMIC and an aldehyde give a 5-substituted oxazole: addition, ring closure to an oxazoline, then loss of toluenesulfinic acid. The final elimination needs the hydrogen that the aldehyde brings; a ketone has none, and gives a nitrile instead (Van Leusen reaction).","mc":["Base deprotonates TosMIC; the anion adds to the aldehyde.","The alkoxide attacks the isocyanide carbon, giving a 4-tosyl-2-oxazoline.","Base removes the hydrogen next to the ring oxygen and toluenesulfinate leaves, giving the oxazole."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Van Leusen oxazole synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Van_Leusen_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Van Leusen oxazole synthesis"},{"x":"Wenker synthesis"},{"x":"Gewald reaction"},{"x":"Friedländer synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[285,1356],"cond":["TosMIC, K₂CO₃, MeOH, reflux"],"p":1393},"ex":"Van Leusen oxazole synthesis: TosMIC and an aldehyde give a 5-substituted oxazole: addition, ring closure to an oxazoline, then loss of toluenesulfinic acid. The final elimination needs the hydrogen that the aldehyde brings; a ketone has none, and gives a nitrile instead (Van Leusen reaction).","mc":["Base deprotonates TosMIC; the anion adds to the aldehyde.","The alkoxide attacks the isocyanide carbon, giving a 4-tosyl-2-oxazoline.","Base removes the hydrogen next to the ring oxygen and toluenesulfinate leaves, giving the oxazole."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Van Leusen oxazole synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Van_Leusen_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1394},{"m":1395},{"m":1396},{"m":1397}],"t":"Wenker synthesis","fig":{"r":[1398],"cond":["H₂SO₄, heat","NaOH (aq), heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"A 1,2-amino alcohol is esterified with sulfuric acid; base then closes the aziridine by intramolecular substitution. The hot acid step can char sensitive substrates; milder modern versions make the sulfate ester with chlorosulfonic acid.","mc":["Sulfuric acid esterifies the hydroxyl group, giving the 2-aminoalkyl hydrogen sulfate (a zwitterion).","Hydroxide deprotonates the ammonium group.","The free amine displaces sulfate intramolecularly (SN2), closing the aziridine."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Wenker synthesis","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wenker_synthesis"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Wenker synthesis"},{"x":"Paal–Knorr furan synthesis"},{"x":"Pechmann condensation"},{"x":"Gewald reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1398],"cond":["H₂SO₄, heat","NaOH (aq), heat"],"p":1399},"ex":"Wenker synthesis: A 1,2-amino alcohol is esterified with sulfuric acid; base then closes the aziridine by intramolecular substitution. The hot acid step can char sensitive substrates; milder modern versions make the sulfate ester with chlorosulfonic acid.","mc":["Sulfuric acid esterifies the hydroxyl group, giving the 2-aminoalkyl hydrogen sulfate (a zwitterion).","Hydroxide deprotonates the ammonium group.","The free amine displaces sulfate intramolecularly (SN2), closing the aziridine."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Wenker synthesis","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wenker_synthesis"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1400},{"m":1401},{"m":1402},{"m":1403}],"t":"Brook rearrangement","fig":{"r":[1404],"cond":["NaH (cat.), THF, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"An α-silyl alkoxide moves its silyl group from carbon to oxygen; the carbanion left behind is protonated, giving a silyl ether. The strong Si–O bond drives it, and groups that stabilise the carbanion (here two phenyls) help. Lithium, which holds the alkoxide tightly, favours the reverse (retro-Brook) migration.","mc":["Base removes the O–H proton, giving the alkoxide.","The alkoxide attacks silicon, giving a three-membered pentacoordinate silicon intermediate; the C–Si bond breaks, leaving a carbanion stabilised by the two phenyls.","The carbanion takes a proton from another molecule of carbinol, giving the silyl ether and a new alkoxide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Brook rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brook_rearrangement#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Brook rearrangement"},{"x":"Meyers modification"},{"x":"Fleming–Tamao oxidation"},{"x":"Newman–Kwart rearrangement"}],"fig":{"r":[1404],"cond":["NaH (cat.), THF, rt"],"p":1405},"ex":"Brook rearrangement: An α-silyl alkoxide moves its silyl group from carbon to oxygen; the carbanion left behind is protonated, giving a silyl ether. The strong Si–O bond drives it, and groups that stabilise the carbanion (here two phenyls) help. Lithium, which holds the alkoxide tightly, favours the reverse (retro-Brook) migration.","mc":["Base removes the O–H proton, giving the alkoxide.","The alkoxide attacks silicon, giving a three-membered pentacoordinate silicon intermediate; the C–Si bond breaks, leaving a carbanion stabilised by the two phenyls.","The carbanion takes a proton from another molecule of carbinol, giving the silyl ether and a new alkoxide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Brook rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brook_rearrangement#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1406},{"m":1407},{"m":1408},{"m":1409}],"t":"Fleming–Tamao oxidation","fig":{"r":[1410],"cond":["HBF₄·OEt₂, CH₂Cl₂","mCPBA, Et₃N, Et₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"Turns a dimethylphenylsilyl group into a hydroxyl group: acid removes the phenyl to give a fluorosilane, which a peracid then oxidises, with retention of configuration at carbon. The robust PhMe₂Si group serves as a masked hydroxyl that survives many steps. The Tamao–Kumada version starts directly from fluoro-, chloro- or alkoxysilanes.","mc":["HBF₄ protonates the phenyl ring at the carbon bonded to silicon; benzene leaves and fluoride binds silicon.","The peracid adds to the fluorosilane, giving a pentacoordinate peroxysilicate.","The alkyl group migrates from silicon to oxygen with retention; hydrolysis releases the alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Fleming–Tamao oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fleming%E2%80%93Tamao_oxidation#Mechanisms"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Fleming–Tamao oxidation"},{"x":"Willgerodt–Kindler reaction"},{"x":"Corey–Winter olefin synthesis"},{"x":"Newman–Kwart rearrangement"}],"fig":{"r":[1410],"cond":["HBF₄·OEt₂, CH₂Cl₂","mCPBA, Et₃N, Et₂O"],"p":1411},"ex":"Fleming–Tamao oxidation: Turns a dimethylphenylsilyl group into a hydroxyl group: acid removes the phenyl to give a fluorosilane, which a peracid then oxidises, with retention of configuration at carbon. The robust PhMe₂Si group serves as a masked hydroxyl that survives many steps. The Tamao–Kumada version starts directly from fluoro-, chloro- or alkoxysilanes.","mc":["HBF₄ protonates the phenyl ring at the carbon bonded to silicon; benzene leaves and fluoride binds silicon.","The peracid adds to the fluorosilane, giving a pentacoordinate peroxysilicate.","The alkyl group migrates from silicon to oxygen with retention; hydrolysis releases the alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Fleming–Tamao oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fleming%E2%80%93Tamao_oxidation#Mechanisms"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1412},{"m":1413},{"m":1414},{"m":1415}],"t":"Tamao–Kumada oxidation","fig":{"r":[1416],"cond":["H₂O₂, KF, KHCO₃, MeOH/THF"],"veil":true},"ex":"Hydrogen peroxide with fluoride oxidises the C–Si bond of an alkoxy- or fluorosilane to C–OH, with retention of configuration. Often follows a hydrosilylation, turning an alkene into an alcohol. The Fleming version first converts a less reactive phenylsilane.","mc":["Fluoride adds to silicon, giving a pentacoordinate silicate that is a stronger Lewis acid.","Hydroperoxide binds silicon, giving a hexacoordinate peroxysilicate.","The cyclohexyl group migrates from silicon to oxygen with retention; hydrolysis gives cyclohexanol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Tamao–Kumada oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fleming%E2%80%93Tamao_oxidation#Mechanisms"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Tamao–Kumada oxidation"},{"x":"Brook rearrangement"},{"x":"Michaelis–Arbuzov reaction"},{"x":"Corey–Winter olefin synthesis"}],"fig":{"r":[1416],"cond":["H₂O₂, KF, KHCO₃, MeOH/THF"],"p":1417},"ex":"Tamao–Kumada oxidation: Hydrogen peroxide with fluoride oxidises the C–Si bond of an alkoxy- or fluorosilane to C–OH, with retention of configuration. Often follows a hydrosilylation, turning an alkene into an alcohol. The Fleming version first converts a less reactive phenylsilane.","mc":["Fluoride adds to silicon, giving a pentacoordinate silicate that is a stronger Lewis acid.","Hydroperoxide binds silicon, giving a hexacoordinate peroxysilicate.","The cyclohexyl group migrates from silicon to oxygen with retention; hydrolysis gives cyclohexanol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Tamao–Kumada oxidation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fleming%E2%80%93Tamao_oxidation#Mechanisms"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1418},{"m":1419},{"m":1420},{"m":1421}],"t":"Hosomi–Sakurai reaction","fig":{"r":[1422,263],"cond":["TiCl₄, CH₂Cl₂, −78 °C","H₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"A Lewis acid activates a carbonyl compound (or acetal) for attack by an allylsilane; the allyl group adds through its far end. Silicon stabilises the carbocation β to it (β-silicon effect), so the electrophile bonds to the γ-carbon and the double bond shifts. Newer versions need only a catalytic amount of Lewis acid.","mc":["TiCl₄ binds the ketone oxygen and makes the carbonyl carbon strongly electrophilic.","The terminal alkene carbon of the allylsilane attacks it, giving a carbocation β to silicon, stabilised by the C–Si bond.","Loss of the silyl group restores the alkene; work-up gives the homoallylic alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Hosomi–Sakurai reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sakurai_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Hosomi–Sakurai reaction"},{"x":"Fleming–Tamao oxidation"},{"x":"Newman–Kwart rearrangement"},{"x":"Willgerodt–Kindler reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1422,263],"cond":["TiCl₄, CH₂Cl₂, −78 °C","H₂O"],"p":1423},"ex":"Hosomi–Sakurai reaction: A Lewis acid activates a carbonyl compound (or acetal) for attack by an allylsilane; the allyl group adds through its far end. Silicon stabilises the carbocation β to it (β-silicon effect), so the electrophile bonds to the γ-carbon and the double bond shifts. Newer versions need only a catalytic amount of Lewis acid.","mc":["TiCl₄ binds the ketone oxygen and makes the carbonyl carbon strongly electrophilic.","The terminal alkene carbon of the allylsilane attacks it, giving a carbocation β to silicon, stabilised by the C–Si bond.","Loss of the silyl group restores the alkene; work-up gives the homoallylic alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Hosomi–Sakurai reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sakurai_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1424},{"m":1425},{"m":1426},{"m":1427}],"t":"Michaelis–Arbuzov reaction","fig":{"r":[1428,358],"cond":["neat, 150 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"A trialkyl phosphite and an alkyl halide give an alkylphosphonate: the phosphorus is alkylated, then the halide removes one ester alkyl group. The standard way to make phosphonates for the Horner–Wadsworth–Emmons reaction; the ethyl bromide formed distils off.","mc":["The phosphorus lone pair attacks benzyl bromide (SN2), giving a trialkoxyphosphonium bromide.","Bromide attacks an ethyl group on oxygen (SN2), breaking its O–C bond.","This forms the P=O bond, giving the phosphonate and ethyl bromide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Michaelis–Arbuzov reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michaelis%E2%80%93Arbuzov_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Michaelis–Arbuzov reaction"},{"x":"Tamao–Kumada oxidation"},{"x":"Meyers modification"},{"x":"Hosomi–Sakurai reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1428,358],"cond":["neat, 150 °C"],"p":1429},"ex":"Michaelis–Arbuzov reaction: A trialkyl phosphite and an alkyl halide give an alkylphosphonate: the phosphorus is alkylated, then the halide removes one ester alkyl group. The standard way to make phosphonates for the Horner–Wadsworth–Emmons reaction; the ethyl bromide formed distils off.","mc":["The phosphorus lone pair attacks benzyl bromide (SN2), giving a trialkoxyphosphonium bromide.","Bromide attacks an ethyl group on oxygen (SN2), breaking its O–C bond.","This forms the P=O bond, giving the phosphonate and ethyl bromide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Michaelis–Arbuzov reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michaelis%E2%80%93Arbuzov_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1430},{"m":1431},{"m":1432},{"m":1433}],"t":"Corey–Winter olefin synthesis","fig":{"r":[1434],"cond":["1,1'-thiocarbonyldiimidazole, toluene, reflux","P(OMe)₃, reflux"],"veil":true},"ex":"Converts a 1,2-diol stereospecifically into an alkene: the cyclic thiocarbonate is decomposed by a phosphite (syn elimination). A meso diol gives the Z-alkene and a dl diol the E-alkene. Thiophosgene can replace thiocarbonyldiimidazole.","mc":["The diol reacts with thiocarbonyldiimidazole, giving a cyclic thiocarbonate.","Trimethyl phosphite attacks sulfur and removes it as (MeO)₃P=S, leaving a carbene (or ylide) at the former C=S carbon.","Cycloreversion releases CO₂ and the alkene; both C–O bonds break on the same face, so the alkene geometry follows the diol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Corey–Winter olefin synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corey%E2%80%93Winter_olefin_synthesis#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Corey–Winter olefin synthesis"},{"x":"Leuckart thiophenol reaction"},{"x":"Michaelis–Arbuzov reaction"},{"x":"Fleming–Tamao oxidation"}],"fig":{"r":[1434],"cond":["1,1'-thiocarbonyldiimidazole, toluene, reflux","P(OMe)₃, reflux"],"p":1435},"ex":"Corey–Winter olefin synthesis: Converts a 1,2-diol stereospecifically into an alkene: the cyclic thiocarbonate is decomposed by a phosphite (syn elimination). A meso diol gives the Z-alkene and a dl diol the E-alkene. Thiophosgene can replace thiocarbonyldiimidazole.","mc":["The diol reacts with thiocarbonyldiimidazole, giving a cyclic thiocarbonate.","Trimethyl phosphite attacks sulfur and removes it as (MeO)₃P=S, leaving a carbene (or ylide) at the former C=S carbon.","Cycloreversion releases CO₂ and the alkene; both C–O bonds break on the same face, so the alkene geometry follows the diol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Corey–Winter olefin synthesis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corey%E2%80%93Winter_olefin_synthesis#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1436},{"m":1437},{"m":1438},{"m":1439}],"t":"Ramberg–Bäcklund reaction","fig":{"r":[1440],"cond":["KOt-Bu, THF, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"Base turns an α-halo sulfone into an alkene: a three-membered episulfone (thiirane dioxide) forms and loses SO₂. The double bond forms exactly between the two α-carbons and does not migrate under the basic conditions; a cyclic sulfone contracts by one carbon (here to cyclopentene). Open-chain sulfones often give E/Z mixtures.","mc":["Base removes a proton from the α'-carbon of the sulfone.","The carbanion displaces chloride on the other α-carbon (intramolecular SN2), giving a thiirane dioxide (episulfone).","The episulfone loses SO₂, giving the alkene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ramberg–Bäcklund reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramberg%E2%80%93B%C3%A4cklund_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Ramberg–Bäcklund reaction"},{"x":"Fleming–Tamao oxidation"},{"x":"Willgerodt–Kindler reaction"},{"x":"Meyers modification"}],"fig":{"r":[1440],"cond":["KOt-Bu, THF, rt"],"p":1441},"ex":"Ramberg–Bäcklund reaction: Base turns an α-halo sulfone into an alkene: a three-membered episulfone (thiirane dioxide) forms and loses SO₂. The double bond forms exactly between the two α-carbons and does not migrate under the basic conditions; a cyclic sulfone contracts by one carbon (here to cyclopentene). Open-chain sulfones often give E/Z mixtures.","mc":["Base removes a proton from the α'-carbon of the sulfone.","The carbanion displaces chloride on the other α-carbon (intramolecular SN2), giving a thiirane dioxide (episulfone).","The episulfone loses SO₂, giving the alkene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ramberg–Bäcklund reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramberg%E2%80%93B%C3%A4cklund_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1442},{"m":1443},{"m":1444},{"m":1445}],"t":"Meyers modification","fig":{"r":[1446],"cond":["KOH, CCl₄, t-BuOH, reflux"],"veil":true},"ex":"A Ramberg–Bäcklund reaction straight from the sulfone: CCl₄ chlorinates the α-carbon in situ, then the base forms the episulfone, which loses SO₂. Named after C. Y. Meyers (1969) and often spelled Myers; it avoids a separate α-halogenation step. Later versions use CBr₂F₂ with alumina-supported KOH.","mc":["Hydroxide deprotonates the sulfone; the carbanion takes chlorine from CCl₄, giving the α-chloro sulfone.","Deprotonation at the other α-carbon and intramolecular displacement of chloride give the episulfone.","The episulfone loses SO₂, giving stilbene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Meyers modification: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramberg%E2%80%93B%C3%A4cklund_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Meyers modification"},{"x":"Fleming–Tamao oxidation"},{"x":"Corey–Winter olefin synthesis"},{"x":"Leuckart thiophenol reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1446],"cond":["KOH, CCl₄, t-BuOH, reflux"],"p":1447},"ex":"Meyers modification: A Ramberg–Bäcklund reaction straight from the sulfone: CCl₄ chlorinates the α-carbon in situ, then the base forms the episulfone, which loses SO₂. Named after C. Y. Meyers (1969) and often spelled Myers; it avoids a separate α-halogenation step. Later versions use CBr₂F₂ with alumina-supported KOH.","mc":["Hydroxide deprotonates the sulfone; the carbanion takes chlorine from CCl₄, giving the α-chloro sulfone.","Deprotonation at the other α-carbon and intramolecular displacement of chloride give the episulfone.","The episulfone loses SO₂, giving stilbene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Meyers modification: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramberg%E2%80%93B%C3%A4cklund_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1448},{"m":1449},{"m":1450},{"m":1451}],"t":"Newman–Kwart rearrangement","fig":{"r":[1452],"cond":["270 °C (neat)"],"veil":true},"ex":"On strong heating an O-aryl thiocarbamate rearranges to the S-aryl thiocarbamate; hydrolysis then gives a thiophenol, so it turns phenols into thiophenols. Exchanging C=S for C=O drives it (about 13 kcal/mol). It is an intramolecular nucleophilic aromatic substitution, so electron-withdrawing ortho or para groups speed it up; palladium or photoredox catalysts let it run far cooler.","mc":["The thiocarbonyl sulfur attacks the aryl carbon bonded to oxygen, through a four-membered cyclic transition state.","The aryl–O bond breaks as the aryl–S bond forms (an intramolecular nucleophilic aromatic substitution).","This gives the S-aryl thiocarbamate, with a strong C=O in place of the C=S."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Newman–Kwart rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newman%E2%80%93Kwart_rearrangement#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Newman–Kwart rearrangement"},{"x":"Hosomi–Sakurai reaction"},{"x":"Corey–Winter olefin synthesis"},{"x":"Michaelis–Arbuzov reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1452],"cond":["270 °C (neat)"],"p":1453},"ex":"Newman–Kwart rearrangement: On strong heating an O-aryl thiocarbamate rearranges to the S-aryl thiocarbamate; hydrolysis then gives a thiophenol, so it turns phenols into thiophenols. Exchanging C=S for C=O drives it (about 13 kcal/mol). It is an intramolecular nucleophilic aromatic substitution, so electron-withdrawing ortho or para groups speed it up; palladium or photoredox catalysts let it run far cooler.","mc":["The thiocarbonyl sulfur attacks the aryl carbon bonded to oxygen, through a four-membered cyclic transition state.","The aryl–O bond breaks as the aryl–S bond forms (an intramolecular nucleophilic aromatic substitution).","This gives the S-aryl thiocarbamate, with a strong C=O in place of the C=S."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Newman–Kwart rearrangement: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newman%E2%80%93Kwart_rearrangement#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1454},{"m":1455},{"m":1456},{"m":1457}],"t":"Leuckart thiophenol reaction","fig":{"r":[1458],"cond":["NaNO₂, HCl, 0 °C","EtOC(=S)SK, 45 °C","KOH, EtOH, reflux; then H₃O⁺"],"veil":true},"ex":"An aryl diazonium salt and potassium ethyl xanthate give an aryl xanthate, which base hydrolyses to the thiophenol. Warming the aryl xanthate instead gives aryl thioethers. Diazonium xanthates can decompose violently, so the diazonium solution is added slowly to the warm xanthate.","mc":["Nitrous acid turns the aniline into the aryl diazonium ion.","The xanthate sulfur couples with the diazonium ion; loss of N₂ gives the S-aryl xanthate.","Hydroxide cleaves the xanthate, releasing the arenethiolate; acid gives the thiophenol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Leuckart thiophenol reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leuckart_thiophenol_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Leuckart thiophenol reaction"},{"x":"Corey–Winter olefin synthesis"},{"x":"Ramberg–Bäcklund reaction"},{"x":"Tamao–Kumada oxidation"}],"fig":{"r":[1458],"cond":["NaNO₂, HCl, 0 °C","EtOC(=S)SK, 45 °C","KOH, EtOH, reflux; then H₃O⁺"],"p":1459},"ex":"Leuckart thiophenol reaction: An aryl diazonium salt and potassium ethyl xanthate give an aryl xanthate, which base hydrolyses to the thiophenol. Warming the aryl xanthate instead gives aryl thioethers. Diazonium xanthates can decompose violently, so the diazonium solution is added slowly to the warm xanthate.","mc":["Nitrous acid turns the aniline into the aryl diazonium ion.","The xanthate sulfur couples with the diazonium ion; loss of N₂ gives the S-aryl xanthate.","Hydroxide cleaves the xanthate, releasing the arenethiolate; acid gives the thiophenol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Leuckart thiophenol reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leuckart_thiophenol_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1460},{"m":1461},{"m":1462},{"m":1463}],"t":"Willgerodt–Kindler reaction","fig":{"r":[1464],"cond":["S₈, morpholine, reflux"],"veil":true},"ex":"An aryl alkyl ketone, sulfur and a secondary amine give a thioamide at the end of the chain: the carbonyl carbon becomes a CH₂ and the terminal carbon a C(=S)NR₂. Hydrolysing the thioamide gives the arylacetic acid (here phenylacetic acid). The functional group moves along the chain through enamine and aziridinium intermediates.","mc":["Morpholine and the ketone form an enamine, which sulfur thiates.","A cascade of thio-substituted iminium and aziridinium rearrangements moves the functional group to the terminal carbon.","The end carbon becomes the thioamide carbon, and the former carbonyl carbon a CH₂."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Willgerodt rearrangement","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Willgerodt_rearrangement"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Willgerodt–Kindler reaction"},{"x":"Brook rearrangement"},{"x":"Leuckart thiophenol reaction"},{"x":"Ramberg–Bäcklund reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1464],"cond":["S₈, morpholine, reflux"],"p":1465},"ex":"Willgerodt–Kindler reaction: An aryl alkyl ketone, sulfur and a secondary amine give a thioamide at the end of the chain: the carbonyl carbon becomes a CH₂ and the terminal carbon a C(=S)NR₂. Hydrolysing the thioamide gives the arylacetic acid (here phenylacetic acid). The functional group moves along the chain through enamine and aziridinium intermediates.","mc":["Morpholine and the ketone form an enamine, which sulfur thiates.","A cascade of thio-substituted iminium and aziridinium rearrangements moves the functional group to the terminal carbon.","The end carbon becomes the thioamide carbon, and the former carbonyl carbon a CH₂."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Willgerodt rearrangement","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Willgerodt_rearrangement"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1466},{"m":1467},{"m":1468},{"m":1469}],"t":"Van Leusen reaction","fig":{"r":[263,1356],"cond":["TosMIC, KOt-Bu, DME, EtOH (2 equiv), 0 °C → rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"TosMIC turns a ketone into a nitrile with one more carbon (R₂C=O → R₂CH–CN), a 'reductive nitrilation'. A little primary alcohol (1–2 equivalents) speeds up the final deformylation; too much gives a 4-alkoxy-2-oxazoline instead. Aldehydes give oxazoles.","mc":["Deprotonated TosMIC adds to the ketone; the alkoxide attacks the isocyanide carbon, giving a 4-tosyl oxazoline anion.","The ring opens and toluenesulfinate leaves, giving an N-formylated intermediate.","Ethoxide removes the formyl group; the nitrile anion formed is protonated, giving the nitrile."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Van Leusen reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Van_Leusen_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Van Leusen reaction"},{"x":"Willgerodt–Kindler reaction"},{"x":"Corey–Winter olefin synthesis"},{"x":"Brook rearrangement"}],"fig":{"r":[263,1356],"cond":["TosMIC, KOt-Bu, DME, EtOH (2 equiv), 0 °C → rt"],"p":1470},"ex":"Van Leusen reaction: TosMIC turns a ketone into a nitrile with one more carbon (R₂C=O → R₂CH–CN), a 'reductive nitrilation'. A little primary alcohol (1–2 equivalents) speeds up the final deformylation; too much gives a 4-alkoxy-2-oxazoline instead. Aldehydes give oxazoles.","mc":["Deprotonated TosMIC adds to the ketone; the alkoxide attacks the isocyanide carbon, giving a 4-tosyl oxazoline anion.","The ring opens and toluenesulfinate leaves, giving an N-formylated intermediate.","Ethoxide removes the formyl group; the nitrile anion formed is protonated, giving the nitrile."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Van Leusen reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Van_Leusen_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1471},{"m":1472},{"m":1473},{"m":1474}],"t":"Giese reaction","fig":{"r":[1475,515],"cond":["Bu₃SnH, AIBN, benzene, 80 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"A nucleophilic alkyl radical adds to an electron-poor alkene; the new radical abstracts H from tin hydride. Polarity matching: the cyclohexyl radical is nucleophilic, so it adds fast to the acrylate's β-carbon and barely to electron-rich alkenes. Photoredox versions make the radical from a carboxylic acid or a trifluoroborate instead, without tin.","mc":["AIBN decomposes on heating; its radical abstracts H from Bu₃SnH, giving Bu₃Sn•.","Bu₃Sn• abstracts iodine from cyclohexyl iodide, giving the nucleophilic cyclohexyl radical.","The radical adds to the β-carbon of methyl acrylate (polarity-matched), giving an α-ester radical.","That electrophilic radical abstracts H from Bu₃SnH, giving the product and a new Bu₃Sn• (chain)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Giese reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free-radical_addition#Mechanism_and_regiochemistry"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Giese reaction"},{"x":"Barton decarboxylation"},{"x":"Hofmann–Löffler reaction"},{"x":"Anti-Markovnikov HBr addition"}],"fig":{"r":[1475,515],"cond":["Bu₃SnH, AIBN, benzene, 80 °C"],"p":1476},"ex":"Giese reaction: A nucleophilic alkyl radical adds to an electron-poor alkene; the new radical abstracts H from tin hydride. Polarity matching: the cyclohexyl radical is nucleophilic, so it adds fast to the acrylate's β-carbon and barely to electron-rich alkenes. Photoredox versions make the radical from a carboxylic acid or a trifluoroborate instead, without tin.","mc":["AIBN decomposes on heating; its radical abstracts H from Bu₃SnH, giving Bu₃Sn•.","Bu₃Sn• abstracts iodine from cyclohexyl iodide, giving the nucleophilic cyclohexyl radical.","The radical adds to the β-carbon of methyl acrylate (polarity-matched), giving an α-ester radical.","That electrophilic radical abstracts H from Bu₃SnH, giving the product and a new Bu₃Sn• (chain)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Giese reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free-radical_addition#Mechanism_and_regiochemistry"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1477},{"m":1478},{"m":1479},{"m":1480}],"t":"Minisci reaction","fig":{"r":[1481,1482],"cond":["t-BuCO₂H, AgNO₃ (cat.), (NH₄)₂S₂O₈, H₂SO₄, H₂O, 70 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"A nucleophilic alkyl radical adds to a protonated N-heteroarene next to nitrogen, alkylating a C–H bond. Acid protonates the quinoline, making C2 and C4 electron-poor, and the nucleophilic t-Bu radical attacks there (C4 carries the methyl). Silver and persulfate make the radical from pivalic acid by decarboxylation.","mc":["Persulfate oxidises Ag(I) to Ag(II), which oxidises pivalate; the carboxyl radical loses CO₂, giving t-Bu•.","Sulfuric acid protonates lepidine, which makes C2 and C4 electron-poor.","The nucleophilic t-Bu radical adds to C2 (C4 carries the methyl), giving a radical cation.","Oxidation and loss of a proton restore aromaticity, giving 2-tert-butyl-4-methylquinoline."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Minisci reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minisci_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Minisci reaction"},{"x":"Barton decarboxylation"},{"x":"Cristol–Firth modification"},{"x":"Wohl–Ziegler bromination"}],"fig":{"r":[1481,1482],"cond":["t-BuCO₂H, AgNO₃ (cat.), (NH₄)₂S₂O₈, H₂SO₄, H₂O, 70 °C"],"p":1483},"ex":"Minisci reaction: A nucleophilic alkyl radical adds to a protonated N-heteroarene next to nitrogen, alkylating a C–H bond. Acid protonates the quinoline, making C2 and C4 electron-poor, and the nucleophilic t-Bu radical attacks there (C4 carries the methyl). Silver and persulfate make the radical from pivalic acid by decarboxylation.","mc":["Persulfate oxidises Ag(I) to Ag(II), which oxidises pivalate; the carboxyl radical loses CO₂, giving t-Bu•.","Sulfuric acid protonates lepidine, which makes C2 and C4 electron-poor.","The nucleophilic t-Bu radical adds to C2 (C4 carries the methyl), giving a radical cation.","Oxidation and loss of a proton restore aromaticity, giving 2-tert-butyl-4-methylquinoline."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Minisci reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minisci_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1484},{"m":1485},{"m":1486},{"m":1487}],"t":"Ni/photoredox decarboxylative arylation","fig":{"r":[1488,1489],"cond":["Ir[dF(CF₃)ppy]₂(dtbbpy)PF₆, NiCl₂·glyme, dtbbpy, Cs₂CO₃, DMF, blue LEDs"],"veil":true},"ex":"An amino acid loses CO₂ under photoredox catalysis and nickel couples the radical with an aryl halide. Two catalytic cycles: the excited iridium photocatalyst oxidises the carboxylate, which loses CO₂ to give a nucleophilic α-amino radical, and nickel joins that radical to the aryl group. Trifluoroborates, silicates and C–H bonds (via HAT) serve as radical sources too.","mc":["Blue light excites the Ir(III) photocatalyst, which oxidises the caesium carboxylate by single-electron transfer.","The carboxyl radical loses CO₂, leaving a nucleophilic α-amino radical.","Meanwhile Ni(0) adds oxidatively into the aryl bromide, giving Ar–Ni(II)–Br.","The alkyl radical adds to nickel, giving Ar–Ni(III)(alkyl)Br.","Reductive elimination forms the C–C bond and leaves Ni(I)Br.","The reduced photocatalyst, Ir(II), turns Ni(I) back into Ni(0), closing both cycles (radical capture by Ni(0) before oxidative addition is also proposed)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Photoredox catalysis","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Photoredox_catalysis"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Ni/photoredox decarboxylative arylation"},{"x":"Cristol–Firth modification"},{"x":"Wohl–Ziegler bromination"},{"x":"Minisci reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1488,1489],"cond":["Ir[dF(CF₃)ppy]₂(dtbbpy)PF₆, NiCl₂·glyme, dtbbpy, Cs₂CO₃, DMF, blue LEDs"],"p":1490},"ex":"Ni/photoredox decarboxylative arylation: An amino acid loses CO₂ under photoredox catalysis and nickel couples the radical with an aryl halide. Two catalytic cycles: the excited iridium photocatalyst oxidises the carboxylate, which loses CO₂ to give a nucleophilic α-amino radical, and nickel joins that radical to the aryl group. Trifluoroborates, silicates and C–H bonds (via HAT) serve as radical sources too.","mc":["Blue light excites the Ir(III) photocatalyst, which oxidises the caesium carboxylate by single-electron transfer.","The carboxyl radical loses CO₂, leaving a nucleophilic α-amino radical.","Meanwhile Ni(0) adds oxidatively into the aryl bromide, giving Ar–Ni(II)–Br.","The alkyl radical adds to nickel, giving Ar–Ni(III)(alkyl)Br.","Reductive elimination forms the C–C bond and leaves Ni(I)Br.","The reduced photocatalyst, Ir(II), turns Ni(I) back into Ni(0), closing both cycles (radical capture by Ni(0) before oxidative addition is also proposed)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Photoredox catalysis","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Photoredox_catalysis"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1491},{"m":1492},{"m":1493},{"m":1494}],"t":"Barton decarboxylation","fig":{"r":[1495],"cond":["N-hydroxypyridine-2-thione, DCC","Bu₃SnH, AIBN, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"The acid becomes a thiohydroxamate (Barton) ester, which fragments to an alkyl radical, CO₂ and a pyridyl sulfide; the radical is reduced to R–H. Other traps give other products: BrCCl₃ gives R–Br, O₂ gives R–OH and an acrylate gives a Giese adduct. Without a hydrogen donor the radical attacks the thione itself, giving the 2-pyridyl sulfide.","mc":["DCC couples the acid with N-hydroxypyridine-2-thione, giving the thiohydroxamate (Barton) ester.","Bu₃Sn• (from AIBN) adds to the thione sulfur; the weak N–O bond breaks, giving a carboxyl radical and a pyridyl sulfide.","The carboxyl radical loses CO₂, giving the alkyl radical.","The alkyl radical abstracts H from Bu₃SnH, giving the alkane and a new Bu₃Sn•."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Barton decarboxylation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barton_decarboxylation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Barton decarboxylation"},{"x":"Mukaiyama hydration"},{"x":"Hunsdiecker reaction"},{"x":"Hofmann–Löffler reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1495],"cond":["N-hydroxypyridine-2-thione, DCC","Bu₃SnH, AIBN, heat"],"p":1496},"ex":"Barton decarboxylation: The acid becomes a thiohydroxamate (Barton) ester, which fragments to an alkyl radical, CO₂ and a pyridyl sulfide; the radical is reduced to R–H. Other traps give other products: BrCCl₃ gives R–Br, O₂ gives R–OH and an acrylate gives a Giese adduct. Without a hydrogen donor the radical attacks the thione itself, giving the 2-pyridyl sulfide.","mc":["DCC couples the acid with N-hydroxypyridine-2-thione, giving the thiohydroxamate (Barton) ester.","Bu₃Sn• (from AIBN) adds to the thione sulfur; the weak N–O bond breaks, giving a carboxyl radical and a pyridyl sulfide.","The carboxyl radical loses CO₂, giving the alkyl radical.","The alkyl radical abstracts H from Bu₃SnH, giving the alkane and a new Bu₃Sn•."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Barton decarboxylation: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barton_decarboxylation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1497},{"m":1498},{"m":1499},{"m":1500}],"t":"Hofmann–Löffler reaction","fig":{"r":[1501],"cond":["NCS","H₂SO₄, hν","NaOH"],"veil":true},"ex":"An N-chloroamine in strong acid gives an aminium radical that abstracts a δ-hydrogen; the δ-chloroamine then closes to a pyrrolidine with base. The aminium radical is strongly electrophilic and abstracts the C–H five atoms away through a six-membered transition state (1,5-HAT). The Suárez version uses amides and iodine(III) under milder conditions.","mc":["NCS chlorinates the amine to the N-chloroamine, which strong acid protonates.","Light breaks the N–Cl bond, giving an electrophilic aminium radical.","The aminium radical abstracts the δ-hydrogen through a six-membered transition state (1,5-HAT).","The carbon radical abstracts Cl from another N-chloroammonium ion, giving the δ-chloroamine and continuing the chain.","Base frees the amine, which displaces chloride intramolecularly (SN2), closing the pyrrolidine."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Hofmann–Löffler reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hofmann%E2%80%93L%C3%B6ffler_reaction#Generally_accepted_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Hofmann–Löffler reaction"},{"x":"Ni/photoredox decarboxylative arylation"},{"x":"Kharasch addition"},{"x":"Kochi reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1501],"cond":["NCS","H₂SO₄, hν","NaOH"],"p":1502},"ex":"Hofmann–Löffler reaction: An N-chloroamine in strong acid gives an aminium radical that abstracts a δ-hydrogen; the δ-chloroamine then closes to a pyrrolidine with base. The aminium radical is strongly electrophilic and abstracts the C–H five atoms away through a six-membered transition state (1,5-HAT). The Suárez version uses amides and iodine(III) under milder conditions.","mc":["NCS chlorinates the amine to the N-chloroamine, which strong acid protonates.","Light breaks the N–Cl bond, giving an electrophilic aminium radical.","The aminium radical abstracts the δ-hydrogen through a six-membered transition state (1,5-HAT).","The carbon radical abstracts Cl from another N-chloroammonium ion, giving the δ-chloroamine and continuing the chain.","Base frees the amine, which displaces chloride intramolecularly (SN2), closing the pyrrolidine."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Hofmann–Löffler reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hofmann%E2%80%93L%C3%B6ffler_reaction#Generally_accepted_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1503},{"m":1504},{"m":1505},{"m":1506}],"t":"Thiol–ene reaction","fig":{"r":[1507,1508],"cond":["DMPA (cat.), hν (365 nm)"],"veil":true},"ex":"A thiyl radical adds to the less substituted end of an alkene and the carbon radical abstracts H from the next thiol, giving the anti-Markovnikov thioether. Thiyl radicals are electrophilic and add fast to electron-rich and unactivated alkenes; the chain is so efficient that thiol–ene is used as click chemistry on polymers and proteins.","mc":["Light cleaves the photoinitiator (DMPA); its radicals abstract H from the thiol, giving the thiyl radical RS•.","RS• adds to the terminal CH₂ of the alkene, giving the more stable secondary radical.","The carbon radical abstracts H from another thiol, giving the anti-Markovnikov thioether and a new RS•."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Thiol–ene reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thiol-ene_reaction#Mechanisms"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Thiol–ene reaction"},{"x":"Kolbe electrolysis"},{"x":"Hunsdiecker reaction"},{"x":"Barton reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1507,1508],"cond":["DMPA (cat.), hν (365 nm)"],"p":1509},"ex":"Thiol–ene reaction: A thiyl radical adds to the less substituted end of an alkene and the carbon radical abstracts H from the next thiol, giving the anti-Markovnikov thioether. Thiyl radicals are electrophilic and add fast to electron-rich and unactivated alkenes; the chain is so efficient that thiol–ene is used as click chemistry on polymers and proteins.","mc":["Light cleaves the photoinitiator (DMPA); its radicals abstract H from the thiol, giving the thiyl radical RS•.","RS• adds to the terminal CH₂ of the alkene, giving the more stable secondary radical.","The carbon radical abstracts H from another thiol, giving the anti-Markovnikov thioether and a new RS•."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Thiol–ene reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thiol-ene_reaction#Mechanisms"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1510},{"m":1511},{"m":1512},{"m":1513}],"t":"Kharasch addition","fig":{"r":[1507,1514],"cond":["CCl₄ (solvent), (PhCO₂)₂ (cat.), 80 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"•CCl₃ adds to the terminal carbon of an alkene and the new radical abstracts Cl from CCl₄: Cl and CCl₃ add across the C=C (atom-transfer radical addition). Copper or ruthenium complexes catalyse the same atom transfer (ATRA) with less telomer.","mc":["Benzoyl peroxide breaks into radicals that abstract Cl from CCl₄, giving •CCl₃.","•CCl₃ adds to the terminal carbon of the alkene, giving the secondary radical.","The secondary radical abstracts Cl from CCl₄, giving the product and a new •CCl₃ (chain)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Kharasch addition","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kharasch_addition"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Kharasch addition"},{"x":"Mukaiyama hydration"},{"x":"Barton decarboxylation"},{"x":"Giese reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1507,1514],"cond":["CCl₄ (solvent), (PhCO₂)₂ (cat.), 80 °C"],"p":1515},"ex":"Kharasch addition: •CCl₃ adds to the terminal carbon of an alkene and the new radical abstracts Cl from CCl₄: Cl and CCl₃ add across the C=C (atom-transfer radical addition). Copper or ruthenium complexes catalyse the same atom transfer (ATRA) with less telomer.","mc":["Benzoyl peroxide breaks into radicals that abstract Cl from CCl₄, giving •CCl₃.","•CCl₃ adds to the terminal carbon of the alkene, giving the secondary radical.","The secondary radical abstracts Cl from CCl₄, giving the product and a new •CCl₃ (chain)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Kharasch addition","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kharasch_addition"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product under these conditions?","o":[{"m":1516},{"m":1517},{"m":1518},{"m":1519}],"t":"Anti-Markovnikov HBr addition","fig":{"r":[1520,1521],"cond":["HBr, (PhCO₂)₂ (cat.)"],"veil":true,"key":["(PhCO₂)₂"]},"ex":"With a radical initiator HBr adds to alkenes anti-Markovnikov: Br ends up on the less substituted carbon. Br• adds to the terminal CH₂ to give the more stable secondary radical, which abstracts H from HBr. Only HBr does this: with HCl and HI one of the two chain steps is uphill.","mc":["The peroxide gives benzoyloxyl radicals that abstract H from HBr, giving Br•.","Br• adds to the terminal CH₂, giving the more stable secondary radical.","The secondary radical abstracts H from HBr, giving 1-bromohexane and a new Br•."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Markovnikov's rule","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markovnikov's_rule"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product under these conditions?","o":[{"m":1522},{"m":1516},{"m":1518},{"m":1523}],"t":"Anti-Markovnikov HBr addition","fig":{"r":[1520,1521],"cond":["HBr, no peroxides, dark"],"veil":true,"key":["no peroxides"]},"ex":"With a radical initiator HBr adds to alkenes anti-Markovnikov: Br ends up on the less substituted carbon. Without peroxides, and kept from light and air, HBr adds by the ionic route: protonation gives the secondary cation and bromide adds there (Markovnikov). Old HBr bottles that have met air contain peroxides and give mixtures.","mc":["HBr protonates the terminal CH₂, giving the more stable secondary carbocation.","Bromide adds to the cation, giving 2-bromohexane."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Markovnikov's rule","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markovnikov's_rule"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Anti-Markovnikov HBr addition"},{"x":"Barton reaction"},{"x":"Hofmann–Löffler reaction"},{"x":"Mukaiyama hydration"}],"fig":{"r":[1520,1521],"cond":["HBr, (PhCO₂)₂ (cat.)"],"p":1524,"key":["(PhCO₂)₂"]},"ex":"Anti-Markovnikov HBr addition: With a radical initiator HBr adds to alkenes anti-Markovnikov: Br ends up on the less substituted carbon. Br• adds to the terminal CH₂ to give the more stable secondary radical, which abstracts H from HBr. Only HBr does this: with HCl and HI one of the two chain steps is uphill.","mc":["The peroxide gives benzoyloxyl radicals that abstract H from HBr, giving Br•.","Br• adds to the terminal CH₂, giving the more stable secondary radical.","The secondary radical abstracts H from HBr, giving 1-bromohexane and a new Br•."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Markovnikov's rule","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markovnikov's_rule"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1525},{"m":1526},{"m":1527},{"m":1528}],"t":"Mukaiyama hydration","fig":{"r":[1529],"cond":["Co(acac)₂ (cat.), PhSiH₃, O₂, i-PrOH"],"veil":true},"ex":"A cobalt hydride gives H• to the alkene, the more substituted radical traps O₂ and the peroxide is reduced: Markovnikov hydration. No mercury and wide functional-group tolerance. The same metal–hydride HAT (MHAT) step with Fe, Co or Mn drives Baran's olefin couplings and Shenvi's hydrogenations.","mc":["Phenylsilane turns the cobalt catalyst into a cobalt(III) hydride.","Co–H gives H• to the terminal carbon (metal–hydride HAT), leaving the more substituted secondary radical.","The radical traps O₂, giving a peroxyl radical that ends up as a cobalt or silyl peroxide.","The peroxide is reduced (by the silane, or on work-up) to the Markovnikov alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Mukaiyama hydration: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mukaiyama_hydration#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Mukaiyama hydration"},{"x":"Wohl–Ziegler bromination"},{"x":"Thiol–ene reaction"},{"x":"Hofmann–Löffler reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1529],"cond":["Co(acac)₂ (cat.), PhSiH₃, O₂, i-PrOH"],"p":1530},"ex":"Mukaiyama hydration: A cobalt hydride gives H• to the alkene, the more substituted radical traps O₂ and the peroxide is reduced: Markovnikov hydration. No mercury and wide functional-group tolerance. The same metal–hydride HAT (MHAT) step with Fe, Co or Mn drives Baran's olefin couplings and Shenvi's hydrogenations.","mc":["Phenylsilane turns the cobalt catalyst into a cobalt(III) hydride.","Co–H gives H• to the terminal carbon (metal–hydride HAT), leaving the more substituted secondary radical.","The radical traps O₂, giving a peroxyl radical that ends up as a cobalt or silyl peroxide.","The peroxide is reduced (by the silane, or on work-up) to the Markovnikov alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Mukaiyama hydration: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mukaiyama_hydration#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1531},{"m":1532},{"m":1533},{"m":1534}],"t":"Wohl–Ziegler bromination","fig":{"r":[1535],"cond":["NBS, AIBN (cat.), CCl₄, reflux"],"veil":true},"ex":"NBS and a radical initiator brominate allylic and benzylic C–H bonds by a radical chain. NBS keeps Br₂ very low (Goldfinger mechanism), so substitution beats addition to C=C bonds. The weakest C–H, the benzylic one, reacts; the methyl group is left alone. Trifluorotoluene can replace CCl₄.","mc":["AIBN starts the chain; traces of HBr turn NBS into a little Br₂, which gives Br•.","Br• removes the benzylic hydrogen, giving a resonance-stabilised benzylic radical and HBr.","The radical abstracts Br from Br₂, giving the benzylic bromide and a new Br•; NBS turns the HBr back into Br₂."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Wohl–Ziegler bromination: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wohl%E2%80%93Ziegler_bromination#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Wohl–Ziegler bromination"},{"x":"Hunsdiecker reaction"},{"x":"Ni/photoredox decarboxylative arylation"},{"x":"Thiol–ene reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1535],"cond":["NBS, AIBN (cat.), CCl₄, reflux"],"p":1536},"ex":"Wohl–Ziegler bromination: NBS and a radical initiator brominate allylic and benzylic C–H bonds by a radical chain. NBS keeps Br₂ very low (Goldfinger mechanism), so substitution beats addition to C=C bonds. The weakest C–H, the benzylic one, reacts; the methyl group is left alone. Trifluorotoluene can replace CCl₄.","mc":["AIBN starts the chain; traces of HBr turn NBS into a little Br₂, which gives Br•.","Br• removes the benzylic hydrogen, giving a resonance-stabilised benzylic radical and HBr.","The radical abstracts Br from Br₂, giving the benzylic bromide and a new Br•; NBS turns the HBr back into Br₂."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Wohl–Ziegler bromination: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wohl%E2%80%93Ziegler_bromination#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1537},{"m":1538},{"m":1539},{"m":1540}],"t":"Hunsdiecker reaction","fig":{"r":[1541],"cond":["Br₂, CCl₄, reflux"],"veil":true},"ex":"A silver carboxylate and bromine give an acyl hypobromite that loses CO₂ by a radical chain: an alkyl bromide with one carbon fewer. Modern versions avoid dry silver salts: Cristol–Firth (HgO, Br₂), Barton esters with BrCCl₃, or silver- and photoredox-catalysed decarboxylative halogenations.","mc":["Bromine reacts with the silver carboxylate, giving an acyl hypobromite RCO₂Br and AgBr.","The weak O–Br bond breaks homolytically, giving a carboxyl radical.","The carboxyl radical loses CO₂, giving the alkyl radical.","The alkyl radical abstracts Br from another acyl hypobromite, giving the alkyl bromide and continuing the chain."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Hunsdiecker reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hunsdiecker_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Hunsdiecker reaction"},{"x":"Giese reaction"},{"x":"Barton reaction"},{"x":"Minisci reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1541],"cond":["Br₂, CCl₄, reflux"],"p":1542},"ex":"Hunsdiecker reaction: A silver carboxylate and bromine give an acyl hypobromite that loses CO₂ by a radical chain: an alkyl bromide with one carbon fewer. Modern versions avoid dry silver salts: Cristol–Firth (HgO, Br₂), Barton esters with BrCCl₃, or silver- and photoredox-catalysed decarboxylative halogenations.","mc":["Bromine reacts with the silver carboxylate, giving an acyl hypobromite RCO₂Br and AgBr.","The weak O–Br bond breaks homolytically, giving a carboxyl radical.","The carboxyl radical loses CO₂, giving the alkyl radical.","The alkyl radical abstracts Br from another acyl hypobromite, giving the alkyl bromide and continuing the chain."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Hunsdiecker reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hunsdiecker_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1543},{"m":1544},{"m":1545},{"m":1546}],"t":"Cristol–Firth modification","fig":{"r":[1547],"cond":["HgO (red), Br₂, CCl₄, reflux"],"veil":true},"ex":"A Hunsdiecker reaction run on the free carboxylic acid: mercury(II) oxide and bromine replace CO₂H by Br, with no dry silver salt to prepare. The acyl hypobromite forms in situ, then the Hunsdiecker radical chain follows. Methyl 5-bromovalerate is made this way from monomethyl adipate.","mc":["The acid and HgO give the mercury carboxylate, which bromine turns into the acyl hypobromite RCO₂Br.","The weak O–Br bond breaks homolytically; the carboxyl radical loses CO₂, giving an alkyl radical.","The alkyl radical abstracts Br from another acyl hypobromite, giving the alkyl bromide and continuing the chain."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Cristol–Firth modification: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hunsdiecker_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Cristol–Firth modification"},{"x":"Meerwein arylation"},{"x":"Giese reaction"},{"x":"Barton reaction"}],"fig":{"r":[1547],"cond":["HgO (red), Br₂, CCl₄, reflux"],"p":1548},"ex":"Cristol–Firth modification: A Hunsdiecker reaction run on the free carboxylic acid: mercury(II) oxide and bromine replace CO₂H by Br, with no dry silver salt to prepare. The acyl hypobromite forms in situ, then the Hunsdiecker radical chain follows. Methyl 5-bromovalerate is made this way from monomethyl adipate.","mc":["The acid and HgO give the mercury carboxylate, which bromine turns into the acyl hypobromite RCO₂Br.","The weak O–Br bond breaks homolytically; the carboxyl radical loses CO₂, giving an alkyl radical.","The alkyl radical abstracts Br from another acyl hypobromite, giving the alkyl bromide and continuing the chain."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Cristol–Firth modification: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hunsdiecker_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1549},{"m":1550},{"m":1551},{"m":1552}],"t":"Kolbe electrolysis","fig":{"r":[1553,1553],"cond":["electrolysis (Pt anode), NaOMe, MeOH"],"veil":true},"ex":"Anodic oxidation of carboxylates gives carboxyl radicals that lose CO₂; two alkyl radicals join (R–R). A platinum anode and high current density favour the dimer; at lower current or on carbon the radical is oxidised on to a cation, giving alcohols and ethers instead. Two different acids give R–R′ with both homodimers.","mc":["At the anode each carboxylate loses one electron, giving a carboxyl radical.","The carboxyl radical loses CO₂, giving a pentyl radical near the electrode surface.","Two pentyl radicals combine, giving decane."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Kolbe electrolysis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kolbe_electrolysis#Mechanism_and_side-reactions"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Kolbe electrolysis"},{"x":"Meerwein arylation"},{"x":"Gomberg–Bachmann reaction"},{"x":"Anti-Markovnikov HBr addition"}],"fig":{"r":[1553,1553],"cond":["electrolysis (Pt anode), NaOMe, MeOH"],"p":1554},"ex":"Kolbe electrolysis: Anodic oxidation of carboxylates gives carboxyl radicals that lose CO₂; two alkyl radicals join (R–R). A platinum anode and high current density favour the dimer; at lower current or on carbon the radical is oxidised on to a cation, giving alcohols and ethers instead. Two different acids give R–R′ with both homodimers.","mc":["At the anode each carboxylate loses one electron, giving a carboxyl radical.","The carboxyl radical loses CO₂, giving a pentyl radical near the electrode surface.","Two pentyl radicals combine, giving decane."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Kolbe electrolysis: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kolbe_electrolysis#Mechanism_and_side-reactions"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1555},{"m":1556},{"m":1557},{"m":1558}],"t":"Barton reaction","fig":{"r":[1559],"cond":["NOCl, pyridine","hν"],"veil":true},"ex":"Light breaks an alkyl nitrite into an alkoxyl radical and NO; the alkoxyl radical abstracts a δ-hydrogen and the carbon radical takes up NO, giving an oxime. Barton used it to functionalise the unactivated C18 methyl of a steroid in a short synthesis of aldosterone acetate.","mc":["NOCl turns the alcohol into its nitrite ester, R–O–N=O.","Light breaks the weak O–NO bond, giving an alkoxyl radical and NO.","The electrophilic alkoxyl radical abstracts the δ-hydrogen through a six-membered transition state (1,5-HAT).","The carbon radical combines with NO, giving a nitroso compound.","The nitroso compound tautomerises to the oxime."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Barton reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barton_nitrite_ester_reaction#Reaction_mechanism_and_regioselectivity"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Barton reaction"},{"x":"Ni/photoredox decarboxylative arylation"},{"x":"Meerwein arylation"},{"x":"Barton decarboxylation"}],"fig":{"r":[1559],"cond":["NOCl, pyridine","hν"],"p":1560},"ex":"Barton reaction: Light breaks an alkyl nitrite into an alkoxyl radical and NO; the alkoxyl radical abstracts a δ-hydrogen and the carbon radical takes up NO, giving an oxime. Barton used it to functionalise the unactivated C18 methyl of a steroid in a short synthesis of aldosterone acetate.","mc":["NOCl turns the alcohol into its nitrite ester, R–O–N=O.","Light breaks the weak O–NO bond, giving an alkoxyl radical and NO.","The electrophilic alkoxyl radical abstracts the δ-hydrogen through a six-membered transition state (1,5-HAT).","The carbon radical combines with NO, giving a nitroso compound.","The nitroso compound tautomerises to the oxime."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Barton reaction: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barton_nitrite_ester_reaction#Reaction_mechanism_and_regioselectivity"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1561},{"m":1562},{"m":1563},{"m":1564}],"t":"Meerwein arylation","fig":{"r":[1565,1566],"cond":["ArN₂⁺ Cl⁻, CuCl₂ (cat.), acetone, H₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"Copper turns an aryl diazonium salt into an aryl radical that adds to an electron-poor alkene; the new radical picks up chlorine from copper(II). The high-energy aryl radical adds to the CH₂ end; chlorine transfer from CuCl₂ ends each cycle. Losing HCl afterwards gives the cinnamonitrile.","mc":["Copper(I), formed from the CuCl₂ catalyst, gives an electron to the diazonium ion, which loses N₂: an aryl radical.","The aryl radical adds to the CH₂ end of acrylonitrile, giving an α-cyano radical.","That radical abstracts Cl from CuCl₂, giving the product and regenerating copper(I)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Meerwein arylation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meerwein_arylation"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Meerwein arylation"},{"x":"Mukaiyama hydration"},{"x":"Anti-Markovnikov HBr addition"},{"x":"Kharasch addition"}],"fig":{"r":[1565,1566],"cond":["ArN₂⁺ Cl⁻, CuCl₂ (cat.), acetone, H₂O"],"p":1567},"ex":"Meerwein arylation: Copper turns an aryl diazonium salt into an aryl radical that adds to an electron-poor alkene; the new radical picks up chlorine from copper(II). The high-energy aryl radical adds to the CH₂ end; chlorine transfer from CuCl₂ ends each cycle. Losing HCl afterwards gives the cinnamonitrile.","mc":["Copper(I), formed from the CuCl₂ catalyst, gives an electron to the diazonium ion, which loses N₂: an aryl radical.","The aryl radical adds to the CH₂ end of acrylonitrile, giving an α-cyano radical.","That radical abstracts Cl from CuCl₂, giving the product and regenerating copper(I)."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Meerwein arylation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meerwein_arylation"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1568},{"m":1569},{"m":1570},{"m":1571}],"t":"Dowd–Beckwith ring expansion","fig":{"r":[1572],"cond":["Bu₃SnH, AIBN, benzene, 80 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"A primary radical next to a cyclic β-keto ester adds to the ketone and the alkoxyl radical reopens the other way, enlarging the ring by one carbon. The ester stabilises the radical left behind when the cyclopropyloxy radical opens, which drives the expansion; with longer halo chains the ring grows by three or four carbons.","mc":["Bu₃Sn• abstracts bromine, giving the primary radical.","The radical adds to the ketone carbonyl (3-exo), giving a cyclopropyloxy radical.","The three-membered ring opens at the old ring bond instead, leaving a radical next to the ester in a six-membered ring.","That radical abstracts H from Bu₃SnH, giving the ring-expanded keto ester."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Dowd–Beckwith ring expansion: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dowd%E2%80%93Beckwith_ring-expansion_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Dowd–Beckwith ring expansion"},{"x":"Wohl–Ziegler bromination"},{"x":"Gomberg–Bachmann reaction"},{"x":"Kolbe electrolysis"}],"fig":{"r":[1572],"cond":["Bu₃SnH, AIBN, benzene, 80 °C"],"p":1573},"ex":"Dowd–Beckwith ring expansion: A primary radical next to a cyclic β-keto ester adds to the ketone and the alkoxyl radical reopens the other way, enlarging the ring by one carbon. The ester stabilises the radical left behind when the cyclopropyloxy radical opens, which drives the expansion; with longer halo chains the ring grows by three or four carbons.","mc":["Bu₃Sn• abstracts bromine, giving the primary radical.","The radical adds to the ketone carbonyl (3-exo), giving a cyclopropyloxy radical.","The three-membered ring opens at the old ring bond instead, leaving a radical next to the ester in a six-membered ring.","That radical abstracts H from Bu₃SnH, giving the ring-expanded keto ester."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Dowd–Beckwith ring expansion: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dowd%E2%80%93Beckwith_ring-expansion_reaction#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1574},{"m":1575},{"m":1576},{"m":1577}],"t":"Gomberg–Bachmann reaction","fig":{"r":[1578,613],"cond":["ArN₂⁺ Cl⁻, NaOH (aq), benzene"],"veil":true},"ex":"Under base an aryl diazonium salt gives aryl radicals that attack another arene, forming a biaryl. Yields are often below 40%, as the diazonium decomposes in many ways; the aryl radical barely discriminates between the positions of a substituted arene, so Suzuki coupling usually replaced it.","mc":["Base turns the diazonium ion into a diazohydroxide, which breaks down to the aryl radical and N₂.","The aryl radical adds to benzene, giving a cyclohexadienyl radical.","Loss of the hydrogen atom (to another radical) restores aromaticity, giving the biaryl."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Gomberg–Bachmann reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gomberg%E2%80%93Bachmann_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Gomberg–Bachmann reaction"},{"x":"Thiol–ene reaction"},{"x":"Barton reaction"},{"x":"Wohl–Ziegler bromination"}],"fig":{"r":[1578,613],"cond":["ArN₂⁺ Cl⁻, NaOH (aq), benzene"],"p":1579},"ex":"Gomberg–Bachmann reaction: Under base an aryl diazonium salt gives aryl radicals that attack another arene, forming a biaryl. Yields are often below 40%, as the diazonium decomposes in many ways; the aryl radical barely discriminates between the positions of a substituted arene, so Suzuki coupling usually replaced it.","mc":["Base turns the diazonium ion into a diazohydroxide, which breaks down to the aryl radical and N₂.","The aryl radical adds to benzene, giving a cyclohexadienyl radical.","Loss of the hydrogen atom (to another radical) restores aromaticity, giving the biaryl."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Gomberg–Bachmann reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gomberg%E2%80%93Bachmann_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1580},{"m":1581},{"m":1582},{"m":1583}],"t":"Kochi reaction","fig":{"r":[1584],"cond":["Pb(OAc)₄, LiCl, benzene, 80 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Lead(IV) acetate and lithium chloride turn a carboxylic acid into the alkyl chloride with one carbon fewer (decarboxylative chlorination). A radical alternative to the Hunsdiecker reaction that works best for secondary and tertiary acids and needs no silver salt.","mc":["The acid exchanges onto lead(IV), giving a lead carboxylate that also carries chloride.","The Pb–O bond breaks homolytically; the carboxyl radical loses CO₂, giving the cyclohexyl radical.","The radical abstracts a chlorine atom from a chlorolead species, giving chlorocyclohexane and continuing the chain."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Kochi reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kochi_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Named reaction","a":"Which named reaction is this?","o":[{"x":"Kochi reaction"},{"x":"Hunsdiecker reaction"},{"x":"Anti-Markovnikov HBr addition"},{"x":"Kharasch addition"}],"fig":{"r":[1584],"cond":["Pb(OAc)₄, LiCl, benzene, 80 °C"],"p":1585},"ex":"Kochi reaction: Lead(IV) acetate and lithium chloride turn a carboxylic acid into the alkyl chloride with one carbon fewer (decarboxylative chlorination). A radical alternative to the Hunsdiecker reaction that works best for secondary and tertiary acids and needs no silver salt.","mc":["The acid exchanges onto lead(IV), giving a lead carboxylate that also carries chloride.","The Pb–O bond breaks homolytically; the carboxyl radical loses CO₂, giving the cyclohexyl radical.","The radical abstracts a chlorine atom from a chlorolead species, giving chlorocyclohexane and continuing the chain."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Kochi reaction","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kochi_reaction"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1586},{"m":1587},{"m":1588},{"m":1589}],"t":"IBX","fig":{"r":[1590],"cond":["IBX, DMSO, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"Hypervalent iodine oxidant that turns alcohols into aldehydes and ketones. Insoluble in most solvents except DMSO; reacts in hot EtOAc as a suspension.","mc":["The alcohol displaces the OH on iodine(V), binding as an alkoxide.","A 'hypervalent twist' rearranges the iodine ligands so the alkoxide and an oxo group line up.","The oxo group removes the α-hydrogen as the I–O bond breaks, giving the aldehyde and an iodine(III) by-product."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["IBX: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2-Iodoxybenzoic_acid#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"IBX"},{"x":"DIBAL-H"},{"x":"Sodium cyanoborohydride"},{"x":"Collins reagent"}],"fig":{"r":[1590],"cond":["?, DMSO, rt"],"p":1591},"ex":"IBX: Hypervalent iodine oxidant that turns alcohols into aldehydes and ketones. Insoluble in most solvents except DMSO; reacts in hot EtOAc as a suspension. Also works here: Dess–Martin periodinane, PCC.","mc":["The alcohol displaces the OH on iodine(V), binding as an alkoxide.","A 'hypervalent twist' rearranges the iodine ligands so the alkoxide and an oxo group line up.","The oxo group removes the α-hydrogen as the I–O bond breaks, giving the aldehyde and an iodine(III) by-product."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["IBX: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2-Iodoxybenzoic_acid#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1592},{"m":1593},{"m":1594},{"m":1595}],"t":"Dess–Martin periodinane","fig":{"r":[1596],"cond":["DMP, CH₂Cl₂, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"Mild, neutral oxidation of alcohols to aldehydes and ketones at room temperature. Made from IBX and acetic anhydride, which makes it soluble in CH₂Cl₂.","mc":["The alcohol replaces one acetate on iodine(V), releasing acetic acid.","An acetate removes the α-hydrogen as the I–O bond breaks.","This gives the aldehyde, acetic acid and an iodine(III) by-product."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Dess–Martin periodinane: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dess%E2%80%93Martin_periodinane#Oxidation_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Dess–Martin periodinane"},{"x":"Sodium borohydride"},{"x":"Samarium(II) iodide"},{"x":"Sarett reagent"}],"fig":{"r":[1596],"cond":["?, CH₂Cl₂, rt"],"p":1597},"ex":"Dess–Martin periodinane: Mild, neutral oxidation of alcohols to aldehydes and ketones at room temperature. Made from IBX and acetic anhydride, which makes it soluble in CH₂Cl₂. Also works here: PCC, Manganese dioxide, Collins reagent.","mc":["The alcohol replaces one acetate on iodine(V), releasing acetic acid.","An acetate removes the α-hydrogen as the I–O bond breaks.","This gives the aldehyde, acetic acid and an iodine(III) by-product."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Dess–Martin periodinane: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dess%E2%80%93Martin_periodinane#Oxidation_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1598},{"m":1599},{"m":1600},{"m":1601}],"t":"PCC","fig":{"r":[1602],"cond":["PCC, CH₂Cl₂"],"veil":true},"ex":"Cr(VI) oxidant that stops at the aldehyde under anhydrous conditions. Without water no aldehyde hydrate forms, so there is no over-oxidation to the acid.","mc":["The alcohol attacks chromium, forming a chromate ester.","A base removes the α-hydrogen as Cr(VI) is reduced to Cr(IV), giving the aldehyde.","Without water no hydrate forms, so the aldehyde is not oxidised further."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Pyridinium chlorochromate","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pyridinium_chlorochromate"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"PCC"},{"x":"mCPBA"},{"x":"Sodium triacetoxyborohydride"},{"x":"DIBAL-H"}],"fig":{"r":[1602],"cond":["?, CH₂Cl₂"],"p":1603},"ex":"PCC: Cr(VI) oxidant that stops at the aldehyde under anhydrous conditions. Without water no aldehyde hydrate forms, so there is no over-oxidation to the acid. Also works here: Dess–Martin periodinane, Collins reagent.","mc":["The alcohol attacks chromium, forming a chromate ester.","A base removes the α-hydrogen as Cr(VI) is reduced to Cr(IV), giving the aldehyde.","Without water no hydrate forms, so the aldehyde is not oxidised further."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Pyridinium chlorochromate","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pyridinium_chlorochromate"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1604},{"m":1605},{"m":1606},{"m":1607}],"t":"Manganese dioxide","fig":{"r":[1608],"cond":["MnO₂ (excess), CH₂Cl₂"],"veil":true},"ex":"Selectively oxidises allylic and benzylic alcohols; ordinary alcohols react slowly. A heterogeneous reagent used in large excess.","mc":["The allylic alcohol binds to the MnO₂ surface as an alkoxide.","A hydrogen atom is removed from the allylic carbon, which is easy because the allylic radical is stabilised.","A second electron transfer gives the enal and reduced manganese."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Manganese dioxide","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manganese_dioxide"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Manganese dioxide"},{"x":"DMDO"},{"x":"DIBAL-H"},{"x":"Lithium aluminium hydride"}],"fig":{"r":[1608],"cond":["? (excess), CH₂Cl₂"],"p":1609},"ex":"Manganese dioxide: Selectively oxidises allylic and benzylic alcohols; ordinary alcohols react slowly. A heterogeneous reagent used in large excess. Also works here: Dess–Martin periodinane, PCC, Collins reagent.","mc":["The allylic alcohol binds to the MnO₂ surface as an alkoxide.","A hydrogen atom is removed from the allylic carbon, which is easy because the allylic radical is stabilised.","A second electron transfer gives the enal and reduced manganese."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Manganese dioxide","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manganese_dioxide"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1610},{"m":1611},{"m":1612},{"m":379}],"t":"Osmium tetroxide","fig":{"r":[785],"cond":["OsO₄ (cat.), NMO, acetone/H₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"syn-Dihydroxylates alkenes; used catalytically with NMO or K₃Fe(CN)₆ as the reoxidant. Volatile and very toxic.","mc":["OsO₄ adds to the C=C in a concerted [3+2] cycloaddition, giving a cyclic osmate ester.","Hydrolysis releases the syn-diol and Os(VI).","NMO reoxidises Os(VI) to OsO₄."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Osmium tetroxide","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Osmium_tetroxide"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Osmium tetroxide"},{"x":"DMDO"},{"x":"Tributyltin hydride"},{"x":"Manganese dioxide"}],"fig":{"r":[785],"cond":["? (cat.), NMO, acetone/H₂O"],"p":1613},"ex":"Osmium tetroxide: syn-Dihydroxylates alkenes; used catalytically with NMO or K₃Fe(CN)₆ as the reoxidant. Volatile and very toxic.","mc":["OsO₄ adds to the C=C in a concerted [3+2] cycloaddition, giving a cyclic osmate ester.","Hydrolysis releases the syn-diol and Os(VI).","NMO reoxidises Os(VI) to OsO₄."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Osmium tetroxide","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Osmium_tetroxide"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1614},{"m":746},{"m":1615},{"m":1616}],"t":"mCPBA","fig":{"r":[749],"cond":["mCPBA, CH₂Cl₂"],"veil":true},"ex":"Peracid that epoxidises alkenes and carries out Baeyer–Villiger oxidations. The epoxidation is concerted (the 'butterfly' transition state), so it is stereospecific.","mc":["The alkene's π electrons attack the outer oxygen of the peracid.","In a concerted 'butterfly' transition state, the O–O bond breaks and the peracid's proton moves to its own carbonyl oxygen.","The epoxide forms with the alkene geometry kept, and m-chlorobenzoic acid is released."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Meta-Chloroperoxybenzoic acid","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meta-Chloroperoxybenzoic_acid"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"mCPBA"},{"x":"9-BBN"},{"x":"L-Selectride"},{"x":"Sodium cyanoborohydride"}],"fig":{"r":[749],"cond":["?, CH₂Cl₂"],"p":1617},"ex":"mCPBA: Peracid that epoxidises alkenes and carries out Baeyer–Villiger oxidations. The epoxidation is concerted (the 'butterfly' transition state), so it is stereospecific. Also works here: DMDO.","mc":["The alkene's π electrons attack the outer oxygen of the peracid.","In a concerted 'butterfly' transition state, the O–O bond breaks and the peracid's proton moves to its own carbonyl oxygen.","The epoxide forms with the alkene geometry kept, and m-chlorobenzoic acid is released."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Meta-Chloroperoxybenzoic acid","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meta-Chloroperoxybenzoic_acid"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1618},{"m":1619},{"m":1620},{"m":1621}],"t":"DMDO","fig":{"r":[1622],"cond":["DMDO, acetone, 0 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Neutral, metal-free epoxidising agent; the only by-product is acetone. Made as a dilute solution in acetone from Oxone.","mc":["The alkene attacks one oxygen of the strained dioxirane ring.","The O–O bond breaks in a concerted spiro transition state, transferring one oxygen.","The epoxide and acetone form."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Dimethyldioxirane","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dimethyldioxirane"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"DMDO"},{"x":"Tris(trimethylsilyl)silane"},{"x":"Sodium cyanoborohydride"},{"x":"Davis oxaziridine"}],"fig":{"r":[1622],"cond":["?, acetone, 0 °C"],"p":1623},"ex":"DMDO: Neutral, metal-free epoxidising agent; the only by-product is acetone. Made as a dilute solution in acetone from Oxone. Also works here: mCPBA.","mc":["The alkene attacks one oxygen of the strained dioxirane ring.","The O–O bond breaks in a concerted spiro transition state, transferring one oxygen.","The epoxide and acetone form."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Dimethyldioxirane","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dimethyldioxirane"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1624},{"m":1625},{"m":1626},{"m":1627}],"t":"Davis oxaziridine","fig":{"r":[1628],"cond":["NaHMDS, THF, −78 °C","Davis oxaziridine"],"veil":true},"ex":"Transfers an oxygen atom to enolates, giving α-hydroxy carbonyl compounds. Chiral versions give enantioenriched α-hydroxy ketones.","mc":["NaHMDS forms the ketone enolate.","The enolate carbon attacks the oxaziridine oxygen, breaking the weak N–O bond.","The intermediate fragments into the sulfonimine and the α-alkoxide, which work-up protonates to the α-hydroxy ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Davis oxaziridine: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Davis_oxidation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Davis oxaziridine"},{"x":"Collins reagent"},{"x":"Koser's reagent"},{"x":"Sodium cyanoborohydride"}],"fig":{"r":[1628],"cond":["NaHMDS, THF, −78 °C","?"],"p":1629},"ex":"Davis oxaziridine: Transfers an oxygen atom to enolates, giving α-hydroxy carbonyl compounds. Chiral versions give enantioenriched α-hydroxy ketones.","mc":["NaHMDS forms the ketone enolate.","The enolate carbon attacks the oxaziridine oxygen, breaking the weak N–O bond.","The intermediate fragments into the sulfonimine and the α-alkoxide, which work-up protonates to the α-hydroxy ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Davis oxaziridine: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Davis_oxidation#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1630},{"m":1631},{"m":1632},{"m":1633}],"t":"TEMPO","fig":{"r":[1634],"cond":["TEMPO (cat.), NaOCl, KBr, CH₂Cl₂/H₂O, 0 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Stable nitroxyl radical; with bleach it catalyses the oxidation of primary alcohols to aldehydes. The real oxidant is the oxoammonium ion, regenerated by the bleach (Anelli oxidation).","mc":["Hypochlorite oxidises TEMPO to the oxoammonium ion (N⁺=O).","The alcohol adds to the oxoammonium ion and loses its α-hydrogen, giving the aldehyde and the hydroxylamine.","Bleach reoxidises the hydroxylamine to the oxoammonium ion."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["TEMPO","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TEMPO"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"TEMPO"},{"x":"Sodium cyanoborohydride"},{"x":"Sarett reagent"},{"x":"mCPBA"}],"fig":{"r":[1634],"cond":["? (cat.), NaOCl, KBr, CH₂Cl₂/H₂O, 0 °C"],"p":1635},"ex":"TEMPO: Stable nitroxyl radical; with bleach it catalyses the oxidation of primary alcohols to aldehydes. The real oxidant is the oxoammonium ion, regenerated by the bleach (Anelli oxidation).","mc":["Hypochlorite oxidises TEMPO to the oxoammonium ion (N⁺=O).","The alcohol adds to the oxoammonium ion and loses its α-hydrogen, giving the aldehyde and the hydroxylamine.","Bleach reoxidises the hydroxylamine to the oxoammonium ion."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["TEMPO","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TEMPO"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1636},{"m":1637},{"m":1638},{"m":1639}],"t":"DDQ","fig":{"r":[1640],"cond":["DDQ, CH₂Cl₂/H₂O"],"veil":true},"ex":"Strong quinone oxidant: dehydrogenates rings to arenes and removes PMB ethers. Removes PMB groups but leaves plain benzyl ethers in place.","mc":["DDQ forms a charge-transfer complex with the electron-rich PMB ring.","Hydride is removed from the benzylic CH₂, giving an oxocarbenium ion stabilised by the p-methoxy group.","Water adds; the hemiacetal falls apart to the free alcohol and p-anisaldehyde."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["2,3-Dichloro-5,6-dicyano-1,4-benzoquinone","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2%2C3-Dichloro-5%2C6-dicyano-1%2C4-benzoquinone"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"DDQ"},{"x":"DIBAL-H"},{"x":"Sodium cyanoborohydride"},{"x":"DMDO"}],"fig":{"r":[1640],"cond":["?, CH₂Cl₂/H₂O"],"p":1641},"ex":"DDQ: Strong quinone oxidant: dehydrogenates rings to arenes and removes PMB ethers. Removes PMB groups but leaves plain benzyl ethers in place.","mc":["DDQ forms a charge-transfer complex with the electron-rich PMB ring.","Hydride is removed from the benzylic CH₂, giving an oxocarbenium ion stabilised by the p-methoxy group.","Water adds; the hemiacetal falls apart to the free alcohol and p-anisaldehyde."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["2,3-Dichloro-5,6-dicyano-1,4-benzoquinone","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2%2C3-Dichloro-5%2C6-dicyano-1%2C4-benzoquinone"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1642},{"m":1643},{"m":1644},{"m":1645}],"t":"Sodium borohydride","fig":{"r":[1646],"cond":["NaBH₄, MeOH, 0 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Mild hydride donor: reduces aldehydes and ketones but leaves esters, amides and acids alone. Safe to use in alcohols and even water.","mc":["Borohydride delivers a hydride to the ketone carbonyl carbon; methanol hydrogen-bonds to the oxygen and activates it.","Methanol protonates the alkoxide.","The ester carbonyl is too weakly electrophilic to react with NaBH₄ at 0 °C."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Sodium borohydride","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sodium_borohydride"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Sodium borohydride"},{"x":"Sarett reagent"},{"x":"DMDO"},{"x":"IBX"}],"fig":{"r":[1646],"cond":["?, MeOH, 0 °C"],"p":1647},"ex":"Sodium borohydride: Mild hydride donor: reduces aldehydes and ketones but leaves esters, amides and acids alone. Safe to use in alcohols and even water.","mc":["Borohydride delivers a hydride to the ketone carbonyl carbon; methanol hydrogen-bonds to the oxygen and activates it.","Methanol protonates the alkoxide.","The ester carbonyl is too weakly electrophilic to react with NaBH₄ at 0 °C."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Sodium borohydride","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sodium_borohydride"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1648},{"m":1649},{"m":1650},{"m":1651}],"t":"Lithium aluminium hydride","fig":{"r":[1652],"cond":["LiAlH₄, THF","H₂O work-up"],"veil":true},"ex":"Powerful hydride donor: also reduces esters, acids, amides and nitriles. Reacts violently with water; amides are reduced all the way to amines. DIBAL-H also takes esters to alcohols when it is not kept at −78 °C.","mc":["Hydride adds to the ester carbonyl, giving a tetrahedral intermediate.","The intermediate collapses, expelling methoxide, to the aldehyde.","The aldehyde, more reactive than the ester, takes a second hydride; aqueous work-up gives the primary alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Lithium aluminium hydride","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lithium_aluminium_hydride"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Lithium aluminium hydride"},{"x":"Koser's reagent"},{"x":"Samarium(II) iodide"},{"x":"9-BBN"}],"fig":{"r":[1652],"cond":["?, THF","H₂O work-up"],"p":1653},"ex":"Lithium aluminium hydride: Powerful hydride donor: also reduces esters, acids, amides and nitriles. Reacts violently with water; amides are reduced all the way to amines. DIBAL-H also takes esters to alcohols when it is not kept at −78 °C.","mc":["Hydride adds to the ester carbonyl, giving a tetrahedral intermediate.","The intermediate collapses, expelling methoxide, to the aldehyde.","The aldehyde, more reactive than the ester, takes a second hydride; aqueous work-up gives the primary alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Lithium aluminium hydride","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lithium_aluminium_hydride"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1654},{"m":1655},{"m":1656},{"m":1657}],"t":"DIBAL-H","fig":{"r":[1658],"cond":["DIBAL-H, CH₂Cl₂, −78 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Bulky aluminium hydride that reduces esters and nitriles to aldehydes at −78 °C. The tetrahedral intermediate survives at −78 °C and only collapses to the aldehyde on work-up.","mc":["The Lewis-acidic aluminium binds the ester carbonyl oxygen.","Hydride transfers intramolecularly to the carbonyl carbon, giving a tetrahedral aluminium hemiacetal.","At −78 °C this intermediate survives and only collapses to the aldehyde on aqueous work-up, so no second reduction occurs."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Diisobutylaluminium hydride","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diisobutylaluminium_hydride"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"DIBAL-H"},{"x":"Osmium tetroxide"},{"x":"Collins reagent"},{"x":"IBX"}],"fig":{"r":[1658],"cond":["?, CH₂Cl₂, −78 °C"],"p":1659},"ex":"DIBAL-H: Bulky aluminium hydride that reduces esters and nitriles to aldehydes at −78 °C. The tetrahedral intermediate survives at −78 °C and only collapses to the aldehyde on work-up.","mc":["The Lewis-acidic aluminium binds the ester carbonyl oxygen.","Hydride transfers intramolecularly to the carbonyl carbon, giving a tetrahedral aluminium hemiacetal.","At −78 °C this intermediate survives and only collapses to the aldehyde on aqueous work-up, so no second reduction occurs."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Diisobutylaluminium hydride","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diisobutylaluminium_hydride"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1660},{"m":1661},{"m":1662},{"m":487}],"t":"L-Selectride","fig":{"r":[1663],"cond":["L-Selectride, THF, −78 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Very bulky borohydride: attacks hindered ketones from the less hindered face, giving axial alcohols. 4-tert-Butylcyclohexanone gives the cis alcohol; NaBH₄ gives mostly trans.","mc":["The bulky tri-sec-butylborohydride cannot approach the ring from the axial direction.","It delivers hydride from the equatorial direction, pushing the oxygen into the axial position.","Work-up gives the axial (cis) alcohol; small hydrides such as NaBH₄ attack axially and give the equatorial (trans) alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["L-selectride","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L-selectride"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"L-Selectride"},{"x":"Tributyltin hydride"},{"x":"PCC"},{"x":"Lithium aluminium hydride"}],"fig":{"r":[1663],"cond":["?, THF, −78 °C"],"p":1664},"ex":"L-Selectride: Very bulky borohydride: attacks hindered ketones from the less hindered face, giving axial alcohols. 4-tert-Butylcyclohexanone gives the cis alcohol; NaBH₄ gives mostly trans.","mc":["The bulky tri-sec-butylborohydride cannot approach the ring from the axial direction.","It delivers hydride from the equatorial direction, pushing the oxygen into the axial position.","Work-up gives the axial (cis) alcohol; small hydrides such as NaBH₄ attack axially and give the equatorial (trans) alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["L-selectride","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/L-selectride"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1665},{"m":276},{"m":1666},{"m":1667}],"t":"Sodium cyanoborohydride","fig":{"r":[278,272],"cond":["NaBH₃CN, MeOH, pH 6"],"veil":true},"ex":"Reduces iminium ions much faster than ketones, so it is used for reductive amination at pH ~6. Toxic: acid releases HCN. Unlike NaBH(OAc)₃, which decomposes in methanol, it can be used in MeOH.","mc":["The aldehyde and dimethylamine form an iminium ion at pH ~6.","The electron-poor cyanoborohydride reduces the iminium ion but reacts only slowly with the aldehyde at this pH.","Hydride transfer gives the tertiary amine."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Sodium cyanoborohydride","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sodium_cyanoborohydride"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Sodium cyanoborohydride"},{"x":"Samarium(II) iodide"},{"x":"DIBAL-H"},{"x":"Tributyltin hydride"}],"fig":{"r":[278,272],"cond":["?, MeOH, pH 6"],"p":1668},"ex":"Sodium cyanoborohydride: Reduces iminium ions much faster than ketones, so it is used for reductive amination at pH ~6. Toxic: acid releases HCN. Unlike NaBH(OAc)₃, which decomposes in methanol, it can be used in MeOH.","mc":["The aldehyde and dimethylamine form an iminium ion at pH ~6.","The electron-poor cyanoborohydride reduces the iminium ion but reacts only slowly with the aldehyde at this pH.","Hydride transfer gives the tertiary amine."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Sodium cyanoborohydride","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sodium_cyanoborohydride"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1669},{"m":468},{"m":1670},{"m":1671}],"t":"Sodium triacetoxyborohydride","fig":{"r":[263,628],"cond":["NaBH(OAc)₃, AcOH, DCE"],"veil":true},"ex":"Mild, non-toxic hydride for one-pot reductive amination of aldehydes and ketones. The acetoxy groups weaken it enough to leave the carbonyl alone until the iminium ion forms.","mc":["The ketone and morpholine form an iminium ion, helped by acetic acid.","NaBH(OAc)₃, a weak donor because of its three acetates, transfers hydride to the iminium ion.","The tertiary amine forms; the ketone itself is reduced only slowly."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Sodium triacetoxyborohydride","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sodium_triacetoxyborohydride"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Sodium triacetoxyborohydride"},{"x":"Tributyltin hydride"},{"x":"Sodium borohydride"},{"x":"DIBAL-H"}],"fig":{"r":[263,628],"cond":["?, AcOH, DCE"],"p":1672},"ex":"Sodium triacetoxyborohydride: Mild, non-toxic hydride for one-pot reductive amination of aldehydes and ketones. The acetoxy groups weaken it enough to leave the carbonyl alone until the iminium ion forms. Also works here: Sodium cyanoborohydride.","mc":["The ketone and morpholine form an iminium ion, helped by acetic acid.","NaBH(OAc)₃, a weak donor because of its three acetates, transfers hydride to the iminium ion.","The tertiary amine forms; the ketone itself is reduced only slowly."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Sodium triacetoxyborohydride","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sodium_triacetoxyborohydride"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1673},{"m":1674},{"m":1675},{"m":1676}],"t":"9-BBN","fig":{"r":[1677],"cond":["9-BBN, THF","H₂O₂, NaOH"],"veil":true},"ex":"Bulky borane for highly regioselective anti-Markovnikov hydroboration. Only one B–H, so it adds once and its bulk sends boron to the terminal carbon.","mc":["The B–H bond adds across the alkene in one four-centred step, with boron on the terminal carbon.","Basic H₂O₂ adds to boron, and the alkyl group migrates from boron to oxygen with retention.","Hydrolysis gives the primary alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["9-Borabicyclo(3.3.1)nonane","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/9-Borabicyclo(3.3.1)nonane"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"9-BBN"},{"x":"Collins reagent"},{"x":"DIBAL-H"},{"x":"PCC"}],"fig":{"r":[1677],"cond":["?, THF","H₂O₂, NaOH"],"p":1678},"ex":"9-BBN: Bulky borane for highly regioselective anti-Markovnikov hydroboration. Only one B–H, so it adds once and its bulk sends boron to the terminal carbon.","mc":["The B–H bond adds across the alkene in one four-centred step, with boron on the terminal carbon.","Basic H₂O₂ adds to boron, and the alkyl group migrates from boron to oxygen with retention.","Hydrolysis gives the primary alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["9-Borabicyclo(3.3.1)nonane","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/9-Borabicyclo(3.3.1)nonane"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1679},{"m":1680},{"m":1681},{"m":1682}],"t":"Lindlar catalyst","fig":{"r":[1683],"cond":["H₂, Lindlar catalyst, quinoline, hexane"],"veil":true},"ex":"Poisoned palladium (Pd/CaCO₃ with lead) that stops alkyne hydrogenation at the cis-alkene. For trans-alkenes use Na in liquid NH₃ instead.","mc":["H₂ dissociates on the palladium surface.","The alkyne adsorbs more strongly than the alkene and receives two hydrogens on the same face (syn), giving the cis-alkene.","Lead and quinoline block the most active sites, so the alkene comes off before it is reduced further."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Lindlar catalyst","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lindlar_catalyst"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Lindlar catalyst"},{"x":"Manganese dioxide"},{"x":"Sodium borohydride"},{"x":"L-Selectride"}],"fig":{"r":[1683],"cond":["H₂, ?, quinoline, hexane"],"p":1684},"ex":"Lindlar catalyst: Poisoned palladium (Pd/CaCO₃ with lead) that stops alkyne hydrogenation at the cis-alkene. For trans-alkenes use Na in liquid NH₃ instead.","mc":["H₂ dissociates on the palladium surface.","The alkyne adsorbs more strongly than the alkene and receives two hydrogens on the same face (syn), giving the cis-alkene.","Lead and quinoline block the most active sites, so the alkene comes off before it is reduced further."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Lindlar catalyst","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lindlar_catalyst"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1685},{"m":1686},{"m":1473},{"m":1687}],"t":"Tributyltin hydride","fig":{"r":[1688],"cond":["Bu₃SnH, AIBN, toluene, 80 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"With a radical initiator (AIBN) it reduces C–X to C–H and drives radical cyclisations. Here the primary radical cyclises 5-exo before tin hydride delivers the H.","mc":["AIBN breaks down on heating into radicals that abstract H from Bu₃SnH, giving Bu₃Sn•.","Bu₃Sn• abstracts the bromine atom, giving a primary hexenyl radical.","The radical cyclises 5-exo-trig onto the alkene, giving a cyclopentylmethyl radical.","This radical abstracts H from Bu₃SnH, giving methylcyclopentane and a new Bu₃Sn•."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Tributyltin hydride","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tributyltin_hydride"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Tributyltin hydride"},{"x":"IBX"},{"x":"Lithium aluminium hydride"},{"x":"Manganese dioxide"}],"fig":{"r":[1688],"cond":["?, AIBN, toluene, 80 °C"],"p":1689},"ex":"Tributyltin hydride: With a radical initiator (AIBN) it reduces C–X to C–H and drives radical cyclisations. Here the primary radical cyclises 5-exo before tin hydride delivers the H. Also works here: Tris(trimethylsilyl)silane.","mc":["AIBN breaks down on heating into radicals that abstract H from Bu₃SnH, giving Bu₃Sn•.","Bu₃Sn• abstracts the bromine atom, giving a primary hexenyl radical.","The radical cyclises 5-exo-trig onto the alkene, giving a cyclopentylmethyl radical.","This radical abstracts H from Bu₃SnH, giving methylcyclopentane and a new Bu₃Sn•."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Tributyltin hydride","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tributyltin_hydride"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1690},{"m":1691},{"m":1692},{"m":1693}],"t":"Tris(trimethylsilyl)silane","fig":{"r":[1694,257],"cond":["(TMS)₃SiH, AIBN, toluene, 80 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Tin-free hydrogen-atom donor: its Si–H bond carries radical chains, so it replaces Bu₃SnH in reductions, cyclisations and Giese additions. The nucleophilic (TMS)₃Si• radical abstracts halogen atoms fast, and its by-products are far less toxic and easier to remove than tin residues.","mc":["AIBN radicals abstract H from (TMS)₃SiH, giving the silyl radical (TMS)₃Si•.","The nucleophilic silyl radical abstracts iodine, giving the t-Bu radical.","The t-Bu radical adds to the CH₂ end of methyl vinyl ketone (a Giese addition).","The α-keto radical abstracts H from (TMS)₃SiH, giving the product and continuing the chain."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Tris(trimethylsilyl)silane","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tris(trimethylsilyl)silane"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Tris(trimethylsilyl)silane"},{"x":"Collins reagent"},{"x":"Dess–Martin periodinane"},{"x":"DDQ"}],"fig":{"r":[1694,257],"cond":["?, AIBN, toluene, 80 °C"],"p":1695},"ex":"Tris(trimethylsilyl)silane: Tin-free hydrogen-atom donor: its Si–H bond carries radical chains, so it replaces Bu₃SnH in reductions, cyclisations and Giese additions. The nucleophilic (TMS)₃Si• radical abstracts halogen atoms fast, and its by-products are far less toxic and easier to remove than tin residues. Also works here: Tributyltin hydride.","mc":["AIBN radicals abstract H from (TMS)₃SiH, giving the silyl radical (TMS)₃Si•.","The nucleophilic silyl radical abstracts iodine, giving the t-Bu radical.","The t-Bu radical adds to the CH₂ end of methyl vinyl ketone (a Giese addition).","The α-keto radical abstracts H from (TMS)₃SiH, giving the product and continuing the chain."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Tris(trimethylsilyl)silane","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tris(trimethylsilyl)silane"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1696},{"m":468},{"m":1697},{"m":1698}],"t":"Samarium(II) iodide","fig":{"r":[263,1088],"cond":["SmI₂, THF"],"veil":true},"ex":"Single-electron reductant: Barbier reactions, ketyl couplings and reductive cleavages. HMPA boosts its reducing power. Also called Kagan's reagent.","mc":["SmI₂ transfers electrons one at a time; it either turns MeI into an organosamarium species or reduces the ketone to a ketyl radical (the order is debated).","Either way, a new C–C bond forms at the carbonyl carbon.","Work-up protonates the samarium alkoxide to the tertiary alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Samarium(II) iodide","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samarium(II)_iodide"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Samarium(II) iodide"},{"x":"Sodium triacetoxyborohydride"},{"x":"Lithium aluminium hydride"},{"x":"Collins reagent"}],"fig":{"r":[263,1088],"cond":["?, THF"],"p":1699},"ex":"Samarium(II) iodide: Single-electron reductant: Barbier reactions, ketyl couplings and reductive cleavages. HMPA boosts its reducing power. Also called Kagan's reagent.","mc":["SmI₂ transfers electrons one at a time; it either turns MeI into an organosamarium species or reduces the ketone to a ketyl radical (the order is debated).","Either way, a new C–C bond forms at the carbonyl carbon.","Work-up protonates the samarium alkoxide to the tertiary alcohol."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Samarium(II) iodide","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samarium(II)_iodide"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1700},{"m":1701},{"m":1260},{"m":1621}],"t":"Collins reagent","fig":{"r":[1702],"cond":["CrO₃·2 pyridine (6 equiv), CH₂Cl₂, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"CrO₃·2 pyridine dissolved in CH₂Cl₂: a water-free Cr(VI) oxidant that turns primary alcohols into aldehydes and secondary alcohols into ketones. Less basic than the Sarett reagent and water-free unlike Jones reagent, so it suits acid- and base-sensitive substrates and stops at the aldehyde. Used in large excess.","mc":["The alcohol adds to chromium(VI), giving a chromate ester.","Pyridine removes the α-hydrogen as Cr(VI) is reduced to Cr(IV), giving the aldehyde.","Without water no aldehyde hydrate forms, so it is not oxidised further."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Collins reagent","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collins_reagent"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Collins reagent"},{"x":"DMDO"},{"x":"Manganese dioxide"},{"x":"DDQ"}],"fig":{"r":[1702],"cond":["? (6 equiv), CH₂Cl₂, rt"],"p":1703},"ex":"Collins reagent: CrO₃·2 pyridine dissolved in CH₂Cl₂: a water-free Cr(VI) oxidant that turns primary alcohols into aldehydes and secondary alcohols into ketones. Less basic than the Sarett reagent and water-free unlike Jones reagent, so it suits acid- and base-sensitive substrates and stops at the aldehyde. Used in large excess. Also works here: PCC, Dess–Martin periodinane.","mc":["The alcohol adds to chromium(VI), giving a chromate ester.","Pyridine removes the α-hydrogen as Cr(VI) is reduced to Cr(IV), giving the aldehyde.","Without water no aldehyde hydrate forms, so it is not oxidised further."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Collins reagent","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collins_reagent"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":804},{"m":805},{"m":1704},{"m":901}],"t":"Sarett reagent","fig":{"r":[808],"cond":["CrO₃·2 pyridine, pyridine, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"CrO₃·2 pyridine in pyridine: a Cr(VI) oxidant for alcohols under basic, water-free conditions. Hygroscopic and risky to prepare (adding CrO₃ to pyridine can catch fire), and pyridine as solvent rules out base-sensitive substrates. In CH₂Cl₂ the same complex is the Collins reagent.","mc":["The alcohol adds to chromium(VI), giving a chromate ester.","Pyridine removes the α-hydrogen as Cr(VI) is reduced to Cr(IV).","This gives the ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Sarett oxidation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sarett_oxidation"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Sarett reagent"},{"x":"DDQ"},{"x":"Sodium triacetoxyborohydride"},{"x":"DIBAL-H"}],"fig":{"r":[808],"cond":["?, pyridine, rt"],"p":809},"ex":"Sarett reagent: CrO₃·2 pyridine in pyridine: a Cr(VI) oxidant for alcohols under basic, water-free conditions. Hygroscopic and risky to prepare (adding CrO₃ to pyridine can catch fire), and pyridine as solvent rules out base-sensitive substrates. In CH₂Cl₂ the same complex is the Collins reagent.","mc":["The alcohol adds to chromium(VI), giving a chromate ester.","Pyridine removes the α-hydrogen as Cr(VI) is reduced to Cr(IV).","This gives the ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Sarett oxidation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sarett_oxidation"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1705},{"m":1706},{"m":1707},{"m":1708}],"t":"Koser's reagent","fig":{"r":[364],"cond":["Koser's reagent, MeCN, reflux"],"veil":true},"ex":"Hydroxy(tosyloxy)iodobenzene (HTIB): a hypervalent iodine(III) reagent that puts a tosyloxy group α to a ketone. Alkenes give 1,2-bis(tosyloxy) compounds by syn addition. Catalytic versions make the reagent in situ from iodobenzene, mCPBA and TsOH.","mc":["The enol of the ketone attacks iodine(III), displacing water, giving an α-iodanyl ketone.","Tosylate displaces iodobenzene from the α-carbon (reductive elimination at iodine).","This gives the α-tosyloxy ketone and iodobenzene."],"lk":[{"h":"Organic Chemistry Portal","l":[["Koser's reagent","https://www.organic-chemistry.org/chemicals/oxidations/kosers-reagent.shtm"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Koser's reagent"},{"x":"Manganese dioxide"},{"x":"Lindlar catalyst"},{"x":"mCPBA"}],"fig":{"r":[364],"cond":["?, MeCN, reflux"],"p":1709},"ex":"Koser's reagent: Hydroxy(tosyloxy)iodobenzene (HTIB): a hypervalent iodine(III) reagent that puts a tosyloxy group α to a ketone. Alkenes give 1,2-bis(tosyloxy) compounds by syn addition. Catalytic versions make the reagent in situ from iodobenzene, mCPBA and TsOH.","mc":["The enol of the ketone attacks iodine(III), displacing water, giving an α-iodanyl ketone.","Tosylate displaces iodobenzene from the α-carbon (reductive elimination at iodine).","This gives the α-tosyloxy ketone and iodobenzene."],"lk":[{"h":"Organic Chemistry Portal","l":[["Koser's reagent","https://www.organic-chemistry.org/chemicals/oxidations/kosers-reagent.shtm"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1710},{"m":1711},{"m":1712},{"m":1713}],"t":"Dimethylsulfoxonium methylide","fig":{"r":[1714],"cond":["Me₃S(O)⁺ I⁻, NaH, DMSO"],"veil":true},"ex":"Sulfoxonium ylide (Corey–Chaykovsky) that transfers CH₂: epoxides from ketones, cyclopropanes from enones. Softer and more stable than the sulfonium ylide, so it adds 1,4 to enones.","mc":["NaH deprotonates trimethylsulfoxonium iodide, giving the ylide Me₂S(O)=CH₂.","The soft, stabilised ylide adds reversibly at the β-carbon of the enone (1,4), giving an enolate.","The enolate carbon displaces DMSO intramolecularly (SN2), closing the cyclopropane."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Dimethylsulfoxonium methylide: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johnson%E2%80%93Corey%E2%80%93Chaykovsky_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Dimethylsulfoxonium methylide"},{"x":"Dimsyl sodium"},{"x":"Tebbe reagent"},{"x":"Deoxo-Fluor"}],"fig":{"r":[1714],"cond":["?, DMSO"],"p":1715},"ex":"Dimethylsulfoxonium methylide: Sulfoxonium ylide (Corey–Chaykovsky) that transfers CH₂: epoxides from ketones, cyclopropanes from enones. Softer and more stable than the sulfonium ylide, so it adds 1,4 to enones.","mc":["NaH deprotonates trimethylsulfoxonium iodide, giving the ylide Me₂S(O)=CH₂.","The soft, stabilised ylide adds reversibly at the β-carbon of the enone (1,4), giving an enolate.","The enolate carbon displaces DMSO intramolecularly (SN2), closing the cyclopropane."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Dimethylsulfoxonium methylide: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johnson%E2%80%93Corey%E2%80%93Chaykovsky_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1716},{"m":1717},{"m":1718},{"m":1712}],"t":"Dimethylsulfonium methylide","fig":{"r":[1719],"cond":["Me₃S⁺ I⁻, n-BuLi, THF"],"veil":true},"ex":"Sulfonium ylide that adds 1,2 to carbonyls, even enones, giving epoxides. More reactive and less stable than the sulfoxonium ylide.","mc":["n-BuLi deprotonates trimethylsulfonium iodide, giving the ylide Me₂S=CH₂.","The reactive ylide adds quickly and irreversibly to the carbonyl carbon (1,2), giving a betaine.","The alkoxide displaces dimethyl sulfide (SN2), closing the epoxide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Dimethylsulfonium methylide: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johnson%E2%80%93Corey%E2%80%93Chaykovsky_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Dimethylsulfonium methylide"},{"x":"N-Bromosuccinimide"},{"x":"Azobisisobutyronitrile"},{"x":"Eschenmoser's salt"}],"fig":{"r":[1719],"cond":["?, THF"],"p":1720},"ex":"Dimethylsulfonium methylide: Sulfonium ylide that adds 1,2 to carbonyls, even enones, giving epoxides. More reactive and less stable than the sulfoxonium ylide.","mc":["n-BuLi deprotonates trimethylsulfonium iodide, giving the ylide Me₂S=CH₂.","The reactive ylide adds quickly and irreversibly to the carbonyl carbon (1,2), giving a betaine.","The alkoxide displaces dimethyl sulfide (SN2), closing the epoxide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Dimethylsulfonium methylide: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johnson%E2%80%93Corey%E2%80%93Chaykovsky_reaction#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1721},{"m":1722},{"m":1723},{"m":1724}],"t":"Dimsyl sodium","fig":{"r":[1725],"cond":["NaH + DMSO (dimsyl sodium), DMSO"],"veil":true},"ex":"DMSO's conjugate base (pKa 35): a strong base, and a nucleophile that turns esters into β-keto sulfoxides. Also used to make Wittig ylides from phosphonium salts.","mc":["NaH deprotonates DMSO, giving the dimsyl anion CH₃S(O)CH₂⁻.","The anion attacks the ester carbonyl; loss of methoxide gives the β-keto sulfoxide.","A second equivalent deprotonates the acidic CH₂ of the product (the driving force); acid work-up reprotonates it."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Sodium methylsulfinylmethylide","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sodium_methylsulfinylmethylide"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Dimsyl sodium"},{"x":"TBAF"},{"x":"Burgess reagent"},{"x":"Lawesson's reagent"}],"fig":{"r":[1725],"cond":["?, DMSO"],"p":1726},"ex":"Dimsyl sodium: DMSO's conjugate base (pKa 35): a strong base, and a nucleophile that turns esters into β-keto sulfoxides. Also used to make Wittig ylides from phosphonium salts.","mc":["NaH deprotonates DMSO, giving the dimsyl anion CH₃S(O)CH₂⁻.","The anion attacks the ester carbonyl; loss of methoxide gives the β-keto sulfoxide.","A second equivalent deprotonates the acidic CH₂ of the product (the driving force); acid work-up reprotonates it."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Sodium methylsulfinylmethylide","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sodium_methylsulfinylmethylide"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1146},{"m":1727},{"m":1143},{"m":1144}],"t":"TMS-diazomethane","fig":{"r":[1728],"cond":["TMSCHN₂, MeOH/toluene, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"Safer, stable substitute for diazomethane: methylates carboxylic acids in methanol. Still toxic, but not explosive like diazomethane.","mc":["The carboxylic acid protonates the diazo carbon, giving a carboxylate and a diazonium ion.","Methanol removes the silyl group, turning the diazonium ion into CH₃N₂⁺; this is why methanol is needed.","The carboxylate displaces N₂ from CH₃N₂⁺ by SN2, giving the methyl ester."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Trimethylsilyldiazomethane","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trimethylsilyldiazomethane"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"TMS-diazomethane"},{"x":"Dimsyl sodium"},{"x":"Eschenmoser's salt"},{"x":"Seyferth–Gilbert reagent"}],"fig":{"r":[1728],"cond":["?, MeOH/toluene, rt"],"p":1729},"ex":"TMS-diazomethane: Safer, stable substitute for diazomethane: methylates carboxylic acids in methanol. Still toxic, but not explosive like diazomethane.","mc":["The carboxylic acid protonates the diazo carbon, giving a carboxylate and a diazonium ion.","Methanol removes the silyl group, turning the diazonium ion into CH₃N₂⁺; this is why methanol is needed.","The carboxylate displaces N₂ from CH₃N₂⁺ by SN2, giving the methyl ester."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Trimethylsilyldiazomethane","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trimethylsilyldiazomethane"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1730},{"m":1731},{"m":1732},{"m":1733}],"t":"Ohira–Bestmann reagent","fig":{"r":[1734],"cond":["Ohira–Bestmann reagent, K₂CO₃, MeOH"],"veil":true},"ex":"Diazophosphonate that converts aldehydes into terminal alkynes under mild base. Releases the Seyferth–Gilbert reagent in situ.","mc":["Methoxide removes the acetyl group, releasing the diazomethylphosphonate anion.","The anion adds to the aldehyde, and elimination of dimethyl phosphate gives a vinyl diazo compound.","Loss of N₂ gives a vinylidene carbene, and a 1,2-hydrogen shift gives the terminal alkyne."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ohira–Bestmann reagent: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seyferth%E2%80%93Gilbert_homologation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Ohira–Bestmann reagent"},{"x":"N-Bromosuccinimide"},{"x":"Wilkinson's catalyst"},{"x":"DAST"}],"fig":{"r":[1734],"cond":["?, K₂CO₃, MeOH"],"p":1735},"ex":"Ohira–Bestmann reagent: Diazophosphonate that converts aldehydes into terminal alkynes under mild base. Releases the Seyferth–Gilbert reagent in situ.","mc":["Methoxide removes the acetyl group, releasing the diazomethylphosphonate anion.","The anion adds to the aldehyde, and elimination of dimethyl phosphate gives a vinyl diazo compound.","Loss of N₂ gives a vinylidene carbene, and a 1,2-hydrogen shift gives the terminal alkyne."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Ohira–Bestmann reagent: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seyferth%E2%80%93Gilbert_homologation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1736},{"m":1737},{"m":1738},{"m":1739}],"t":"Seyferth–Gilbert reagent","fig":{"r":[1740],"cond":["Seyferth–Gilbert reagent, KOt-Bu, THF, −78 °C → rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"Dimethyl (diazomethyl)phosphonate: with KOt-Bu it turns aldehydes and aryl ketones into alkynes with one more carbon. Aryl ketones give internal alkynes, since an aryl group migrates to the vinylidene carbene. Enolisable aldehydes would undergo aldol condensation with KOt-Bu; the Ohira–Bestmann reagent releases the same anion with mild K₂CO₃ in methanol and suits them.","mc":["KOt-Bu deprotonates the reagent; the anion adds to the ketone and an oxaphosphetane forms.","Loss of dimethyl phosphate gives a vinyl diazo compound, which loses N₂ to a vinylidene carbene.","A phenyl group migrates to the carbene carbon (1,2-shift), giving diphenylacetylene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Seyferth–Gilbert reagent: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seyferth%E2%80%93Gilbert_homologation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Seyferth–Gilbert reagent"},{"x":"Deoxo-Fluor"},{"x":"Ohira–Bestmann reagent"},{"x":"LDA"}],"fig":{"r":[1740],"cond":["?, KOt-Bu, THF, −78 °C → rt"],"p":1741},"ex":"Seyferth–Gilbert reagent: Dimethyl (diazomethyl)phosphonate: with KOt-Bu it turns aldehydes and aryl ketones into alkynes with one more carbon. Aryl ketones give internal alkynes, since an aryl group migrates to the vinylidene carbene. Enolisable aldehydes would undergo aldol condensation with KOt-Bu; the Ohira–Bestmann reagent releases the same anion with mild K₂CO₃ in methanol and suits them.","mc":["KOt-Bu deprotonates the reagent; the anion adds to the ketone and an oxaphosphetane forms.","Loss of dimethyl phosphate gives a vinyl diazo compound, which loses N₂ to a vinylidene carbene.","A phenyl group migrates to the carbene carbon (1,2-shift), giving diphenylacetylene."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Seyferth–Gilbert reagent: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seyferth%E2%80%93Gilbert_homologation#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1742},{"m":1722},{"m":1743},{"m":1744}],"t":"Tebbe reagent","fig":{"r":[1652],"cond":["Tebbe reagent, THF, −40 °C → rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"Titanium methylidene source that methylenates esters and lactones (C=O → C=CH₂), not just ketones. Wittig reagents do not react with esters; Tebbe and Petasis reagents do.","mc":["The Tebbe reagent releases the titanium methylidene Cp₂Ti=CH₂ (with loss of Me₂AlCl).","Cp₂Ti=CH₂ and the ester carbonyl combine in a [2+2] cycloaddition to an oxatitanacyclobutane.","Retro-[2+2] gives the enol ether and Cp₂Ti=O; the strong Ti–O bond drives it."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Tebbe reagent: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tebbe's_reagent#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Tebbe reagent"},{"x":"Lawesson's reagent"},{"x":"Grubbs catalyst"},{"x":"Deoxo-Fluor"}],"fig":{"r":[1652],"cond":["?, THF, −40 °C → rt"],"p":1745},"ex":"Tebbe reagent: Titanium methylidene source that methylenates esters and lactones (C=O → C=CH₂), not just ketones. Wittig reagents do not react with esters; Tebbe and Petasis reagents do.","mc":["The Tebbe reagent releases the titanium methylidene Cp₂Ti=CH₂ (with loss of Me₂AlCl).","Cp₂Ti=CH₂ and the ester carbonyl combine in a [2+2] cycloaddition to an oxatitanacyclobutane.","Retro-[2+2] gives the enol ether and Cp₂Ti=O; the strong Ti–O bond drives it."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Tebbe reagent: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tebbe's_reagent#Reaction_mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1746},{"m":1747},{"m":1748},{"m":1749}],"t":"Petasis reagent","fig":{"r":[1750],"cond":["Petasis reagent, toluene, 65 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Dimethyltitanocene, Cp₂TiMe₂: on heating it gives Cp₂Ti=CH₂, which methylenates aldehydes, ketones and esters (C=O → C=CH₂). Made from Cp₂TiCl₂ and MeMgCl or MeLi. Unlike the Tebbe reagent it contains no aluminium, and its homologues Cp₂Ti(CH₂R)₂ transfer other alkylidenes.","mc":["On heating, Cp₂TiMe₂ loses methane (α-hydrogen abstraction), giving the methylidene Cp₂Ti=CH₂.","Cp₂Ti=CH₂ and the ester carbonyl combine in a [2+2] cycloaddition to an oxatitanacyclobutane.","Retro-[2+2] gives the enol ether and Cp₂Ti=O; the strong Ti–O bond drives it."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Petasis reagent","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petasis_reagent"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Petasis reagent"},{"x":"Dimethylsulfoxonium methylide"},{"x":"Comins' reagent"},{"x":"LDA"}],"fig":{"r":[1750],"cond":["?, toluene, 65 °C"],"p":1751},"ex":"Petasis reagent: Dimethyltitanocene, Cp₂TiMe₂: on heating it gives Cp₂Ti=CH₂, which methylenates aldehydes, ketones and esters (C=O → C=CH₂). Made from Cp₂TiCl₂ and MeMgCl or MeLi. Unlike the Tebbe reagent it contains no aluminium, and its homologues Cp₂Ti(CH₂R)₂ transfer other alkylidenes.","mc":["On heating, Cp₂TiMe₂ loses methane (α-hydrogen abstraction), giving the methylidene Cp₂Ti=CH₂.","Cp₂Ti=CH₂ and the ester carbonyl combine in a [2+2] cycloaddition to an oxatitanacyclobutane.","Retro-[2+2] gives the enol ether and Cp₂Ti=O; the strong Ti–O bond drives it."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Petasis reagent","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petasis_reagent"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1752},{"m":1753},{"m":1754},{"m":1755}],"t":"Grubbs catalyst","fig":{"r":[1756,515],"cond":["Grubbs II (5 mol%), CH₂Cl₂, 40 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Ruthenium carbene catalyst for olefin metathesis: ring-closing, cross and ring-opening. Tolerates air, water and most functional groups. Nobel Prize 2005.","mc":["A phosphine leaves the ruthenium precatalyst, giving the active 14-electron ruthenium carbene.","An alkene binds and forms a ruthenacyclobutane by [2+2] cycloaddition.","Retro-[2+2] releases a new alkene and a new ruthenium carbene; in this cross-metathesis the E-enoate forms and ethylene is lost."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Grubbs catalyst","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grubbs_catalyst"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Grubbs catalyst"},{"x":"PhNTf₂"},{"x":"Deoxo-Fluor"},{"x":"N-Bromosuccinimide"}],"fig":{"r":[1756,515],"cond":["? (5 mol%), CH₂Cl₂, 40 °C"],"p":1757},"ex":"Grubbs catalyst: Ruthenium carbene catalyst for olefin metathesis: ring-closing, cross and ring-opening. Tolerates air, water and most functional groups. Nobel Prize 2005.","mc":["A phosphine leaves the ruthenium precatalyst, giving the active 14-electron ruthenium carbene.","An alkene binds and forms a ruthenacyclobutane by [2+2] cycloaddition.","Retro-[2+2] releases a new alkene and a new ruthenium carbene; in this cross-metathesis the E-enoate forms and ethylene is lost."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Grubbs catalyst","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grubbs_catalyst"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1758},{"m":1759},{"m":1760},{"m":1761}],"t":"Burgess reagent","fig":{"r":[1762],"cond":["Burgess reagent, benzene, 60 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Mild dehydrating agent: secondary and tertiary alcohols to alkenes by syn-elimination. Here only one alkene can form, since a bridgehead alkene is impossible. An alcohol with two kinds of β-hydrogen gives a mixture: 2-methylbutan-2-ol gives the more and the less substituted alkene 2.4 : 1. Primary alcohols give carbamates instead; primary amides give nitriles.","mc":["The alcohol attacks sulfur and displaces triethylamine, giving a sulfamate ester.","On gentle heating the C–O bond starts to ionise, and the carbamate nitrogen abstracts a β-hydrogen on the same side (syn, Ei).","The alkene forms before the cation can rearrange; only C3 can lose its hydrogen, since a bridgehead alkene is impossible."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Burgess reagent","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burgess_reagent"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Burgess reagent"},{"x":"N,O-Dimethylhydroxylamine"},{"x":"Dimsyl sodium"},{"x":"Eschenmoser's salt"}],"fig":{"r":[1762],"cond":["?, benzene, 60 °C"],"p":1763},"ex":"Burgess reagent: Mild dehydrating agent: secondary and tertiary alcohols to alkenes by syn-elimination. Here only one alkene can form, since a bridgehead alkene is impossible. An alcohol with two kinds of β-hydrogen gives a mixture: 2-methylbutan-2-ol gives the more and the less substituted alkene 2.4 : 1. Primary alcohols give carbamates instead; primary amides give nitriles.","mc":["The alcohol attacks sulfur and displaces triethylamine, giving a sulfamate ester.","On gentle heating the C–O bond starts to ionise, and the carbamate nitrogen abstracts a β-hydrogen on the same side (syn, Ei).","The alkene forms before the cation can rearrange; only C3 can lose its hydrogen, since a bridgehead alkene is impossible."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Burgess reagent","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burgess_reagent"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1764},{"m":1765},{"m":1766},{"m":1767}],"t":"Lawesson's reagent","fig":{"r":[1768],"cond":["Lawesson's reagent, toluene, heat"],"veil":true},"ex":"Thionating agent: converts the C=O of amides, ketones and esters into C=S.","mc":["In solution the reagent splits into two reactive dithiophosphine ylides, ArP(=S)S⁻.","The ylide adds to the amide carbonyl, forming a four-membered thiaoxaphosphetane.","Cycloreversion, like the last step of a Wittig reaction, gives the C=S bond and a strong P=O bond."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Lawesson's reagent: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lawesson's_reagent#Mechanism_of_action"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Lawesson's reagent"},{"x":"LDA"},{"x":"Dimethylsulfonium methylide"},{"x":"Petasis reagent"}],"fig":{"r":[1768],"cond":["?, toluene, heat"],"p":1769},"ex":"Lawesson's reagent: Thionating agent: converts the C=O of amides, ketones and esters into C=S.","mc":["In solution the reagent splits into two reactive dithiophosphine ylides, ArP(=S)S⁻.","The ylide adds to the amide carbonyl, forming a four-membered thiaoxaphosphetane.","Cycloreversion, like the last step of a Wittig reaction, gives the C=S bond and a strong P=O bond."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Lawesson's reagent: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lawesson's_reagent#Mechanism_of_action"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1770},{"m":1771},{"m":1772},{"m":1773}],"t":"DAST","fig":{"r":[1774],"cond":["DAST, CH₂Cl₂, −78 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Deoxyfluorination: alcohols to alkyl fluorides, and aldehydes or ketones to gem-difluorides. Can decompose violently above ~90 °C; Deoxo-Fluor is a more stable alternative.","mc":["The alcohol attacks sulfur and displaces fluoride, giving an alkoxyaminosulfur difluoride and HF.","This turns the oxygen into an excellent leaving group.","Fluoride displaces it by SN2 (or SN1 for stabilised cations), giving the alkyl fluoride."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Diethylaminosulfur trifluoride","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diethylaminosulfur_trifluoride"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"DAST"},{"x":"Lawesson's reagent"},{"x":"LDA"},{"x":"Petasis reagent"}],"fig":{"r":[1774],"cond":["?, CH₂Cl₂, −78 °C"],"p":1775},"ex":"DAST: Deoxyfluorination: alcohols to alkyl fluorides, and aldehydes or ketones to gem-difluorides. Can decompose violently above ~90 °C; Deoxo-Fluor is a more stable alternative.","mc":["The alcohol attacks sulfur and displaces fluoride, giving an alkoxyaminosulfur difluoride and HF.","This turns the oxygen into an excellent leaving group.","Fluoride displaces it by SN2 (or SN1 for stabilised cations), giving the alkyl fluoride."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Diethylaminosulfur trifluoride","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diethylaminosulfur_trifluoride"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1776},{"m":1777},{"m":1778},{"m":1779}],"t":"Deoxo-Fluor","fig":{"r":[1780],"cond":["Deoxo-Fluor, CH₂Cl₂, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"Deoxyfluorinating agent like DAST: alcohols to alkyl fluorides, aldehydes and ketones to gem-difluorides. Bis(2-methoxyethyl)aminosulfur trifluoride is less prone to explosive decomposition than DAST, so it is preferred on a larger scale.","mc":["The aldehyde oxygen attacks sulfur while fluoride adds to the carbonyl carbon, giving an α-fluoro aminosulfinyl ether, ArCHF–O–SF₂NR₂.","The O–S group leaves, helped by the aryl ring and the fluorine, giving a fluorocarbenium ion.","A second fluoride adds, giving the gem-difluoride."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Deoxo-Fluor","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deoxo-Fluor"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Deoxo-Fluor"},{"x":"PhNTf₂"},{"x":"TBAF"},{"x":"Ohira–Bestmann reagent"}],"fig":{"r":[1780],"cond":["?, CH₂Cl₂, rt"],"p":1781},"ex":"Deoxo-Fluor: Deoxyfluorinating agent like DAST: alcohols to alkyl fluorides, aldehydes and ketones to gem-difluorides. Bis(2-methoxyethyl)aminosulfur trifluoride is less prone to explosive decomposition than DAST, so it is preferred on a larger scale.","mc":["The aldehyde oxygen attacks sulfur while fluoride adds to the carbonyl carbon, giving an α-fluoro aminosulfinyl ether, ArCHF–O–SF₂NR₂.","The O–S group leaves, helped by the aryl ring and the fluorine, giving a fluorocarbenium ion.","A second fluoride adds, giving the gem-difluoride."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Deoxo-Fluor","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deoxo-Fluor"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1782},{"m":1783},{"m":1784},{"m":1785}],"t":"Selectfluor","fig":{"r":[1786],"cond":["Selectfluor, MeCN"],"veil":true},"ex":"Bench-stable electrophilic fluorinating agent (an F⁺ source). Sold as the bis(tetrafluoroborate) salt.","mc":["The keto ester is in equilibrium with its enol.","The enol attacks the fluorine of the N⁺–F bond, a formal F⁺ transfer (SN2 at fluorine or single-electron transfer).","Loss of a proton gives the α-fluoro keto ester."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Selectfluor: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Selectfluor#Mechanism_of_fluorination"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Selectfluor"},{"x":"Deoxo-Fluor"},{"x":"TMS-diazomethane"},{"x":"Eschenmoser's salt"}],"fig":{"r":[1786],"cond":["?, MeCN"],"p":1787},"ex":"Selectfluor: Bench-stable electrophilic fluorinating agent (an F⁺ source). Sold as the bis(tetrafluoroborate) salt.","mc":["The keto ester is in equilibrium with its enol.","The enol attacks the fluorine of the N⁺–F bond, a formal F⁺ transfer (SN2 at fluorine or single-electron transfer).","Loss of a proton gives the α-fluoro keto ester."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Selectfluor: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Selectfluor#Mechanism_of_fluorination"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1788},{"m":1789},{"m":1790},{"m":1791}],"t":"N-Bromosuccinimide","fig":{"r":[722],"cond":["NBS, AIBN (cat.), CCl₄, reflux"],"veil":true},"ex":"Keeps Br₂ at a low concentration: allylic and benzylic bromination, or bromohydrins in water. Low Br₂ means radical substitution wins over addition to the alkene (Wohl–Ziegler).","mc":["Traces of HBr react with NBS to release a low concentration of Br₂; AIBN starts the chain by forming Br•.","Br• removes an allylic hydrogen, giving a resonance-stabilised allylic radical.","The allylic radical abstracts Br from Br₂, giving the allylic bromide and a new Br•; the HBr formed releases more Br₂ from NBS."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["N-Bromosuccinimide","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N-Bromosuccinimide"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"N-Bromosuccinimide"},{"x":"LDA"},{"x":"Dimethylsulfonium methylide"},{"x":"Dimsyl sodium"}],"fig":{"r":[722],"cond":["?, AIBN (cat.), CCl₄, reflux"],"p":1792},"ex":"N-Bromosuccinimide: Keeps Br₂ at a low concentration: allylic and benzylic bromination, or bromohydrins in water. Low Br₂ means radical substitution wins over addition to the alkene (Wohl–Ziegler).","mc":["Traces of HBr react with NBS to release a low concentration of Br₂; AIBN starts the chain by forming Br•.","Br• removes an allylic hydrogen, giving a resonance-stabilised allylic radical.","The allylic radical abstracts Br from Br₂, giving the allylic bromide and a new Br•; the HBr formed releases more Br₂ from NBS."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["N-Bromosuccinimide","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N-Bromosuccinimide"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1793},{"m":1794},{"m":1795},{"m":1796}],"t":"TBAF","fig":{"r":[1797],"cond":["TBAF, THF, rt"],"veil":true},"ex":"Fluoride source that removes silyl protecting groups; the Si–F bond is very strong. Basic: can cause side reactions such as acyl migration.","mc":["Fluoride attacks silicon, forming a pentacoordinate silicate.","The Si–O bond breaks, releasing the alkoxide; the very strong Si–F bond is the driving force.","Water in the reagent or the work-up protonates the alkoxide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Tetra-n-butylammonium fluoride","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tetra-n-butylammonium_fluoride"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"TBAF"},{"x":"Tebbe reagent"},{"x":"Wilkinson's catalyst"},{"x":"Eschenmoser's salt"}],"fig":{"r":[1797],"cond":["?, THF, rt"],"p":1798},"ex":"TBAF: Fluoride source that removes silyl protecting groups; the Si–F bond is very strong. Basic: can cause side reactions such as acyl migration.","mc":["Fluoride attacks silicon, forming a pentacoordinate silicate.","The Si–O bond breaks, releasing the alkoxide; the very strong Si–F bond is the driving force.","Water in the reagent or the work-up protonates the alkoxide."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Tetra-n-butylammonium fluoride","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tetra-n-butylammonium_fluoride"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1799},{"m":1800},{"m":1698},{"m":1801}],"t":"Eschenmoser's salt","fig":{"r":[314],"cond":["LDA, THF","Eschenmoser's salt","MeI, then NaHCO₃"],"veil":true},"ex":"Dimethyl(methylene)ammonium iodide: aminomethylates enolates; elimination then gives α-methylene carbonyls. A pre-formed Mannich electrophile.","mc":["LDA forms the ketone enolate.","The enolate attacks the CH₂ of the iminium salt, giving a β-dimethylamino ketone (a Mannich base).","MeI quaternises the nitrogen, and base eliminates trimethylamine (E1cB), giving the α-methylene ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Eschenmoser's salt","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eschenmoser's_salt"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Eschenmoser's salt"},{"x":"Dimsyl sodium"},{"x":"Seyferth–Gilbert reagent"},{"x":"Tebbe reagent"}],"fig":{"r":[314],"cond":["LDA, THF","?","MeI, then NaHCO₃"],"p":1802},"ex":"Eschenmoser's salt: Dimethyl(methylene)ammonium iodide: aminomethylates enolates; elimination then gives α-methylene carbonyls. A pre-formed Mannich electrophile.","mc":["LDA forms the ketone enolate.","The enolate attacks the CH₂ of the iminium salt, giving a β-dimethylamino ketone (a Mannich base).","MeI quaternises the nitrogen, and base eliminates trimethylamine (E1cB), giving the α-methylene ketone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Eschenmoser's salt","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eschenmoser's_salt"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1803},{"m":1804},{"m":1805},{"m":987}],"t":"PhNTf₂","fig":{"r":[484],"cond":["LDA, THF, −78 °C","PhNTf₂"],"veil":true},"ex":"Triflating agent that turns enolates into enol (vinyl) triflates for cross-coupling. Comins' reagent (a pyridyl triflimide) does the same job and is more reactive.","mc":["LDA forms the kinetic enolate at −78 °C.","The enolate oxygen attacks one sulfur of PhNTf₂.","PhNTf⁻ leaves, giving the enol triflate."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)aniline","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)aniline"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"PhNTf₂"},{"x":"Tebbe reagent"},{"x":"LDA"},{"x":"Dimethylsulfoxonium methylide"}],"fig":{"r":[484],"cond":["LDA, THF, −78 °C","?"],"p":1806},"ex":"PhNTf₂: Triflating agent that turns enolates into enol (vinyl) triflates for cross-coupling. Comins' reagent (a pyridyl triflimide) does the same job and is more reactive.","mc":["LDA forms the kinetic enolate at −78 °C.","The enolate oxygen attacks one sulfur of PhNTf₂.","PhNTf⁻ leaves, giving the enol triflate."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)aniline","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)aniline"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1807},{"m":1808},{"m":1809},{"m":1810}],"t":"Comins' reagent","fig":{"r":[1811],"cond":["LDA, THF, −78 °C","Comins' reagent"],"veil":true},"ex":"Pyridine-derived triflating agent: turns ketone enolates into vinyl (enol) triflates for cross-coupling. More reactive than PhNTf₂, so it also traps hindered enolates and dienolates. LDA at −78 °C makes the kinetic enolate, so the triflate forms on the less substituted side.","mc":["LDA removes a proton from the less hindered α-carbon at −78 °C, giving the kinetic lithium enolate.","The enolate oxygen attacks one sulfur of Comins' reagent.","The stabilised N-(5-chloropyridin-2-yl)triflamide anion leaves, giving the enol triflate."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Comins' reagent: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comins'_reagent#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Comins' reagent"},{"x":"Dimsyl sodium"},{"x":"Deoxo-Fluor"},{"x":"Azobisisobutyronitrile"}],"fig":{"r":[1811],"cond":["LDA, THF, −78 °C","?"],"p":1812},"ex":"Comins' reagent: Pyridine-derived triflating agent: turns ketone enolates into vinyl (enol) triflates for cross-coupling. More reactive than PhNTf₂, so it also traps hindered enolates and dienolates. LDA at −78 °C makes the kinetic enolate, so the triflate forms on the less substituted side.","mc":["LDA removes a proton from the less hindered α-carbon at −78 °C, giving the kinetic lithium enolate.","The enolate oxygen attacks one sulfur of Comins' reagent.","The stabilised N-(5-chloropyridin-2-yl)triflamide anion leaves, giving the enol triflate."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Comins' reagent: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Comins'_reagent#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1813},{"m":1814},{"m":1815},{"m":1816}],"t":"N,O-Dimethylhydroxylamine","fig":{"r":[1817],"cond":["MeNH(OMe)·HCl, pyridine","MeMgBr, THF","H₃O⁺"],"veil":true},"ex":"Forms Weinreb amides, which react only once with Grignard or organolithium reagents to give ketones. A stable chelated intermediate stops the second addition.","mc":["N,O-Dimethylhydroxylamine attacks the acid chloride, giving the Weinreb amide.","MeMgBr adds to the amide carbonyl; magnesium chelates the alkoxide and the N-methoxy oxygen, which stabilises the tetrahedral intermediate.","The chelate only collapses to the ketone on acid work-up, so a second addition cannot happen."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["N,O-Dimethylhydroxylamine: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weinreb_ketone_synthesis#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"N,O-Dimethylhydroxylamine"},{"x":"Dimethylsulfoxonium methylide"},{"x":"Azobisisobutyronitrile"},{"x":"Comins' reagent"}],"fig":{"r":[1817],"cond":["?, pyridine","MeMgBr, THF","H₃O⁺"],"p":1818},"ex":"N,O-Dimethylhydroxylamine: Forms Weinreb amides, which react only once with Grignard or organolithium reagents to give ketones. A stable chelated intermediate stops the second addition.","mc":["N,O-Dimethylhydroxylamine attacks the acid chloride, giving the Weinreb amide.","MeMgBr adds to the amide carbonyl; magnesium chelates the alkoxide and the N-methoxy oxygen, which stabilises the tetrahedral intermediate.","The chelate only collapses to the ketone on acid work-up, so a second addition cannot happen."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["N,O-Dimethylhydroxylamine: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weinreb_ketone_synthesis#Mechanism"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1819},{"m":1820},{"m":1821},{"m":1822}],"t":"LDA","fig":{"r":[1823],"cond":["LDA, THF, −78 °C","MeI"],"veil":true},"ex":"Strong, bulky, non-nucleophilic base that forms kinetic (less substituted) enolates at −78 °C. Its conjugate acid, diisopropylamine, has a pKa of about 36.","mc":["At −78 °C LDA removes the more accessible proton, on the less substituted α-carbon (kinetic control).","The lithium enolate forms quickly, completely and irreversibly.","MeI alkylates the enolate carbon by SN2, giving 2,6-dimethylcyclohexanone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Lithium diisopropylamide","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lithium_diisopropylamide"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"LDA"},{"x":"PhNTf₂"},{"x":"N,O-Dimethylhydroxylamine"},{"x":"Ohira–Bestmann reagent"}],"fig":{"r":[1823],"cond":["?, THF, −78 °C","MeI"],"p":1824},"ex":"LDA: Strong, bulky, non-nucleophilic base that forms kinetic (less substituted) enolates at −78 °C. Its conjugate acid, diisopropylamine, has a pKa of about 36.","mc":["At −78 °C LDA removes the more accessible proton, on the less substituted α-carbon (kinetic control).","The lithium enolate forms quickly, completely and irreversibly.","MeI alkylates the enolate carbon by SN2, giving 2,6-dimethylcyclohexanone."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Lithium diisopropylamide","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lithium_diisopropylamide"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1825},{"m":1826},{"m":1827},{"m":1828}],"t":"Wilkinson's catalyst","fig":{"r":[1829],"cond":["H₂, RhCl(PPh₃)₃, benzene"],"veil":true},"ex":"Homogeneous Rh catalyst that hydrogenates the least hindered C=C bond selectively. RhCl(PPh₃)₃; leaves trisubstituted alkenes, like the ring alkene of limonene, alone.","mc":["One PPh₃ leaves, and H₂ adds oxidatively to rhodium, giving a Rh(III) dihydride.","The least hindered alkene coordinates and inserts into a Rh–H bond.","Reductive elimination of the alkyl and hydride gives the alkane and regenerates the Rh(I) catalyst."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Wilkinson's catalyst","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilkinson's_catalyst"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Wilkinson's catalyst"},{"x":"Eschenmoser's salt"},{"x":"Petasis reagent"},{"x":"LDA"}],"fig":{"r":[1829],"cond":["H₂, ?, benzene"],"p":1830},"ex":"Wilkinson's catalyst: Homogeneous Rh catalyst that hydrogenates the least hindered C=C bond selectively. RhCl(PPh₃)₃; leaves trisubstituted alkenes, like the ring alkene of limonene, alone.","mc":["One PPh₃ leaves, and H₂ adds oxidatively to rhodium, giving a Rh(III) dihydride.","The least hindered alkene coordinates and inserts into a Rh–H bond.","Reductive elimination of the alkyl and hydride gives the alkane and regenerates the Rh(I) catalyst."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Wilkinson's catalyst","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilkinson's_catalyst"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Product","a":"Which of these is the product?","o":[{"m":1831},{"m":1832},{"m":1833},{"m":1834}],"t":"Azobisisobutyronitrile","fig":{"r":[1835],"cond":["Bu₃SnH, AIBN, benzene, 80 °C"],"veil":true},"ex":"Azo radical initiator: on heating to about 65–85 °C it loses N₂, giving 2-cyanoprop-2-yl radicals that start radical chains. A few mol% is enough, since it only starts the chain (by making Bu₃Sn•). Here the aryl radical cyclises 5-exo onto the allyl ether. Et₃B/O₂ starts chains at room temperature or below.","mc":["AIBN loses N₂ on heating, giving 2-cyanoprop-2-yl radicals that abstract H from Bu₃SnH.","Bu₃Sn• abstracts bromine, giving the aryl radical.","The aryl radical cyclises 5-exo onto the allyl ether, giving a primary radical.","The primary radical abstracts H from Bu₃SnH, giving 3-methyl-2,3-dihydrobenzofuran and a new Bu₃Sn•."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Azobisisobutyronitrile: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Azobisisobutyronitrile#Mechanism_of_decomposition"]]}]},{"s":"rxn","k":"Reagent","a":"Which reagent is the “?”","o":[{"x":"Azobisisobutyronitrile"},{"x":"Seyferth–Gilbert reagent"},{"x":"Ohira–Bestmann reagent"},{"x":"N,O-Dimethylhydroxylamine"}],"fig":{"r":[1835],"cond":["Bu₃SnH, ?, benzene, 80 °C"],"p":1836},"ex":"Azobisisobutyronitrile: Azo radical initiator: on heating to about 65–85 °C it loses N₂, giving 2-cyanoprop-2-yl radicals that start radical chains. A few mol% is enough, since it only starts the chain (by making Bu₃Sn•). Here the aryl radical cyclises 5-exo onto the allyl ether. Et₃B/O₂ starts chains at room temperature or below.","mc":["AIBN loses N₂ on heating, giving 2-cyanoprop-2-yl radicals that abstract H from Bu₃SnH.","Bu₃Sn• abstracts bromine, giving the aryl radical.","The aryl radical cyclises 5-exo onto the allyl ether, giving a primary radical.","The primary radical abstracts H from Bu₃SnH, giving 3-methyl-2,3-dihydrobenzofuran and a new Bu₃Sn•."],"lk":[{"h":"Wikipedia","l":[["Azobisisobutyronitrile: mechanism with drawings","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Azobisisobutyronitrile#Mechanism_of_decomposition"]]}]},{"s":"pg","k":"Which group comes off","a":"Which protecting group do these conditions remove?","o":[{"x":"Acetonide (1,3-diol)"},{"x":"TBDPS ether (2° OH)"},{"x":"Benzyl ether (2° OH)"}],"t":"Methyl α-D-glucopyranoside","h":true,"fig":{"r":[1837],"cond":["HCl, THF/H₂O"],"after":1838,"rm":0},"ex":"HCl, THF/H₂O: Dilute aqueous acid (about 1 M HCl) in THF, room temperature. Hydrolyses acetals and acid-labile ethers. Aqueous acid hydrolyses the ketal; warming speeds it up."},{"s":"pg","k":"Which group comes off","a":"Which protecting group do these conditions remove?","o":[{"x":"Fmoc (NH₂)"},{"x":"Boc (NH₂)"},{"x":"Benzyl ester (CO₂H)"}],"t":"Lysine","h":true,"fig":{"r":[1839],"cond":["TBAF, THF"],"after":1840,"rm":0},"ex":"TBAF, THF: Tetrabutylammonium fluoride in THF, room temperature. Fluoride attacks silicon; the reagent is also basic. Fluoride is basic enough to remove Fmoc."},{"s":"pg","k":"Which group comes off","a":"Which protecting group do these conditions remove?","o":[{"x":"Boc (NH₂)"},{"x":"Fmoc (NH₂)"},{"x":"Methyl ester (CO₂H)"}],"y":1,"t":"Lysine","h":true,"fig":{"r":[1841],"cond":["TBAF, THF"],"after":1842,"rm":1},"ex":"TBAF, THF: Tetrabutylammonium fluoride in THF, room temperature. Fluoride attacks silicon; the reagent is also basic. Fluoride is basic enough to remove Fmoc."},{"s":"pg","k":"Which group comes off","a":"Which protecting group do these conditions remove?","o":[{"x":"Acetate (2° OH)"},{"x":"TBS ether (1° OH)"}],"y":1,"t":"Thymidine","h":true,"fig":{"r":[1843],"cond":["TBAF, THF"],"after":1844,"rm":1},"ex":"TBAF, THF: Tetrabutylammonium fluoride in THF, room temperature. Fluoride attacks silicon; the reagent is also basic."},{"s":"pg","k":"Which group comes off","a":"Which protecting group do these conditions remove?","o":[{"x":"Benzoate (2° OH)"},{"x":"PMB ether (2° OH)"},{"x":"TBS ether (2° OH)"},{"x":"MOM ether (1° OH)"}],"y":2,"t":"Methyl α-D-glucopyranoside","h":true,"fig":{"r":[1845],"cond":["HCl, THF/H₂O"],"after":1846,"rm":2},"ex":"HCl, THF/H₂O: Dilute aqueous acid (about 1 M HCl) in THF, room temperature. Hydrolyses acetals and acid-labile ethers. Primary TBS ethers go within hours; hindered secondary ones are slower."},{"s":"pg","k":"Which group comes off","a":"Which protecting group do these conditions remove?","o":[{"x":"1,3-Dioxolane (C=O)"},{"x":"TBDPS ether (2° OH)"}],"t":"4-Hydroxycyclohexanone","h":true,"fig":{"r":[1847],"cond":["HCl, THF/H₂O"],"after":1848,"rm":0},"ex":"HCl, THF/H₂O: Dilute aqueous acid (about 1 M HCl) in THF, room temperature. Hydrolyses acetals and acid-labile ethers. Aqueous acid hydrolyses the acetal back to the carbonyl."},{"s":"pg","k":"Which group comes off","a":"Which protecting group do these conditions remove?","o":[{"x":"TBDPS ether (2° OH)"},{"x":"tert-Butyl ester (CO₂H)"}],"y":1,"t":"3-Hydroxybutanoic acid","h":true,"fig":{"r":[1849],"cond":["TFA, CH₂Cl₂"],"after":1850,"rm":1},"ex":"TFA, CH₂Cl₂: Trifluoroacetic acid (20–50 %) in CH₂Cl₂, room temperature: a strong anhydrous acid. A scavenger (Et₃SiH, anisole or water) often traps the cations. Leaves as the tert-butyl cation (then isobutylene), like Boc."},{"s":"pg","k":"Which group comes off","a":"Which protecting group do these conditions remove?","o":[{"x":"1,3-Dioxolane (CHO)"},{"x":"TBDPS ether (1° OH)"}],"t":"4-(2-Hydroxyethyl)benzaldehyde","h":true,"fig":{"r":[1851],"cond":["HCl, THF/H₂O"],"after":1852,"rm":0},"ex":"HCl, THF/H₂O: Dilute aqueous acid (about 1 M HCl) in THF, room temperature. Hydrolyses acetals and acid-labile ethers. Aqueous acid hydrolyses the acetal back to the carbonyl."},{"s":"pg","k":"Which group comes off","a":"Which protecting group do these conditions remove?","o":[{"x":"Acetonide (1,2-diol)"},{"x":"MOM ether (2° OH)"},{"x":"Benzyl ether (1° OH)"}],"t":"Methyl β-D-galactopyranoside","h":true,"fig":{"r":[1853],"cond":["HCl, THF/H₂O"],"after":1854,"rm":0},"ex":"HCl, THF/H₂O: Dilute aqueous acid (about 1 M HCl) in THF, room temperature. Hydrolyses acetals and acid-labile ethers. Aqueous acid hydrolyses the ketal; warming speeds it up."},{"s":"pg","k":"Which group comes off","a":"Which protecting group do these conditions remove?","o":[{"x":"TBDPS ether (2° OH)"},{"x":"Benzoate (2° OH)"},{"x":"THP ether (2° OH)"},{"x":"Benzyl ether (1° OH)"}],"y":2,"t":"Methyl α-D-glucopyranoside","h":true,"fig":{"r":[1855],"cond":["TFA, CH₂Cl₂"],"after":1856,"rm":2},"ex":"TFA, CH₂Cl₂: Trifluoroacetic acid (20–50 %) in CH₂Cl₂, room temperature: a strong anhydrous acid. A scavenger (Et₃SiH, anisole or water) often traps the cations. Very acid-labile; strong acid removes it quickly when water or an alcohol is present."},{"s":"pg","k":"Which group comes off","a":"Which protecting group do these conditions remove?","o":[{"x":"PMB ether (2° OH)"},{"x":"MOM ether (2° OH)"},{"x":"Benzyl ether (2° OH)"},{"x":"TBS ether (1° OH)"}],"y":3,"t":"Methyl α-D-glucopyranoside","h":true,"fig":{"r":[1857],"cond":["HCl, THF/H₂O"],"after":1858,"rm":3},"ex":"HCl, THF/H₂O: Dilute aqueous acid (about 1 M HCl) in THF, room temperature. Hydrolyses acetals and acid-labile ethers. Primary TBS ethers go within hours; hindered secondary ones are slower."},{"s":"pg","k":"Which group comes off","a":"Which protecting group do these conditions remove?","o":[{"x":"TBDPS ether (1° OH)"},{"x":"Boc (NH₂)"}],"y":1,"t":"Phenylalaninol","h":true,"fig":{"r":[1859],"cond":["TFA, CH₂Cl₂"],"after":1860,"rm":1},"ex":"TFA, CH₂Cl₂: Trifluoroacetic acid (20–50 %) in CH₂Cl₂, room temperature: a strong anhydrous acid. A scavenger (Et₃SiH, anisole or water) often traps the cations. Protonation, loss of the tert-butyl cation, then decarboxylation of the carbamic acid."},{"s":"pg","k":"Which group comes off","a":"Which protecting group do these conditions remove?","o":[{"x":"Acetonide (1,3-diol)"},{"x":"Acetate (2° OH)"},{"x":"TBS ether (2° OH)"}],"y":2,"t":"Methyl α-D-glucopyranoside","h":true,"fig":{"r":[1861],"cond":["TBAF, THF"],"after":1862,"rm":2},"ex":"TBAF, THF: Tetrabutylammonium fluoride in THF, room temperature. Fluoride attacks silicon; the reagent is also basic."},{"s":"pg","k":"Which group comes off","a":"Which protecting group do these conditions remove?","o":[{"x":"Acetonide (1,2-diol)"},{"x":"TBS ether (2° OH)"},{"x":"Benzyl ether (1° OH)"}],"y":1,"t":"Methyl β-D-galactopyranoside","fig":{"r":[1863],"cond":["TBAF, THF"],"after":1864,"rm":1},"ex":"TBAF, THF: Tetrabutylammonium fluoride in THF, room temperature. Fluoride attacks silicon; the reagent is also basic."},{"s":"pg","k":"Which group comes off","a":"Which protecting group do these conditions remove?","o":[{"x":"TBS ether (2° OH)"},{"x":"Benzyl ether (2° OH)"},{"x":"Acetate (2° OH)"},{"x":"PMB ether (1° OH)"}],"y":2,"t":"Methyl α-D-glucopyranoside","fig":{"r":[1865],"cond":["LiOH, THF/H₂O"],"after":1866,"rm":2},"ex":"LiOH, THF/H₂O: Lithium hydroxide in aqueous THF, room temperature (saponification)."},{"s":"pg","k":"Which group comes off","a":"Which protecting group do these conditions remove?","o":[{"x":"Acetonide (1,2-diol)"},{"x":"Fmoc (NH₂)"}],"t":"3-Amino-1,2-propanediol","fig":{"r":[1867],"cond":["HCl, THF/H₂O"],"after":1868,"rm":0},"ex":"HCl, THF/H₂O: Dilute aqueous acid (about 1 M HCl) in THF, room temperature. Hydrolyses acetals and acid-labile ethers. Aqueous acid hydrolyses the ketal; warming speeds it up."},{"s":"pg","k":"Which group comes off","a":"Which protecting group do these conditions remove?","o":[{"x":"Acetonide (1,2-diol)"},{"x":"Acetate (1° OH)"}],"y":1,"t":"1,2,4-Butanetriol","fig":{"r":[1869],"cond":["LiOH, THF/H₂O"],"after":1870,"rm":1},"ex":"LiOH, THF/H₂O: Lithium hydroxide in aqueous THF, room temperature (saponification)."},{"s":"pg","k":"Which group comes off","a":"Which protecting group do these conditions remove?","o":[{"x":"THP ether (2° OH)"},{"x":"Benzyl ester (CO₂H)"}],"t":"3-Hydroxybutanoic acid","fig":{"r":[1871],"cond":["AcOH, THF/H₂O"],"after":1872,"rm":0},"ex":"AcOH, THF/H₂O: Acetic acid, THF and water (for example 3:1:1), room temperature to 45 °C: a mild aqueous acid. AcOH/THF/water at 45 °C is the textbook way to remove THP."},{"s":"pg","k":"Which group comes off","a":"Which protecting group do these conditions remove?","o":[{"x":"Acetonide (1,3-diol)"},{"x":"PMB ether (2° OH)"},{"x":"Acetate (2° OH)"}],"t":"Methyl α-D-glucopyranoside","fig":{"r":[1873],"cond":["HCl, THF/H₂O"],"after":1874,"rm":0},"ex":"HCl, THF/H₂O: Dilute aqueous acid (about 1 M HCl) in THF, room temperature. Hydrolyses acetals and acid-labile ethers. Aqueous acid hydrolyses the ketal; warming speeds it up."},{"s":"pg","k":"Which group comes off","a":"Which protecting group do these conditions remove?","o":[{"x":"tert-Butyl ester (CO₂H)"},{"x":"Boc (NH₂)"},{"x":"Benzyl ester (CO₂H)"}],"y":2,"t":"Aspartic acid","fig":{"r":[1875],"cond":["LiOH, THF/H₂O"],"after":1876,"rm":2},"ex":"LiOH, THF/H₂O: Lithium hydroxide in aqueous THF, room temperature (saponification)."},{"s":"pg","k":"Which group comes off","a":"Whi