n MeOH, EtOH or EtOAc: hydrogenolysis of benzylic C–O bonds."},{"s":"pg","k":"Which group comes off","a":"Which protecting group do these conditions remove?","o":[{"x":"Acetonide (1,2-diol)"},{"x":"TMS alkyne (C≡CH)"}],"t":"5-Hexyne-1,2-diol","fig":{"r":[2159],"cond":["HCl, THF/H₂O"],"after":2182,"rm":0},"ex":"HCl, THF/H₂O: Dilute aqueous acid (about 1 M HCl) in THF, room temperature. Hydrolyses acetals and acid-labile ethers. Aqueous acid hydrolyses the ketal; warming speeds it up."},{"s":"pg","k":"Which group comes off","a":"Which protecting group do these conditions remove?","o":[{"x":"SEM ether (2° OH)"},{"x":"Benzyl ether (2° OH)"},{"x":"Acetate (2° OH)"},{"x":"TMS ether (1° OH)"}],"y":3,"t":"Methyl α-D-glucopyranoside","fig":{"r":[2183],"cond":["AcOH, THF/H₂O"],"after":2184,"rm":3},"ex":"AcOH, THF/H₂O: Acetic acid, THF and water (for example 3:1:1), room temperature to 45 °C: a mild aqueous acid."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a TBS ether?","o":[{"x":"TBAF, THF"},{"x":"DDQ, CH₂Cl₂/H₂O"},{"x":"PhSH, K₂CO₃, DMF"},{"x":"N₂H₄·H₂O, AcOH, pyridine"}],"t":"TBS ether","f":"tert-butyldimethylsilyl ether","fig":{"m":2185},"ex":"The everyday silyl ether: survives base, oxidation and most reagents; fluoride or aqueous acid removes it."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a TBDPS ether?","o":[{"x":"TBAF, THF"},{"x":"N₂H₄·H₂O, EtOH, heat"},{"x":"HgCl₂, CaCO₃, MeCN/H₂O"},{"x":"DIBAL-H, CH₂Cl₂, −78 °C"}],"t":"TBDPS ether","f":"tert-butyldiphenylsilyl ether","fig":{"m":2186},"ex":"A bulky silyl ether far more stable to acid than TBS; fluoride removes it."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Benzyl ether?","o":[{"x":"H₂, Pd/C"},{"x":"Pd(PPh₃)₄, PhSiH₃"},{"x":"Piperidine, DMF"},{"x":"TFA, CH₂Cl₂"}],"t":"Benzyl ether","f":"benzyl ether","fig":{"m":2187},"ex":"A robust ether that survives acid and base; hydrogenolysis (H₂, Pd/C) or dissolving metal removes it."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a PMB ether?","o":[{"x":"Na, NH₃(l)"},{"x":"HF·pyridine, THF"},{"x":"TBAF, THF"},{"x":"LiOH, THF/H₂O"}],"t":"PMB ether","f":"p-methoxybenzyl ether","fig":{"m":2188},"ex":"Like a benzyl ether, but its electron-rich ring lets DDQ or CAN remove it by oxidation while Bn stays."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a MOM ether?","o":[{"x":"BBr₃, CH₂Cl₂"},{"x":"N₂H₄·H₂O, AcOH, pyridine"},{"x":"HF·pyridine, THF"},{"x":"CSA, MeOH, 0 °C"}],"t":"MOM ether","f":"methoxymethyl ether","fig":{"m":2189},"ex":"An acetal ether that survives base, nucleophiles and mild acid; strong acid or a Lewis acid removes it."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a THP ether?","o":[{"x":"HCl, THF/H₂O"},{"x":"Piperidine, DMF"},{"x":"Pd(PPh₃)₄, PhSiH₃"},{"x":"NaOMe, MeOH"}],"t":"THP ether","f":"tetrahydropyranyl ether","fig":{"m":2190},"ex":"A cheap acetal, stable to base and nucleophiles; very mild acid removes it."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Acetate?","o":[{"x":"N₂H₄·H₂O, EtOH, heat"},{"x":"AcOH, THF/H₂O"},{"x":"Zn, AcOH"},{"x":"TFA, CH₂Cl₂"}],"t":"Acetate","f":"acetate ester","fig":{"m":2191},"ex":"The simplest ester protecting group: stable to acid, removed by mild base such as K₂CO₃ in MeOH. Hydrazine hydrate cleaves esters (hydrazinolysis)."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Benzoate?","o":[{"x":"NaOMe, MeOH"},{"x":"HgCl₂, CaCO₃, MeCN/H₂O"},{"x":"DDQ, CH₂Cl₂/H₂O"},{"x":"N₂H₄·H₂O, AcOH, pyridine"}],"t":"Benzoate","f":"benzoate ester","fig":{"m":2192},"ex":"A sturdier ester than acetate that migrates less; methoxide (Zemplén) or hydroxide removes it. Zemplén deacylation: catalytic NaOMe in MeOH."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Acetonide?","o":[{"x":"HCl, THF/H₂O"},{"x":"H₂, Pd/C"},{"x":"Na, NH₃(l)"},{"x":"K₂CO₃, MeOH"}],"t":"Acetonide","f":"isopropylidene ketal","fig":{"m":2193},"ex":"Protects a 1,2- or 1,3-diol as a cyclic ketal; stable to base, aqueous acid removes it. Aqueous acid hydrolyses the ketal; warming speeds it up."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a 1,3-Dioxolane?","o":[{"x":"HCl, THF/H₂O"},{"x":"DIBAL-H, CH₂Cl₂, −78 °C"},{"x":"N₂H₄·H₂O, EtOH, heat"},{"x":"Pd(PPh₃)₄, PhSiH₃"}],"t":"1,3-Dioxolane","f":"ethylene acetal or ketal","fig":{"m":2194},"ex":"Protects an aldehyde or ketone as a cyclic acetal; stable to base, nucleophiles and hydrides, aqueous acid removes it. Aqueous acid hydrolyses the acetal back to the carbonyl."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Boc?","o":[{"x":"BBr₃, CH₂Cl₂"},{"x":"DIBAL-H, CH₂Cl₂, −78 °C"},{"x":"Piperidine, DMF"},{"x":"PhSH, K₂CO₃, DMF"}],"t":"Boc","f":"tert-butyl carbamate","fig":{"m":2195},"ex":"The standard acid-labile carbamate: stable to base, nucleophiles and hydrogenation; TFA or HCl removes it."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Cbz?","o":[{"x":"H₂, Pd/C"},{"x":"N₂H₄·H₂O, AcOH, pyridine"},{"x":"DIBAL-H, CH₂Cl₂, −78 °C"},{"x":"Zn, AcOH"}],"t":"Cbz","f":"benzyl carbamate","fig":{"m":2196},"ex":"A carbamate stable to acid and base; hydrogenolysis removes it."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Fmoc?","o":[{"x":"Piperidine, DMF"},{"x":"Zn, AcOH"},{"x":"AcOH, THF/H₂O"},{"x":"PPTS, MeOH"}],"t":"Fmoc","f":"9-fluorenylmethyl carbamate","fig":{"m":2197},"ex":"A base-labile carbamate that piperidine removes (E1cB); stable to acid. The basis of Fmoc/tBu peptide synthesis. Piperidine removes the acidic fluorenyl C–H; E1cB elimination gives dibenzofulvene, which piperidine traps."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Methyl ester?","o":[{"x":"LiOH, THF/H₂O"},{"x":"NaOMe, MeOH"},{"x":"PPTS, MeOH"},{"x":"H₂, Pd/C"}],"t":"Methyl ester","f":"methyl ester","fig":{"m":2198},"ex":"The simplest ester: saponification with LiOH or NaOH removes it."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a tert-Butyl ester?","o":[{"x":"TFA, CH₂Cl₂"},{"x":"PhSH, K₂CO₃, DMF"},{"x":"HgCl₂, CaCO₃, MeCN/H₂O"},{"x":"AcOH, THF/H₂O"}],"t":"tert-Butyl ester","f":"tert-butyl ester","fig":{"m":2199},"ex":"A bulky ester that resists saponification and nucleophiles; acid (TFA) removes it. Leaves as the tert-butyl cation (then isobutylene), like Boc."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Benzyl ester?","o":[{"x":"LiOH, THF/H₂O"},{"x":"Pd(PPh₃)₄, PhSiH₃"},{"x":"PPTS, MeOH"},{"x":"AcOH, THF/H₂O"}],"t":"Benzyl ester","f":"benzyl ester","fig":{"m":2200},"ex":"An ester removed by hydrogenolysis under neutral conditions, or by saponification."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Aryl methyl ether?","o":[{"x":"BBr₃, CH₂Cl₂"},{"x":"LiOH, THF/H₂O"},{"x":"Pd(PPh₃)₄, PhSiH₃"},{"x":"AcOH, THF/H₂O"}],"t":"Aryl methyl ether","f":"aryl methyl ether (anisole)","fig":{"m":2201},"ex":"A very robust phenol ether, usually cleaved with BBr₃. Boron coordinates the ether oxygen; bromide attacks the methyl group."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a TES ether?","o":[{"x":"HCl, THF/H₂O"},{"x":"PhSH, K₂CO₃, DMF"},{"x":"N₂H₄·H₂O, EtOH, heat"},{"x":"N₂H₄·H₂O, AcOH, pyridine"}],"t":"TES ether","f":"triethylsilyl ether","fig":{"m":2202},"h":true,"ex":"Between TMS and TBS in stability: mild acid removes it while TBS stays."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a TIPS ether?","o":[{"x":"TBAF, THF"},{"x":"NaOMe, MeOH"},{"x":"H₂, Pd/C"},{"x":"Pd(PPh₃)₄, PhSiH₃"}],"t":"TIPS ether","f":"triisopropylsilyl ether","fig":{"m":2203},"h":true,"ex":"A very hindered silyl ether: more stable than TBS to acid and much more stable to base; fluoride removes it."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a tert-Butyl ether?","o":[{"x":"BBr₃, CH₂Cl₂"},{"x":"H₂, Pd/C"},{"x":"HF·pyridine, THF"},{"x":"TBAF, THF"}],"t":"tert-Butyl ether","f":"tert-butyl ether","fig":{"m":2204},"h":true,"ex":"An acid-labile ether, stable to base and nucleophiles; TFA removes it."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a MEM ether?","o":[{"x":"BBr₃, CH₂Cl₂"},{"x":"CSA, MeOH, 0 °C"},{"x":"Na, NH₃(l)"},{"x":"HCl, THF/H₂O"}],"t":"MEM ether","f":"2-methoxyethoxymethyl ether","fig":{"m":2205},"h":true,"ex":"An acetal ether like MOM; its extra ether oxygen helps Lewis acids (ZnBr₂, TiCl₄) bind and remove it."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Nap ether?","o":[{"x":"H₂, Pd/C"},{"x":"NaOMe, MeOH"},{"x":"LiOH, THF/H₂O"},{"x":"N₂H₄·H₂O, AcOH, pyridine"}],"t":"Nap ether","f":"2-naphthylmethyl ether","fig":{"m":2206},"h":true,"ex":"A benzyl-type ether that DDQ removes, though more slowly than PMB; hydrogenolysis also removes it."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Trityl ether?","o":[{"x":"HCl, THF/H₂O"},{"x":"NaOMe, MeOH"},{"x":"N₂H₄·H₂O, EtOH, heat"},{"x":"Piperidine, DMF"}],"t":"Trityl ether","f":"triphenylmethyl ether","fig":{"m":2207},"h":true,"ex":"A very bulky, very acid-labile ether that protects primary alcohols selectively."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Pivalate?","o":[{"x":"DIBAL-H, CH₂Cl₂, −78 °C"},{"x":"H₂, Pd/C"},{"x":"Pd(PPh₃)₄, PhSiH₃"},{"x":"CSA, MeOH, 0 °C"}],"t":"Pivalate","f":"pivalate ester","fig":{"m":2208},"h":true,"ex":"A hindered ester that survives the mild base that removes acetates; DIBAL-H, LiAlH₄ or hot methoxide removes it. Reduces the ester, giving back the alcohol (and neopentyl alcohol)."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Levulinate?","o":[{"x":"N₂H₄·H₂O, AcOH, pyridine"},{"x":"CSA, MeOH, 0 °C"},{"x":"HCl, THF/H₂O"},{"x":"HgCl₂, CaCO₃, MeCN/H₂O"}],"t":"Levulinate","f":"levulinate (4-oxopentanoate) ester","fig":{"m":2209},"h":true,"ex":"An ester whose ketone hydrazine attacks, so it comes off under conditions that leave acetates and benzoates. The hydrazone of the ketone cyclises onto the ester and releases the alcohol."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Benzylidene acetal?","o":[{"x":"H₂, Pd/C"},{"x":"Piperidine, DMF"},{"x":"LiOH, THF/H₂O"},{"x":"K₂CO₃, MeOH"}],"t":"Benzylidene acetal","f":"benzylidene acetal","fig":{"m":2210},"h":true,"ex":"Protects a 1,3-diol (classically the 4,6-OH of a pyranoside) as a cyclic acetal; acid or hydrogenolysis removes it. Hydrogenolysis gives the diol and toluene."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a TIPDS?","o":[{"x":"TBAF, THF"},{"x":"DIBAL-H, CH₂Cl₂, −78 °C"},{"x":"Piperidine, DMF"},{"x":"PPTS, MeOH"}],"t":"TIPDS","f":"1,1,3,3-tetraisopropyldisiloxane-1,3-diyl","fig":{"m":2211},"h":true,"ex":"Bridges a 1,3-diol (classically 3′,5′ of a nucleoside) with a disiloxane; fluoride removes it."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Dimethyl acetal?","o":[{"x":"HCl, THF/H₂O"},{"x":"K₂CO₃, MeOH"},{"x":"TBAF, THF"},{"x":"Na, NH₃(l)"}],"t":"Dimethyl acetal","f":"dimethyl acetal","fig":{"m":2212},"h":true,"ex":"Protects an aldehyde as an open-chain acetal; stable to base, aqueous acid removes it."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a 1,3-Dithiane?","o":[{"x":"HgCl₂, CaCO₃, MeCN/H₂O"},{"x":"DIBAL-H, CH₂Cl₂, −78 °C"},{"x":"K₂CO₃, MeOH"},{"x":"LiOH, THF/H₂O"}],"t":"1,3-Dithiane","f":"1,3-dithiane (thioacetal)","fig":{"m":2213},"h":true,"ex":"A thioacetal stable to acid and base; Hg(II), alkylating agents or oxidants remove it. Hg(II) binds sulfur and the thioacetal hydrolyses."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Alloc?","o":[{"x":"Pd(PPh₃)₄, PhSiH₃"},{"x":"HCl, THF/H₂O"},{"x":"Piperidine, DMF"},{"x":"AcOH, THF/H₂O"}],"t":"Alloc","f":"allyl carbamate","fig":{"m":2214},"h":true,"ex":"A carbamate that Pd(0) and an allyl scavenger remove; orthogonal to Boc, Fmoc and Cbz. Pd(0) forms a π-allyl complex; the silane takes the allyl group and CO₂ leaves."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Troc?","o":[{"x":"Zn, AcOH"},{"x":"CSA, MeOH, 0 °C"},{"x":"PPTS, MeOH"},{"x":"Piperidine, DMF"}],"t":"Troc","f":"2,2,2-trichloroethyl carbamate","fig":{"m":2215},"h":true,"ex":"A carbamate that zinc removes by reductive β-elimination; stable to acid. Zinc reduces the C–Cl bond; β-elimination releases the carbamate, which loses CO₂."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Teoc?","o":[{"x":"TBAF, THF"},{"x":"AcOH, THF/H₂O"},{"x":"PhSH, K₂CO₃, DMF"},{"x":"N₂H₄·H₂O, EtOH, heat"}],"t":"Teoc","f":"2-(trimethylsilyl)ethyl carbamate","fig":{"m":2216},"h":true,"ex":"A carbamate that fluoride removes by β-elimination of the silyl group. Fluoride attacks silicon; β-elimination releases ethylene and CO₂."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Nosyl?","o":[{"x":"PhSH, K₂CO₃, DMF"},{"x":"AcOH, THF/H₂O"},{"x":"LiOH, THF/H₂O"},{"x":"HF·pyridine, THF"}],"t":"Nosyl","f":"2-nitrobenzenesulfonamide","fig":{"m":2217},"h":true,"ex":"A sulfonamide that thiolates remove by aromatic substitution (Fukuyama), unlike the robust tosyl. Thiolate adds to the nitroarene (Meisenheimer complex) and releases SO₂ and the amine."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Tosyl?","o":[{"x":"Na, NH₃(l)"},{"x":"NaOMe, MeOH"},{"x":"DIBAL-H, CH₂Cl₂, −78 °C"},{"x":"BBr₃, CH₂Cl₂"}],"t":"Tosyl","f":"p-toluenesulfonamide","fig":{"m":2218},"h":true,"ex":"A very robust sulfonamide; removing it needs dissolving metal or another strong reductant. Dissolving metal reduces the S–N bond."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Phthalimide?","o":[{"x":"N₂H₄·H₂O, EtOH, heat"},{"x":"Pd(PPh₃)₄, PhSiH₃"},{"x":"HgCl₂, CaCO₃, MeCN/H₂O"},{"x":"Zn, AcOH"}],"t":"Phthalimide","f":"phthalimide (N,N-phthaloyl)","fig":{"m":2219},"h":true,"ex":"Protects a primary amine on both N–H bonds; hydrazine removes it (Ing–Manske). Hydrazine attacks both carbonyls and releases the amine and phthalhydrazide."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Trifluoroacetamide?","o":[{"x":"K₂CO₃, MeOH"},{"x":"Zn, AcOH"},{"x":"TFA, CH₂Cl₂"},{"x":"BBr₃, CH₂Cl₂"}],"t":"Trifluoroacetamide","f":"trifluoroacetamide","fig":{"m":2220},"h":true,"ex":"An amide made labile by its CF₃ group: mild base (K₂CO₃ in MeOH, or LiOH) removes it, unlike an acetamide. The CF₃ group makes the amide carbonyl reactive enough for methoxide."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Allyl ester?","o":[{"x":"LiOH, THF/H₂O"},{"x":"PPTS, MeOH"},{"x":"Zn, AcOH"},{"x":"CSA, MeOH, 0 °C"}],"t":"Allyl ester","f":"allyl ester","fig":{"m":2221},"h":true,"ex":"An ester that Pd(0) and a scavenger remove under neutral conditions."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a TMSE ester?","o":[{"x":"TBAF, THF"},{"x":"Pd(PPh₃)₄, PhSiH₃"},{"x":"CSA, MeOH, 0 °C"},{"x":"AcOH, THF/H₂O"}],"t":"TMSE ester","f":"2-(trimethylsilyl)ethyl ester","fig":{"m":2222},"h":true,"ex":"An ester that fluoride removes by β-elimination (ethylene and Me₃SiF leave). Fluoride attacks silicon; β-elimination releases ethylene."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a TMS alkyne?","o":[{"x":"NaOMe, MeOH"},{"x":"Piperidine, DMF"},{"x":"Pd(PPh₃)₄, PhSiH₃"},{"x":"CSA, MeOH, 0 °C"}],"t":"TMS alkyne","f":"trimethylsilyl alkyne","fig":{"m":2223},"h":true,"ex":"Caps a terminal alkyne C–H; K₂CO₃ in MeOH or TBAF removes it."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a TIPS alkyne?","o":[{"x":"TBAF, THF"},{"x":"PPTS, MeOH"},{"x":"CSA, MeOH, 0 °C"},{"x":"DDQ, CH₂Cl₂/H₂O"}],"t":"TIPS alkyne","f":"triisopropylsilyl alkyne","fig":{"m":2224},"h":true,"ex":"A bulky alkyne silyl that survives K₂CO₃/MeOH, so a TMS alkyne can be removed next to it; TBAF removes it."},{"s":"pg","k":"Removed by","a":"Which of these conditions removes a Aryl benzyl ether?","o":[{"x":"H₂, Pd/C"},{"x":"TBAF, THF"},{"x":"Piperidine, DMF"},{"x":"Pd(PPh₃)₄, PhSiH₃"}],"t":"Aryl benzyl ether","f":"aryl benzyl ether","fig":{"m":2225},"h":true,"ex":"A phenol benzyl ether: hydrogenolysis or BBr₃ removes it."},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ni have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"16 e"}],"t":"Nickel tetracarbonyl","f":"Ni(CO)₄","fig":{"m":2226},"ex":"Covalent count: Ni (group 10) 10 e; 4 × CO (L) 8 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ni(0), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ni?","o":[{"x":"0"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"−1"},{"x":"−2"}],"t":"Nickel tetracarbonyl","f":"Ni(CO)₄","fig":{"m":2226},"ex":"Covalent count: Ni (group 10) 10 e; 4 × CO (L) 8 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ni(0), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ni?","o":[{"x":"d¹⁰"},{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Nickel tetracarbonyl","f":"Ni(CO)₄","fig":{"m":2226},"ex":"Covalent count: Ni (group 10) 10 e; 4 × CO (L) 8 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ni(0), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Tetrahedral"},{"x":"Square planar"},{"x":"Square pyramidal"},{"x":"Trigonal bipyramidal"}],"t":"Nickel tetracarbonyl","f":"Ni(CO)₄","fig":{"m":2226},"ex":"Tetrahedral. Covalent count: Ni (group 10) 10 e; 4 × CO (L) 8 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ni(0), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Fe have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"16 e"}],"t":"Iron pentacarbonyl","f":"Fe(CO)₅","fig":{"m":2227},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Fe(0), d⁸; d⁸ + 10 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Fe?","o":[{"x":"0"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"−1"},{"x":"−2"}],"t":"Iron pentacarbonyl","f":"Fe(CO)₅","fig":{"m":2227},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Fe(0), d⁸; d⁸ + 10 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Fe?","o":[{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁶"}],"t":"Iron pentacarbonyl","f":"Fe(CO)₅","fig":{"m":2227},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Fe(0), d⁸; d⁸ + 10 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Trigonal bipyramidal"},{"x":"Square pyramidal"},{"x":"Trigonal planar"},{"x":"Octahedral"}],"t":"Iron pentacarbonyl","f":"Fe(CO)₅","fig":{"m":2227},"ex":"Trigonal bipyramidal. Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Fe(0), d⁸; d⁸ + 10 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Cr have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Chromium hexacarbonyl","f":"Cr(CO)₆","fig":{"m":2228},"ex":"Covalent count: Cr (group 6) 6 e; 6 × CO (L) 12 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Cr(0), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Cr?","o":[{"x":"0"},{"x":"−1"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+2"}],"t":"Chromium hexacarbonyl","f":"Cr(CO)₆","fig":{"m":2228},"ex":"Covalent count: Cr (group 6) 6 e; 6 × CO (L) 12 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Cr(0), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Cr?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁴"}],"t":"Chromium hexacarbonyl","f":"Cr(CO)₆","fig":{"m":2228},"ex":"Covalent count: Cr (group 6) 6 e; 6 × CO (L) 12 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Cr(0), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Linear"},{"x":"Tetrahedral"}],"t":"Chromium hexacarbonyl","f":"Cr(CO)₆","fig":{"m":2228},"ex":"Octahedral. Covalent count: Cr (group 6) 6 e; 6 × CO (L) 12 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Cr(0), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does V have?","o":[{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"16 e"},{"x":"19 e"}],"t":"Vanadium hexacarbonyl","f":"V(CO)₆","fig":{"m":2229},"ex":"Covalent count: V (group 5) 5 e; 6 × CO (L) 12 e: 17 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is V(0), d⁵; d⁵ + 12 e from the ligands = 17 e. One of the few stable 17-electron complexes; it is easily reduced to [V(CO)₆]⁻.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of V?","o":[{"x":"0"},{"x":"−1"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+2"}],"t":"Vanadium hexacarbonyl","f":"V(CO)₆","fig":{"m":2229},"ex":"Covalent count: V (group 5) 5 e; 6 × CO (L) 12 e: 17 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is V(0), d⁵; d⁵ + 12 e from the ligands = 17 e. One of the few stable 17-electron complexes; it is easily reduced to [V(CO)₆]⁻.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of V?","o":[{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁴"},{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁷"}],"t":"Vanadium hexacarbonyl","f":"V(CO)₆","fig":{"m":2229},"ex":"Covalent count: V (group 5) 5 e; 6 × CO (L) 12 e: 17 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is V(0), d⁵; d⁵ + 12 e from the ligands = 17 e. One of the few stable 17-electron complexes; it is easily reduced to [V(CO)₆]⁻.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Trigonal bipyramidal"},{"x":"Square planar"}],"t":"Vanadium hexacarbonyl","f":"V(CO)₆","fig":{"m":2229},"ex":"Octahedral. Covalent count: V (group 5) 5 e; 6 × CO (L) 12 e: 17 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is V(0), d⁵; d⁵ + 12 e from the ligands = 17 e. One of the few stable 17-electron complexes; it is easily reduced to [V(CO)₆]⁻.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Co have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"16 e"}],"t":"Tetracarbonylcobaltate","f":"[Co(CO)₄]⁻","fig":{"m":2230},"ex":"Covalent count: Co (group 9) 9 e; 4 × CO (L) 8 e; the 1− charge adds 1 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 1− charge, so the metal is Co(−I), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 18 e. The negative charge adds one electron in the neutral count.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Co?","o":[{"x":"−1"},{"x":"−2"},{"x":"0"},{"x":"−3"}],"t":"Tetracarbonylcobaltate","f":"[Co(CO)₄]⁻","fig":{"m":2230},"ex":"Covalent count: Co (group 9) 9 e; 4 × CO (L) 8 e; the 1− charge adds 1 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 1− charge, so the metal is Co(−I), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 18 e. The negative charge adds one electron in the neutral count.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Co?","o":[{"x":"d¹⁰"},{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":