ount: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Co(I), d⁸; d⁸ + 10 e from the ligands = 18 e. The hydroformylation catalyst. H is an X ligand, so cobalt is Co(I).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Co?","o":[{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d¹⁰"}],"t":"Cobalt tetracarbonyl hydride","f":"HCo(CO)₄","fig":{"m":2231},"ex":"Covalent count: Co (group 9) 9 e; 1 × H (X) 1 e; 4 × CO (L) 8 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Co(I), d⁸; d⁸ + 10 e from the ligands = 18 e. The hydroformylation catalyst. H is an X ligand, so cobalt is Co(I).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Trigonal bipyramidal"},{"x":"Square pyramidal"},{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"}],"t":"Cobalt tetracarbonyl hydride","f":"HCo(CO)₄","fig":{"m":2231},"ex":"Trigonal bipyramidal. Covalent count: Co (group 9) 9 e; 1 × H (X) 1 e; 4 × CO (L) 8 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Co(I), d⁸; d⁸ + 10 e from the ligands = 18 e. The hydroformylation catalyst. H is an X ligand, so cobalt is Co(I).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Mn have per metal?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"16 e"}],"t":"Dimanganese decacarbonyl","f":"Mn₂(CO)₁₀","fig":{"m":2232},"ex":"18 electrons per metal; Mn(0), d⁷. Each Mn has 17 e from Mn + 5 CO; the Mn–Mn bond adds one more to each.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Mn?","o":[{"x":"0"},{"x":"−1"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+2"}],"t":"Dimanganese decacarbonyl","f":"Mn₂(CO)₁₀","fig":{"m":2232},"ex":"18 electrons per metal; Mn(0), d⁷. Each Mn has 17 e from Mn + 5 CO; the Mn–Mn bond adds one more to each.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Mn?","o":[{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d⁹"}],"t":"Dimanganese decacarbonyl","f":"Mn₂(CO)₁₀","fig":{"m":2232},"ex":"18 electrons per metal; Mn(0), d⁷. Each Mn has 17 e from Mn + 5 CO; the Mn–Mn bond adds one more to each.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Co have per metal?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Dicobalt octacarbonyl","f":"Co₂(CO)₈","fig":{"m":2233},"ex":"18 electrons per metal; Co(0), d⁹. Two bridging CO and a Co–Co bond.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Co?","o":[{"x":"0"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"−1"},{"x":"−2"}],"t":"Dicobalt octacarbonyl","f":"Co₂(CO)₈","fig":{"m":2233},"ex":"18 electrons per metal; Co(0), d⁹. Two bridging CO and a Co–Co bond.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Co?","o":[{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d¹⁰"},{"x":"d⁷"}],"t":"Dicobalt octacarbonyl","f":"Co₂(CO)₈","fig":{"m":2233},"ex":"18 electrons per metal; Co(0), d⁹. Two bridging CO and a Co–Co bond.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Fe have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Collman's reagent","f":"Na₂[Fe(CO)₄]","fig":{"m":2234},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 4 × CO (L) 8 e; the 2− charge adds 2 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 2− charge, so the metal is Fe(−II), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 18 e. A carbonyl dianion: iron is Fe(−II), d¹⁰.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Fe?","o":[{"x":"−2"},{"x":"−3"},{"x":"−1"},{"x":"0"}],"t":"Collman's reagent","f":"Na₂[Fe(CO)₄]","fig":{"m":2234},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 4 × CO (L) 8 e; the 2− charge adds 2 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 2− charge, so the metal is Fe(−II), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 18 e. A carbonyl dianion: iron is Fe(−II), d¹⁰.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Fe?","o":[{"x":"d¹⁰"},{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Collman's reagent","f":"Na₂[Fe(CO)₄]","fig":{"m":2234},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 4 × CO (L) 8 e; the 2− charge adds 2 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 2− charge, so the metal is Fe(−II), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 18 e. A carbonyl dianion: iron is Fe(−II), d¹⁰.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Tetrahedral"},{"x":"Square planar"},{"x":"Linear"},{"x":"Trigonal bipyramidal"}],"t":"Collman's reagent","f":"Na₂[Fe(CO)₄]","fig":{"m":2234},"ex":"Tetrahedral. Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 4 × CO (L) 8 e; the 2− charge adds 2 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 2− charge, so the metal is Fe(−II), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 18 e. A carbonyl dianion: iron is Fe(−II), d¹⁰.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Fe have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Ferrocene","f":"Cp₂Fe","fig":{"m":2235},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Fe(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Fe?","o":[{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"0"}],"t":"Ferrocene","f":"Cp₂Fe","fig":{"m":2235},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Fe(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Fe?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁴"}],"t":"Ferrocene","f":"Cp₂Fe","fig":{"m":2235},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Fe(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Co have?","o":[{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"20 e"},{"x":"17 e"}],"t":"Cobaltocene","f":"Cp₂Co","fig":{"m":2236},"ex":"Covalent count: Co (group 9) 9 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e: 19 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Co(II), d⁷; d⁷ + 12 e from the ligands = 19 e. 19 electrons: cobaltocene is a strong reductant that readily loses one.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Co?","o":[{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+4"}],"t":"Cobaltocene","f":"Cp₂Co","fig":{"m":2236},"ex":"Covalent count: Co (group 9) 9 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e: 19 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Co(II), d⁷; d⁷ + 12 e from the ligands = 19 e. 19 electrons: cobaltocene is a strong reductant that readily loses one.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Co?","o":[{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁵"}],"t":"Cobaltocene","f":"Cp₂Co","fig":{"m":2236},"ex":"Covalent count: Co (group 9) 9 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e: 19 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Co(II), d⁷; d⁷ + 12 e from the ligands = 19 e. 19 electrons: cobaltocene is a strong reductant that readily loses one.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Co have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Cobaltocenium","f":"[Cp₂Co]⁺","fig":{"m":2236},"ex":"Covalent count: Co (group 9) 9 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e; the 1+ charge removes 1 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex has a 1+ charge, so the metal is Co(III), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Co?","o":[{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+4"},{"x":"+5"}],"t":"Cobaltocenium","f":"[Cp₂Co]⁺","fig":{"m":2236},"ex":"Covalent count: Co (group 9) 9 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e; the 1+ charge removes 1 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex has a 1+ charge, so the metal is Co(III), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Co?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Cobaltocenium","f":"[Cp₂Co]⁺","fig":{"m":2236},"ex":"Covalent count: Co (group 9) 9 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e; the 1+ charge removes 1 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex has a 1+ charge, so the metal is Co(III), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Cr have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"16 e"}],"t":"Benzene chromium tricarbonyl","f":"(η⁶-C₆H₆)Cr(CO)₃","fig":{"m":2237},"ex":"Covalent count: Cr (group 6) 6 e; 1 × η⁶-C₆H₆ (L₃) 6 e; 3 × CO (L) 6 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Cr(0), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Cr?","o":[{"x":"0"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"−1"},{"x":"+2"}],"t":"Benzene chromium tricarbonyl","f":"(η⁶-C₆H₆)Cr(CO)₃","fig":{"m":2237},"ex":"Covalent count: Cr (group 6) 6 e; 1 × η⁶-C₆H₆ (L₃) 6 e; 3 × CO (L) 6 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Cr(0), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Cr?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁴"}],"t":"Benzene chromium tricarbonyl","f":"(η⁶-C₆H₆)Cr(CO)₃","fig":{"m":2237},"ex":"Covalent count: Cr (group 6) 6 e; 1 × η⁶-C₆H₆ (L₃) 6 e; 3 × CO (L) 6 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Cr(0), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Mn have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Cymantrene","f":"CpMn(CO)₃","fig":{"m":2238},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × Cp (L₂X) 5 e; 3 × CO (L) 6 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Mn?","o":[{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+2"},{"x":"0"},{"x":"+3"}],"t":"Cymantrene","f":"CpMn(CO)₃","fig":{"m":2238},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × Cp (L₂X) 5 e; 3 × CO (L) 6 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Mn?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁴"}],"t":"Cymantrene","f":"CpMn(CO)₃","fig":{"m":2238},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × Cp (L₂X) 5 e; 3 × CO (L) 6 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Mn have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Methylmanganese pentacarbonyl","f":"MeMn(CO)₅","fig":{"m":2239},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × Me (X) 1 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. The classic substrate for CO migratory insertion.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Mn?","o":[{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+2"},{"x":"0"},{"x":"+3"}],"t":"Methylmanganese pentacarbonyl","f":"MeMn(CO)₅","fig":{"m":2239},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × Me (X) 1 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. The classic substrate for CO migratory insertion.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Mn?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Methylmanganese pentacarbonyl","f":"MeMn(CO)₅","fig":{"m":2239},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × Me (X) 1 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. The classic substrate for CO migratory insertion.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Square pyramidal"},{"x":"Tetrahedral"}],"t":"Methylmanganese pentacarbonyl","f":"MeMn(CO)₅","fig":{"m":2239},"ex":"Octahedral. Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × Me (X) 1 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. The classic substrate for CO migratory insertion.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Rh have?","o":[{"x":"16 e"},{"x":"15 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"14 e"}],"t":"Wilkinson's catalyst","f":"RhCl(PPh₃)₃","fig":{"m":2240},"ex":"Covalent count: Rh (group 9) 9 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e; 3 × PPh₃ (L) 6 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Rh(I), d⁸; d⁸ + 8 e from the ligands = 16 e. Square-planar d⁸: 16 electrons is the stable count here.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Rh?","o":[{"x":"+1"},{"x":"0"},{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+3"}],"t":"Wilkinson's catalyst","f":"RhCl(PPh₃)₃","fig":{"m":2240},"ex":"Covalent count: Rh (group 9) 9 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e; 3 × PPh₃ (L) 6 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Rh(I), d⁸; d⁸ + 8 e from the ligands = 16 e. Square-planar d⁸: 16 electrons is the stable count here.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Rh?","o":[{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d¹⁰"}],"t":"Wilkinson's catalyst","f":"RhCl(PPh₃)₃","fig":{"m":2240},"ex":"Covalent count: Rh (group 9) 9 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e; 3 × PPh₃ (L) 6 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Rh(I), d⁸; d⁸ + 8 e from the ligands = 16 e. Square-planar d⁸: 16 electrons is the stable count here.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Square planar"},{"x":"Tetrahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal bipyramidal"},{"x":"Linear"}],"t":"Wilkinson's catalyst","f":"RhCl(PPh₃)₃","fig":{"m":2240},"ex":"Square planar. Covalent count: Rh (group 9) 9 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e; 3 × PPh₃ (L) 6 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Rh(I), d⁸; d⁸ + 8 e from the ligands = 16 e. Square-planar d⁸: 16 electrons is the stable count here.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ir have?","o":[{"x":"16 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"15 e"},{"x":"14 e"}],"t":"Vaska's complex","f":"trans-IrCl(CO)(PPh₃)₂","fig":{"m":2241},"ex":"Covalent count: Ir (group 9) 9 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e; 2 × PPh₃ (L) 4 e; 1 × CO (L) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ir(I), d⁸; d⁸ + 8 e from the ligands = 16 e. Adds H₂, O₂ or MeI by oxidative addition to give 18-electron Ir(III).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ir?","o":[{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+2"},{"x":"0"},{"x":"+3"}],"t":"Vaska's complex","f":"trans-IrCl(CO)(PPh₃)₂","fig":{"m":2241},"ex":"Covalent count: Ir (group 9) 9 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e; 2 × PPh₃ (L) 4 e; 1 × CO (L) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ir(I), d⁸; d⁸ + 8 e from the ligands = 16 e. Adds H₂, O₂ or MeI by oxidative addition to give 18-electron Ir(III).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ir?","o":[{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁶"}],"t":"Vaska's complex","f":"trans-IrCl(CO)(PPh₃)₂","fig":{"m":2241},"ex":"Covalent count: Ir (group 9) 9 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e; 2 × PPh₃ (L) 4 e; 1 × CO (L) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ir(I), d⁸; d⁸ + 8 e from the ligands = 16 e. Adds H₂, O₂ or MeI by oxidative addition to give 18-electron Ir(III).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Square planar"},{"x":"Tetrahedral"},{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal bipyramidal"}],"t":"Vaska's complex","f":"trans-IrCl(CO)(PPh₃)₂","fig":{"m":2241},"ex":"Square planar. Covalent count: Ir (group 9) 9 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e; 2 × PPh₃ (L) 4 e; 1 × CO (L) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ir(I), d⁸; d⁸ + 8 e from the ligands = 16 e. Adds H₂, O₂ or MeI by oxidative addition to give 18-electron Ir(III).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Pt have?","o":[{"x":"16 e"},{"x":"15 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"18 e"}],"t":"Zeise's salt","f":"K[PtCl₃(η²-C₂H₄)]","fig":{"m":2242},"ex":"Covalent count: Pt (group 10) 10 e; 3 × Cl (X) 3 e; 1 × η²-C₂H₄ (L) 2 e; the 1− charge adds 1 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −3 in all and the complex has a 1− charge, so the metal is Pt(II), d⁸; d⁸ + 8 e from the ligands = 16 e. The first organometallic complex (1827). Ethylene is a 2-electron L ligand.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Pt?","o":[{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+4"}],"t":"Zeise's salt","f":"K[PtCl₃(η²-C₂H₄)]","fig":{"m":2242},"ex":"Covalent count: Pt (group 10) 10 e; 3 × Cl (X) 3 e; 1 × η²-C₂H₄ (L) 2 e; the 1− charge adds 1 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −3 in all and the complex has a 1− charge, so the metal is Pt(II), d⁸; d⁸ + 8 e from the ligands = 16 e. The first organometallic complex (1827). Ethylene is a 2-electron L ligand.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Pt?","o":[{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁶"}],"t":"Zeise's salt","f":"K[PtCl₃(η²-C₂H₄)]","fig":{"m":2242},"ex":"Covalent count: Pt (group 10) 10 e; 3 × Cl (X) 3 e; 1 × η²-C₂H₄ (L) 2 e; the 1− charge adds 1 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −3 in all and the complex has a 1− charge, so the metal is Pt(II), d⁸; d⁸ + 8 e from the ligands = 16 e. The first organometallic complex (1827). Ethylene is a 2-electron L ligand.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Square planar"},{"x":"Tetrahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal bipyramidal"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"}],"t":"Zeise's salt","f":"K[PtCl₃(η²-C₂H₄)]","fig":{"m":2242},"ex":"Square planar. Covalent count: Pt (group 10) 10 e; 3 × Cl (X) 3 e; 1 × η²-C₂H₄ (L) 2 e; the 1− charge adds 1 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −3 in all and the complex has a 1− charge, so the metal is Pt(II), d⁸; d