1/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Pd?","o":[{"x":"d¹⁰"},{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Tetrakis(triphenylphosphine)palladium","f":"Pd(PPh₃)₄","fig":{"m":2246},"ex":"Covalent count: Pd (group 10) 10 e; 4 × PPh₃ (L) 8 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Pd(0), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 18 e. \"Tetrakis\". In solution it loses PPh₃ to give the reactive 14- and 16-electron species.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Tetrahedral"},{"x":"Square planar"},{"x":"Trigonal bipyramidal"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"}],"t":"Tetrakis(triphenylphosphine)palladium","f":"Pd(PPh₃)₄","fig":{"m":2246},"ex":"Tetrahedral. Covalent count: Pd (group 10) 10 e; 4 × PPh₃ (L) 8 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Pd(0), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 18 e. \"Tetrakis\". In solution it loses PPh₃ to give the reactive 14- and 16-electron species.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ni have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"16 e"}],"t":"Bis(cyclooctadiene)nickel","f":"Ni(cod)₂","fig":{"m":2247},"ex":"Covalent count: Ni (group 10) 10 e; 2 × cod (L₂) 8 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ni(0), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ni?","o":[{"x":"0"},{"x":"−1"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+2"}],"t":"Bis(cyclooctadiene)nickel","f":"Ni(cod)₂","fig":{"m":2247},"ex":"Covalent count: Ni (grou