hloride","f":"Cp₂TiCl₂","fig":{"m":2248},"ex":"Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e; 2 × Cl (X) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 16 e from the ligands = 16 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ti?","o":[{"x":"d⁰"},{"x":"d¹"},{"x":"d²"},{"x":"d³"}],"t":"Titanocene dichloride","f":"Cp₂TiCl₂","fig":{"m":2248},"ex":"Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e; 2 × Cl (X) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 16 e from the ligands = 16 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Zr have?","o":[{"x":"16 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"15 e"},{"x":"18 e"}],"t":"Zirconocene dichloride","f":"Cp₂ZrCl₂","fig":{"m":2249},"ex":"Covalent count: Zr (group 4) 4 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e; 2 × Cl (X) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Zr(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 16 e from the ligands = 16 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Zr?","o":[{"x":"+4"},{"x":"+5"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+2"}],"t":"Zirconocene dichloride","f":"Cp₂ZrCl₂","fig":{"m":2249},"ex":"Covalent count: Zr (group 4) 4 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e; 2 × Cl (X) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Zr(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 16 e from the ligands = 16 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Zr?","o":[{"x":"d⁰"},{"x":"d¹"},{"x":"d²"},{"x":"d³"}],"t":"Zirconocene dichloride","f":"Cp₂ZrCl₂","fig":{"m":2249},"ex":"Covalent count: Zr (group 4) 4 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e; 2 × Cl (X) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Zr(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 16 e from the ligands = 16 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ru have?","o":[{"x":"16 e"},{"x":"15 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"18 e"}],"t":"Grubbs catalyst, 1st generation","f":"RuCl₂(PCy₃)₂(=CHPh)","fig":{"m":2250},"ex":"Covalent count: Ru (group 8) 8 e; 1 × =CHPh (L) 2 e; 2 × PCy₃ (L) 4 e; 2 × Cl (X) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ru(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 10 e from the ligands = 16 e. The benzylidene is counted as a neutral 2-electron carbene, so ruthenium is Ru(II).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ru?","o":[{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+4"}],"t":"Grubbs catalyst, 1st generation","f":"RuCl₂(PCy₃)₂(=CHPh)","fig":{"m":2250},"ex":"Covalent count: Ru (group 8) 8 e; 1 × =CHPh (L) 2 e; 2 × PCy₃ (L) 4 e; 2 × Cl (X) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ru(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 10 e from the ligands = 16 e. The benzylidene is counted as a neutral 2-electron carbene, so ruthenium is Ru(II).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ru?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Grubbs catalyst, 1st generation","f":"RuCl₂(PCy₃)₂(=CHPh)","fig":{"m":2250},"ex":"Covalent count: Ru (group 8) 8 e; 1 × =CHPh (L) 2 e; 2 × PCy₃ (L) 4 e; 2 × Cl (X) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ru(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 10 e from the ligands = 16 e. The benzylidene is counted as a neutral 2-electron carbene, so ruthenium is Ru(II).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Square pyramidal"},{"x":"Trigonal bipyramidal"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Octahedral"}],"t":"Grubbs catalyst, 1st generation","f":"RuCl₂(PCy₃)₂(=CHPh)","fig":{"m":2250},"ex":"Square pyramidal. Covalent count: Ru (group 8) 8 e; 1 × =CHPh (L) 2 e; 2 × PCy₃ (L) 4 e; 2 × Cl (X) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ru(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 10 e from the ligands = 16 e. The benzylidene is counted as a neutral 2-electron carbene, so ruthenium is Ru(II).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does W have?","o":[{"x":"12 e"},{"x":"13 e"},{"x":"11 e"},{"x":"14 e"}],"t":"Hexamethyltungsten","f":"WMe₆","fig":{"m":2251},"ex":"Covalent count: W (group 6) 6 e; 6 × Me (X) 6 e: 12 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is W(VI), d⁰; d⁰ + 12 e from the ligands = 12 e. Only 12 electrons, yet stable: d⁰, and no easy decomposition path. It is a trigonal prism, not an octahedron: with no π-donor ligands, a d⁰ ML₆ prefers the prism.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of W?","o":[{"x":"+6"},{"x":"+5"},{"x":"+7"},{"x":"+4"}],"t":"Hexamethyltungsten","f":"WMe₆","fig":{"m":2251},"ex":"Covalent count: W (group 6) 6 e; 6 × Me (X) 6 e: 12 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is W(VI), d⁰; d⁰ + 12 e from the ligands = 12 e. Only 12 electrons, yet stable: d⁰, and no easy decomposition path. It is a trigonal prism, not an octahedron: with no π-donor ligands, a d⁰ ML₆ prefers the prism.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of W?","o":[{"x":"d⁰"},{"x":"d¹"},{"x":"d²"},{"x":"d³"}],"t":"Hexamethyltungsten","f":"WMe₆","fig":{"m":2251},"ex":"Covalent count: W (group 6) 6 e; 6 × Me (X) 6 e: 12 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is W(VI), d⁰; d⁰ + 12 e from the ligands = 12 e. Only 12 electrons, yet stable: d⁰, and no easy decomposition path. It is a trigonal prism, not an octahedron: with no π-donor ligands, a d⁰ ML₆ prefers the prism.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Square pyramidal"},{"x":"Tetrahedral"}],"t":"Hexamethyltungsten","f":"WMe₆","fig":{"m":2251},"ex":"Trigonal prismatic. Covalent count: W (group 6) 6 e; 6 × Me (X) 6 e: 12 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is W(VI), d⁰; d⁰ + 12 e from the ligands = 12 e. Only 12 electrons, yet stable: d⁰, and no easy decomposition path. It is a trigonal prism, not an octahedron: with no π-donor ligands, a d⁰ ML₆ prefers the prism.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Co have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Hexaamminecobalt(III)","f":"[Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺","fig":{"m":2252},"ex":"Covalent count: Co (group 9) 9 e; 6 × NH₃ (L) 12 e; the 3+ charge removes 3 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 3+ charge, so the metal is Co(III), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Co?","o":[{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+4"},{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+1"}],"t":"Hexaamminecobalt(III)","f":"[Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺","fig":{"m":2252},"ex":"Covalent count: Co (group 9) 9 e; 6 × NH₃ (L) 12 e; the 3+ charge removes 3 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 3+ charge, so the metal is Co(III), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Co?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Hexaamminecobalt(III)","f":"[Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺","fig":{"m":2252},"ex":"Covalent count: Co (group 9) 9 e; 6 × NH₃ (L) 12 e; the 3+ charge removes 3 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 3+ charge, so the metal is Co(III), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Square pyramidal"},{"x":"Tetrahedral"}],"t":"Hexaamminecobalt(III)","f":"[Co(NH₃)₆]³⁺","fig":{"m":2252},"ex":"Octahedral. Covalent count: Co (group 9) 9 e; 6 × NH₃ (L) 12 e; the 3+ charge removes 3 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 3+ charge, so the metal is Co(III), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Fe have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Hexacyanoferrate(II)","f":"[Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻","fig":{"m":2253},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 6 × CN (X) 6 e; the 4− charge adds 4 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex has a 4− charge, so the metal is Fe(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. Ferrocyanide.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Fe?","o":[{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+4"}],"t":"Hexacyanoferrate(II)","f":"[Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻","fig":{"m":2253},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 6 × CN (X) 6 e; the 4− charge adds 4 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex has a 4− charge, so the metal is Fe(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. Ferrocyanide.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Fe?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Hexacyanoferrate(II)","f":"[Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻","fig":{"m":2253},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 6 × CN (X) 6 e; the 4− charge adds 4 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex has a 4− charge, so the metal is Fe(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. Ferrocyanide.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Tetrahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal planar"}],"t":"Hexacyanoferrate(II)","f":"[Fe(CN)₆]⁴⁻","fig":{"m":2253},"ex":"Octahedral. Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 6 × CN (X) 6 e; the 4− charge adds 4 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex has a 4− charge, so the metal is Fe(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. Ferrocyanide.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Fe have?","o":[{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"16 e"},{"x":"19 e"}],"t":"Hexacyanoferrate(III)","f":"[Fe(CN)₆]³⁻","fig":{"m":2253},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 6 × CN (X) 6 e; the 3− charge adds 3 e: 17 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex has a 3− charge, so the metal is Fe(III), d⁵; d⁵ + 12 e from the ligands = 17 e. Ferricyanide.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Fe?","o":[{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+4"},{"x":"+5"}],"t":"Hexacyanoferrate(III)","f":"[Fe(CN)₆]³⁻","fig":{"m":2253},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 6 × CN (X) 6 e; the 3− charge adds 3 e: 17 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex has a 3− charge, so the metal is Fe(III), d⁵; d⁵ + 12 e from the ligands = 17 e. Ferricyanide.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Fe?","o":[{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁴"},{"x":"d³"}],"t":"Hexacyanoferrate(III)","f":"[Fe(CN)₆]³⁻","fig":{"m":2253},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 6 × CN (X) 6 e; the 3− charge adds 3 e: 17 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex has a 3− charge, so the metal is Fe(III), d⁵; d⁵ + 12 e from the ligands = 17 e. Ferricyanide.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Trigonal planar"},{"x":"Trigonal bipyramidal"}],"t":"Hexacyanoferrate(III)","f":"[Fe(CN)₆]³⁻","fig":{"m":2253},"ex":"Octahedral. Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 6 × CN (X) 6 e; the 3− charge adds 3 e: 17 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex has a 3− charge, so the metal is Fe(III), d⁵; d⁵ + 12 e from the ligands = 17 e. Ferricyanide.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ti have?","o":[{"x":"13 e"},{"x":"14 e"},{"x":"12 e"},{"x":"11 e"}],"t":"Hexaaquatitanium(III)","f":"[Ti(H₂O)₆]³⁺","fig":{"m":2254},"ex":"Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 6 × H₂O (L) 12 e; the 3+ charge removes 3 e: 13 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 3+ charge, so the metal is Ti(III), d¹; d¹ + 12 e from the ligands = 13 e. Coordination complexes of early metals are often far below 18 electrons.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ti?","o":[{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+4"},{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+5"}],"t":"Hexaaquatitanium(III)","f":"[Ti(H₂O)₆]³⁺","fig":{"m":2254},"ex":"Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 6 × H₂O (L) 12 e; the 3+ charge removes 3 e: 13 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 3+ charge, so the metal is Ti(III), d¹; d¹ + 12 e from the ligands = 13 e. Coordination complexes of early metals are often far below 18 electrons.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ti?","o":[{"x":"d¹"},{"x":"d⁰"},{"x":"d²"},{"x":"d³"}],"t":"Hexaaquatitanium(III)","f":"[Ti(H₂O)₆]³⁺","fig":{"m":2254},"ex":"Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 6 × H₂O (L) 12 e; the 3+ charge removes 3 e: 13 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 3+ charge, so the metal is Ti(III), d¹; d¹ + 12 e from the ligands = 13 e. Coordination complexes of early metals are often far below 18 electrons.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Tetrahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal bipyramidal"}],"t":"Hexaaquatitanium(III)","f":"[Ti(H₂O)₆]³⁺","fig":{"m":2254},"ex":"Octahedral. Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 6 × H₂O (L) 12 e; the 3+ charge removes 3 e: 13 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 3+ charge, so the metal is Ti(III), d¹; d¹ + 12 e from the ligands = 13 e. Coordination complexes of early metals are often far below 18 electrons.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Cu have?","o":[{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"16 e"},{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"15 e"}],"t":"Tetraamminecopper(II)","f":"[Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺","fig":{"m":2255},"ex":"Covalent count: Cu (group 11) 11 e; 4 × NH₃ (L) 8 e; the 2+ charge removes 2 e: 17 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 2+ charge, so the metal is Cu(II), d⁹; d⁹ + 8 e from the ligands = 17 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Cu?","o":[{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+4"}],"t":"Tetraamminecopper(II)","f":"[Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺","fig":{"m":2255},"ex":"Covalent count: Cu (group 11) 11 e; 4 × NH₃ (L) 8 e; the 2+ charge removes 2 e: 17 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 2+ charge, so the metal is Cu(II), d⁹; d⁹ + 8 e from the ligands = 17 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Cu?","o":[{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d¹⁰"},{"x":"d⁷"}],"t":"Tetraamminecopper(II)","f":"[Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺","fig":{"m":2255},"ex":"Covalent count: Cu (group 11) 11 e; 4 × NH₃ (L) 8 e; the 2+ charge removes 2 e: 17 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 2+ charge, so the metal is Cu(II), d⁹; d⁹ + 8 e from the ligands = 17 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Square planar"},{"x":"Tetrahedral"},{"x":"Linear"},{"x":"Trigonal bipyramidal"}],"t":"Tetraamminecopper(II)","f":"[Cu(NH₃)₄]²⁺","fig":{"m":2255},"ex":"Square planar. Covalent count: Cu (group 11) 11 e; 4 × NH₃ (L) 8 e; the 2+ charge removes 2 e: 17 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 2+ charge, so the metal is Cu(II), d⁹; d⁹ + 8 e from the ligands = 17 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Ligand electrons","a":"How many electrons does this ligand give in the covalent (neutral) count?","o":[{"x":"2 e"},{"x":"3 e"},{"x":"1 e"},{"x":"4 e"}],"t":"carbonyl","f":"CO","fig":{"m":2256},"ex":"CO is an L ligand: 2 electrons in the covalent count, 2 in the ionic count.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Ligand class","a":"What is its L/X class?","o":[{"x":"L"},{"x":"X₂"},{"x":"L₂X₂"},{"x":"LX"}],"t":"carbonyl","f":"CO","fig":{"m":2256},"ex":"CO (carbonyl) is an L ligand: each L gives 2 electrons and each X one, so 2 electrons in the covalent count (2 in the ionic coun