al is Re(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Tetrahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal bipyramidal"}],"t":"Hexacarbonylrhenium(I)","f":"[Re(CO)₆]⁺","fig":{"m":2306},"ex":"Octahedral. Covalent count: Re (group 7) 7 e; 6 × CO (L) 12 e; the 1+ charge removes 1 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 1+ charge, so the metal is Re(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Mn have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Pentacarbonylmanganate","f":"[Mn(CO)₅]⁻","fig":{"m":2307},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e; the 1− charge adds 1 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 1− charge, so the metal is Mn(−I), d⁸; d⁸ + 10 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Mn?","o":[{"x":"−1"},{"x":"0"},{"x":"−2"},{"x":"−3"}],"t":"Pentacarbonylmanganate","f":"[Mn(CO)₅]⁻","fig":{"m":2307},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e; the 1− charge adds 1 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 1− charge, so the metal is Mn(−I), d⁸; d⁸ + 10 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Mn?","o":[{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁶"}],"t":"Pentacarbonylmanganate","f":"[Mn(CO)₅]⁻","fig":{"m":2307},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e; the 1− charge adds 1 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 1− charge, so the metal is Mn(−I), d⁸; d⁸ + 10 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Trigonal bipyramidal"},{"x":"Square pyramidal"},{"x":"Trigonal planar"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"}],"t":"Pentacarbonylmanganate","f":"[Mn(CO)₅]⁻","fig":{"m":2307},"ex":"Trigonal bipyramidal. Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e; the 1− charge adds 1 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 1− charge, so the metal is Mn(−I), d⁸; d⁸ + 10 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Cr have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Pentacarbonylchromate","f":"[Cr(CO)₅]²⁻","fig":{"m":2308},"ex":"Covalent count: Cr (group 6) 6 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e; the 2− charge adds 2 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 2− charge, so the metal is Cr(−II), d⁸; d⁸ + 10 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Cr?","o":[{"x":"−2"},{"x":"−1"},{"x":"−3"},{"x":"0"}],"t":"Pentacarbonylchromate","f":"[Cr(CO)₅]²⁻","fig":{"m":2308},"ex":"Covalent count: Cr (group 6) 6 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e; the 2− charge adds 2 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 2− charge, so the metal is Cr(−II), d⁸; d⁸ + 10 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Cr?","o":[{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁶"}],"t":"Pentacarbonylchromate","f":"[Cr(CO)₅]²⁻","fig":{"m":2308},"ex":"Covalent count: Cr (group 6) 6 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e; the 2− charge adds 2 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 2− charge, so the metal is Cr(−II), d⁸; d⁸ + 10 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Trigonal bipyramidal"},{"x":"Square pyramidal"},{"x":"Tetrahedral"},{"x":"Linear"}],"t":"Pentacarbonylchromate","f":"[Cr(CO)₅]²⁻","fig":{"m":2308},"ex":"Trigonal bipyramidal. Covalent count: Cr (group 6) 6 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e; the 2− charge adds 2 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 2− charge, so the metal is Cr(−II), d⁸; d⁸ + 10 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Mn have?","o":[{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"16 e"},{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"15 e"}],"t":"Pentacarbonylmanganese radical","f":"•Mn(CO)₅","fig":{"m":2309},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 17 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(0), d⁷; d⁷ + 10 e from the ligands = 17 e. Made by breaking the Mn–Mn bond of Mn₂(CO)₁₀ with light; it dimerises back.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Mn?","o":[{"x":"0"},{"x":"−1"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+2"}],"t":"Pentacarbonylmanganese radical","f":"•Mn(CO)₅","fig":{"m":2309},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 17 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(0), d⁷; d⁷ + 10 e from the ligands = 17 e. Made by breaking the Mn–Mn bond of Mn₂(CO)₁₀ with light; it dimerises back.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Mn?","o":[{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d⁹"}],"t":"Pentacarbonylmanganese radical","f":"•Mn(CO)₅","fig":{"m":2309},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 17 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(0), d⁷; d⁷ + 10 e from the ligands = 17 e. Made by breaking the Mn–Mn bond of Mn₂(CO)₁₀ with light; it dimerises back.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Square pyramidal"},{"x":"Trigonal bipyramidal"},{"x":"Linear"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"}],"t":"Pentacarbonylmanganese radical","f":"•Mn(CO)₅","fig":{"m":2309},"ex":"Square pyramidal. Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 17 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(0), d⁷; d⁷ + 10 e from the ligands = 17 e. Made by breaking the Mn–Mn bond of Mn₂(CO)₁₀ with light; it dimerises back.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Re have per metal?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Dirhenium decacarbonyl","f":"Re₂(CO)₁₀","fig":{"m":2310},"ex":"18 electrons per metal; Re(0), d⁷.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Re?","o":[{"x":"0"},{"x":"−1"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"−2"}],"t":"Dirhenium decacarbonyl","f":"Re₂(CO)₁₀","fig":{"m":2310},"ex":"18 electrons per metal; Re(0), d⁷.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Re?","o":[{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d⁹"}],"t":"Dirhenium decacarbonyl","f":"Re₂(CO)₁₀","fig":{"m":2310},"ex":"18 electrons per metal; Re(0), d⁷.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Fe have per metal?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"16 e"}],"t":"Diiron nonacarbonyl","f":"Fe₂(CO)₉","ex":"18 electrons per metal; Fe(0), d⁸. Three bridging CO; the Fe–Fe bond is needed to reach 18.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Fe?","o":[{"x":"0"},{"x":"−1"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"−2"}],"t":"Diiron nonacarbonyl","f":"Fe₂(CO)₉","ex":"18 electrons per metal; Fe(0), d⁸. Three bridging CO; the Fe–Fe bond is needed to reach 18.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Fe?","o":[{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁶"}],"t":"Diiron nonacarbonyl","f":"Fe₂(CO)₉","ex":"18 electrons per metal; Fe(0), d⁸. Three bridging CO; the Fe–Fe bond is needed to reach 18.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Fe have per metal?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"16 e"}],"t":"Triiron dodecacarbonyl","f":"Fe₃(CO)₁₂","ex":"18 electrons per metal; Fe(0), d⁸. A triangle of three Fe–Fe bonds; two CO bridge one edge.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Fe?","o":[{"x":"0"},{"x":"−1"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+2"}],"t":"Triiron dodecacarbonyl","f":"Fe₃(CO)₁₂","ex":"18 electrons per metal; Fe(0), d⁸. A triangle of three Fe–Fe bonds; two CO bridge one edge.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Fe?","o":[{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d¹⁰"}],"t":"Triiron dodecacarbonyl","f":"Fe₃(CO)₁₂","ex":"18 electrons per metal; Fe(0), d⁸. A triangle of three Fe–Fe bonds; two CO bridge one edge.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ru have per metal?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"16 e"}],"t":"Triruthenium dodecacarbonyl","f":"Ru₃(CO)₁₂","fig":{"m":2311},"ex":"18 electrons per metal; Ru(0), d⁸. Each Ru: 8 + 4 CO + 2 Ru–Ru bonds = 18.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ru?","o":[{"x":"0"},{"x":"−1"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"−2"}],"t":"Triruthenium dodecacarbonyl","f":"Ru₃(CO)₁₂","fig":{"m":2311},"ex":"18 electrons per metal; Ru(0), d⁸. Each Ru: 8 + 4 CO + 2 Ru–Ru bonds = 18.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ru?","o":[{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁶"}],"t":"Triruthenium dodecacarbonyl","f":"Ru₃(CO)₁₂","fig":{"m":2311},"ex":"18 electrons per metal; Ru(0), d⁸. Each Ru: 8 + 4 CO + 2 Ru–Ru bonds = 18.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Os have per metal?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Triosmium dodecacarbonyl","f":"Os₃(CO)₁₂","fig":{"m":2312},"ex":"18 electrons per metal; Os(0), d⁸.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Os?","o":[{"x":"0"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"−1"},{"x":"+2"}],"t":"Triosmium dodecacarbonyl","f":"Os₃(CO)₁₂","fig":{"m":2312},"ex":"18 electrons per metal; Os(0), d⁸.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Os?","o":[{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d¹⁰"}],"t":"Triosmium dodecacarbonyl","f":"Os₃(CO)₁₂","fig":{"m":2312},"ex":"18 electrons per metal; Os(0), d⁸.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ir have per metal?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Tetrairidium dodecacarbonyl","f":"Ir₄(CO)₁₂","ex":"18 electrons per metal; Ir(0), d⁹. A tetrahedron: each Ir has three CO and three Ir–Ir bonds.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ir?","o":[{"x":"0"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"−1"},{"x":"+2"}],"t":"Tetrairidium dodecacarbonyl","f":"Ir₄(CO)₁₂","ex":"18 electrons per metal; Ir(0), d⁹. A tetrahedron: each Ir has three CO and three Ir–Ir bonds.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ir?","o":[{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d¹⁰"},{"x":"d⁷"}],"t":"Tetrairidium dodecacarbonyl","f":"Ir₄(CO)₁₂","ex":"18 electrons per metal; Ir(0), d⁹. A tetrahedron: each Ir has three CO and three Ir–Ir bonds.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Mn have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Pentacarbonylmanganese hydride","f":"HMn(CO)₅","fig":{"m":2313},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × H (X) 1 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Mn?","o":[{"x":"+1"},{"x":"0"},{"x":"+2"},{"x":"−1"}],"t":"Pentacarbonylmanganese hydride","f":"HMn(CO)₅","fig":{"m":2313},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × H (X) 1 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Mn?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Pentacarbonylmanganese hydride","f":"HMn(CO)₅","fig":{"m":2313},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × H (X) 1 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Linear"},{"x":"Trigonal planar"}],"t":"Pentacarbonylmanganese hydride","f":"HMn(CO)₅","fig":{"m":2313},"ex":"Octahedral. Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × H (X) 1 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Fe have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Iron tetracarbonyl dihydride","f":"H₂Fe(CO)₄","fig":{"m":2314},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 2 × H (X) 2 e; 4 × CO (L) 8 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Fe(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Fe?","o":[{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+4"}],"t":"Iron tetracarbonyl dihydride","f":"H₂Fe(CO)₄","fig":{"m":2314},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 2 × H (X) 2 e; 4 × CO (L) 8 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Fe(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Fe?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Iron tetracarbonyl dihydride","f":"H₂Fe(CO)₄","fig":{"m":2314},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 2 × H (X) 2 e; 4 × CO (L) 8 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Fe(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Linear"},{"x":"Trigonal bipyramidal"}],"t":"Iron tetracarbonyl dihydride","f":"H₂Fe(CO)₄","fig":{"m":2314},"ex":"Octahedral. Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 2 × H (X) 2 e; 4 × CO (L) 8 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Fe(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Fe have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"16 e"}],"t":"Pentacarbonylhydridoiron(II)","f":"[HFe(CO)₅]⁺","fig":{"m":2315},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 1 × H (X) 1 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e; the 1+ charge removes 1 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex has a 1+ charge, so the metal is Fe(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. Fe(CO)₅ protonated on the metal: protonation is an oxidation of the metal by two.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Fe?","o":[{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"0"}],"t":"Pentacarbonylhydridoiron(II)","f":"[HFe(CO)₅]⁺","fig":{"m":2315},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 1 × H (X) 1 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e; the 1+ charge removes 1 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex has a 1+ charge, so the metal is Fe(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. Fe(CO)₅ protonated on the metal: protonation is an oxidation of the metal by two.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Fe?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Pentacarbonylhydridoiron(II)","f":"[HFe(CO)₅]⁺","fig":{"m":2315},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 1 × H (X) 1 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e; the 1+ charge removes 1 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex has a 1+ charge, so the metal is Fe(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. Fe(CO)₅ protonated on the metal: protonation is an oxidation of the metal by two.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Square planar"},{"x":"Linear"}],"t":"Pentacarbonylhydridoiron(II)","f":"[HFe(CO)₅]⁺","fig":{"m":2315},"ex":"Octahedral. Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 1 × H (X) 1 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e; the 1+ charge removes 1 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex has a 1+ charge, so the metal is Fe(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. Fe(CO)₅ protonated on the metal: protonation is an oxidation of the metal by two.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Mn have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Bromopentacarbonylmanganese","f":"Mn(CO)₅Br","fig":{"m":2316},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × Br (X) 1 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Mn?","o":[{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+2"},{"x":"0"},{"x":"−1"}],"t":"Bromopentacarbonylmanganese","f":"Mn(CO)₅Br","fig":{"m":2316},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × Br (X) 1 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Mn?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Bromopentacarbonylmanganese","f":"Mn(CO)₅Br","fig":{"m":2316},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × Br (X) 1 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Tetrahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal planar"}],"t":"Bromopentacarbonylmanganese","f":"Mn(CO)₅Br","fig":{"m":2316},"ex":"Octahedral. Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × Br (X) 1 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Mn have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"16 e"}],"t":"Acetylmanganese pentacarbonyl","f":"MeC(O)Mn(CO)₅","fig":{"m":2317},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × C(O)Me (X) 1 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. Product of CO insertion into MeMn(CO)₅ (after a CO adds back).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Mn?","o":[{"x":"+1"},{"x":"0"},{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+3"}],"t":"Acetylmanganese pentacarbonyl","f":"MeC(O)Mn(CO)₅","fig":{"m":2317},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × C(O)Me (X) 1 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. Product of CO insertion into MeMn(CO)₅ (after a CO adds back).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Mn?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁴"}],"t":"Acetylmanganese pentacarbonyl","f":"MeC(O)Mn(CO)₅","fig":{"m":2317},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × C(O)Me (X) 1 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. Product of CO insertion into MeMn(CO)₅ (after a CO adds back).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Trigonal planar"},{"x":"Square planar"}],"t":"Acetylmanganese pentacarbonyl","f":"MeC(O)Mn(CO)₅","fig":{"m":2317},"ex":"Octahedral. Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × C(O)Me (X) 1 e; 5 × CO (L) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. Product of CO insertion into MeMn(CO)₅ (after a CO adds back).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Mn have?","o":[{"x":"16 e"},{"x":"15 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"14 e"}],"t":"Acetylmanganese tetracarbonyl","f":"MeC(O)Mn(CO)₄","fig":{"m":2318},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × C(O)Me (X) 1 e; 4 × CO (L) 8 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 10 e from the ligands = 16 e. The 16-electron intermediate right after migratory insertion; it picks up a ligand to return to 18.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Mn?","o":[{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+2"},{"x":"0"},{"x":"+3"}],"t":"Acetylmanganese tetracarbonyl","f":"MeC(O)Mn(CO)₄","fig":{"m":2318},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × C(O)Me (X) 1 e; 4 × CO (L) 8 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 10 e from the ligands = 16 e. The 16-electron intermediate right after migratory insertion; it picks up a ligand to return to 18.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Mn?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Acetylmanganese tetracarbonyl","f":"MeC(O)Mn(CO)₄","fig":{"m":2318},"ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × C(O)Me (X) 1 e; 4 × CO (L) 8 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 10 e from the ligands = 16 e. The 16-electron intermediate right after migratory insertion; it picks up a ligand to return to 18.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x"