","fig":{"m":2324},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 2 × NO (LX) 6 e; 2 × CO (L) 4 e: 18 e. Linear NO: 3 e as a neutral ligand, or NO⁺ (2 e) in the ionic count. The oxidation state depends on that choice. Isoelectronic with Ni(CO)₄.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Tetrahedral"},{"x":"Square planar"},{"x":"Square pyramidal"},{"x":"Trigonal planar"}],"t":"Dicarbonyldinitrosyliron","f":"Fe(CO)₂(NO)₂","fig":{"m":2324},"ex":"Tetrahedral. Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 2 × NO (LX) 6 e; 2 × CO (L) 4 e: 18 e. Linear NO: 3 e as a neutral ligand, or NO⁺ (2 e) in the ionic count. The oxidation state depends on that choice. Isoelectronic with Ni(CO)₄.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Co have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Tricarbonylnitrosylcobalt","f":"Co(CO)₃(NO)","fig":{"m":2325},"ex":"Covalent count: Co (group 9) 9 e; 1 × NO (LX) 3 e; 3 × CO (L) 6 e: 18 e. Linear NO: 3 e as a neutral ligand, or NO⁺ (2 e) in the ionic count. The oxidation state depends on that choice.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Tetrahedral"},{"x":"Square planar"},{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Linear"}],"t":"Tricarbonylnitrosylcobalt","f":"Co(CO)₃(NO)","fig":{"m":2325},"ex":"Tetrahedral. Covalent count: Co (group 9) 9 e; 1 × NO (LX) 3 e; 3 × CO (L) 6 e: 18 e. Linear NO: 3 e as a neutral ligand, or NO⁺ (2 e) in the ionic count. The oxidation state depends on that choice.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Co have per metal?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Dicobalt hexacarbonyl alkyne complex","f":"Co₂(CO)₆(μ-PhC≡CPh)","ex":"18 electrons per metal; Co(0), d⁹. The alkyne gives two electrons to each Co, one π bond each. Used in the Nicholas and Pauson–Khand reactions.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Co?","o":[{"x":"0"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"−1"},{"x":"−2"}],"t":"Dicobalt hexacarbonyl alkyne complex","f":"Co₂(CO)₆(μ-PhC≡CPh)","ex":"18 electrons per metal; Co(0), d⁹. The alkyne gives two electrons to each Co, one π bond each. Used in the Nicholas and Pauson–Khand reactions.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Co?","o":[{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d¹⁰"},{"x":"d⁷"}],"t":"Dicobalt hexacarbonyl alkyne complex","f":"Co₂(CO)₆(μ-PhC≡CPh)","ex":"18 electrons per metal; Co(0), d⁹. The alkyne gives two electrons to each Co, one π bond each. Used in the Nicholas and Pauson–Khand reactions.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ru have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Ruthenocene","f":"Cp₂Ru","fig":{"m":2326},"ex":"Covalent count: Ru (group 8) 8 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ru(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ru?","o":[{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"0"}],"t":"Ruthenocene","f":"Cp₂Ru","fig":{"m":2326},"ex":"Covalent count: Ru (group 8) 8 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ru(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ru?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Ruthenocene","f":"Cp₂Ru","fig":{"m":2326},"ex":"Covalent count: Ru (group 8) 8 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ru(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Fe have?","o":[{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"16 e"},{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"15 e"}],"t":"Ferrocenium","f":"[Cp₂Fe]⁺","fig":{"m":2235},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e; the 1+ charge removes 1 e: 17 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex has a 1+ charge, so the metal is Fe(III), d⁵; d⁵ + 12 e from the ligands = 17 e. Ferrocene loses one electron easily and reversibly: the reference couple in electrochemistry.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Fe?","o":[{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+4"},{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+5"}],"t":"Ferrocenium","f":"[Cp₂Fe]⁺","fig":{"m":2235},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e; the 1+ charge removes 1 e: 17 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex has a 1+ charge, so the metal is Fe(III), d⁵; d⁵ + 12 e from the ligands = 17 e. Ferrocene loses one electron easily and reversibly: the reference couple in electrochemistry.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Fe?","o":[{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁴"},{"x":"d³"}],"t":"Ferrocenium","f":"[Cp₂Fe]⁺","fig":{"m":2235},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e; the 1+ charge removes 1 e: 17 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex has a 1+ charge, so the metal is Fe(III), d⁵; d⁵ + 12 e from the ligands = 17 e. Ferrocene loses one electron easily and reversibly: the reference couple in electrochemistry.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ni have?","o":[{"x":"20 e"},{"x":"21 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"18 e"}],"t":"Nickelocene","f":"Cp₂Ni","fig":{"m":2327},"ex":"Covalent count: Ni (group 10) 10 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e: 20 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ni(II), d⁸; d⁸ + 12 e from the ligands = 20 e. 20 electrons, two of them in antibonding orbitals: nickelocene is paramagnetic and reactive.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ni?","o":[{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"0"}],"t":"Nickelocene","f":"Cp₂Ni","fig":{"m":2327},"ex":"Covalent count: Ni (group 10) 10 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e: 20 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ni(II), d⁸; d⁸ + 12 e from the ligands = 20 e. 20 electrons, two of them in antibonding orbitals: nickelocene is paramagnetic and reactive.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"