) 1 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −3 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Re(III), d⁴; d⁴ + 14 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Fe have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Butadiene iron tricarbonyl","f":"(η⁴-C₄H₆)Fe(CO)₃","fig":{"m":2346},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 1 × η⁴-C₄H₆ (L₂) 4 e; 3 × CO (L) 6 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Fe(0), d⁸; d⁸ + 10 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Fe?","o":[{"x":"0"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"−1"},{"x":"+2"}],"t":"Butadiene iron tricarbonyl","f":"(η⁴-C₄H₆)Fe(CO)₃","fig":{"m":2346},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 1 × η⁴-C₄H₆ (L₂) 4 e; 3 × CO (L) 6 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Fe(0), d⁸; d⁸ + 10 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Fe?","o":[{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d¹⁰"}],"t":"Butadiene iron tricarbonyl","f":"(η⁴-C₄H₆)Fe(CO)₃","fig":{"m":2346},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 1 × η⁴-C₄H₆ (L₂) 4 e; 3 × CO (L) 6 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Fe(0), d⁸; d⁸ + 10 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Fe have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":