x":"d¹⁰"}],"t":"Allylpalladium chloride dimer","f":"[(η³-C₃H₅)PdCl]₂","fig":{"m":2350},"ex":"16 electrons per metal; Pd(II), d⁸. Each Pd: allyl (3) + one Cl as X (1) + the other Cl as L (2) + Pd 10 = 16.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ti have?","o":[{"x":"12 e"},{"x":"11 e"},{"x":"13 e"},{"x":"10 e"}],"t":"Cyclopentadienyltitanium trichloride","f":"CpTiCl₃","fig":{"m":2351},"ex":"Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 1 × Cp (L₂X) 5 e; 3 × Cl (X) 3 e: 12 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 12 e from the ligands = 12 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ti?","o":[{"x":"+4"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+5"},{"x":"+6"}],"t":"Cyclopentadienyltitanium trichloride","f":"CpTiCl₃","fig":{"m":2351},"ex":"Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 1 × Cp (L₂X) 5 e; 3 × Cl (X) 3 e: 12 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 12 e from the ligands = 12 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ti?","o":[{"x":"d⁰"},{"x":"d¹"},{"x":"d²"},{"x":"d³"}],"t":"Cyclopentadienyltitanium trichloride","f":"CpTiCl₃","fig":{"m":2351},"ex":"Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 1 × Cp (L₂X) 5 e; 3 × Cl (X) 3 e: 12 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 12 e from the ligands = 12 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"Ho