he complex has a 1+ charge, so the metal is Zr(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 14 e from the ligands = 14 e. The active species in metallocene olefin polymerisation: 14 electrons and an open site.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Zr?","o":[{"x":"+4"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+5"},{"x":"+2"}],"t":"Zirconocene methyl cation","f":"[Cp₂ZrMe]⁺","fig":{"m":2353},"ex":"Covalent count: Zr (group 4) 4 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e; 1 × Me (X) 1 e; the 1+ charge removes 1 e: 14 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −3 in all and the complex has a 1+ charge, so the metal is Zr(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 14 e from the ligands = 14 e. The active species in metallocene olefin polymerisation: 14 electrons and an open site.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Zr?","o":[{"x":"d⁰"},{"x":"d¹"},{"x":"d²"},{"x":"d³"}],"t":"Zirconocene methyl cation","f":"[Cp₂ZrMe]⁺","fig":{"m":2353},"ex":"Covalent count: Zr (group 4) 4 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e; 1 × Me (X) 1 e; the 1+ charge removes 1 e: 14 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −3 in all and the complex has a 1+ charge, so the metal is Zr(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 14 e from the ligands = 14 e. The active species in metallocene olefin polymerisation: 14 electrons and an open site.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Zr have?","o":[{"x":"16 e"},{"x":"15 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"18 e"}],"t":"Zirconocene dimethyl","f":"Cp₂ZrMe₂","fig":{"m":2354},"ex":"Covalent count: Zr (group 4) 4 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e; 2 × Me (X) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Zr(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 16 e from the ligands = 16 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Zr?","o":[{"x":"+4"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+5"},{"x":"+2"}],"t":"Zirconocene dimethyl","f":"Cp₂ZrMe₂","fig":{"m":2354},"ex":"Covalent count: Zr (group 4) 4 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e; 2 × Me (X) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Zr(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 16 e from the ligands = 16 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Zr?","o":[{"x":"d⁰"},{"x":"d¹"},{"x":"d²"},{"x":"d³"}],"t":"Zirconocene dimethyl","f":"Cp₂ZrMe₂","fig":{"m":2354},"ex":"Covalent count: Zr (group 4) 4 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e; 2 × Me (X) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Zr(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 16 e from the ligands = 16 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ti have?","o":[{"x":"15 e"},{"x":"16 e"},{"x":"14 e"},{"x":"17 e"}],"t":"Nugent–RajanBabu reagent","f":"Cp₂TiCl","fig":{"m":2355},"ex":"Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e: 15 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −3 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(III), d¹; d¹ + 14 e from the ligands = 15 e. Ti(III), d¹: a one-electron reductant that opens epoxides to radicals.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ti?","o":[{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+4"},{"x":"+5"}],"t":"Nugent–RajanBabu reagent","f":"Cp₂TiCl","fig":{"m":2355},"ex":"Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e: 15 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −3 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(III), d¹; d¹ + 14 e from the ligands = 15 e. Ti(III), d¹: a one-electron reductant that opens epoxides to radicals.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ti?","o":[{"x":"d¹"},{"x":"d⁰"},{"x":"d²"},{"x":"d³"}],"t":"Nugent–RajanBabu reagent","f":"Cp₂TiCl","fig":{"m":2355},"ex":"Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e: 15 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −3 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(III), d¹; d¹ + 14 e from the ligands = 15 e. Ti(III), d¹: a one-electron reductant that opens epoxides to radicals.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Rh have per metal?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"16 e"}],"t":"Pentamethylcyclopentadienylrhodium dichloride dimer","f":"[Cp*RhCl₂]₂","fig":{"m":2356},"ex":"18 electrons per metal; Rh(III), d⁶. Each Rh: Rh 9 + Cp* 5 + Cl 1 + the bridging Cl pair 3 = 18.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Rh?","o":[{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+4"},{"x":"+5"}],"t":"Pentamethylcyclopentadienylrhodium dichloride dimer","f":"[Cp*RhCl₂]₂","fig":{"m":2356},"ex":"18 electrons per metal; Rh(III), d⁶. Each Rh: Rh 9 + Cp* 5 + Cl 1 + the bridging Cl pair 3 = 18.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Rh?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁴"}],"t":"Pentamethylcyclopentadienylrhodium dichloride dimer","f":"[Cp*RhCl₂]₂","fig":{"m":2356},"ex":"18 electrons per metal; Rh(III), d⁶. Each Rh: Rh 9 + Cp* 5 + Cl 1 + the bridging Cl pair 3 = 18.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ru have per metal?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"16 e"}],"t":"Dichloro(p-cymene)ruthenium dimer","f":"[RuCl₂(p-cymene)]₂","fig":{"m":2357},"ex":"18 electrons per metal; Ru(II), d⁶.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ru?","o":[{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+4"}],"t":"Dichloro(p-cymene)ruthenium dimer","f":"[RuCl₂(p-cymene)]₂","fig":{"m":2357},"ex":"18 electrons per metal; Ru(II), d⁶.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ru?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁴"}],"t":"Dichloro(p-cymene)ruthenium dimer","f":"[RuCl₂(p-cymene)]₂","fig":{"m":2357},"ex":"18 electrons per metal; Ru(II), d⁶.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ru have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Noyori transfer-hydrogenation catalyst","f":"RuCl(p-cymene)(TsDPEN)","fig":{"m":2358},"ex":"Covalent count: Ru (group 8) 8 e; 1 × η⁶-p-cymene (L₃) 6 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e; 1 × TsDPEN (LX) 3 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ru(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. TsDPEN binds through an amine (L) and a sulfonamide nitrogen (X).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ru?","o":[{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"0"}],"t":"Noyori transfer-hydrogenation catalyst","f":"RuCl(p-cymene)(TsDPEN)","fig":{"m":2358},"ex":"Covalent count: Ru (group 8) 8 e; 1 × η⁶-p-cymene (L₃) 6 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e; 1 × TsDPEN (LX) 3 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ru(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. TsDPEN binds through an amine (L) and a sulfonamide nitrogen (X).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ru?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Noyori transfer-hydrogenation catalyst","f":"RuCl(p-cymene)(TsDPEN)","fig":{"m":2358},"ex":"Covalent count: Ru (group 8) 8 e; 1 × η⁶-p-cymene (L₃) 6 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e; 1 × TsDPEN (LX) 3 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ru(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. TsDPEN binds through an amine (L) and a sulfonamide nitrogen (X).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ru have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Pentamethylcyclopentadienylruthenium chloride cod","f":"Cp*RuCl(cod)","fig":{"m":2359},"ex":"Covalent count: Ru (group 8) 8 e; 1 × Cp* (L₂X) 5 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e; 1 × cod (L₂) 4 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ru(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ru?","o":[{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"0"}],"t":"Pentamethylcyclopentadienylruthenium chloride cod","f":"Cp*RuCl(cod)","fig":{"m":2359},"ex":"Covalent count: Ru (group 8) 8 e; 1 × Cp* (L₂X) 5 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e; 1 × cod (L₂) 4 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ru(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ru?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Pentamethylcyclopentadienylruthenium chloride cod","f":"Cp*RuCl(cod)","fig":{"m":2359},"ex":"Covalent count: Ru (group 8) 8 e; 1 × Cp* (L₂X) 5 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e; 1 × cod (L₂) 4 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ru(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ir have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"16 e"}],"t":"Bergman's iridium dihydride","f":"Cp*Ir(PMe₃)H₂","fig":{"m":2360},"ex":"Covalent count: Ir (group 9) 9 e; 1 × Cp* (L₂X) 5 e; 1 × PMe₃ (L) 2 e; 2 × H (X) 2 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −3 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ir(III), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. With light it loses H₂ to give 16-electron Cp*Ir(PMe₃), which breaks C–H bonds of alkanes.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ir?","o":[{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+4"},{"x":"+1"}],"t":"Bergman's iridium dihydride","f":"Cp*Ir(PMe₃)H₂","fig":{"m":2360},"ex":"Covalent count: Ir (group 9) 9 e; 1 × Cp* (L₂X) 5 e; 1 × PMe₃ (L) 2 e; 2 × H (X) 2 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −3 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ir(III), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. With light it loses H₂ to give 16-electron Cp*Ir(PMe₃), which breaks C–H bonds of alkanes.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ir?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁴"}],"t":"Bergman's iridium dihydride","f":"Cp*Ir(PMe₃)H₂","fig":{"m":2360},"ex":"Covalent count: Ir (group 9) 9 e; 1 × Cp* (L₂X) 5 e; 1 × PMe₃ (L) 2 e; 2 × H (X) 2 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −3 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ir(III), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. With light it loses H₂ to give 16-electron Cp*Ir(PMe₃), which breaks C–H bonds of alkanes.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Mn have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"16 e"}],"t":"Trispyrazolylborate manganese tricarbonyl","f":"TpMn(CO)₃","ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × Tp (L₂X) 5 e; 3 × CO (L) 6 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. Tp⁻ is a 6-electron, facially binding analogue of Cp⁻.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Mn?","o":[{"x":"+1"},{"x":"0"},{"x":"+2"},{"x":"−1"}],"t":"Trispyrazolylborate manganese tricarbonyl","f":"TpMn(CO)₃","ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × Tp (L₂X) 5 e; 3 × CO (L) 6 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. Tp⁻ is a 6-electron, facially binding analogue of Cp⁻.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Mn?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Trispyrazolylborate manganese tricarbonyl","f":"TpMn(CO)₃","ex":"Covalent count: Mn (group 7) 7 e; 1 × Tp (L₂X) 5 e; 3 × CO (L) 6 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mn(I), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. Tp⁻ is a 6-electron, facially binding analogue of Cp⁻.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Pd have?","o":[{"x":"16 e"},{"x":"15 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"14 e"}],"t":"Tetrachloropalladate","f":"[PdCl₄]²⁻","fig":{"m":2361},"ex":"Covalent count: Pd (group 10) 10 e; 4 × Cl (X) 4 e; the 2− charge adds 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex has a 2− charge, so the metal is Pd(II), d⁸; d⁸ + 8 e from the ligands = 16 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Pd?","o":[{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"0"}],"t":"Tetrachloropalladate","f":"[PdCl₄]²⁻","fig":{"m":2361},"ex":"Covalent count: Pd (group 10) 10 e; 4 × Cl (X) 4 e; the 2− charge adds 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex has a 2− charge, so the metal is Pd(II), d⁸; d⁸ + 8 e from the ligands = 16 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]