Pd(II), d⁸; d⁸ + 8 e from the ligands = 16 e. Pd(0) + PhBr: oxidative addition raises the oxidation state by two and the count from 14 to 16.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Pd?","o":[{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+4"}],"t":"Oxidative addition product","f":"trans-PdPh(Br)(PPh₃)₂","fig":{"m":2364},"ex":"Covalent count: Pd (group 10) 10 e; 1 × Ph (X) 1 e; 2 × PPh₃ (L) 4 e; 1 × Br (X) 1 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Pd(II), d⁸; d⁸ + 8 e from the ligands = 16 e. Pd(0) + PhBr: oxidative addition raises the oxidation state by two and the count from 14 to 16.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Pd?","o":[{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d¹⁰"}],"t":"Oxidative addition product","f":"trans-PdPh(Br)(PPh₃)₂","fig":{"m":2364},"ex":"Covalent count: Pd (group 10) 10 e; 1 × Ph (X) 1 e; 2 × PPh₃ (L) 4 e; 1 × Br (X) 1 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Pd(II), d⁸; d⁸ + 8 e from the ligands = 16 e. Pd(0) + PhBr: oxidative addition raises the oxidation state by two and the count from 14 to 16.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Square planar"},{"x":"Tetrahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal planar"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"}],"t":"Oxidative addition product","f":"trans-PdPh(Br)(PPh₃)₂","fig":{"m":2364},"ex":"Square planar. Covalent count: Pd (group 10) 10 e; 1 × Ph (X) 1 e; 2 × PPh₃ (L) 4 e; 1 × Br (X) 1 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Pd(II), d⁸; d⁸ + 8 e from the ligands = 16 e. Pd(0) + PhBr: oxidative addition raises the oxidation state by two and the count from 14 to 16.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Pd have?","o":[{"x":"14 e"},{"x":"13 e"},{"x":"15 e"},{"x":"12 e"}],"t":"Bis(tri-tert-butylphosphine)palladium","f":"Pd(Pt-Bu₃)₂","fig":{"m":2365},"ex":"Covalent count: Pd (group 10) 10 e; 2 × P(t-Bu)₃ (L) 4 e: 14 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Pd(0), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 4 e from the ligands = 14 e. Bulky phosphines keep Pd(0) two-coordinate and very reactive.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Pd?","o":[{"x":"0"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"−1"},{"x":"+2"}],"t":"Bis(tri-tert-butylphosphine)palladium","f":"Pd(Pt-Bu₃)₂","fig":{"m":2365},"ex":"Covalent count: Pd (group 10) 10 e; 2 × P(t-Bu)₃ (L) 4 e: 14 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Pd(0), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 4 e from the ligands = 14 e. Bulky phosphines keep Pd(0) two-coordinate and very reactive.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Pd?","o":[{"x":"d¹⁰"},{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Bis(tri-tert-butylphosphine)palladium","f":"Pd(Pt-Bu₃)₂","fig":{"m":2365},"ex":"Covalent count: Pd (group 10) 10 e; 2 × P(t-Bu)₃ (L) 4 e: 14 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Pd(0), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 4 e from the ligands = 14 e. Bulky phosphines keep Pd(0) two-coordinate and very reactive.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","http