ld","f":"Au(PPh₃)Cl","fig":{"m":2391},"ex":"Covalent count: Au (group 11) 11 e; 1 × PPh₃ (L) 2 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e: 14 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Au(I), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 4 e from the ligands = 14 e. Linear d¹⁰ Au(I): 14 electrons is normal for gold(I).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Au?","o":[{"x":"+1"},{"x":"0"},{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+3"}],"t":"Chloro(triphenylphosphine)gold","f":"Au(PPh₃)Cl","fig":{"m":2391},"ex":"Covalent count: Au (group 11) 11 e; 1 × PPh₃ (L) 2 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e: 14 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Au(I), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 4 e from the ligands = 14 e. Linear d¹⁰ Au(I): 14 electrons is normal for gold(I).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Au?","o":[{"x":"d¹⁰"},{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Chloro(triphenylphosphine)gold","f":"Au(PPh₃)Cl","fig":{"m":2391},"ex":"Covalent count: Au (group 11) 11 e; 1 × PPh₃ (L) 2 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e: 14 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Au(I), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 4 e from the ligands = 14 e. Linear d¹⁰ Au(I): 14 electrons is normal for gold(I).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Linear"},{"x":"Trigonal planar"},{"x":"Square planar"},{"x":"Octahedral"}],"t":"Chloro(triphenylphosphine)gold","f":"Au(PPh₃)Cl","fig":{"m":2391},"ex":"Linear. Covalent count: Au (group 11) 11 e; 1 × PPh₃ (L) 2 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e: 14 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −1 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Au(I), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 4 e from the ligands = 14 e. Linear d¹⁰ Au(I): 14 electrons is normal for gold(I).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Au have?","o":[{"x":"14 e"},{"x":"13 e"},{"x":"15 e"},{"x":"12 e"}],"t":"Dicyanoaurate","f":"[Au(CN)₂]⁻","fig":{"m":2392},"ex":"Covalent count: Au (group 11) 11 e; 2 × CN (X) 2 e; the 1− charge adds 1 e: 14 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex has a 1− charge, so the metal is Au(I), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 4 e from the ligands = 14 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Au?","o":[{"x":"+1"},{"x":"0"},{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+3"}],"t":"Dicyanoaurate","f":"[Au(CN)₂]⁻","fig":{"m":2392},"ex":"Covalent count: Au (group 11) 11 e; 2 × CN (X) 2 e; the 1− charge adds 1 e: 14 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex has a 1− charge, so the metal is Au(I), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 4 e from the ligands = 14 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Au?","o":[{"x":"d¹⁰"},{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Dicyanoaurate","f":"[Au(CN)₂]⁻","fig":{"m":2392},"ex":"Covalent count: Au (group 11) 11 e; 2 × CN (X) 2 e; the 1− charge adds 1 e: 14 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex has a 1− charge, so the metal is Au(I), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 4 e from the ligands = 14 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Linear"},{"x":"Trigonal planar"},{"x":"Square pyramidal"},{"x":"Trigonal bipyramidal"}],"t":"Dicyanoaurate","f":"[Au(CN)₂]⁻","fig":{"m":2392},"ex":"Linear. Covalent count: Au (group 11) 11 e; 2 × CN (X) 2 e; the 1− charge adds 1 e: 14 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex has a 1− charge, so the metal is Au(I), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 4 e from the ligands = 14 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ag have?","o":[{"x":"14 e"},{"x":"13 e"},{"x":"15 e"},{"x":"16 e"}],"t":"Diamminesilver","f":"[Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺","fig":{"m":2393},"ex":"Covalent count: Ag (group 11) 11 e; 2 × NH₃ (L) 4 e; the 1+ charge removes 1 e: 14 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 1+ charge, so the metal is Ag(I), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 4 e from the ligands = 14 e. Tollens' reagent.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ag?","o":[{"x":"+1"},{"x":"0"},{"x":"+2"},{"x":"−1"}],"t":"Diamminesilver","f":"[Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺","fig":{"m":2393},"ex":"Covalent count: Ag (group 11) 11 e; 2 × NH₃ (L) 4 e; the 1+ charge removes 1 e: 14 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 1+ charge, so the metal is Ag(I), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 4 e from the ligands = 14 e. Tollens' reagent.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ag?","o":[{"x":"d¹⁰"},{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Diamminesilver","f":"[Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺","fig":{"m":2393},"ex":"Covalent count: Ag (group 11) 11 e; 2 × NH₃ (L) 4 e; the 1+ charge removes 1 e: 14 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 1+ charge, so the metal is Ag(I), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 4 e from the ligands = 14 e. Tollens' reagent.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807",