 metal is Re(VII), d⁰; d⁰ + 18 e from the ligands = 18 e. Nine hydrides, Re(VII), d⁰: a coordination number of 9.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Re?","o":[{"x":"d⁰"},{"x":"d¹"},{"x":"d²"},{"x":"d³"}],"t":"Nonahydridorhenate","f":"[ReH₉]²⁻","ex":"Covalent count: Re (group 7) 7 e; 9 × H (X) 9 e; the 2− charge adds 2 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −9 in all and the complex has a 2− charge, so the metal is Re(VII), d⁰; d⁰ + 18 e from the ligands = 18 e. Nine hydrides, Re(VII), d⁰: a coordination number of 9.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does W have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"16 e"}],"t":"Hexahydridotris(trimethylphosphine)tungsten","f":"WH₆(PMe₃)₃","ex":"Covalent count: W (group 6) 6 e; 6 × H (X) 6 e; 3 × PMe₃ (L) 6 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is W(VI), d⁰; d⁰ + 18 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of W?","o":[{"x":"+6"},{"x":"+7"},{"x":"+5"},{"x":"+4"}],"t":"Hexahydridotris(trimethylphosphine)tungsten","f":"WH₆(PMe₃)₃","ex":"Covalent count: W (group 6) 6 e; 6 × H (X) 6 e; 3 × PMe₃ (L) 6 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is W(VI), d⁰; d⁰ + 18 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of W?","o":[{"x":"d⁰"},{"x":"d¹"},{"x":"d²"},{"x":"d³"}],"t":"Hexahydridotris(trimethylphosphine)tungsten","f":"WH₆(PMe₃)₃","ex":"Covalent count: W (group 6) 6 e; 6 × H (X) 6 e; 3 × PMe₃ (L) 6 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is W(VI), d⁰; d⁰ + 18 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Fe have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Hexahydridoferrate","f":"[FeH₆]⁴⁻","fig":{"m":2396},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 6 × H (X) 6 e; the 4− charge adds 4 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex has a 4− charge, so the metal is Fe(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. As in Mg₂FeH₆, a hydrogen-storage material.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Fe?","o":[{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"0"}],"t":"Hexahydridoferrate","f":"[FeH₆]⁴⁻","fig":{"m":2396},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 6 × H (X) 6 e; the 4− charge adds 4 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex has a 4− charge, so the metal is Fe(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. As in Mg₂FeH₆, a hydrogen-storage material.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Fe?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Hexahydridoferrate","f":"[FeH₆]⁴⁻","fig":{"m":2396},"ex":"Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 6 × H (X) 6 e; the 4− charge adds 4 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex has a 4− charge, so the metal is Fe(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. As in Mg₂FeH₆, a hydrogen-storage material.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Linear"},{"x":"Tetrahedral"}],"t":"Hexahydridoferrate","f":"[FeH₆]⁴⁻","fig":{"m":2396},"ex":"Octahedral. Covalent count: Fe (group 8) 8 e; 6 × H (X) 6 e; the 4− charge adds 4 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex has a 4− charge, so the metal is Fe(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. As in Mg₂FeH₆, a hydrogen-storage material.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does W have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"16 e"}],"t":"Kubas' complex","f":"W(CO)₃(P(i-Pr)₃)₂(η²-H₂)","fig":{"m":2397},"ex":"Covalent count: W (group 6) 6 e; 2 × P(i-Pr)₃ (L) 4 e; 1 × η²-H₂ (L) 2 e; 3 × CO (L) 6 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is W(0), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. The first σ-H₂ complex: H₂ binds intact as a 2-electron L ligand, so W stays W(0).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of W?","o":[{"x":"0"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"−1"},{"x":"+2"}],"t":"Kubas' complex","f":"W(CO)₃(P(i-Pr)₃)₂(η²-H₂)","fig":{"m":2397},"ex":"Covalent count: W (group 6) 6 e; 2 × P(i-Pr)₃ (L) 4 e; 1 × η²-H₂ (L) 2 e; 3 × CO (L) 6 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is W(0), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. The first σ-H₂ complex: H₂ binds intact as a 2-electron L ligand, so W stays W(0).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of W?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Kubas' complex","f":"W(CO)₃(P(i-Pr)₃)₂(η²-H₂)","fig":{"m":2397},"ex":"Covalent count: W (group 6) 6 e; 2 × P(i-Pr)₃ (L) 4 e; 1 × η²-H₂ (L) 2 e; 3 × CO (L) 6 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is W(0), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. The first σ-H₂ complex: H₂ binds intact as a 2-electron L ligand, so W stays W(0).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Square pyramidal"},{"x":"Square planar"}],"t":"Kubas' complex","f":"W(CO)₃(P(i-Pr)₃)₂(η²-H₂)","fig":{"m":2397},"ex":"Octahedral. Covalent count: W (group 6) 6 e; 2 × P(i-Pr)₃ (L) 4 e; 1 × η²-H₂ (L) 2 e; 3 × CO (L) 6 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex is neutral, so the metal is W(0), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. The first σ-H₂ complex: H₂ binds intact as a 2-electron L ligand, so W stays W(0).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ir have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Pentahydridobis(triisopropylphosphine)iridium","f":"IrH₅(P(i-Pr)₃)₂","ex":"Covalent count: Ir (group 9) 9 e; 5 × H (X) 5 e; 2 × P(i-Pr)₃ (L) 4 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −5 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ir(V), d⁴; d⁴ + 14 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ir?","o":[{"x":"+5"},{"x":"+4"},{"x":"+6"},{"x":"+3"}],"t":"Pentahydridobis(triisopropylphosphine)iridium","f":"IrH₅(P(i-Pr)₃)₂","ex":"Covalent count: Ir (group 9) 9 e; 5 × H (X) 5 e; 2 × P(i-Pr)₃ (L) 4 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −5 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ir(V), d⁴; d⁴ + 14 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ir?","o":[{"x":"d⁴"},{"x":"d³"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁶"}],"t":"Pentahydridobis(triisopropylphosphine)iridium","f":"IrH₅(P(i-Pr)₃)₂","ex":"Covalent count: Ir (group 9) 9 e; 5 × H (X) 5 e; 2 × P(i-Pr)₃ (L) 4 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −5 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ir(V), d⁴; d⁴ + 14 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ru have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Bis(dihydrogen)ruthenium dihydride","f":"RuH₂(η²-H₂)₂(PCy₃)₂","fig":{"m":2398},"ex":"Covalent count: Ru (group 8) 8 e; 2 × PCy₃ (L) 4 e; 2 × H (X) 2 e; 2 × η²-H₂ (L) 4 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ru(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. Two H are hydrides (X) and two H₂ are σ-complexes (L).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ru?","o":[{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"0"}],"t":"Bis(dihydrogen)ruthenium dihydride","f":"RuH₂(η²-H₂)₂(PCy₃)₂","fig":{"m":2398},"ex":"Covalent count: Ru (group 8) 8 e; 2 × PCy₃ (L) 4 e; 2 × H (X) 2 e; 2 × η²-H₂ (L) 4 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ru(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. Two H are hydrides (X) and two H₂ are σ-complexes (L).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ru?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁴"}],"t":"Bis(dihydrogen)ruthenium dihydride","f":"RuH₂(η²-H₂)₂(PCy₃)₂","fig":{"m":2398},"ex":"Covalent count: Ru (group 8) 8 e; 2 × PCy₃ (L) 4 e; 2 × H (X) 2 e; 2 × η²-H₂ (L) 4 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ru(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. Two H are hydrides (X) and two H₂ are σ-complexes (L).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Square planar"},{"x":"Tetrahedral"}],"t":"Bis(dihydrogen)ruthenium dihydride","f":"RuH₂(η²-H₂)₂(PCy₃)₂","fig":{"m":2398},"ex":"Octahedral. Covalent count: Ru (group 8) 8 e; 2 × PCy₃ (L) 4 e; 2 × H (X) 2 e; 2 × η²-H₂ (L) 4 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ru(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. Two H are hydrides (X) and two H₂ are σ-complexes (L).","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ir have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"16 e"}],"t":"Vaska's complex + H₂","f":"IrH₂Cl(CO)(PPh₃)₂","fig":{"m":2399},"ex":"Covalent count: Ir (group 9) 9 e; 2 × PPh₃ (L) 4 e; 2 × H (X) 2 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e; 1 × CO (L) 2 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −3 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ir(III), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. Oxidative addition of H₂: two new X ligands, Ir(