atic"},{"x":"Square planar"},{"x":"Square pyramidal"}],"t":"Vaska's complex + MeI","f":"IrMe(I)Cl(CO)(PPh₃)₂","fig":{"m":2400},"ex":"Octahedral. Covalent count: Ir (group 9) 9 e; 2 × PPh₃ (L) 4 e; 1 × Me (X) 1 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e; 1 × I (X) 1 e; 1 × CO (L) 2 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −3 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ir(III), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Rh have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"16 e"}],"t":"Monsanto oxidative addition product","f":"[RhMe(I)₃(CO)₂]⁻","fig":{"m":2401},"ex":"Covalent count: Rh (group 9) 9 e; 1 × Me (X) 1 e; 2 × CO (L) 4 e; 3 × I (X) 3 e; the 1− charge adds 1 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex has a 1− charge, so the metal is Rh(III), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Rh?","o":[{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+4"},{"x":"+1"}],"t":"Monsanto oxidative addition product","f":"[RhMe(I)₃(CO)₂]⁻","fig":{"m":2401},"ex":"Covalent count: Rh (group 9) 9 e; 1 × Me (X) 1 e; 2 × CO (L) 4 e; 3 × I (X) 3 e; the 1− charge adds 1 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex has a 1− charge, so the metal is Rh(III), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Rh?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Monsanto oxidative addition product","f":"[RhMe(I)₃(CO)₂]⁻","fig":{"m":2401},"ex":"Covalent count: Rh (group 9) 9 e; 1 × Me (X) 1 e; 2 × CO (L) 4 e; 3 × I (X) 3 e; the 1− charge adds 1 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex has a 1− charge, so the metal is Rh(III), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Trigonal planar"},{"x":"Tetrahedral"}],"t":"Monsanto oxidative addition product","f":"[RhMe(I)₃(CO)₂]⁻","fig":{"m":2401},"ex":"Octahedral. Covalent count: Rh (group 9) 9 e; 1 × Me (X) 1 e; 2 × CO (L) 4 e; 3 × I (X) 3 e; the 1− charge adds 1 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex has a 1− charge, so the metal is Rh(III), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ti have?","o":[{"x":"8 e"},{"x":"7 e"},{"x":"9 e"},{"x":"6 e"}],"t":"Titanium tetrachloride","f":"TiCl₄","fig":{"m":2402},"ex":"Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 4 × Cl (X) 4 e: 8 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 8 e. Only 8 electrons: a strong Lewis acid.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ti?","o":[{"x":"+4"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+5"},{"x":"+2"}],"t":"Titanium tetrachloride","f":"TiCl₄","fig":{"m":2402},"ex":"Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 4 × Cl (X) 4 e: 8 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 8 e. Only 8 electrons: a strong Lewis acid.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ti?","o":[{"x":"d⁰"},{"x":"d¹"},{"x":"d²"},{"x":"d³"}],"t":"Titanium tetrachloride","f":"TiCl₄","fig":{"m":2402},"ex":"Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 4 × Cl (X) 4 e: 8 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 8 e. Only 8 electrons: a strong Lewis acid.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Tetrahedral"},{"x":"Square planar"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Linear"}],"t":"Titanium tetrachloride","f":"TiCl₄","fig":{"m":2402},"ex":"Tetrahedral. Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 4 × Cl (X) 4 e: 8 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 8 e. Only 8 electrons: a strong Lewis acid.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ti have?","o":[{"x":"8 e"},{"x":"7 e"},{"x":"9 e"},{"x":"10 e"}],"t":"Tetrakis(dimethylamido)titanium","f":"Ti(NMe₂)₄","fig":{"m":2403},"ex":"Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 4 × NMe₂ (X) 4 e: 8 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 8 e. 8 electrons if the N lone pairs are not counted; π-donation makes up some of the gap.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ti?","o":[{"x":"+4"},{"x":"+5"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+2"}],"t":"Tetrakis(dimethylamido)titanium","f":"Ti(NMe₂)₄","fig":{"m":2403},"ex":"Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 4 × NMe₂ (X) 4 e: 8 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 8 e. 8 electrons if the N lone pairs are not counted; π-donation makes up some of the gap.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ti?","o":[{"x":"d⁰"},{"x":"d¹"},{"x":"d²"},{"x":"d³"}],"t":"Tetrakis(dimethylamido)titanium","f":"Ti(NMe₂)₄","fig":{"m":2403},"ex":"Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 4 × NMe₂ (X) 4 e: 8 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 8 e. 8 electrons if the N lone pairs are not counted; π-donation makes up some of the gap.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Tetrahedral"},{"x":"Square planar"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Square pyramidal"}],"t":"Tetrakis(dimethylamido)titanium","f":"Ti(NMe₂)₄","fig":{"m":2403},"ex":"Tetrahedral. Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 4 × NMe₂ (X) 4 e: 8 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 8 e. 8 electrons if the N lone pairs are not counted; π-donation makes up some of the gap.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ti have?","o":[{"x":"8 e"},{"x":"9 e"},{"x":"7 e"},{"x":"10 e"}],"t":"Titanium isopropoxide","f":"Ti(Oi-Pr)₄","fig":{"m":2404},"ex":"Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 4 × Oi-Pr (X) 4 e: 8 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 8 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ti?","o":[{"x":"+4"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+5"},{"x":"+2"}],"t":"Titanium isopropoxide","f":"Ti(Oi-Pr)₄","fig":{"m":2404},"ex":"Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 4 × Oi-Pr (X) 4 e: 8 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 8 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ti?","o":[{"x":"d⁰"},{"x":"d¹"},{"x":"d²"},{"x":"d³"}],"t":"Titanium isopropoxide","f":"Ti(Oi-Pr)₄","fig":{"m":2404},"ex":"Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 4 × Oi-Pr (X) 4 e: 8 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 8 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Tetrahedral"},{"x":"Square planar"},{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"}],"t":"Titanium isopropoxide","f":"Ti(Oi-Pr)₄","fig":{"m":2404},"ex":"Tetrahedral. Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 4 × Oi-Pr (X) 4 e: 8 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 8 e from the ligands = 8 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does W have?","o":[{"x":"12 e"},{"x":"13 e"},{"x":"11 e"},{"x":"14 e"}],"t":"Tungsten hexachloride","f":"WCl₆","fig":{"m":2405},"ex":"Covalent count: W (group 6) 6 e; 6 × Cl (X) 6 e: 12 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is W(VI), d⁰; d⁰ + 12 e from the ligands = 12 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of W?","o":[{"x":"+6"},{"x":"+7"},{"x":"+5"},{"x":"+4"}],"t":"Tungsten hexachloride","f":"WCl₆","fig":{"m":2405},"ex":"Covalent count: W (group 6) 6 e; 6 × Cl (X) 6 e: 12 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is W(VI), d⁰; d⁰ + 12 e from the ligands = 12 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of W?","o":[{"x":"d⁰"},{"x":"d¹"},{"x":"d²"},{"x":"d³"}],"t":"Tungsten hexachloride","f":"WCl₆","fig":{"m":2405},"ex":"Covalent count: W (group 6) 6 e; 6 × Cl (X) 6 e: 12 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is W(VI), d⁰; d⁰ + 12 e from the ligands = 12 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Linear"},{"x":"Trigonal planar"}],"t":"Tungsten hexachloride","f":"WCl₆","fig":{"m":2405},"ex":"Octahedral. Covalent count: W (group 6) 6 e; 6 × Cl (X) 6 e: 12 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is W(VI), d⁰; d⁰ + 12 e from the ligands = 12 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ta have?","o":[{"x":"10 e"},{"x":"11 e"},{"x":"9 e"},{"x":"12 e"}],"t":"Pentamethyltantalum","f":"TaMe₅","fig":{"m":2406},"ex":"Covalent count: Ta (group 5) 5 e; 5 × Me (X) 5 e: 10 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −5 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ta(V), d⁰; d⁰ + 10 e from the ligands = 10 e. Square pyramidal, not trigonal bipyramidal: d⁰ ML₅ with only σ-donors often prefers the pyramid.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ta?","o":[{"x":"+5"},{"x":"+6"},{"x":"+4"},{"x":"+7"}],"t":"Pentamethyltantalum","f":"TaMe₅","fig":{"m":2406},"ex":"Covalent count: Ta (group 5) 5 e; 5 × Me (X) 5 e: 10 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −5 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ta(V), d⁰; d⁰ + 10 e from the ligands = 10 e. Square pyramidal, not trigonal bipyramidal: d⁰ ML₅ with only σ-donors often prefers the pyramid.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ta?","o":[{"x":"d⁰"},{"x":"d¹"},{"x":"d²"},{"x":"d³"}],"t":"Pentamethyltantalum","f":"TaMe₅","fig":{"m":2406},"ex":"Covalent count: Ta (group 5) 5 e; 5 × Me (X) 5 e: 10 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −5 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ta(V), d⁰; d⁰ + 10 e from the ligands = 10 e. Square pyramidal, not trigonal bipyramidal: d⁰ ML₅ with only σ-donors often prefers the pyramid.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Square pyramidal"},{"x":"Trigonal bipyramidal"},{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Linear"}],"t":"Pentamethyltantalum","f":"TaMe₅","fig":{"m":2406},"ex":"Square pyramidal. Covalent count: Ta (group 5) 5 e; 5 × Me (X) 5 e: 10 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −5 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ta(V), d⁰; d⁰ + 10 e from the ligands = 10 e. Square pyramidal, not trigonal bipyramidal: d⁰ ML₅ with only σ-donors often prefers the pyramid.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ta have?","o":[{"x":"10 e"},{"x":"9 e"},{"x":"11 e"},{"x":"8 e"}],"t":"Schrock's first alkylidene","f":"Ta(CH₂t-Bu)₃(=CHt-Bu)","fig":{"m":2407},"ex":"Covalent count: Ta (group 5) 5 e; 1 × =CHt-Bu (X₂) 2 e; 3 × CH₂t-Bu (X) 3 e: 10 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −5 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ta(V), d⁰; d⁰ + 10 e from the ligands = 10 e. A Schrock alkylidene is an X2 ligand: Ta(V), d⁰.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ta?","o":[{"x":"+5"},{"x":"+4"},{"x":"+6"},{"x":"+3"}],"t":"Schrock's first alkylidene","f":"Ta(CH₂t-Bu)₃(=CHt-Bu)","fig":{"m":2407},"ex":"Covalent count: Ta (group 5) 5 e; 1 × =CHt-Bu (X₂) 2 e; 3 × CH₂t-Bu (X) 3 e: 10 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −5 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ta(V), d⁰; d⁰ + 10 e from the ligands = 10 e. A Schrock alkylidene is an X2 ligand: Ta(V), d⁰.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ta?","o":[{"x":"d⁰"},{"x":"d¹"},{"x":"d²"},{"x":"d³"}],"t":"Schrock's first alkylidene","f":"Ta(CH₂t-Bu)₃(=CHt-Bu)","fig":{"m":2407},"ex":"Covalent count: Ta (group 5) 5 e; 1 × =CHt-Bu (X₂) 2 e; 3 × CH₂t-Bu (X) 3 e: 10 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −5 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ta(V), d⁰; d⁰ + 10 e from the ligands = 10 e. A Schrock alkylidene is an X2 ligand: Ta(V), d⁰.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Tetrahedral"},{"x":"Square planar"},{"x":"Square pyramidal"},{"x":"Trigonal bipyramidal"}],"t":"Schrock's first alkylidene","f":"Ta(CH₂t-Bu)₃(=CHt-Bu)","fig":{"m":2407},"ex":"Tetrahedral. Covalent count: Ta (group 5) 5 e; 1 × =CHt-Bu (X₂) 2 e; 3 × CH₂t-Bu (X) 3 e: 10 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −5 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ta(V), d⁰; d⁰ + 10 e from the ligands = 10 e. A Schrock alkylidene is an X2 ligand: Ta(V), d⁰.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Mo have?","o":[{"x":"14 e"},{"x":"13 e"},{"x":"15 e"},{"x":"16 e"}],"t":"Schrock's catalyst","f":"Mo(=NAr)(=CHCMe₂Ph)(OCMe(CF₃)₂)₂","fig":{"m":2408},"ex":"Covalent count: Mo (group 6) 6 e; 1 × =NAr (LX₂) 4 e; 1 × =CHCMe₂Ph (X₂) 2 e; 2 × OCMe(CF₃)₂ (X) 2 e: 14 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mo(VI), d⁰; d⁰ + 14 e from the ligands = 14 e. Counting the linear imido as a 4-electron LX2 ligand; Mo(VI), d⁰.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Mo?","o":[{"x":"+6"},{"x":"+5"},{"x":"+7"},{"x":"+8"}],"t":"Schrock's catalyst","f":"Mo(=NAr)(=CHCMe₂Ph)(OCMe(CF₃)₂)₂","fig":{"m":2408},"ex":"Covalent count: Mo (group 6) 6 e; 1 × =NAr (LX₂) 4 e; 1 × =CHCMe₂Ph (X₂) 2 e; 2 × OCMe(CF₃)₂ (X) 2 e: 14 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mo(VI), d⁰; d⁰ + 14 e from the ligands = 14 e. Counting the linear imido as a 4-electron LX2 ligand; Mo(VI), d⁰.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Mo?","o":[{"x":"d⁰"},{"x":"d¹"},{"x":"d²"},{"x":"d³"}],"t":"Schrock's catalyst","f":"Mo(=NAr)(=CHCMe₂Ph)(OCMe(CF₃)₂)₂","fig":{"m":2408},"ex":"Covalent count: Mo (group 6) 6 e; 1 × =NAr (LX₂) 4 e; 1 × =CHCMe₂Ph (X₂) 2 e; 2 × OCMe(CF₃)₂ (X) 2 e: 14 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mo(VI), d⁰; d⁰ + 14 e from the ligands = 14 e. Counting the linear imido as a 4-electron LX2 ligand; Mo(VI), d⁰.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Tetrahedral"},{"x":"Square planar"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Octahedral"}],"t":"Schrock's catalyst","f":"Mo(=NAr)(=CHCMe₂Ph)(OCMe(CF₃)₂)₂","fig":{"m":2408},"ex":"Tetrahedral. Covalent count: Mo (group 6) 6 e; 1 × =NAr (LX₂) 4 e; 1 × =CHCMe₂Ph (X₂) 2 e; 2 × OCMe(CF₃)₂ (X) 2 e: 14 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Mo(VI), d⁰; d⁰ + 14 e from the ligands = 14 e. Counting the linear imido as a 4-electron LX2 ligand; Mo(VI), d⁰.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does W have?","o":[{"x":"12 e"},{"x":"13 e"},{"x":"11 e"},{"x":"14 e"}],"t":"Schrock alkylidyne","f":"W(≡Ct-Bu)(Ot-Bu)₃","fig":{"m":2409},"ex":"Covalent count: W (group 6) 6 e; 1 × ≡Ct-Bu (X₃) 3 e; 3 × Ot-Bu (X) 3 e: 12 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is W(VI), d⁰; d⁰ + 12 e from the ligands = 12 e. An alkyne-metathesis catalyst. The alkylidyne is X3.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation stat