 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 16 e from the ligands = 16 e. The reactive species released from Tebbe's reagent.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ti?","o":[{"x":"+4"},{"x":"+5"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+2"}],"t":"Tebbe methylidene","f":"Cp₂Ti=CH₂","fig":{"m":2410},"ex":"Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e; 1 × =CH₂ (X₂) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 16 e from the ligands = 16 e. The reactive species released from Tebbe's reagent.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ti?","o":[{"x":"d⁰"},{"x":"d¹"},{"x":"d²"},{"x":"d³"}],"t":"Tebbe methylidene","f":"Cp₂Ti=CH₂","fig":{"m":2410},"ex":"Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e; 1 × =CH₂ (X₂) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 16 e from the ligands = 16 e. The reactive species released from Tebbe's reagent.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ti have?","o":[{"x":"16 e"},{"x":"15 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"18 e"}],"t":"Petasis reagent","f":"Cp₂TiMe₂","fig":{"m":2411},"ex":"Covalent count: Ti (group 4) 4 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e; 2 × Me (X) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ti(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 16 e from the ligands = 16 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.10