plex is neutral, so the metal is Zr(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 16 e from the ligands = 16 e. Hydrozirconation of alkenes and alkynes.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Zr?","o":[{"x":"d⁰"},{"x":"d¹"},{"x":"d²"},{"x":"d³"}],"t":"Schwartz's reagent","f":"Cp₂Zr(H)Cl","fig":{"m":2412},"ex":"Covalent count: Zr (group 4) 4 e; 2 × Cp (L₂X) 10 e; 1 × H (X) 1 e; 1 × Cl (X) 1 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Zr(IV), d⁰; d⁰ + 16 e from the ligands = 16 e. Hydrozirconation of alkenes and alkynes.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ru have?","o":[{"x":"16 e"},{"x":"15 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"14 e"}],"t":"Grubbs catalyst, 2nd generation","f":"RuCl₂(SIMes)(PCy₃)(=CHPh)","fig":{"m":2413},"ex":"Covalent count: Ru (group 8) 8 e; 1 × =CHPh (L) 2 e; 1 × SIMes (L) 2 e; 2 × Cl (X) 2 e; 1 × PCy₃ (L) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ru(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 10 e from the ligands = 16 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Ru?","o":[{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+4"}],"t":"Grubbs catalyst, 2nd generation","f":"RuCl₂(SIMes)(PCy₃)(=CHPh)","fig":{"m":2413},"ex":"Covalent count: Ru (group 8) 8 e; 1 × =CHPh (L) 2 e; 1 × SIMes (L) 2 e; 2 × Cl (X) 2 e; 1 × PCy₃ (L) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ru(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 10 e from the ligands = 16 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ru?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁴"}],"t":"Grubbs catalyst, 2nd generation","f":"RuCl₂(SIMes)(PCy₃)(=CHPh)","fig":{"m":2413},"ex":"Covalent count: Ru (group 8) 8 e; 1 × =CHPh (L) 2 e; 1 × SIMes (L) 2 e; 2 × Cl (X) 2 e; 1 × PCy₃ (L) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ru(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 10 e from the ligands = 16 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Square pyramidal"},{"x":"Trigonal bipyramidal"},{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Square planar"}],"t":"Grubbs catalyst, 2nd generation","f":"RuCl₂(SIMes)(PCy₃)(=CHPh)","fig":{"m":2413},"ex":"Square pyramidal. Covalent count: Ru (group 8) 8 e; 1 × =CHPh (L) 2 e; 1 × SIMes (L) 2 e; 2 × Cl (X) 2 e; 1 × PCy₃ (L) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ru(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 10 e from the ligands = 16 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Ru have?","o":[{"x":"16 e"},{"x":"15 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"14 e"}],"t":"Hoveyda–Grubbs catalyst, 2nd generation","f":"RuCl₂(SIMes)(=CH-o-Oi-PrC₆H₄)","fig":{"m":2414},"ex":"Covalent count: Ru (group 8) 8 e; 1 × =CH(C₆H₄-o-Oi-Pr) (L₂) 4 e; 2 × Cl (X) 2 e; 1 × SIMes (L) 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −2 in all and the complex is neutral, so the metal is Ru(II), d⁶; d⁶ + 10 e from the ligands = 16 e. The ether oxygen of the chelating benzylidene replaces PCy₃ as the L ligand.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation st