 in all and the complex has a 1+ charge, so the metal is Re(V), d²; d² + 16 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Re?","o":[{"x":"+5"},{"x":"+4"},{"x":"+6"},{"x":"+7"}],"t":"Dioxotetrapyridinerhenium(V)","f":"trans-[ReO₂(py)₄]⁺","fig":{"m":2422},"ex":"Covalent count: Re (group 7) 7 e; 2 × O (X₂) 4 e; 4 × py (L) 8 e; the 1+ charge removes 1 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex has a 1+ charge, so the metal is Re(V), d²; d² + 16 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Re?","o":[{"x":"d²"},{"x":"d³"},{"x":"d¹"},{"x":"d⁰"}],"t":"Dioxotetrapyridinerhenium(V)","f":"trans-[ReO₂(py)₄]⁺","fig":{"m":2422},"ex":"Covalent count: Re (group 7) 7 e; 2 × O (X₂) 4 e; 4 × py (L) 8 e; the 1+ charge removes 1 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex has a 1+ charge, so the metal is Re(V), d²; d² + 16 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Trigonal planar"},{"x":"Tetrahedral"}],"t":"Dioxotetrapyridinerhenium(V)","f":"trans-[ReO₂(py)₄]⁺","fig":{"m":2422},"ex":"Octahedral. Covalent count: Re (group 7) 7 e; 2 × O (X₂) 4 e; 4 × py (L) 8 e; the 1+ charge removes 1 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex has a 1+ charge, so the metal is Re(V), d²; d² + 16 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electro