e 2− charge adds 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex has a 2− charge, so the metal is Ni(II), d⁸; d⁸ + 8 e from the ligands = 16 e. Tetrahedral, although Ni(II) is d⁸: chloride is a weak-field ligand, unlike cyanide in square-planar [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Ni?","o":[{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁶"}],"t":"Tetrachloronickelate","f":"[NiCl₄]²⁻","fig":{"m":2441},"ex":"Covalent count: Ni (group 10) 10 e; 4 × Cl (X) 4 e; the 2− charge adds 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex has a 2− charge, so the metal is Ni(II), d⁸; d⁸ + 8 e from the ligands = 16 e. Tetrahedral, although Ni(II) is d⁸: chloride is a weak-field ligand, unlike cyanide in square-planar [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Tetrahedral"},{"x":"Square planar"},{"x":"Trigonal planar"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"}],"t":"Tetrachloronickelate","f":"[NiCl₄]²⁻","fig":{"m":2441},"ex":"Tetrahedral. Covalent count: Ni (group 10) 10 e; 4 × Cl (X) 4 e; the 2− charge adds 2 e: 16 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex has a 2− charge, so the metal is Ni(II), d⁸; d⁸ + 8 e from the ligands = 16 e. Tetrahedral, although Ni(II) is d⁸: chloride is a weak-field ligand, unlike cyanide in square-planar [Ni(CN)₄]²⁻.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Cu have?","o":[{"x":"21 e"},{"x":"22 e"},{"x":"20 e"},{"x":"19 e"}],"t":"Hexaaquacopper(II)","f":"[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺","fig":{"m":2442},"ex":"Covalent count: Cu (group 11) 11 e; 6 × H₂O (L) 12 e; the 2+ charge removes 2 e: 21 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 2+ charge, so the metal is Cu(II), d⁹; d⁹ + 12 e from the ligands = 21 e. Jahn–Teller distorted: two water molecules sit far from the copper.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Cu?","o":[{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"0"}],"t":"Hexaaquacopper(II)","f":"[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺","fig":{"m":2442},"ex":"Covalent count: Cu (group 11) 11 e; 6 × H₂O (L) 12 e; the 2+ charge removes 2 e: 21 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 2+ charge, so the metal is Cu(II), d⁹; d⁹ + 12 e from the ligands = 21 e. Jahn–Teller distorted: two water molecules sit far from the copper.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Cu?","o":[{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d¹⁰"},{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d⁷"}],"t":"Hexaaquacopper(II)","f":"[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺","fig":{"m":2442},"ex":"Covalent count: Cu (group 11) 11 e; 6 × H₂O (L) 12 e; the 2+ charge removes 2 e: 21 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 2+ charge, so the metal is Cu(II), d⁹; d⁹ + 12 e from the ligands = 21 e. Jahn–Teller distorted: two water molecules sit far from the copper.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Square pyramidal"},{"x":"Linear"}],"t":"Hexaaquacopper(II)","f":"[Cu(H₂O)₆]²⁺","fig":{"m":2442},"ex":"Octahedral. Covalent count: Cu (group 11) 11 e; 6 × H₂O (L) 12 e; the 2+ charge removes 2 e: 21 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 2+ charge, so the metal is Cu(II), d⁹; d⁹ + 12 e from the ligands = 21 e. Jahn–Teller distorted: two water molecules sit far from the copper.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Cu have?","o":[{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"16 e"},{"x":"15 e"}],"t":"Tetrachlorocuprate","f":"[CuCl₄]²⁻","fig":{"m":2443},"ex":"Covalent count: Cu (group 11) 11 e; 4 × Cl (X) 4 e; the 2− charge adds 2 e: 17 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex has a 2− charge, so the metal is Cu(II), d⁹; d⁹ + 8 e from the ligands = 17 e. A flattened tetrahedron: d⁹ Cu(II) is Jahn–Teller distorted.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Cu?","o":[{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+4"}],"t":"Tetrachlorocuprate","f":"[CuCl₄]²⁻","fig":{"m":2443},"ex":"Covalent count: Cu (group 11) 11 e; 4 × Cl (X) 4 e; the 2− charge adds 2 e: 17 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex has a 2− charge, so the metal is Cu(II), d⁹; d⁹ + 8 e from the ligands = 17 e. A flattened tetrahedron: d⁹ Cu(II) is Jahn–Teller distorted.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Cu?","o":[{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁸"},{"x":"d¹⁰"},{"x":"d⁷"}],"t":"Tetrachlorocuprate","f":"[CuCl₄]²⁻","fig":{"m":2443},"ex":"Covalent count: Cu (group 11) 11 e; 4 × Cl (X) 4 e; the 2− charge adds 2 e: 17 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex has a 2− charge, so the metal is Cu(II), d⁹; d⁹ + 8 e from the ligands = 17 e. A flattened tetrahedron: d⁹ Cu(II) is Jahn–Teller distorted.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Tetrahedral"},{"x":"Square planar"},{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Square pyramidal"}],"t":"Tetrachlorocuprate","f":"[CuCl₄]²⁻","fig":{"m":2443},"ex":"Tetrahedral. Covalent count: Cu (group 11) 11 e; 4 × Cl (X) 4 e; the 2− charge adds 2 e: 17 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −4 in all and the complex has a 2− charge, so the metal is Cu(II), d⁹; d⁹ + 8 e from the ligands = 17 e. A flattened tetrahedron: d⁹ Cu(II) is Jahn–Teller distorted.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Zn have?","o":[{"x":"22 e"},{"x":"23 e"},{"x":"21 e"},{"x":"24 e"}],"t":"Hexaaquazinc","f":"[Zn(H₂O)₆]²⁺","fig":{"m":2444},"ex":"Covalent count: Zn (group 12) 12 e; 6 × H₂O (L) 12 e; the 2+ charge removes 2 e: 22 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 2+ charge, so the metal is Zn(II), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 12 e from the ligands = 22 e. Zinc(II) is d¹⁰; counting electrons tells you little for group 12.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Zn?","o":[{"x":"+2"},{"x":"+1"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+4"}],"t":"Hexaaquazinc","f":"[Zn(H₂O)₆]²⁺","fig":{"m":2444},"ex":"Covalent count: Zn (group 12) 12 e; 6 × H₂O (L) 12 e; the 2+ charge removes 2 e: 22 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 2+ charge, so the metal is Zn(II), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 12 e from the ligands = 22 e. Zinc(II) is d¹⁰; counting electrons tells you little for group 12.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Zn?","o":[{"x":"d¹⁰"},{"x":"d⁹"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Hexaaquazinc","f":"[Zn(H₂O)₆]²⁺","fig":{"m":2444},"ex":"Covalent count: Zn (group 12) 12 e; 6 × H₂O (L) 12 e; the 2+ charge removes 2 e: 22 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 2+ charge, so the metal is Zn(II), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 12 e from the ligands = 22 e. Zinc(II) is d¹⁰; counting electrons tells you little for group 12.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Geometry","a":"What shape is it?","o":[{"x":"Octahedral"},{"x":"Trigonal prismatic"},{"x":"Square pyramidal"},{"x":"Tetrahedral"}],"t":"Hexaaquazinc","f":"[Zn(H₂O)₆]²⁺","fig":{"m":2444},"ex":"Octahedral. Covalent count: Zn (group 12) 12 e; 6 × H₂O (L) 12 e; the 2+ charge removes 2 e: 22 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry no charge and the complex has a 2+ charge, so the metal is Zn(II), d¹⁰; d¹⁰ + 12 e from the ligands = 22 e. Zinc(II) is d¹⁰; counting electrons tells you little for group 12.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Mo have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Octacyanomolybdate(IV)","f":"[Mo(CN)₈]⁴⁻","ex":"Covalent count: Mo (group 6) 6 e; 8 × CN (X) 8 e; the 4− charge adds 4 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −8 in all and the complex has a 4− charge, so the metal is Mo(IV), d²; d² + 16 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Mo?","o":[{"x":"+4"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+5"},{"x":"+6"}],"t":"Octacyanomolybdate(IV)","f":"[Mo(CN)₈]⁴⁻","ex":"Covalent count: Mo (group 6) 6 e; 8 × CN (X) 8 e; the 4− charge adds 4 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −8 in all and the complex has a 4− charge, so the metal is Mo(IV), d²; d² + 16 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Mo?","o":[{"x":"d²"},{"x":"d¹"},{"x":"d³"},{"x":"d⁴"}],"t":"Octacyanomolybdate(IV)","f":"[Mo(CN)₈]⁴⁻","ex":"Covalent count: Mo (group 6) 6 e; 8 × CN (X) 8 e; the 4− charge adds 4 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −8 in all and the complex has a 4− charge, so the metal is Mo(IV), d²; d² + 16 e from the ligands = 18 e.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Electron count","a":"How many valence electrons does Pt have?","o":[{"x":"18 e"},{"x":"19 e"},{"x":"17 e"},{"x":"20 e"}],"t":"Hexachloroplatinate","f":"[PtCl₆]²⁻","fig":{"m":2445},"ex":"Covalent count: Pt (group 10) 10 e; 6 × Cl (X) 6 e; the 2− charge adds 2 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex has a 2− charge, so the metal is Pt(IV), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. As in H₂PtCl₆, Speier's catalyst.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"Oxidation state","a":"What is the oxidation state of Pt?","o":[{"x":"+4"},{"x":"+5"},{"x":"+3"},{"x":"+6"}],"t":"Hexachloroplatinate","f":"[PtCl₆]²⁻","fig":{"m":2445},"ex":"Covalent count: Pt (group 10) 10 e; 6 × Cl (X) 6 e; the 2− charge adds 2 e: 18 e. Ionic count: the ligands carry a charge of −6 in all and the complex has a 2− charge, so the metal is Pt(IV), d⁶; d⁶ + 12 e from the ligands = 18 e. As in H₂PtCl₆, Speier's catalyst.","lk":[{"h":"Counting rules","l":[["Green & Parkin, J. Chem. Educ. 2014, 91, 807","https://doi.org/10.1021/ed400504f"]]}]},{"s":"tmc","k":"dⁿ count","a":"What is the dⁿ count of Pt?","o":[{"x":"d⁶"},{"x":"d⁵"},{"x":"d⁷"},{"x":"d⁸"}],"t":"Hexachloroplatinate","f":"[PtCl₆]²⁻","fig":{"m":2445},"ex":"C